<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-2.8> a ipbes:SubChapter ;
    ipbes:Chapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ch/NXS24ch2> ;
    ipbes:Report <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/NXS24> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "This chapter provides evidence that direct and indirect drivers that impact the nexus elements are increasing over the last five decades and negatively affecting biodiversity, water, food and health and leading to climate change. This chapter also shows that there is a complex interrelationship between the nexus elements, with decreases in biodiversity negatively impacting water, food, health and climate systems, which in turn affect human well-being and environmental sustainability. Changes in biodiversity, water, food, health and climate significantly impact people worldwide, albeit unequally. About 65 per cent of the global population lives in areas with beneficial hotspots of at least one nexus element, yet 52 per cent live in degradation hotspots, particularly affecting people in low- and lower middle-income countries who are experiencing the greatest impacts of this degradation. Indigenous Peoples and local communities perceive more negative trends than positive ones in local biodiversity, water, food, health and climate change, with negative biodiversity trends being most prominent. Economic growth, population dynamics, urbanization and institutional policies impact biodiversity, water, food, health and climate change. Economic expansion drives land and resource use changes, intensifying exploitation and pollution. Population growth and urbanization increase demand for housing, food and energy, leading to land- and sea-use changes and resource extraction. Effective governance and regulations reduce pollution but often fall short globally. Cultural drivers, such as increasing consumption, intensify resource exploitation. Armed conflicts divert political capacity from addressing climate change and biodiversity decline. These indirect drivers influence direct drivers of biodiversity loss, i.e., land- and sea-use changes, exploitation of species, climate change, pollution and invasive alien species, which in turn impact food security, clean water and health. Deforestation and agricultural expansion are major contributors to biodiversity decline, especially in tropical regions. Urban expansion exacerbates health issues due to pollution and reduced green spaces. Overfishing and resource extraction degrade ecosystems and increase greenhouse gas emissions. Agricultural pollution contaminates water and soil, posing risks to ecosystems and human health. Invasive alien species disrupt ecosystems, leading to biodiversity loss and affecting food supplies. As a consequence of these changes, the global wildlife population, for example, has declined by 69 per cent in nearly 50 years, with the greatest declines being observed in Latin America and freshwater species. Ecosystems play a central role in regulating water cycles, which are crucial for human consumption and food production, but their loss and degradation compromises not only water quantity, but also quality, with feedbacks to the other nexus elements. Biodiversity supports agriculture and food production through pollination, pest control and soil health. However, modern agricultural practices and climate change drive biodiversity loss, reducing food security and nutritional diversity, especially for smallholder farmers. Monocultures and dependence on a few crops affect ecosystem stability and resilience, contributing to diet-related diseases and other aspects of human health. Biodiversity influences human health as a source of medicines, by regulating air and water quality and by improving mental health. Biodiversity loss increases the risk of zoonotic and some vector-borne diseases as human activities encroach on natural habitats. Climate change stresses biodiversity, altering species distributions and ecosystem functions, which cascades to other nexus elements. Water quantity and quality as well as food security can be directly impacted by climate change, while direct and indirect effects on human health are also observed. The failure to recognize the interconnectedness of the nexus elements and of these direct and indirect drivers is a key reason why only 18-20 per cent of SDG targets are on track for 2030, and why challenges in meeting the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework and Paris Agreement targets are also likely to occur." ;
    dcterms:identifier "2.8" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Conclusion" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-3.8> a ipbes:SubChapter ;
    ipbes:Chapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ch/NXS24ch3> ;
    ipbes:Report <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/NXS24> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Understanding future interactions between nexus elements through the analysis of scenarios and other diverse visions of the future, including the understandings of IPLC, is critical to support policy and management actions today and contribute to sustainable resource management and just outcomes for nature and people. Scenarios of continuation of current trends place biodiversity at risk and have negative implications for water, food, health, climate change and NCP. These scenarios also indicate that the decline in biodiversity will continue unless rapid, integrated and transformative change is undertaken across the nexus. On the other hand, scenarios exploring expansion of nature conservation deliver positive outcomes for all nexus elements when planned in an integrated and just manner and coupled with broader measures such as climate change mitigation, changes in food production, equitable consumption and sustainable management. Consequently, transformative change across the food system is central to unlocking co-benefits for biodiversity, water, nutritional health and climate change. Human-induced climate change is expected to impact the entire nexus, becoming worse over the coming decades. In this respect, scenarios indicate the importance of early climate change mitigation actions, with further delays expected to be more costly and leading to additional trade-offs. In general, scenarios leading to better outcomes for the nexus elements tend to also support achieving globally agreed policy goals such as the SDGs. However, they require flexible and well-functioning institutions, mainstreaming and enforcing environmental and pro-sustainability regulations and facilitating cooperation between countries and societal sectors such as governments, non-governmental organizations and businesses, as well as sustainable consumption and production patterns." ;
    dcterms:identifier "3.8" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Conclusion" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-4.1> a ipbes:SubChapter ;
    ipbes:Chapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ch/NXS24ch4> ;
    ipbes:Report <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/NXS24> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "While current challenges of biodiversity loss and its impact on human well-being are rooted in previous decisions and actions, future options for responding to social-ecological changes are shaped by decisions and actions taken today. The imperative to act swiftly to address interlinked human and ecological crises has been highlighted by numerous science-policy assessments in recent years (see Section 1.4.2). While these have provided a suite of options for addressing many environmental and development challenges, none have had an explicit nexus focus. In particular, none have identified the policy and sociopolitical options, and the actors and institutions involved in decision-making processes, that can lead to just and sustainable outcomes by explicitly considering the interlinkages, feedbacks and relationships between biodiversity, water, food, health and climate change. It is therefore important to understand how existing frameworks and options for decision-making can consider nexus issues, such as trade-offs, opportunities for transitions, benefits and burdens, in addition to identifying who needs to act, at what scale and with what mechanisms to amplify synergies and reduce entrenched social-ecological inequities. In previous chapters of the assessment, the conceptual foundations for the nexus and five nexus challenges have been established (Chapter 1), and key trends, drivers (Chapter 2) and pathways for more just and sustainable futures (Chapter 3) have been assessed. The aim of this chapter is to identify, assess, synthesize and classify existing policy and sociopolitical frameworks and options that address biodiversity, water, food, health and climate change issues. This includes classifying and identifying response options that address the nexus challenges introduced in Section 1.1.2. Chapter 4 also identifies cross-cutting issues and enablers and barriers that impact these options, as well as decision-support tools for facilitating holistic decisions across different sectors, actor groups, institutions and scales. These policy and sociopolitical options, decision-support tools and frameworks have been assessed in terms of their strengths for improved governance of nexus interlinkages. This informs further development of the concept of nexus governance, a term increasingly used in the scientific literature, but which remains incomplete and poorly implemented in practice. Based on reviews of the nexus governance literature and existing theory and implementation gaps, the chapter defines the core components of nexus governance that enable improved holistic decision-making and identifies the related policy and sociopolitical options that have transformative potential. This broad framework serves as a basis for Chapters 5 and 6 to assess and outline actionable response options across the nexus for catalysing change, which are then summarized in Chapter 7 into a practical implementation roadmap for just and sustainable futures. Chapter 4 focuses on addressing the following policy-relevant question from in Section 1.1.3: “Which factors (economic and financial, technical, social, political, institutional, cultural and behavioural) facilitate or obstruct pathways to achieve a sustainable future, and which tools and policy options exist to help decision makers navigate the complexity of the nexus and take into account the existence of a diversity of values, including perspectives and knowledge of Indigenous Peoples and local communities (IPLC)?”. Within this broad framing, the chapter addresses specific questions to elaborate on key concepts and concerns, and answers these through reviews of key literature: 1. What policy and sociopolitical options have been used to guide decision-making across the nexus, in which contexts and across which scales? (acknowledging the diversity of contexts and scale mismatches) 2. How are these policy and sociopolitical options supported, amplified or constrained through specific enabling conditions and tools? 3. Which actors and arenas of engagement are involved in the governance of nexus interactions and which are key influencers that can facilitate and accelerate change? 4. What are key features that need to be considered for improved nexus governance and how can more transformative potential be realized? This chapter consists of eight sections, which link to other assessment chapters as indicated in Figure 4.1. * Section 4.1 describes the background, objectives and overall structure of the chapter. A glossary of key terms for the chapter is provided in Table 4.1. * Section 4.2 provides key definitions related to policy and sociopolitical options linked to governing nexus interactions, and reviews existing approaches and challenges of governance generally. It also reviews existing approaches to how nexus governance has been defined in the literature as a specific form of governance, and what gaps exist in these approaches. * Section 4.3 identifies, assesses and categorizes a range of governance approaches and policy instruments and their suitability for solving the key nexus challenges across scales, based on a systematic review of literature. This section synthesises existing evidence and gaps, with a focus on what governance approaches and policy instruments have been used to address nexus challenges, including assessment of their key enablers and barriers. * Section 4.4 outlines different scaling processes (scaling up to institutionalize response options, scaling out to increase their uptake in other contexts, scaling down to provincialize them in local unique contexts, and scaling deep to change mindsets and values that underpin the design and adoption of responses) and explores the enabling mechanisms for these scaling processes. * Section 4.5 returns to the concept of nexus governance and synthesizes findings from the systematic review together with reviews of identified gaps around actors and arenas of engagement, the role of IPLC and the importance of equity, justice and intersectionality. It combines these findings into a consolidated approach to nexus governance, identifying key components to help address how governance should be framed, who should be involved, what should be included, and where and when should it be enacted to improve implementation and scaling of policy and sociopolitical options. * Section 4.6 focuses on reviewing existing decision-support tools for various users at different scales that can facilitate decision-making to support the different components of nexus governance. * Section 4.7 identifies key remaining knowledge gaps concerning the policy and sociopolitical options governing the nexus and highlights emerging issues, blind spots and limitations in the current academic literature regarding these options. * Section 4.8 summarizes the chapter and presents conclusions. Figure 4.1. Structure of Chapter 4. The figure includes a summary of the contents of the different sections (4.2 to 4.7) and how they connect, flow and link to or from other sections in Chapter 4, and other chapters of the assessment. Table 4.1. Glossary of key terms of Chapter 4. Policy and sociopolitical options A range of theoretical and practical frameworks for implementing sustainable management approaches, including diverse sets of human actions to address specific issues, needs, objectives and opportunities in the governance and management of nexus challenges (see Section 1.1.2 for further detail on the nexus challenges). The policy and sociopolitical options that are the focus of this chapter include those that account for a diversity of world views, behaviours, norms and values and which are aimed at accelerating transitions towards just and sustainable futures. Nexus governance Refers to the systematic coordinating structures and processes that enhance the engagement of multiple actors through horizontal (e.g., across various nexus elements at one scale) and vertical (e.g., cross-scale connectivity or multilevel governance) channels to address nexus challenges, identify policy and sociopolitical options, and manage their implementation towards just sustainable futures. Scaling Accelerating the adoption and implementation of policy and sociopolitical options/response options, amplifying their impacts and normalizing them as mainstream solutions to nexus challenges. Includes scaling up, scaling out, scaling down and scaling deep. Enablers and barriers Specific characteristics or contexts of a nexus challenge that facilitate or impair the implementation, operationalization or effectiveness of nexus response options (including governance approaches). Policy instruments The different interventions (formal rules, laws, social norms and processes etc.) made by decision makers (governments and public authorities, intergovernmental organizations, companies etc.) to ensure that (public) policy objectives are supported and achieved by influencing the behaviour of other stakeholders. These can include economic and financial, legal and regulatory, social and cultural, rights-based and customary policy response options to support nexus governance. Decision-support tools, including policy support Methods, approaches and techniques that can inform, assist and enhance relevant decision-making, policymaking, governance and implementation at the local, national, regional and international levels, based on science and other knowledge systems including ILK." ;
    dcterms:identifier "4.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Introduction" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-4.8> a ipbes:SubChapter ;
    ipbes:Chapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ch/NXS24ch4> ;
    ipbes:Report <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/NXS24> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Given the tremendous social-ecological challenges society is currently faced with and that impacts are not distributed equally across space and time for either people or nature, identifying and selecting interventions and implementing actions that have the greatest potential for catalysing change towards just and sustainable futures is critical. Faced with a multitude of response options, how can decision makers respond in ways that acknowledge diverse contexts, world views, behaviours, norms, knowledge and values, and account for changes in interconnections among nexus elements through direct and indirect drivers of change at different scales? This chapter’s assessment of existing policy and sociopolitical options across the nexus can facilitate and enable transitions towards more just and sustainable futures across scales, sectors and actor groups. This included assessing the degree to which interlinkages among nexus elements are considered within governance literatures on response options, thus providing an evidence base of options, tools and scaling mechanisms that are discussed in more detail in subsequent chapters. Specific response option design features will depend on the nature of the actors and institutions involved and the scale of implementation. The five components of nexus governance that were considered can help drive stronger engagement by multiple actors and sectors to improve decision-making by helping create a framework for integrative framings of problems, inclusive approaches to engagement, consideration of equity and justice, enhanced coordination, and more adaptive and experimental approaches (Figure 4.19). The evidence assessed shows that those options that are co-developed with these components in mind can increase transformative potential to meet nexus challenges, but some actors remain underrepresented in the nexus literature, particularly IPLC. Through this assessment, the policy and sociopolitical response options, which acknowledge enablers, trade-offs and barriers for these options, and associated decision-support tools and scaling mechanisms that have the greatest potential for facilitating change were defined. Policy design and implementation, as well as institutional capacity are the most common enablers and barriers of nexus challenges; they are important criteria to take into consideration when assessing the potential of different response options. The assessment of different scaling practices (up, out, down and deep) provides a useful framing for response options to help accelerate their adoption and implementation, amplify their impacts and normalize them as mainstream solutions to nexus challenges (Figure 4.19). Finally, decision-support tools can assist in advancing nexus governance and response option selection. While most decision-support tools that address nexus challenges were assessed to be holistic, the most common tools focused on assembling data and knowledge (including monitoring) and assessment and evaluation, which are less likely to be inclusive (e.g., do not address gender dynamics, include ILK or local languages, or use participatory approaches) and have less transformative potential (e.g., do not incorporate diverse values, foster institutional change, or are integrative and adaptive) than other types of tools. This argues for a development of new tools to fill these gaps and the expansion of existing tools to address nexus governance needs. Figure 4.19. The components of nexus governance discussed in Chapter 4. The components ensure effective implementation of policy and sociopolitical response options towards a range of sustainable and just futures for current and future generations. Nexus governance requires the mobilisation and strengthening of nexus governance capacities which include enhancing analytical, bridging, negotiation, social networking and motivational capacities (Section 4.5.5, Table 4.18). These nexus governance capacities are needed for coordinated action of actors and institutions working in a variety of arenas of engagement to drive change (Section 4.5.1; Table 4.7). Enhanced nexus governance requires several components including integrative and holistic framings, inclusive approaches, considerations of equity and accountability and processes for collaboration and coordination that are adaptive, reflexive and experimental (Section 4.5.4; Figure 4.17). These components are important for guiding the implementation and scaling of response options to address negative direct drivers and indirect drivers of change and unsustainable and unjust values and behaviour (Section 4.4; Figure 4.14)." ;
    dcterms:identifier "4.8" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Summary and conclusions" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.0.3> a ipbes:SubChapter ;
    ipbes:Chapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ch/NXS24ch5.0> ;
    ipbes:Report <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/NXS24> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "No assessment can evaluate all possible response options, given the significant diversity of contexts and world views and the reality that innovation is continually resulting in new response options. Subchapters within Chapter 5 do not offer comprehensive sets of response options. Instead, each subchapter identifies and assesses a representative set of response options that involve a range of actors, spatial and temporal scales, feasibility levels and impacts across the nexus. Because this chapter cannot be exhaustive, it is important to be transparent about how response options were chosen for assessment and how a subset of these was selected for more detailed analysis. Subchapters followed a common overall approach, adapted by each subchapter (and described in each): 1. Develop a long list of candidate response options. Author teams generated a list of potential response options to assess, based on peer-reviewed literature, grey literature, and previous assessments. 2. Choose response options to assess. From the list generated in step one, author teams selected a subset of ~15 response options to evaluate, based on authors’ expertise and knowledge of the literature. The selected response options are not intended as priorities or recommendations. Rather, they are chosen as a subset of response options with likely impacts on other elements, with sufficient evidence for rigorous assessment and which represent a range of potential responses by key actors within that nexus element. 3. Assess response options. The chosen response options from step two were assessed with a more formal literature review. Literature review methodologies varied among subchapters, but all combined database searches with expert knowledge of key publications and other literature cited therein. Based on assessed evidence, subchapter teams scored each response option against a common set of 10 criteria (Table 5.0.1), which facilitate comparison and synthesis within and among nexus elements. These criteria were deemed essential for understanding important attributes of each response option, including its potential to produce benefits across multiple elements of the nexus, its feasibility and breadth of applicability, its impact on equity and its potential to advance the goals of existing global policy frameworks. 4. Focal response options. From the set of response options assessed in step three, author teams selected four or five to describe and analyse in more depth. Again, these response options are not meant to represent priorities or recommendations. They are chosen to illustrate the range of issues and actors involved in response options, via more detailed descriptions and examples of real-world application. Table 5.0.1. Ten criteria used in the Chapter 5 subchapters to assess and compare response options. For each criterion, indicators were identified and explained conceptually in the form of the key question addressed. Details on indicators and scoring are provided in a data management report,2 and context dependence for scoring is described in each subchapter. Criterion Indicators Explanation Spatial scale Scale of intervention At what scale is the response option typically implemented? Extent of applicability Over how wide an area does the response option apply? Temporal scale Temporal scale How quickly can impacts from this response option be realized? Impact on each element of the nexus Biodiversity (6 indicators) To what extent, and how, does the response option affect the other nexus elements? Water (2 indicators) Food (3 indicators) Health (3 indicators) Climate change (2 indicators) Social acceptability Social acceptability To what extent does the response option suit the sociocultural values of stakeholders? Alignment with political priorities Alignment with political priorities To what extent is the response option aligned with current policies and has support from leaders, stakeholders, local communities and the public? Economic implications Gross economic costs To what extent do economic factors enable or constrain the implementation of the response option? Individual economic costs Economic benefits Economic risks Technical feasibility Technical feasibility How straightforward is the response option to deploy currently? Equity Distributional equity To what degree is there fair distribution of the costs and benefits of the response option? Inclusion and/or recognition To what degree are multiple identities, knowledge, world views and values recognized? Procedural equity To what degree does the response option enable inclusive and participatory processes, particularly for IPLC? Alignment with international frameworks Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework How strongly does the response option advance the goals or targets of each framework? Paris Agreement Sustainable Development Goals Transformative change potential Social, cultural and technical innovation To what degree does the response option facilitate new actions, practices, habits or innovations? Structures and institutions To what degree does the response option enable structural or institutional change, shifts in power relations and imbalances, or new forms of agency? Views and values To what degree does the response option facilitate shifts in views and values towards alternative futures?" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.0.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Selecting response options for assessment" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.0.4> a ipbes:SubChapter ;
    ipbes:Chapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ch/NXS24ch5.0> ;
    ipbes:Report <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/NXS24> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "This chapter is for actors – individuals or groups that are directly or indirectly, formally or informally, associated with nexus elements and response options (Section 1.3.3). Many actors play a role in managing elements within the nexus, including water resources managers, IPLC, public health officials, conservation organizations, agricultural businesses, municipalities and elected leaders. All have opportunities to devise and implement responses that take account of nexus interactions (Chapters 2 and 3) and address nexus challenges (Section 1.1.2) as described so far in this assessment. The chapter is therefore organized to allow actors within each nexus element to find the information that is most relevant to them through five subchapters: biodiversity, water, food, health and climate change. Each subchapter identifies and assesses specific, evidence-based response options available to actors within the corresponding nexus element, emphasizing response options with strong potential to benefit other elements. The chapter concludes with a synthesis that analyses trends in response options, identifies those that are shared among multiple nexus elements, examines trade-offs and synergies among responses options, and highlights links between response options and global goals (i.e., SDGs, Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework targets and the Paris Agreement long-term goals for mitigation and adaptation). All subchapters follow a shared structure and methodology to ensure consistency in the information presented and facilitate comparisons and syntheses among subchapters (“X” represents the subchapter number): 5.X.1: Role of the nexus element (e.g., biodiversity) in the nexus and the important interactions connecting it to the other nexus elements. 5.X.2: Key actors involved in managing the element or who are impacted by its management, including actor categories and examples to make clear the core audience for each subchapter. Actors are categorized following the definitions in Chapter 1 (Section 1.3.3) and Chapter 4 (Section 4.3.4). 5.X.3: Response options available to key actors within the nexus element. Subchapters each present available evidence supporting ~15 response options, chosen to represent a range of potential responses to nexus challenges and with potential benefits in several nexus elements (Section 5.0.3). Along with brief narrative descriptions, subchapters present the scoring of response options on a common set of criteria (Table 5.0.1) and compare them in common figures. For five response options (referred to hereafter as focal response options), subchapters present greater detail on evidence, key actors, enabling conditions and impacts across nexus elements. Each focal response option includes a case study that presents a real-world example of its implementation. 5.X.4: Nexus governance addresses the contextual factors that influence response option success, including enabling conditions, types of policy instruments and implementing institutions, institutional capacities, financial systems and involvement of required actors, including the role of world views, behaviours, norms and values. This section includes tables outlining the specific roles played by actor types in each set of response options, as well as general categories of enablers and barriers most relevant to successful implementation. 5.X.5: Knowledge gaps revealed from the assessment of available evidence. 5.X.6: Synthesis and conclusions" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.0.4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Chapter design" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.0.5> a ipbes:SubChapter ;
    ipbes:Chapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ch/NXS24ch5.0> ;
    ipbes:Report <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/NXS24> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "There are several ways to read this chapter. For actors whose roles fall largely within a given element of the nexus (e.g. water managers), the corresponding subchapter (i.e., 5.2 for water) provides evidence and analysis relevant to response options in that element. For those seeking an overview of response options across nexus elements, the executive summaries and synthesis sections for each chapter provide this broad view. For an overall synthesis of response options that cuts across all subchapters, subchapter 5.6 provides this summary. For those seeking inspiration on implementation, case study boxes within each subchapter highlight real-world examples of response options in action." ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.0.5" ;
    skos:prefLabel "How to read this chapter" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.1.2> a ipbes:SubChapter ;
    ipbes:Chapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ch/NXS24ch5.1> ;
    ipbes:Report <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/NXS24> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Biodiversity actors are the formal or informal collectives, organizations and/or individuals who act politically, economically and socially to safeguard biodiversity, share its benefits and promote its sustainable use (Table 5.1.3). Actors who influence nature conservation, either positively or negatively, in design and implementation of response options for biodiversity are also considered. Actors can be as powerful as national governments, private companies and large NGOs, but can also be those under-represented in power structures, such as IPLC. Table 5.1.3. Examples of biodiversity actors involved in the implementation of biodiversity response options. See Section 1.3.3 and Table 1.1 for actor category descriptions. Actor category Actors/institutions Global and regional institutions * Global conventions and agreements targeting biodiversity (e.g., Convention on Biological Diversity and the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework; Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora; Ramsar Convention on Wetlands; Convention on the Conservation of Migratory Species of Wild Animals; United Nations Convention on the Law of the Sea) * Regional programs and agreements (e.g., The Atlantic Forest (Mata Atlantica) Restoration Pact; Pacific Regional Environment Programme; Nauru Agreement Concerning Cooperation in the Management of Fisheries of Common Interest) * Global and regional institutions that address environmental conservation and sustainable use of biodiversity (e.g., Global Environment Facility; Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations; European Union; United Nations; United Nations Educational, Scientific and Cultural Organization National governments * National environmental agencies * Agencies in charge of landscape and seascape planning and sustainable use of biodiversity Local governments and municipalities * Agencies of provincial and municipal governments with authority in environment, fauna and flora, landscape and seascape planning and urban development, as well as non-environmental agencies (e.g., development or finance) Knowledge and educational communities * Universities, research centres, research groups working on biodiversity conservation and sustainable use, often developing applied research to guide and monitor response options * Members of IPLC holding and transmitting ILK related to conserving biodiversity, customary sustainable use, culturally appropriate educational systems, and sustainable use * Local schools Civil society organizations * International conservation organizations (e.g., Birdlife International; Conservation International; International Union for the Conservation of Nature; The Nature Conservancy; Wildlife Conservation Society; World Wildlife Fund) * Local and regional environmental organizations (e.g., Australian Marine Conservation Society; Aves Argentina; Aves Uruguay; Bird Society of Northeastern Scania; CORDIO; Defensa y Conservacion Ecologica de Intag) * Philanthropic organizations (e.g., David and Lucile Packard Foundation; Fundación Slim) * Network organizations (e.g., Black Environment Network; Foundation for Alternative Tropical Agriculture and Sustainable Development; Tizu Valley Biodiversity Conservation and Livelihood Network; Sustainable Agriculture Network) * Producer-consumer organizations (e.g., ISEAL Alliance; Rainforest Alliance) Community-based organizations * Community biodiversity user groups, associations and cooperatives; community conserved areas joint management committees Indigenous Peoples and local communities (IPLC) * Indigenous customary institutions, International Indigenous Forum for Biodiversity, Indigenous communities, Indigenous women’s groups/forums * Artisanal fishers and small-scale farmers; forest-dependent rural communities; local women Private sector and business organizations * Medium to large landowners; farmers and fishers * Agri-food, fishing and forestry industry companies and organizations * Environmentally active multinational companies and negative influencers in conservation action (e.g., some oil companies) * Tourism operators, companies and chambers * Construction sector and urban developers Science-policy interfaces * Intergovernmental science-policy bodies (e.g., IPBES; IPCC) * National platform and ecosystem assessments (e.g., Brazilian Platform for Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services; United Kingdom National Ecosystem Assessment) Financial institutions * Banks (e.g., Banco de la Mujer; African Development Bank; European Central Bank) * Large international and private funders; * Development organizations (e.g., Global Environmental Facility; United Kingdom Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office) Media and the arts * Individuals and organizations aiming to influence public awareness on biodiversity conservation and sustainable use" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.1.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Key actors and institutions" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.2.6> a ipbes:SubChapter ;
    ipbes:Chapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ch/NXS24ch5.2> ;
    ipbes:Report <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/NXS24> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "The evidence presented in this chapter underlines that water is fundamental for life, interacting with nature and society in diverse ways, and being shaped by various discourses, conceptual framings and entry points for dealing with water processes and challenges. Water, within a nexus framing, acts as a connector to all nexus elements and with water-related challenges never just being about water. Many decisions made outside of the water sector have impacts on water yet are too rarely considered, resulting in unintended consequences for other nexus elements, as well as for water (Figure 5.2.3). This subchapter reviewed the evidence for 15 (clusters of) response options originating from the nexus element of water but that were linked to other nexus elements. The water-oriented response options in general have synergies with many of the other nexus elements (Figure 5.2.3). This is particularly evident with biodiversity, and less so with food, health and climate change. Of the 15 response options, most had some impact on at least one aspect of each of the other nexus elements. Three response options (W05, Sustainable inland fisheries (Section 5.2.3.5); W09, Groundwater governance (Section 5.2.3.9); W12, Reducing pollution (Section 5.2.3.12) have positive effects across all nexus elements. Most (9 of 15) response options had moderate to high positive impact on the change in state and function of biodiversity, suggesting actors from these elements could have synergistic positive effects on (at least) water and biodiversity. However, water response option effects on food were mixed, with four response options (W03, Dam operation (Section 5.2.3.3); W04, Efficient water use in agriculture (Section 5.2.3.4); W10, Finance for water infrastructure (Section 5.2.3.10); W11, Manage alien species (Section 5.2.3.11)) having negative effects on some aspects of food, especially on changes in the environmental performance of food production. However, opportunities exist that show co-benefits between water, biodiversity and food by increasing the efficient use of water in agriculture (Section 5.2.3.4, W04), improving groundwater governance (Section 5.2.3.9, W09) and reducing pollution (Section 5.2.3.12, W12). Dam operation (Section 5.2.3.3, W03), one of the most established response options, has large potential to provide both water for agriculture and energy security but this often comes at the expense of biodiversity and the carbon accumulation potential of freshwater habitats, and can increase the risk of vector borne diseases (5.2.3.3). They also have negative impacts on climate change adaptation potential through release of greenhouse gas emissions or through environmental flows that may contradict flows needed for hydropower. Thus, the review highlights that several response options such as dam operations and financial and economic instruments, while well studied, can fail to deliver across the nexus. A nexus framing encourages water governance to move from a sectoral perspective limited in geographies such as watersheds and river basins to that of “problemsheds” that focus on interdependencies, trade-offs and synergies among nexus elements and promote interaction among traditional sectoral governance structures. Moving beyond unilateral water governance can be operationalized by nexus governance that stresses action that is informed, integrative (across sectors and scales) and inclusive of multiple stakeholders. Actors in the water element have a diversity of response options that can be implemented to benefit other nexus elements, particularly biodiversity and health, and to better manage trade-offs with other elements. The water-related response options can also be combined in bundles or sequences to address these trade-offs or enhance synergies. This is particularly important as the 15 response options were in themselves bundles of over 100 specific response options (see Section 5.2.3). This provides decision-makers the flexibility to implement response options based on evaluation of their synergies and trade-offs. For example, implementing the response options on W02, Integrated water resources infrastructure (Section 5.2.3.2), W03, Dam operation (Section 5.2.3.3), W04, Efficient water use in agriculture (Section 5.2.3.4) and W09, Groundwater governance (Section 5.2.3.9) may collectively be used to reduce water stress. By promoting conjunctive use of surface and groundwater including water storage and regulation by natural ecosystems as well as built infrastructure, integrating environmental flows in dam operations and efficient use of fertilizers may alleviate the trade-offs between water security, food production and biodiversity conservation. Implementation of the 15 response options is influenced by a range of enablers and barriers. Cross-sectoral coordination among actors from the different nexus elements is a common enabler across the response options and includes collaboration across scales and jurisdictions. Another common enabler is the need for the co-production of knowledge on interactions between water and the other nexus elements, to enable collaboration amongst actors based on managing for synergies and trade-offs. Policy effectiveness and institutional capacities were underpinning enablers across all response options, suggesting that response options are feasible if the right policies and capacities are in place. Fewer strong barriers than enablers were identified, with the most common barrier being material endowments. However, many of the response options have not yet been widely implemented, suggesting that these few barriers may be stronger than the many enablers combined. Furthermore, not all of the response options are equitable when considering distributional equity (Figure 5.2.4). This creates inequalities regarding who benefits from these response options, which can be a barrier to action. This highlights the importance of attention to power structures, politics and relationships, recognizing diverse world views and knowledge systems, and placing specific emphasis on integrating IPLC knowledge, beliefs and practices to provide an enabling environment for implementing nexus governance. The response options have social and political support (Figure 5.2.4) and all but two have high technical feasibility (Figure 5.2.5). However, despite being technologically ready, some response options, such as dam operation (W03) had some of the largest negative impacts on other nexus elements, and response options related to integrated water infrastructure (W02) and reducing pollution (W12) are associated with high economic costs (Figure 5.2.4). This may also be interlinked with the generally negative scoring for the economic cost of response options. Several response options do have high economic costs for implementation yet given that there are positive economic benefits provided by most response options, even if modest, this might suggest that benefit-cost analyses might need updating. Water-oriented response options are generally well-aligned with political priorities and international frameworks (Figure 5.2.4). All water response options contribute to at least one SDG, with five options addressing at least five SDGs (W02, Integrated water infrastructure (Section 5.2.3.2); W04, Efficient water use in agriculture (Section 5.2.3.4), W08, Transboundary water cooperation (Section 5.2.3.8); W12, Manage wastewater (Section 5.2.3.12); W13, Water-sensitive urban infrastructure (Section 5.2.3.13)) (Figure 5.2.9).9 Clean water and sanitation (SDG6) is supported by all 15 response options. Only three SDGs (SDG8, Decent work and economic growth, SDG10 Reduced inequalities and SDG17, Partnerships for the goals) are not supported by any of the response options. Every response option also supported the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework targets, with W08 Transboundary water cooperation (Section 5.2.3.8) addressing 10 targets followed by W15, Community water management (Section 5.2.3.15) addressing 9 targets. Three response options (W02, Integrated water resources infrastructure (Section 5.2.3.2); W08, Transboundary water cooperation (Section 5.2.3.8); W13, Water-sensitive urban infrastructure (Section 5.2.3.13)) addressed at least five SDGs and five Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework targets, suggesting these options provide substantial benefits across multiple global frameworks. The assessment presented in this section underlines the value of approaching contemporary water challenges from a nexus framing, and upscaling investments in implementation of response options with benefits across nexus elements. Implementation of these response options, however, requires not just technical fixes but a deeper intervention at the water governance level, enabling a range of water sector actors to engage across policy domains, governance levels and public, private and civic spheres. Advancing the application of response options also needs concurrent investments in ensuring these responses reflect diverse world views and cosmovisions, co-production of knowledge on interactions between water and the other nexus elements, and development of data and indicators on the nexus interactions of water spanning across space and time scales. Figure 5.2.9. Water response options supporting achievement of global goals. The figure shows whether an individual response option contributes to the achievement of the Sustainable Development Goals, the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework targets and the long-term goals for mitigation and adaptation of the Paris Agreement. Response options listed in order by alphanumeric code. Shaded cells indicate support for a goal or a target. Response option titles in bold font indicate that the response option supports the achievement of more than five Sustainable Development Goals and more than five Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework targets." ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.2.6" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Synthesis and conclusions" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.6.1> a ipbes:SubChapter ;
    ipbes:Chapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ch/NXS24ch5.6> ;
    ipbes:Report <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/NXS24> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "This final subchapter presents a synthesis of the response options that were independently assessed by each of the five subchapters in Chapter 5. The synthesis is structured around the key questions posed in the introduction to Chapter 5 (Chapter 5.0), namely: * What response options are available to actors within each nexus element? * How do response options differ in the number and strength of their impacts on the nexus elements? * Which response options are complementary and synergistic with others, and which will involve trade-offs, in achieving pathways towards sustainable futures? * What attributes or enabling conditions are associated with response options that have nexus-wide benefits and potential for transformative change? * How do response options support existing global policy frameworks and their targets and goals to achieve a sustainable future? Each of the subchapters has included a brief synthesis of its own in which these questions are revisited in the context of a single nexus element (i.e., biodiversity, water, food, health and climate change). In this subchapter, emergent patterns across the response options assessed for each of the nexus elements are highlighted and discussed, linkages among subchapters are identified and common lessons distilled." ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.6.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Introduction" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.6.2> a ipbes:SubChapter ;
    ipbes:Chapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ch/NXS24ch5.6> ;
    ipbes:Report <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/NXS24> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Key question: What response options are available to actors within each nexus element? The five subchapters identified and assessed 71 response options.2 Each subchapter assessed 14-16 response options available to actors in that element of the nexus (Table 5.6.1). These options provide evidence-based support to policymakers, communities and other actors to understand the impacts of these actions both within their own element and across all nexus elements. As discussed below, there are several response options in common among multiple nexus elements, highlighting the broad opportunity for collaboration across traditionally siloed sectors. The response options presented here are not priorities or specific recommendations. They instead represent a selection of options that illustrate the range of opportunities that exist. The 71 response options are also not an exhaustive set. They are drawn from a larger pool of existing evidence-based options (see Section 5.0). In addition, there is certainly a pressing need and opportunity to innovate new responses beyond the list presented here. Table 5.6.1. Response options assessed for each nexus element. Response options are listed in numerical order by the unique codes introduced in each subchapter and used throughout. Four response options (agroecology, sustainable healthy diets, sustainable intensification and urban nature-based solutions) were each assessed in two different subchapters and are therefore grouped together at the end of the table. Biodiversity response options Water response options Food response options Health response options Climate change response options B01 Area-based conservation W01 Inclusive water education F01 Halt conversion of ecosystems of high ecological integrity H01 Universal health coverage C01 Increase soil organic carbon B04 Ecosystem-based adaptation in rural landscapes W02 Integrated water infrastructure F02 Restore soil health H02 Intercultural health services C03 Integrated multitrophic aquaculture B05 Forest landscape restoration W03 Dam operation F04 Ecological intensification - croplands H03 Net-zero sustainable healthcare C04 Wetland conservation and restoration B06 Restoration of coastal and marine systems W04 Efficient water use in agriculture F05 Ecological intensification - rangelands H04 Sustainable use of medicinal plants C05 Offshore wind power B07 Restoration of inland water systems W05 Sustainable inland fisheries F06 Ecological intensification - aquatic foods H05 Nature on prescription C06 Solar photovoltaics on land B08 Rewilding W06 Inclusive water management F07 Reduce nutrient pollution H06 Reduce meat overconsumption C07 Sustainable bioeconomy B09 Integrated landscape and seascape approaches W07 Rights of nature F08 Reduce pesticide pollution H07 Pollution prevention C08 Reduce short-lived climate pollutants B10 Rights-based approaches W08 Transboundary water cooperation F09 Reduce plastic pollution H08 Mangrove conservation and restoration for health C09 Multi-hazard early warning systems Table 5.6.1. (continued) Response options assessed for each nexus element. Biodiversity response options Water response options Food response options Health response options Climate change response options B11 Multilateral environmental agreements W09 Groundwater governance F10 Reduce food loss and waste H09 Urban green infrastructure C10 Global cooperation for finance and technology B12 Land and sea planning W10 Finance for water infrastructure F12 City region food systems H10 Forest conservation for health C12 Forest-based practices to address climate change B13 Natural capital accounting W11 Manage alien species F13 Reform public spending H11 Biodiversity management for zoonoses C13 Restoration of coastal and marine ecosystems for carbon sequestration B14 Reconnecting people with nature W12 Manage wastewater F14 Foster gender transformative approaches H12 Integrated watershed-health interventions W13 Water-sensitive urban infrastructure F15 Indigenous food systems H13 Health impact assessments W14 Address gendered burdens of water collection F16 Access to natural resources and land H14 The One Health approach W15 Community water management H15 Integrated health education Response options assessed by more than one subchapter B03/C11 Agroecology F11/C15 Sustainable healthy diets C02/F03 Sustainable intensification B02/C14 Urban nature-based solutions Response options cluster into ten general categories that emerge across nexus elements (Figure 5.6.1). They include: 1) conserve and halt conversion of remaining intact ecosystems; 2) restore natural and semi-natural ecosystems; 3) manage ecosystem functions in human-dominated ecosystems; 4) consume sustainably; 5) reduce pollution and waste; 6) integrate planning and governance; 7) manage risk; 8) ensure rights and equity; 9) align financing; and 10) “others” that do not fit the previous categories. These ten categories represent broad types of actions that are relevant across nexus elements and involve similar types of actors. They provide a useful typology of actions to complement the typology of nexus elements that structures the chapters and is summarized in Table 5.6.1. Figure 5.6.1. Many response options already exist that a range of actors can implement. The 71 response options assessed in Chapter 5 are displayed in wedges representing 10 broad categories of actions (labelled on the outer edge of the circle). Unique alphanumeric codes for each response option indicate its nexus element (B for biodiversity, W for water, F for food, H for health and C for climate change) and its corresponding number in the relevant subchapter of Chapter 5. These codes are used to identify response options throughout the report. Several response options correspond to more than one category but, for clarity, are displayed only in their primary category. The “Others” category includes four important response options that do not correspond easily to any of the nine main categories. Response options with an asterisk (agroecology, sustainable healthy diets, sustainable intensification and urban nature-based solutions) were each assessed in two different subchapters and both alphanumeric codes are therefore shown. Response options are geographically distributed worldwide. Figure 5.6.2 displays the locations of the 24 case studies of focal response options presented throughout Chapter 5. The case studies are examples of response options currently being implemented. The response options presented in the figure are in widespread use or have potential to be replicated over broad areas of land or water. For example, wetland restoration as an urban nature-based solution is illustrated by an example in the Maldives (Section 5.1.3.2), but this response option applies across many coastal and freshwater ecosystems globally. Similarly, W13, Water-sensitive urban infrastructure (Section 5.2.3.13) is illustrated by an example in Adelaide, Australia, but this response option is also essential worldwide. Similar broad applicability is likely for many response options beyond those featured in case study boxes. Figure 5.6.2. Locations of case studies representing the 24 focal response options presented throughout Chapter 5. Points indicate the location of each case study itself, but case studies are examples of response options with broader applicability. Each text box lists the name of the case study, the location for implementation and the subchapter, including the corresponding response option alphanumeric code and, in parentheses, the subchapter section number." ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.6.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Summary of response options assessed" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.6.3> a ipbes:SubChapter ;
    ipbes:Chapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ch/NXS24ch5.6> ;
    ipbes:Report <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/NXS24> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Key question: How do response options differ in the number and strength of their impacts on the nexus elements? Response options are often pursued by actors within one nexus element, yet options originating in one element can have positive or negative impacts on the other nexus elements. Of the 71 response options presented and assessed in Chapter 5, all have positive impacts on at least one other element (Figure 5.6.3). Few response options are associated with negative impacts (although response options were chosen based in part on nexus-wide benefits, and negative impacts tend to be reported less often in the literature). These response options are therefore promising ways to advance holistic, integrated pathways to a sustainable future. Response options differ substantially in the number and strength of their benefits to other nexus elements. The evidence-based scores assigned by subchapter teams (Figures 5.1.2, 5.2.3, 5.3.3, 5.4.5 and 5.5.3 in Chapters 5.1, 5.2, 5.3, 5.4 and 5.5, respectively)3 demonstrate these differences in impact among response options (Figure 5.6.3). Some response options are known to benefit several other elements of the nexus. For example, F02, Restore soil health (Section 5.3.3.2) can support biodiversity of soil invertebrates and plants, improve water infiltration rates to reduce erosion, increase food production, increase nutrient content of foods, and increase carbon sequestration. Similarly, H11, Biodiversity management for zoonoses (Section 5.4.3.11) can conserve biodiversity, reduce greenhouse gas emissions from land use change and reduce spillover risk of pathogens from wildlife to people. Others, such as F08, Reduce pesticide pollution (Section 5.3.3.8) and W11, Manage alien species (Section 5.2.3.11) have important benefits to one or two elements of the nexus, but fewer positive impacts on the other elements. Still other response options have primarily negative impacts on the other nexus elements. For example, C05, Offshore wind power (Section 5.5.3.5) generates abundant renewable energy, but these installations have been known to have detrimental effects on migrating birds, bats, marine mammals and reptiles, on seabed habitats and sedimentary processes, and on fishing for food; further, noise generated by the installations can affect health (Section 5.5.3.5). Importantly, response options with high potential for nexus-wide benefits come from all five elements of the nexus (Figure 5.6.3). Widely impactful responses are therefore available to actors in all related sectors. Indeed, response options that explicitly emphasize integration and cooperation among actors typically have higher impact scores across nexus elements; e.g., B09, Integrated landscape and seascape approaches (Section 5.1.3.9); C10, Global cooperation for finance and technology (Section 5.5.3.10). These response options often act as enablers of others, setting up the governance and policy frameworks for many response options to succeed (see Section 5.6.4; Section 4.2.3; Section 7.3.6). While all categories of action shown in Figure 5.6.1 include at least one response option with nexus-wide benefits, the categories differ noticeably in the frequency of these widely impactful response options. Most response options related to conservation and restoration of natural ecosystems benefit several elements of the nexus. For example, H10, Forest conservation for health (Section 5.4.3.10) and B05, Forest landscape restoration (Section 5.1.3.5) can improve water provision, reduce pollution and mitigate climate change. Mangrove conservation and restoration for health (H08, see Section 5.4.3.8) can improve fish production, biodiversity and the health and security of local communities. Most response options related to managing risks also benefit several elements of the nexus. For example, C09, Multi-hazard early warning systems (Section 5.5.3.9) can increase the resilience of communities and ecosystems by anticipating and generating warnings for floods, fires, droughts and other climate hazards, allowing communities and administrations to prepare, respond and recover effectively. The scores presented in Figure 5.6.3 and throughout Chapter 5 are aggregated metrics, based on evidence from a variety of contexts and locations. They are useful as a general indication of likely nexus-wide impacts, but they can obscure important details for understanding and implementing response options. First, the impacts of any response option depend critically on how it is implemented, and on local context and conditions (Section 5.6.5). For example, biodiversity and water benefits of B02/C14, Urban nature-based solutions (Section 5.1.3.2; Section 5.5.3.14) and H09, Urban green infrastructure (Section 5.4.3.9) depend on the plant species used for restoration, and health benefits depend on their cultural acceptance and use of such natural spaces. Therefore, the way in which a response option is implemented can be more important than the response option itself. Second, while the assessment is based on thorough reviews of available evidence, for some response options evidence was sparse or conflicting. In these cases, scores were assigned through the aggregate judgement of expert teams. Thus, some degree of uncertainty and subjectivity remain for some scores reported in this chapter. Figure 5.6.3. Response options have substantial but widely varying impacts on the five nexus elements of biodiversity, water, food, health and climate change. For each of the response options assessed in Chapter 5, circles indicate the estimated impacts on each element of the nexus. Larger circles indicate stronger impacts on that element, and several large circles in a row indicate more widespread impacts across elements of the nexus. Most impacts are positive (blue), but a few response options have negative impacts (red) on some nexus elements. Response options are organized into the same categories used in Figure 5.6.1. Unique alphanumeric codes for each response option indicate its nexus element (B for biodiversity, W for water, F for food, H for health and C for climate change) and its corresponding number in the relevant subchapter of Chapter 5. These codes are used to identify response options throughout the report. Impact scores are based on a thorough review of existing evidence, synthesized and averaged across several component criteria on a scale of -3 to +3 for each nexus element. Response options for which evidence for component indicators was inconclusive (IC) or non-existent (NE) are labelled as such, and those for which evidence was deemed inconclusive for some criteria and non-existent for others are labelled as IC/NE. Response options with an asterisk (agroecology, sustainable healthy diets, sustainable intensification and urban nature-based solutions) were each assessed in two different subchapters, and both alphanumeric codes are therefore shown. For these response options, circles represent an average score from the two subchapters in which they were assessed.4 In several cases more than one subchapter identified the same response option and assessed it independently; e.g., agroecological practices (B03/C11, F04, F05). There are 11 of these shared response options, involving 30 of the individual response options from across subchapters (Figure 5.6.4). These response options are likely to have nexus-wide benefits, represent clear opportunities for collaboration among actors, and show the potential for joint implementation across multiple nexus elements. As viable options for actors in multiple elements, these shared response options offer opportunities for integration among sectors that often act independently. For example, response options related to sustainable healthy diets (i.e., F11/C15, Sustainable healthy diets; H06, Reduce meat overconsumption) were assessed in the food, health and climate change subchapters with different entry points for actors in each element (Section 5.3.3.11; Section 5.4.3.6; Section 5.5.3.15). A change towards healthier diets presents a real opportunity for cross-sectoral collaboration as it will improve human health and reduce health costs associated with non-communicable diseases, while reducing land use and potentially leading to climate change mitigation. Two response options – those focused on forest and land conservation and urban nature-based solutions – were shared by four of the five subchapters, indicating a high degree of nexus-wide benefits and potential collaborations. Four additional response options were shared by three elements, and five were shared by two elements. Figure 5.6.4. Response options shared by two or more elements of the nexus. Each vertical line represents a shared response option, with a general label. Dots represent the subchapters that identified and scored a given response option. Specific response options that comprise each shared response option are listed in Table 5.6.2. For example, Restoration – coastal and marine systems is a response option chosen and scored by the biodiversity (Section 5.1.3.6, response option B06), health (Section 5.4.3.8, response option H08) and climate change (Section 5.5.3.13, response option C13) subchapters. Numbers and bar lengths to the right indicate the number of shared response options in each subchapter. Those numbers can exceed the number of dots for that element because in several cases multiple specific response options contribute to a shared response option. Table 5.6.2. Response options shared by two or more elements of the nexus. Shared response options are listed in the first column, in alphabetical order by the general labels used in Figure 5.6.4. Specific response options from each nexus element are listed in the following columns. Response options with an asterisk (*) appear in more than one shared response option theme. Shared response option theme Biodiversity response options Water response options Food response options Health response options Climate change response options Agroecological practices B03 Agroecology F04 Ecological intensification – croplands C11 Agroecology F05 Ecological intensification – rangelands Aquaculture F06 Ecological intensification – aquatic foods C03 Integrated multitrophic aquaculture Conservation – coastal & marine systems B01 Area-based conservation* H08 Mangrove conservation and restoration for health* Conservation – forests & other lands B01 Area-based conservation* F01 Halt conversion of ecosystems of high ecological integrity H10 Forest conservation for health C04 Wetland conservation and restoration* C12 Forest-based practices to address climate change* Nutrient and chemical pollution W12 Manage wastewater F07 Reduce nutrient pollution F08 Reduce pesticide pollution Table 5.6.2. continued. Response options shared by two or more nexus elements. Shared response option theme Biodiversity response options Water response options Food response options Health response options Climate change response options Restoration – coastal & marine systems B06 Restoration of coastal and marine systems H08 Mangrove conservation and restoration for health* C13 Restoration of coastal and marine ecosystems for carbon sequestration Restoration – forests & other lands B05 Forest landscape restoration C04 Wetland conservation and restoration* B07 Restoration of inland water systems C12 Forest-based practices to address climate change* B08 Rewilding Soil health F02 Restore soil health C01 Increase soil organic carbon Sustainable healthy diets F11 Sustainable healthy diets H06 Reduce meat overconsumption C15 Sustainable healthy diets Sustainable intensification W04 Efficient water use in agriculture F03 Sustainable intensification C02 Sustainable intensification Urban nature-based solutions B02 Urban nature-based solutions W13 Water-sensitive urban infrastructure H09 Urban green infrastructure C14 Urban nature-based solutions" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.6.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Nexus-wide impacts of response options" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.6.4> a ipbes:SubChapter ;
    ipbes:Chapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ch/NXS24ch5.6> ;
    ipbes:Report <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/NXS24> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Key question: Which response options are complementary and synergistic with others, and which will involve trade-offs, in achieving pathways towards sustainable futures? Response options are likely to interact with each other, both within and across nexus elements. A response option may facilitate beneficial impacts from another option through synergies or may hinder such impacts through trade-offs. As defined here, synergies and trade-offs are not about whether a response option has overall positive or negative impacts on nexus elements; they refer to positive or negative interactions between response options themselves. Synergies among response options are likely to be common. Response options can magnify the impact of others by advancing complementary policies or actions. Some response options may in fact be enablers of others, by establishing the social and governance context for several response options to succeed. For example, F13, Reform public spending (Section 5.3.3.13) can accelerate the adoption of dietary-related response options (F11/C15, Sustainable healthy diets (Section 5.3.3.11; Section 5.5.3.15) and H06, Reduce meat overconsumption (Section 5.4.3.6)), which can reduce deforestation (e.g., F01, Halt conversion of ecosystems of high ecological integrity (Section 5.3.3.1)) and create opportunities for B01, Area-based conservation (Section 5.1.3.1). By being aware of the interlinkages among nexus elements and the potential synergies between response options, actors can improve the impacts of their actions and the likelihood that they will achieve the desired outcomes. Trade-offs are also likely to be common. Some response options may reduce the effectiveness of others. For example, F04, ecological intensification - croplands and F05, ecological intensification - rangelands (Sections 5.3.3.4 and 5.3.3.5) are known to increase productivity for farmers. However, increased profits could drive additional local demand for agricultural land, leading to further deforestation and land use change and reducing the effectiveness of response options such as B01, Area-based conservation (Section 5.1.3.1), F01, Halt conversion of ecosystems of high ecological integrity (Section 5.3.3.1), H10, Forest conservation for health (Section 5.4.3.10) and B05, Forest landscape restoration (Section 5.1.3.5), an outcome known as a “rebound effect.” Response options may also interfere directly in policies and management actions. For example, health policies that encourage the use of medicinal plants may lead to overharvesting in conservation areas, undermining management goals; e.g., B01, Area-based conservation (Section 5.1.3.1) and H04, Sustainable use of medicinal plants (Section 5.4.3.4). As with synergies, awareness of potential trade-offs will help actors manage them, either directly or by ensuring safety nets for those most impacted. In fact, trade-offs are likely to depend more on how response options are implemented than the response options themselves. In the examples above, rebound effects on agricultural land use can be mitigated through strong conservation policies and enforcement, as well as efforts to reduce overall demand for food through reducing food waste and improving diets. Overharvesting of medicinal plants can be alleviated by pairing health policies with management plans and cultivation programs that reduce harvest pressure in conservation areas. Given the interlinkages among nexus elements and interactions between response options, managing trade-offs is essential for achieving just and sustainable futures. Because response options are interconnected, it will likely be advantageous to implement several together. Those with synergies can be bundled and implemented simultaneously, and those that provide enabling conditions for others can be implemented sequentially (Figure 5.6.4). For example, bundling B10, Rights-based approaches (Section 5.1.3.10) with B01, Area-based conservation (Section 5.1.3.1) can achieve synergies as Indigenous Peoples and local communities (IPLC) are able to defend and manage their own lands and waters. In addition, B05, restoring forests (Section 5.1.3.5) will be facilitated by simultaneous efforts to halt the conversion of ecosystems of high ecological integrity (Section 5.3.3.1 (F01)) and to reduce pressure on additional land conversion, including F10, Reducing food loss and waste (Section 5.3.3.10) and encouraging F11/C15, Sustainable healthy diets (Section 5.3.3.11; Section 5.5.3.15) and H06, a reduction in the overconsumption of meat (Section 5.4.3.6). The Amazon Soy Moratorium has reduced pressure on land conversion by implementing industry-wide commitments for sustainable, deforestation-free soybean production. This has led to improved sustainable intensification practices (F03/C02, Sustainable intensification) and a greater integration of state and private stakeholders in land management plans (Section 5.3.3.3; Section 5.5.3.2). Despite the potential benefits of bundling and sequencing response options, current management and governance approaches typically fail to realize these benefits fully because response options are designed and implemented in isolation, without considering interconnections among them. Integrated planning approaches (e.g., B09, Integrated landscape and seascape; B12, Land and sea planning; W15 Community water management; F12, City region food systems; H12, Integrated watershed-health interventions) can facilitate the bundling and sequencing of response options to achieve synergies and address trade-offs (Sections 5.1.3.9 and 5.1.3.12; Section 5.2.3.15; Section 5.3.3.12; Section 5.4.3.12). Ensuring the participation of multiple actors, including IPLC, can avoid additional trade-offs and inequities in the distribution of costs and benefits. Figure 5.6.5 presents a conceptual representation for the potential synergies to be achieved through bundling or sequencing response options. Table 5.6.3 presents case studies that illustrate how implementing response options together can result in greater benefits across more elements of the nexus. There are very few quantitative studies that measure the increased impacts due to bundling or sequencing, compared to individual implementation of response options. The evidence remains in the form of anecdotal case studies. While these case studies are numerous and compelling, more empirical evidence is a significant knowledge gap and priority for future research. Figure 5.6.5. Response options can have amplified effects when implemented together, either in simultaneous bundles or in strategic sequence. From left to right: single response options implemented alone have certain impacts on one or more nexus elements; bundling several response options and implementing them together can result in greater total impacts on more nexus elements, because some response options enable or synergize with others; similarly, by sequencing response options, those implemented first can establish enabling conditions for others implemented later, increasing their impacts compared to individual implementation. Table 5.6.3. Case studies illustrating bundling and sequencing of responses. See the relevant chapter sections for discussions of the case studies. Case study locations with an asterisk (*) are included in the Summary for Policymakers, Figure SPM.9. Case study location System or approach Implementation approach Nexus elements involved Response options bundled or sequenced Chapter section Nagaland, India* Socioecological production landscapes Bundling B01, B05, B09, B10, B12, B14, W05, F15, F16, H04, H10 5.1.3.9 Paris, France* City food system Bundling B03, W02, W10, F04, F11, F12, H06, C11, C15 5.3.3.12 Andhra Pradesh, India Community agroecology Bundling B03, W04, W06, W14, F04, H07, C11 5.5.3.11 Pacific Islands Pacific tuna fishery Bundling B11, B12, W08, F06 5.1.3.11 Xinshe Village, China Integrated landscape-seascape approach Bundling B05, B09, B12, W15, H12, C11 5.1.3.9 California, United States of America* Rice production Sequencing H07 > F04 > B07 + C04 > F15 5.3.3.3 Fiji* Watershed-health interventions Sequencing W06 + H01 > W02 > B09 > H12 5.4.3.12" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.6.4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Synergies and trade-offs among response options" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.6.6> a ipbes:SubChapter ;
    ipbes:Chapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ch/NXS24ch5.6> ;
    ipbes:Report <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/NXS24> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Changing course towards a sustainable future for people and nature will require truly transformative changes in economic, social and governance systems. The response options assessed in each subchapter can contribute to this fundamental course correction. Each option was assessed on three dimensions of transformative potential: the degree to which the response option can generate new practices and innovations; the degree to which a response option can shift values and views; and the degree to which it can change structures and institutions (Chapter 5.0, Table 5.0.1). These indicators were derived from the definition of transformative change in the IPBES Thematic Assessment of the Underlying Causes of Biodiversity Loss, Determinants of Transformative Change and Options for Achieving the 2050 Vision for Biodiversity (Transformative Change Assessment). Several response options aim primarily to shift practices. Examples include response options that shift food production practices such as B03/C11, Agroecology (Section 5.1.3.3; Section 5.5.3.11); F04-F06, ecological intensification of croplands, rangelands and aquatic foods (Sections 5.3.3.4, 5.3.3.5 and 5.3.3.6) and C03, Integrated multitrophic aquaculture (Section 5.5.3.3). Response options that radically change conservation practices such as B08, Rewilding (Section 5.1.3.8) or dietary-related response options such as F11/C15, Sustainable healthy diets (Section 5.5.3.11; Section 5.5.3.15) and H06, Reduce meat overconsumption (Section 5.4.3.6). In addition, C07, Sustainable bioeconomy (Section 5.5.3.7) approaches also harness innovations for changes in practices around life cycles of production and use. Other response options aim to change views and values, often regarding nature. These include sociolegal approaches such as B10, Rights-based approaches to conservation (Section 5.1.3.10) and W07, Rights of nature (Section 5.2.3.7). Options that call attention to gender such as W14, Addressing gendered burdens of water collection (Section 5.2.3.14) and F14, Foster gender transformative approaches (Section 5.3.3.14). Options that recognize Indigenous Peoples as key drivers of transformative such as B09, Integrated landscape and seascape approaches (Section 5.1.3.9); F15, Indigenous food systems (Section 5.3.3.15) and H02, Intercultural health services (Section 5.4.3.2) as well as options influencing attitudes and behaviours through B14, Reconnecting people with nature (Section 5.1.3.14). Response options that shift practices can also shift views and values or be dependent on views and values changing first. For example, B08, Rewilding (Section 5.1.3.8) and dietary-related response options represent major shifts in practices that evidence suggests often first require shifting values and views regarding nature and health. Fewer response options provide opportunities to shape changes in structures and institutions. These include political changes to empower marginalized groups, such as IPLC in B10, Rights-based approaches (Section 5.1.3.10); F16, Access to natural resources and land (Section 5.3.3.16); B12, Land and sea planning (Section 5.1.3.12) and W15, Community water management (Section 5.2.3.15) that allow local groups to engage more in decision-making. Response options that link institutions across scales can also contribute to transformative change; e.g., B11, Multilateral environmental agreements (Section 5.1.3.11); W08, Transboundary water cooperation (Section 5.2.3.8) and C07, Sustainable bioeconomy (Section 5.5.3.7). Finally, changing economic and financial incentives through options such as B13, Natural capital accounting (Section 5.1.3.13) and F13, Reform public spending (Section 5.3.3.13), can have large transformative potential. While many response options on their own are likely to lead to incremental changes, bundling response options can increase the likelihood of transformative change. Table 5.6.3 describes several case studies in which response options were bundled or sequenced, leading to synergies and magnified impacts. Bundling of response options to achieve more transformative change will require co-implementation of social, political and technical options to create the desired changes in values, the enabling environment, and the institutions and technologies for supporting equitable and just change. Because response options tend to integrate across multiple nexus elements, they may lead to more systemic changes which are necessary for transforming economic, social and governance systems. Looking across the assessed response options and case studies, it is evident that those with low transformative potential also tend to have low nexus-wide benefits. However, response options with high transformative potential include those with a wide range of positive nexus impact scores and from all nexus elements." ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.6.6" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Contributions to transformative change" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.6.7> a ipbes:SubChapter ;
    ipbes:Chapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ch/NXS24ch5.6> ;
    ipbes:Report <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/NXS24> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Key question: How do response options support existing global policy frameworks and their targets and goals to achieve a sustainable future? Several global frameworks have established goals for advancing pathways to a sustainable relationship between people and nature. These include the 2030 Agenda for Sustainable Development and its Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs), the Kunming-Montral Global Biodiversity Framework and the Paris Agreement. All three frameworks aim for transformative changes in economic, social and governance systems to allow sustainable management of the planet. Response options assessed here are well aligned with the goals or targets of each of the three frameworks, and as such can contribute to the transformative changes they seek to create. Moreover, many response options align simultaneously with multiple goals across the frameworks. They therefore can serve as a mechanism to integrate efforts across global policy objectives. Multiple response options support each of the 17 SDGs (Figure 5.6.9, panel A).7 As expected, the number of aligned response options is greatest for SDGs that correspond directly to each element of the nexus assessed in this report: for biodiversity - SDG14 (life under water) and SDG15 (life on land); for water - SDG6 (clean water and sanitation); for food - SDG2 (zero hunger); for health – SDG3 (good health and well-being); and for climate change – SDG13 (climate action). In addition, however, response options support every other SDG, including notably SDG1 (no poverty), SDG10 (reduced inequalities) and SDG12 (responsible consumption and production). All response options support the achievement of at least two (and up to 10) SDGs (see the Summary for Policymakers, Appendix 5 and Chapter 5.6 online Annex 5.6.2). In particular, response options that focus on integrated management of biodiversity, water, food, health and climate change help support the achievement of multiple global goals. For example, 34 response options support more than five SDGs simultaneously, demonstrating their synergistic nature. Examples of response options that support more than five SDGs include B12, Land and sea planning (Section 5.1.3.12), W13, water-sensitive urban infrastructure (Section 5.2.3.13), F15, Indigenous food systems (Section 5.5.3.15), H12, Forest conservation for health (Section 5.4.3.12) and C06, Solar photovoltaics on land (Section 5.5.3.6). Figure 5.6.9. Linkages between response options and global policy frameworks. Response options provide broad and varying support to goals and targets of global policy frameworks. (A) Number of response options supporting achievement of the Sustainable Development Goals. Stacked bars correspond to each Goal, with each section of a given bar indicating the number of response options from each nexus element. (B) Number of response options supporting achievement of the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework targets, using the same format as panel A. (C) Number of goals or targets under the three global policy frameworks supported by each category of response options. For each of the 10 categories of response options indicated in Figure5.6.1, bars represent the degree of support for each policy framework. Bar length represents the percentage of all goals or targets supported by response options in that category, while numbers indicate the number of goals or targets supported. For example, the category “Conserve ecosystems” supports 11 (out of 17) of the Sustainable Development Goals, 15 (out of 23) of the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework targets, and both the long-term goal for mitigation and the long-term goal for adaptation of the Paris Agreement (response option support for the third long term goal, which addresses climate finance, was not assessed in the subchapters of Chapter 5). There is equally strong alignment between response options and the targets of the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework. Each of the targets is supported by at least three and up to 45 response options (Figure 5.6.9, panel B). As with SDGs, targets that directly correspond to the five nexus elements have strong support from response options. These include targets 1-3 (manage, restore and conserve nature), target 8 (climate impacts) and target 10 (agriculture and aquaculture). However, several other targets receive equivalent degrees of support from response options, including target 7 (reduce pollution), target 11 (nature’s contributions to people), target 14 (integrate biodiversity into policy) and target 22 (participation and access for all). With one exception, all response options support the achievement of at least one (and up to 16) Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework targets (see the Summary for Policymakers, Appendix 5 and Chapter 5.6, Annex 5.6.2). For example, 47 response options support more than five targets simultaneously, including B03/C11, Agroecology (Section 5.1.3.3; Section 5.5.3.11), F04 and F05, ecological intensification of croplands and rangelands, respectively (Sections 5.3.3.4 and 5.3.3.5),W06, Inclusive water management (Section 5.2.3.6), F13, Reform public spending (Section 5.3.3.13), H05, Nature on prescription (Section 5.4.3.5) and C13, Restoration of coastal and marine ecosystems for carbon sequestration (Section 5.5.3.13). In addition to supporting global policy frameworks individually, response options can foster greater alignment among them. Response options contribute strongly to all three global frameworks (Figure 5.6.9, panel C). For example, response options from the subchapter on water (Chapter 5.2) support 14 of the SDGs, 23 of the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework targets and the long-term goals for climate change mitigation and adaptation of the Paris Agreement. Figure 5.6.9 emphasizes the role of response options in delivering co-benefits to multiple goals. Indeed, response options are mechanisms for integrating efforts across global policy agendas. However, current approaches have failed to harness the full potential of these co-benefits because they are often designed and implemented in isolation, at single scales and without adequate consideration of the interconnections among policy frameworks and their national delivery mechanisms." ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.6.7" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Support for global policy frameworks" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.6.8> a ipbes:SubChapter ;
    ipbes:Chapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ch/NXS24ch5.6> ;
    ipbes:Report <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/NXS24> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Several knowledge gaps in relation to the response options emerge from the Chapter 5 subchapters. These can be classified into the following types: Knowledge related to the interlinkages between the nexus elements. There are few studies that have been explicitly designed to study nexus interactions amongst response options, and this requires novel methods, quantitative and qualitative tools, and expert elicitation processes. For example, links between water response options and health, climate change mitigation, and some aspects of the environmental performance of food had inconclusive evidence or no evidence at all. Extent of implementation and scalability: many response options from all the nexus elements could be implemented across temporal and spatial scales. Identifying the specific extent of application of some of these is challenging and requires further studies on appropriate targeting and understanding specific niches of response options in different ecosystems, together with their means of implementation under different contexts. Justice implications of response options: there is little or incomplete information on the equity impacts of the response options and how they differentially affect or are adopted by more vulnerable groups, women or poorer countries. This is a serious impasse that prevents the attainment of key SDGs and of a just and sustainable transition cemented on improving the interlinkages between the nexus elements. This calls to significant changes in the design of technology and innovation studies to unravel these impacts in the future. Bundling, sequencing and implementing response options: Response options are most likely to be implemented as sociotechnical bundles of several technologies embedded in socioeconomic processes for guaranteeing their success. However, the assessment in Chapter 5 found just a few examples of these bundles evaluated in the literature. How this is designed is central to implementing the nexus approach and achieving nexus-wide benefits and is a topic that requires significant experimentation as the appropriate sequencing and bundling will largely change depending on local conditions. Protocols and methods for evaluating success will need to be developed for identifying the general principles and lessons of what works under a variety of conditions. Technical feasibility and translation into simple mechanisms for implementation: Many practices are complex to implement and require increased managerial capacity, which hampers their adoption. Some response options, such as those that aim to reduce pollution, still require significant research to provide robust science-based metrics for implementing transparent systems and establishing simple thresholds, regulations and penalty systems to monitor and enforce controls. Other response options require additional research to identify methods that can work across ecosystems and are accessible to various stakeholders (e.g., H14, the One Health approach (Section 5.4.3.14)). Financial feasibility: Many technologies are ready for implementation but are still too costly to deploy in terrestrial and aquatic systems. The financial instruments that are a critical part of the technology adoption process are one of the most under-researched examples of response options presented in this assessment (e.g., financial and economic instruments (Chapter 6); B13, Natural capital accounting (Section 5.1.3.13); F13, Reform public spending (Section 5.3.3.13). How these instruments can be deployed in systems that support the livelihoods of farmers, fishermen and forest-dwellers remains a significant gap (Section 6.2). Monitoring and evaluation: Chapters 5.1 to 5.5 identified monitoring and evaluation of the performance of response options as a key knowledge gap preventing the attainment of more conclusive results. Current monitoring systems mostly measure desired outcomes on a limited number of dimensions, but the incorporation of the five nexus elements demands broader monitoring and evaluation systems across different domains. How to do this in a pragmatic way within the institutional context of different actors requires significant research. The establishment of proxy indicators that are easier to monitor is a critical research need." ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.6.8" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Knowledge gaps" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.6.9> a ipbes:SubChapter ;
    ipbes:Chapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ch/NXS24ch5.6> ;
    ipbes:Report <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/NXS24> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "A wide range of feasible and equitable response options are available to actors in all five elements of the nexus. These responses can have important nexus-wide benefits, providing an opportunity to advance an integrated, collaborative approach to managing biodiversity, water, food, health and climate change. Such an approach can contribute towards the transformative changes needed for a shift to sustainable relationships between people and nature and to secure a sustainable and just future for all. The following two chapters, Chapters 6 and 7, address critical issues regarding the resources and governance required to achieve such sustainable futures. Chapter 6 assesses additional response options available to actors in economic and financial institutions. These response options can both increase funding for biodiversity goals and reduce the negative impacts of siloed and unsustainable financing. Chapter 7 integrates across Chapters 4 to 6 to discuss the urgency of action to implement nexus approaches. It provides a road map for actors to assess drivers of change and nexus interlinkages, co-create visions with diverse actors, assess synergies and trade-offs, foster enablers and overcome barriers and monitor change. This road map represents potential pathways to achieving the transformative changes required for securing just and sustainable futures." ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.6.9" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Conclusion" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-6.5> a ipbes:SubChapter ;
    ipbes:Chapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ch/NXS24ch6> ;
    ipbes:Report <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/NXS24> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "This assessment shows the urgency of shifting away from current trajectories leading to declining outcomes across biodiversity, water, food, health and climate change. It also shows the instrumental role of the financial system in engendering a shift towards more sustainable development pathways. Enabling transformative, just and interconnected response options is urgent because current trajectories are closing down opportunities for sustainable development outcomes for current and future generations (Figure 6.11). Figure 6.11. Transformative, interconnected and just response options are urgently needed. The left panel exemplifies (from the bottom to the top) people-nature separation and values underpinning siloed and inequitable governance approaches. Such governance interacts with drivers of change and fragmented, unsustainable actions to produce negative trends, adverse outcomes and increasing risks across the nexus elements that together constrain future response options. The right panel exemplifies (from the bottom to the top) people-nature connected and related values and worldviews that underpin nexus governance and equitable response options. Such transformative values, governance and response options can advance nexus interlinkages and outcomes and enable human well-being and planetary health. Prevailing financial and economic flows represent unsustainable nature-society-economy relationships that lead to decision-making approaches that create unjust social and environmental externalities (Section 6.1). They also entrench systems (industrial, urban and infrastructure, energy, land, water and ecosystems, as well as societal systems) that produce harmful outcomes for biodiversity, water, food, health and climate change. The large investments in, and incentives and subsidies for, activities that adversely impact the state of nature across the nexus elements also combine with the existing gaps in finance for biodiversity and other nexus elements (Section 6.2). Increasing risks resulting from decline in these nexus elements as well as increasing debt burdens further serve to prevent more positive investments in nature, increase the cost of future response options, and create vulnerabilities to systemic financial risks. Business-as-usual interactions are bound by the prevailing political economy and the structures and processes that privilege the few with power and influence over the needs, interests and freedoms of communities and collectives from the local to global level. Moreover, business-as-usual ignores the intrinsic and relational values of nature. The result is pervasive injustice and inequity in the flow and distribution of money and resources. As such, business-as-usual interactions and development pathways run counter to nexus outcomes. However, a reformed financial system can be pivotal in shifting views, structures and practices for transformative societal change. Just, interconnected and transformative actions, such as shifts toward nature-people connectedness and drawing on diverse worldviews like IPLC perspectives of community-nature togetherness, can counter the prevailing disconnect that underpins extractivist and unsustainable resource use. Transformed finance flows – which consider impacts across nexus elements – can drive system transitions, with improved nexus decision-making processes and outcomes iteratively shifting society towards more transformational development pathways. Diverse and inclusive economic and financial arenas of engagement are key to shaping interactions between governance actors and the direction of resulting development pathways. Including diverse knowledges, strengthening deliberative and inclusive processes, understanding the long-term costs of inaction, combatting environmental crime, and supporting plural visions and pathways for transformative change are thus critical to addressing power imbalances and inequities. Transforming financial and economic arenas of engagement through the values, goals, interactions and governance taking place within them can drive transformative change across all arenas in society and address the underlying drivers perpetuating business-as-usual unsustainable development pathways. Transformational change across economic and financial arenas necessitates enabling interactions among governance actors that privilege collective (public) over individual (private) interests; system-wide synergies over sector- or compartmentalized interests; the long-term (including future generations) over the short-term; human-in-nature connections over anthropocentrism; and justice and equity over privilege and domination. Transformation of the political economy and economic and financial systems is therefore urgent and compelling, and all governance actors have a role to play in enabling transformative change." ;
    dcterms:identifier "6.5" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Conclusions" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/NXS24-A2> a ipbes:BackgroundMessage ;
    ipbes:Report <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/NXS24> ;
    ipbes:SubMessage <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-A2-SM0>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-A2-SM1>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-A2-SM2>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-A2-SM3>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-A2-SM4>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-A2-SM5>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-A2-SM6> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription " Freshwater and marine coastal ecosystems are particularly sensitive because they accumulate anthropogenic stressors, such as pollutants and sediments, across ecosystem and watershed boundaries (well established) {2.3.1.1, 2.3.1.3, 2.4.1, 2.5.2}.  Water extraction for food production is responsible for approximately 80 per cent of humanity’s water demand (well established) {2.5.2.1}.  The water cycle is regulated by ecosystem and geophysical processes that support biodiversity and provide nature’s contributions to people that are essential to human health and well-being (Figure SPM.4). Wetlands and inland water bodies cover just 2.6 per cent of the Earth’s terrestrial surface but play a significant role in water regulation and climate change mitigation and adaptation. They are also the water bodies most affected by human activities and climate change, with a substantial proportion having been degraded or lost over past centuries (well established) {2.3.3, 2.4.1, 2.5.1, 2.5.2}.  Forests are also crucially important for water regulation and provision; they capture, filter and regulate water through their vegetation and soils, ensuring clean and accessible fresh water for up to 75 per cent of the world’s population (in 2005) (well established) {2.4.1}.  Consequently, loss of forest cover decreases water regulation, quality and availability, resulting in increasing water treatment costs and negative health outcomes (well established) {2.4.1}. At least 50 diseases are attributable to poor water supply, water quality and sanitation (well established) {2.5.2.3}.  Among marine ecosystems, coral reefs are under combined threats from unsustainable fishing, land-based pollution, climate change and ocean acidification, with approximately a third of reef-building coral species already at high risk of extinction (well established) {2.3.1, 2.5.2}. Coral reefs are the most endangered ecosystems and may disappear globally in the next 10 to 50 years {2.4.1}. These impacts potentially affect nearly 1 billion people who live within 100 km of coral reefs (some 13 per cent of the global population) and who benefit from them in terms of food, medicine, protection from coastal storms and erosion, tourism, recreation and livelihoods (well established) {2.4.1, 2.5.2}." ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "A2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Freshwater biodiversity is being lost faster than terrestrial biodiversity (well established) {2.3.3, 2.4.1}. Unsustainable freshwater withdrawal, wetland degradation and forest loss have decreased water quality and resilience to the impacts of climate change in many areas of the world (well established) {2.4.1, 2.5.2, 2.6}, impacting biodiversity, water and food availability, with consequences for human, plant and animal health (well established) {2.4.1, 2.5.1, 2.5.2.3, 2.6}. Globally, many marine systems have been overharvested and degraded through anthropogenic activities (well established) {2.4.1, 2.5.2, 2.6}, causing declines in biodiversity and nature’s contributions to people." .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/NXS24-A4> a ipbes:BackgroundMessage ;
    ipbes:Report <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/NXS24> ;
    ipbes:SubMessage <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-A4-SM0>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-A4-SM1>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-A4-SM2>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-A4-SM3>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-A4-SM4> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Life expectancy has increased dramatically across the globe in recent centuries, particularly due to improvements in food security and healthcare, including medicines from biodiversity, but average life expectancy varies by as much as 20 years among regions (Figure SPM.2). Child mortality rates are 10 times higher in least developed countries than in high-income countries (well established) {2.3.3}.  Unhealthy diets have become one of the most important drivers of global mortality, accounting for nearly 11 million adult deaths in 2017 and 255 million disability-adjusted life years (15 per cent of all disability-adjusted life years among adults) (well established) {2.3.3, 2.5}.  Unsustainable farming systems contribute to biodiversity loss, excessive water use, pollution and climate change, which further exacerbate health problems. Increased air and water pollution caused an estimated 9 million premature deaths in 2019 (16 per cent of all deaths worldwide) (well established) {2.4.3, 2.5} through diseases such as respiratory disease, cancer, allergies, birth defects, neurodegenerative disease and impaired cognitive development.  Emerging and re-emerging infectious disease events have been rising, with half of these driven by changes in land use, agricultural practices and activities that encroach on natural habitats and lead to increased contact between wildlife, domestic animals and humans (established but incomplete) {2.5}.  In addition, the loss of Indigenous and local knowledge of biodiversity has resulted in declines in traditional medicine use by many Indigenous Peoples and local communities {Box 2.7}. Nature is also integral to physical, emotional and mental health and well-being, as more biodiverse environments and access to them facilitate recovery from stress, depression and other health-related conditions (well established) {2.4.3}." ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "A4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Human health is directly affected by the nexus elements of biodiversity, water, food and climate change. Improvements in human health, including greater life expectancy and childhood survival, are partly a result of increased production of and access to food. Worsening outcomes from several communicable and noncommunicable diseases are linked to biodiversity loss, unhealthy diets, lack of clean water, pollution and climate change, among other causes (well established) {2.3.3, 2.4.3, 2.5.2.3}. Both positive and negative human health outcomes have been highly unequal (well established) {2.3.3, 2.4.3, 2.5}. " .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/NXS24-A5> a ipbes:BackgroundMessage ;
    ipbes:Illustration <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/NXS24-FigureSPM.4> ;
    ipbes:Report <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/NXS24> ;
    ipbes:SubMessage <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-A5-SM0>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-A5-SM1>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-A5-SM2>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-A5-SM3>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-A5-SM4>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-A5-SM5>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-A5-SM6> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Under current trends, climate change leads to irreversible loss of marine biodiversity, such as coral reefs, and negative effects on coastal fisheries, both of which provide diets that prevent malnutrition, stunted child growth and other conditions (well established) {2.5.2.2}.  Climate change also impacts terrestrial food production through numerous pathways (e.g., heat-stress effects on crop yield, water availability and quality) with consequences for human health and wellbeing, including exacerbating food insecurity for vulnerable populations. Other direct health impacts include extreme weather events such as heatwaves, flooding, droughts and wildfires, and increased dispersal of pathogens and pollutants (such as untreated wastewater, fertilizers, pesticides, sediments and air pollutants) that can be transboundary. Currently, 58 per cent (218 out of 375) of known human infectious diseases are likely to worsen owing to climate change (established but incomplete) {2.5.2.2}.  Climate change directly contributed to 62,000 heat-related deaths in Europe in 2022, more than 1,500 in the United States in 2023 and between 12,000 and 19,000 heat-related deaths in children in Africa between 2011 and 2020 (well established) {2.5.2.2}.  In the past 50 years, extreme weather-, climate- and water-related events have caused nearly 12,000 disasters, leading to 2 million human deaths (90 per cent in low- and lower-middle-income countries) and $4.3 trillion in total costs globally (well established) {2.5}. Land-use change associated with food production was responsible for an estimated 21 per cent of global carbon dioxide equivalent emissions in 2018 (well established) {2.5.2.1}.  Functioning and resilient ecosystems make critical contributions to climate change mitigation and adaptation (well established) {5.5.3},  such as by buffering extreme weather events and acting as a carbon sink. For example, coastal ecosystems contribute to more than 50 per cent of carbon sequestration in the oceans and provide protection from flooding (well established) {2.4.1, 2.5, 5.5.3.13}." ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "A5" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Climate change affects biodiversity, water, food and health through changes in average climatic conditions and in the frequency and magnitude of extreme weather events (Figure SPM.4) (well established) {2.5.2.2}. These nexus elements also influence climate change; for example, the food system is responsible for 21 to 37 per cent of all greenhouse gas emissions (well established) {5.3.1, 5.5.1}. Resilient ecosystems also make critical contributions to climate change mitigation and adaptation efforts (well established) {5.5.3}. " .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/NXS24-C1> a ipbes:BackgroundMessage ;
    ipbes:Illustration <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/NXS24-FigureSPM.7> ;
    ipbes:Report <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/NXS24> ;
    ipbes:SubMessage <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C1-SM0>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C1-SM1>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C1-SM2>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C1-SM3>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C1-SM4> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Many successful examples of area-based conservation (B01) exist in different parts of the world that directly support target 3 of the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework, to effectively conserve at least 30 per cent of terrestrial, inland water and coastal and marine areas by 2030; these include the creation of public and private natural reserves, national parks and conservation of areas and other effective area-based conservation measures of special interest. Successful establishment and management are dependent on aligning the values of multiple actors, such as through ensuring the full and effective participation of Indigenous Peoples and local communities in processes from co-design to governance (e.g., through rights-based approaches, among others, in accordance with national legislation and international instruments) and sharing the multiple benefits of conservation across public and private stakeholders and rights holders (well established) {5.1.3.1, 5.3.3.1, 5.4.3.10}.  For example, marine protected areas in Australia and Chile have led to increases in biodiversity, greater abundance of fish for human consumption and improved incomes for local communities and, in the case of Australia, increased tourism revenues (B01) (well established) {5.1.3.1}.  Indirect actions such as improving the sustainable production and consumption of certain foods and increasing resource use efficiency can complement and enable ecosystem conservation and adaptation by reducing pressure on land and aquatic resources (F01) (established but incomplete) {5.3.3.1}.  In some cases, commodity-wide and private industry commitments, such as the Amazon Soy Moratorium in Brazil, have reduced pressures on important ecosystems through better monitoring and more transparent efforts (F01) (well established) {5.3.3.1}.  Conserving or halting conversion of forests and other ecosystems protects human health and well-being by combating climate change, reducing the impact of extreme weather events, such as storms, droughts and landslides, increasing water and air quality and reducing disease risk (H08, H10) (Figure SPM.8) (well established) {5.4.3.8, 5.4.3.10}." ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "C1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Conserving or halting the conversion of terrestrial, freshwater and marine ecosystems of high ecological integrity maintains biodiversity and supports the health and well-being of humans, plants and animals and ecosystems (Figure SPM.7) (well established) {5.1.3.1, 5.3.3.1, 5.4.3.10}. " .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/NXS24-C2> a ipbes:BackgroundMessage ;
    ipbes:Illustration <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/NXS24-FigureSPM.7> ;
    ipbes:Report <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/NXS24> ;
    ipbes:SubMessage <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C2-SM0>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C2-SM1>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C2-SM2>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C2-SM3>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C2-SM4>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C2-SM5>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C2-SM6> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Restoring ecosystems supports targets 2, 8, 10 and 11 of the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework. Restoring ecosystems provides multiple benefits by increasing the capacity of degraded lands and waters to provide habitat functions, to improve water quality and availability and to restore productive capacity (Figure SPM.8) (well established) {5.1.3.5, 5.1.3.6, 5.1.3.7}.  Restoration contributes to climate change adaptation and socioecological resilience and can also contribute to climate change mitigation when it targets carbon storage in forests, peatlands, seagrass beds, salt marshes and marine and coastal ecosystems that contribute to carbon sequestration (B05, B07, H08, C04, C12, C13) (well established) {5.1.3.5, 5.1.3.7, 5.4.3.8, 5.5.3.4, 5.5.3.12, 5.5.3.13}.  For example, mangrove restoration in Senegal (C13) has resulted in significant carbon sequestration, increases in biodiversity, reductions in coastal erosion and improved water quality, food security, livelihoods and health for local populations {Box 5.5.3}. Likewise, soil restoration improves soil health, enhancing water regulation and food production potential (F02) (well established) {5.3.3.2}  and supporting climate change mitigation through increasing carbon storage as well as providing adaptation benefits (well established) {5.5.3.1}. When targeting areas of biodiversity concern, restoration can protect endangered species and preserve culturally important food sources and practices (B08, F15) (well established) {5.1.3.8, 5.3.3.15}.  Restoration is most effective when coordinated across actors (e.g., among Indigenous Peoples and local communities, government actors and other stakeholders and rights holders) and implemented at large scales (B05, B06, B07, F02) (well established) {5.1.3.5, 5.1.3.6, 5.1.3.7, 5.3.3.2}.  However, such broad efforts can face challenges stemming from their long-term nature, financial requirements and dependence on enduring and resilient partnerships (well established) {5.1.3.5, 5.1.3.6}.  Successful projects often address multiple stakeholder concerns, ensure equity in decision-making, implement systematic planning and monitoring and have secure financing (well established) {Box 5.3.1, 5.3.3.2, Box 5.3.2, 5.3.4}. For example, in south-central Niger, the co-development of farmer-managed natural regeneration has empowered local farmers through widely applicable and low-cost efforts and led to the regreening of 5 million ha with native trees and agroforestry systems, thus enhancing soil health and biodiversity and increasing cereal yields by 30 per cent (F02) {5.3.3.2, Box 5.3.3}." ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "C2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Restoration of natural and semi-natural ecosystems complements the protection of ecosystems to secure water, food and health while enhancing biodiversity and mitigating climate change and adapting to its impacts (Figure SPM.7) (well established) {5.1.3.5, 5.1.3.6, 5.1.3.7, 5.1.3.8, 5.3.3.2, 5.4.3.8, 5.5.3.4, 5.5.3.13}. " .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/NXS24-C3> a ipbes:BackgroundMessage ;
    ipbes:Illustration <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/NXS24-FigureSPM.7> ;
    ipbes:Report <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/NXS24> ;
    ipbes:SubMessage <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C3-SM0>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C3-SM1>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C3-SM2>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C3-SM3>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C3-SM4>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C3-SM5>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C3-SM6>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C3-SM7> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Several response options can directly improve the sustainable use and management of ecosystems and support targets 10, 11 and 12 of the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework. This is particularly important in agricultural systems, as the way food is produced, what foods are produced and consumed, where they are produced and how much food is lost and wasted impact both nature and people (well established) {2.5.2.1, 5.3.1}.  Agroecology represents a shift to production systems where equitable access to land and a blend of scientific and Indigenous and local knowledges guide the sustainable management of biodiversity, crops and other resources (B03, F04, F05, F06, C11) (well established) {5.1.3.3, 5.3.3.4, 5.3.3.5, 5.3.3.6, 5.5.3.11}.  Ecological intensification of croplands and rangelands uses ecological processes and reduces external inputs, creating habitats and connectivity for biodiversity (B03, F04, F05) (well established) {5.1.3.3, 5.3.3.4, 5.3.3.5} while enhancing water retention (W04) (established but incomplete) {5.2.3.4},  crop productivity (F04) (well established) {5.3.3.4} and soil health (F02), including soil organic content (C01) (Figure SPM.8) (well established) {5.3.3.2, 5.5.3.1}.  Ecological intensification benefits from creating and supporting markets for sustainable products, payments for ecosystem services and other positive incentives (well established) {5.3.3.3}  when in accordance with relevant international trade obligations. In aquatic ecosystems, ecological intensification of aquatic food production (F06), sustainable inland fisheries management (W05) and integrated multitrophic aquaculture (C03) have positive impacts on food production, nutrition, biodiversity, climate change adaptation and livelihoods (established but incomplete) {5.2.3.5, 5.3.3.6, 5.5.3.3}.  Sustainable intensification can lead to land sparing and is a globally applicable response option for increasing agricultural production efficiency and overall food production, while reducing land conversion and social, environmental and some health impacts (well established) {5.3.3.3, 5.5.3.2}.  Different response options acknowledge the importance of combining land-sparing and land-sharing practices in a context-specific manner. Managing ecosystems in other settings, such as in forests through forest-based practices to address climate change (C12) and in cities through urban nature-based solutions (B02, C14), and/or ecosystem-based approaches can have multiple benefits, including for climate regulation, water availability and mental and physical health (well established) {5.1.3.2, 5.5.3.12, 5.5.3.14}." ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "C3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Managing human-exploited lands and waters to conserve and enhance biodiversity, as well as to support sustainable use, can safeguard the long-term delivery of nature’s contributions to people (Figure SPM.7) (well established) {5.1.3.3, 5.2.3.5, 5.2.3.11, 5.3.3.4, 5.3.3.5, 5.3.3.6, 5.5.3.1, 5.5.3.3, 5.5.3.11, 5.5.3.12, 5.5.3.14}. " .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/NXS24-C4> a ipbes:BackgroundMessage ;
    ipbes:Illustration <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/NXS24-FigureSPM.7> ;
    ipbes:Report <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/NXS24> ;
    ipbes:SubMessage <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C4-SM0>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C4-SM1>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C4-SM2>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C4-SM3>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C4-SM4>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C4-SM5>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C4-SM6>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C4-SM7> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Response options can enable and encourage sustainable consumption and support target 16 of the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework. These include shifting to sustainable healthy diets (F11, H06, C15) and reducing food waste (F10), which together benefit food security and health, reduce greenhouse gas emissions and could free up land, providing in a range of cases co-benefits for nexus elements, such as biodiversity conservation and carbon sinks (well established) {5.3.3.10, 5.3.3.11, 5.4.3.6, 5.5.3.15}.  Other options include improving the efficiency of water use in agriculture (W04), which can benefit food production and water conservation (well established) {5.2.3.4},  and sustainable bioeconomy (C07), which benefits all nexus elements (well established) {5.5.3.7} (Figure SPM.8).  The adoption of new and renewable energy technologies, such as solar and wind power, supports a rapid transition to renewable energy (C05, C06) {5.5.3.5, 5.5.3.6}, helping to mitigate climate change and its negative impacts on all nexus elements (well established) {5.5.1},  but environmental assessments and appropriate policies would be needed to avoid trade-offs, particularly for biodiversity and food systems (well established) {5.5.3.5, 5.5.3.6}.  Behaviour change will be necessary to shift consumption practices and can be enabled by increasing accessibility and desirability, taking into account cultural acceptance of sustainable healthy diets (F11, H06, C15) (well established) {5.3.3.11, 5.4.3.6, 5.5.3.15} and making sustainable energy and water consumption default options (C07) (well established) {5.5.3.7}.  For example, implementing food-based dietary guidelines in public food procurement, particularly targeting public school feeding programmes, can create structured demand for healthy food in combination with increased opportunities for on-farm diversification aimed at increasing the supply and consumption of local seasonal foods (well established) {5.3.3.11, Box 5.3.8, Box 5.3.9}.  Protecting the diversity and availability of medicinal plants can also promote their sustainable consumption (H04) (well established) {5.4.3.4}." ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "C4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Shifting to sustainable consumption patterns reduces pressures on biodiversity, water, food systems and health, while contributing to climate change mitigation (Figure SPM.7) (well established) {5.2.3.4, 5.3.3.10, 5.3.3.11, 5.4.3.4, 5.4.3.6, 5.5.3.5, 5.5.3.6, 5.5.3.7, 5.5.3.15}. " .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/NXS24-C5> a ipbes:BackgroundMessage ;
    ipbes:Illustration <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/NXS24-FigureSPM.7> ;
    ipbes:Report <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/NXS24> ;
    ipbes:SubMessage <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C5-SM0>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C5-SM1>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C5-SM2>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C5-SM3>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C5-SM4> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Response options can reduce pollution directly through regulations and incentives (W12, F07, F08, F09), as well as indirectly through reduced and more efficient use of fertilizers, improved waste management and reduced use of pesticides (F03, C02) (well established) {5.2.3.12, 5.3.3.3, 5.3.3.7, 5.3.3.8, 5.3.3.9, 5.5.3.2}, leading to improved freshwater quality, air quality, ocean quality and soil health (Figure SPM.8).  These response options support target 7 of the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework. Some countries have successfully implemented pollution controls in the form of reduced subsidies to agricultural production systems (e.g., leading to declines in nitrogen pollution in Denmark) {Box 6.2}. However, developing countries may face multiple barriers in the reduction of such subsidies (well established) {6.2.3}. Levels of pollution, such as air contaminants, that are hazardous to health can be reduced through international and national standards and regulations (C08), including emissions standards for motor vehicles and power plants (H07), which also improve environmental protection more broadly (well established) {5.4.3.7, 5.5.3.8}.  Reducing pollution from all sources is particularly significant for people in developing countries. For example, 90 per cent of premature deaths from pollution occur in low- and middle-income countries, with air pollution being the major cause of those deaths {5.4.3.7}, and the benefits to humans and nature are often greater than the costs of such policies. Furthermore, access to adequate sanitation services and domestic wastewater treatment is a critical issue in African, Asian and Latin American and Caribbean countries (well established) {5.2.3.12, 5.3.3.7, 5.3.3.8, 5.3.3.9, 5.4.3.7, 5.5.3.2}.  Reducing plastic pollution (F09) has led to increased water quality and wildlife protection, fewer floods, and reductions in incidence of associated water-borne diseases; nevertheless, some measures to reduce plastic pollution have not been effective in some countries, and subsidizing recycling often requires costly government intervention (established but incomplete) {5.3.3.9}." ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "C5" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Pollution is a key driver of degradation of biodiversity, water quality and human health (well established) {5.1.1, 5.4.1}; however, a range of response options exist to reduce air, soil and water pollution that benefit all nexus elements (Figure SPM.7) (well established) {5.2.3.12, 5.3.3.3, 5.3.3.7, 5.3.3.8, 5.3.3.9, 5.4.3.7, 5.5.3.2, 5.5.3.8}. " .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/NXS24-C7> a ipbes:BackgroundMessage ;
    ipbes:Illustration <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/NXS24-FigureSPM.7> ;
    ipbes:Report <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/NXS24> ;
    ipbes:SubMessage <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C7-SM0>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C7-SM1>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C7-SM2>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C7-SM3>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C7-SM4>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C7-SM5>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C7-SM6> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Response options can be direct actions to limit climate and health risks or contribute indirectly to risk reduction and can support targets 8, 11 and 12 of the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework. Direct actions may include urban nature-based solutions and ecosystem-based approaches (B02, H09, C14) that increase urban green and blue space to manage heat island effects, improve water quality and availability and reduce air pollution as well as allergens and zoonotic disease risk (Figure SPM.8) (well established) {5.1.3.2, 5.4.3.9, 5.5.3.14}.  In rural landscapes, conserving and restoring natural or semi-natural ecosystems (B04), including coastal vegetation and mangroves, reduces risks from flooding and other climate extremes (well established) {5.1.3.4}.  Indirect approaches include systems for early warning and risk communication (C09), which allow communities to respond quickly to extreme weather events and to make longer-term adaptation decisions, with many successful examples including weather forecasts and index-based crop insurance (well established) {5.5.3.9}.  There are opportunities for countries with resource-intensive healthcare sectors to reduce negative impacts across the nexus elements by increasing investments in disease prevention and by reducing pollution, waste and greenhouse gas emissions (H03) (well established) {5.4.3.3}.  Increasing investments in risk identification, disease prevention and health promotion, including as a community-led process, provides multiple nexus benefits (H13) (well established) {5.4.3.13}.  The One Health approach (H14) supports integrating biodiversity and food system management with local health services to reduce risks from zoonotic pathogen emergence and spillover at source (H11), malnutrition and other risks such as those to wildlife health, food production and ecosystems (established but incomplete) {5.4.3.11, 5.4.3.14}.  Successful examples, such as the Unified Health System in Brazil, have involved human health professionals, veterinarians and environmental health practitioners working together with farmers and policymakers to jointly design holistic practices aimed at addressing social and environmental determinants of health and contributing to preventing pathogen emergence, thus reducing disease outbreaks for both people and animals (well established) {5.4.3.11}." ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "C7" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Effective risk management can reduce climate and health risks to people and ecosystems, particularly risks that are multi-scale, multidimensional and interlinked and thus best managed through nexus approaches (Figure SPM.7) (well established) {5.1.3.2, 5.1.3.4, 5.2.3.3, 5.4.3.3, 5.4.3.9, 5.4.3.11, 5.4.3.13, 5.4.3.14, 5.5.3.9}. " .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/NXS24-C9> a ipbes:BackgroundMessage ;
    ipbes:Report <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/NXS24> ;
    ipbes:SubMessage <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C9-SM0>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C9-SM1>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C9-SM2>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C9-SM3>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C9-SM4>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C9-SM5>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C9-SM6>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C9-SM7> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Multiple financial response options, instruments and approaches exist which can support targets 15, 18 and 19 of the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework, but these options face implementation, accessibility and scale challenges (well established) {5.6.4, 6.2.4.1}.  For example, some instruments (e.g., green and blue bonds) can be used to raise funds to explicitly target multiple nexus benefits, but their implementation is limited and scaling up has been slow (established but incomplete) {6.2.2.1}. Other promising response options aim to increase financing and improve access to it; examples include payments for ecosystem services, which have mobilized up to $42 billion per year from both public and private sources (well established) {5.5.3.12, 6.2.6.2}, a nd microfinance, which is currently at low levels for nexus approaches such as agroecology (established but incomplete) {6.2.6.2, Box 6.12}. Response options can also shift enabling environments, such as internalizing costs of environmental degradation through water pricing (W10) (established but incomplete) {5.3.2.10}  and natural capital accounting (B13), which identifies and values natural assets (well established) {5.1.3.13}.  Implementation of response options that incentivize trade in sustainable products, in accordance with relevant international obligations, has shown positive impacts on nexus elements (established but incomplete) {5.3.3.1, 6.2.5.1, 6.3.3}.  Global cooperation (C10) on meeting financing needs and access to technological innovations also remain crucial, in particular for supporting developing countries (well established) {5.5.3.10, 6.2.2}.  Eliminating, phasing out or reforming subsidies that damage nexus elements, if implemented in accordance with international obligations, would contribute to shifting business models towards sustainability and recognize the benefits of and reduce pressures on biodiversity, providing benefits for biodiversity and its contribution to nexus elements (well established) {5.3.3.13},  taking note of the differing needs of developing countries. Some response options, such as natural capital accounting (B13) and integrated watershed-health interventions (H12), can help align the interests of financial and other stakeholders (established but incomplete) {5.1.3.13, 5.4.3.12}." ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "C9" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Additional economic and financial resources are required to implement response options, but their impact and uptake could be amplified with wider reforms to align financial and environmental interests (well established) {6.2}. " .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/NXS24-D1> a ipbes:BackgroundMessage ;
    ipbes:Report <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/NXS24> ;
    ipbes:SubMessage <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-D1-SM0>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-D1-SM1>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-D1-SM2>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-D1-SM3>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-D1-SM4> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Challenges for governing the nexus include navigating socioecological complexity to address fragmented and sectoral decision-making; multiple and diverse values; insufficient, inaccessible and unpredictable finance; and inadequate and inappropriate scaling of actions (established but incomplete) {1.1.2, 4.2, 4.5, 7.1}.  These challenges can be addressed through improved and reoriented “nexus governance approaches” (Figure SPM.11), defined as the development and use of coordinating structures and processes that enhance the engagement of multiple actors through horizontal (e.g., across various nexus elements and associated sectors) and vertical (e.g., cross-scale connectivity or multilevel governance) channels to address nexus challenges, identify policy and sociopolitical options and manage their implementation (established but incomplete) {4.5}.  Nexus governance approaches, building on and in line with different national and international obligations, can provide an alternative to current siloed approaches and address indirect drivers through: (a) integrative, holistic and transdisciplinary framings of problems and solutions; (b) inclusive approaches that bring about enhanced opportunities for diverse actor engagement; (c) considerations of equity and justice, alongside accountability; (d) enhanced mechanisms and processes for collaboration and coordination across scales and sectors; and (e) adaptive, reflexive and experimental approaches to learn from successes and to scale these solutions (established but incomplete) {4.5.4} (Figure SPM.11).  Attention to scaling can also assist in accelerating the adoption, implementation and amplification of response options over wider regions and longer time frames. Scaling opportunities include scaling up (institutionalizing at the level of policy, rules and laws), scaling out (applying response options to new places), scaling down (localizing response options that are highly adaptive) and scaling deep (changing relationships, unsustainable and unjust world views, mindsets or beliefs) (well established) {4.4.1}.  When combined with capacity-strengthening processes {D5}, scaling can enable transformative change (established but incomplete) {4.4, 4.5.5, 7.3}." ;
    ipbes:hasEstablishedIncomplete "Established_but_incomplete" ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "D1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Improved governance approaches across biodiversity, water, food, health and climate change can help respond to interlinked and compounding challenges by focusing on policies, institutions and actions that promote integration, inclusion, equity and accountability and coordinated and adaptive approaches (established but incomplete) {1.3.4, 4.5, 7.3}. However, existing policies and approaches arising from sectoral and narrow perspectives have resulted in misaligned, duplicative and inconsistent governance and have failed to address direct and indirect drivers of change (well established) {1.1, 1.2, 4.2}." .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/NXS24-D3> a ipbes:BackgroundMessage ;
    ipbes:Report <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/NXS24> ;
    ipbes:SubMessage <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-D3-SM0>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-D3-SM1>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-D3-SM2>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-D3-SM3>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-D3-SM4>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-D3-SM5>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-D3-SM6>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-D3-SM7>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-D3-SM8> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Nature-related risks to economic and financial systems, estimated in the trillions of dollars and mutually reinforcing with risks from climate change, are increasing interest in and opportunities for reforming the relationship between the economy and nature (established but incomplete) {6.1.4, 7.2}.  Nevertheless, and despite the fact that financial resources flowing to biodiversity (particularly from public sources) have increased in the past decade, progress has been inadequate, and gaps remain to meet resource needs for biodiversity, which are estimated in the range of $0.3 trillion to $1 trillion per year (Figure SPM.12) (established but incomplete) {6.2.2}.  Gaps in financing to meet the Sustainable Development Goals related to nexus outcomes beyond biodiversity add at least $4 trillion to these investment needs each year (established but incomplete) {6.2.1}.  While some mechanisms and financial flows for biodiversity aim to take advantage of nexus interactions by promoting effectiveness and multiple co-benefits, identifying additional nexus finance options is important to deliver nexus solutions to the degree required for just and sustainable futures (Figure SPM.12) (well established) {1.1.2.5, 6.2.2}.  Three complementary categories of response options were identified which could collectively help align economic and financial systems with biodiversity and/or help direct increased financial resources towards biodiversity and other nexus elements: (a) measures to improve the accessibility, availability and use of information, including information related to the diverse values of nature by economic and financial decision makers. Examples in this category include the adoption and use of metrics beyond gross domestic product, the incorporation of nexus-related information in the appraisal of public spending, and transparency and reporting requirements in the private sector; (b) options aimed at improving access to and the availability of financial resources through the use of financial and economic instruments. Examples in this category include green bonds, microfinance and payments for ecosystem services, as well as tax policies to increase the availability of public funds; and (c) options to reduce negative incentives that drive damage to biodiversity and nexus elements. Examples in this category include improving safeguards and standards for investments and, where contextually appropriate and in accordance with international obligations, tackling negative incentives such as harmful subsidies (well established) {6.2.6}.  Available evidence shows a clear bias in the current distribution of biodiversity finance, with absolute levels of domestic public spending concentrated in countries of North America and Europe and in China. However, only 5 per cent of private financial flows globally for biodiversity are allocated to least developed countries and other low-income countries (well established) {6.2.2.3}.  This highlights the challenges faced by all developing countries, including those already devoting much of their public funding to biodiversity, in mobilizing resources from all sources, recognizes that developing countries may not be able to dedicate sufficient resources to the nexus elements and recognizes the need to strengthen the capacity to implement economic and financial response options. It also highlights the importance of complementary reforms to the economic and financial system, including to tackle existing debt concerns and the cost of finance linked to perceived investment risks, and can help ensure adequate, accessible and affordable finance in developing countries and help finance just and equitable transitions (well established) {6.2.1, 6.2.5.4}.  Indigenous Peoples and local communities face particular challenges in accessing finance and funding across scales, indicating a further area for action (well established) {6.1.3, 6.2.4}.  Wider transition risks related to the uncertain costs of adapting to changes in operations can be reduced through providing policy certainty and improving business awareness of impacts and dependencies on nature and of the opportunities created by innovative business models that contribute to the conservation and sustainable use of biodiversity, and through working with nature (established but incomplete) {6.1.4, 6.2.6}." ;
    ipbes:hasEstablishedIncomplete "Established_but_incomplete" ;
    dcterms:identifier "D3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Financial and economic policy reform can shift incentives, change business models and help resources flow towards supporting and restoring biodiversity and related benefits across nexus elements as well as financing just and equitable transitions (established but incomplete) {6.2.6, 6.3}. However, current economic and financial systems are driving declines in nature, resulting in costs now and growing nature-related risks, thus increasing the urgent need for action (established but incomplete) {6.1.3, 6.1.4, 7.2}. " .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/NXS24-D4> a ipbes:BackgroundMessage ;
    ipbes:Illustration <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/NXS24-FigureSPM.13> ;
    ipbes:Report <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/NXS24> ;
    ipbes:SubMessage <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-D4-SM0>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-D4-SM1>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-D4-SM2>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-D4-SM3>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-D4-SM4>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-D4-SM5>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-D4-SM6>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-D4-SM7>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-D4-SM8> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Key steps towards improved and holistic decision-making may include: characterizing underlying causes of nexus challenges and impacts of direct and indirect drivers on nexus elements (well established) {7.3.1};  identifying and convening governance actors who may currently not work collaboratively across scales (well established) {7.3.2}; understanding nexus elements and the interactions among them (well established) {7.3.3};  co-creating visions for just and sustainable outcomes of interventions and surfacing and aligning values, which can broaden spaces for dialogue, shift power dynamics, increase inclusion and participation and create greater support and legitimacy for response options (well established) {4.5.1, 4.5.3, 7.2, 7.3.4};  identifying response options and assessing trade-offs and synergies (well established) {7.3.5}; assessing enabling conditions and overcoming barriers, which can include considerations of policy design and implementation, equity and diversity, institutional capacity, behaviour and lifestyles, technology and material endowments (well established) {4.2.5, 7.3.6};  negotiating implementation and strengthening transformative outcomes of response options, including through scaling response options and strengthening the capacities of actors (well established) {7.3.6};  and embedding experimentation, evaluation, reflection and learning through monitoring to foster adaptive governance (well established) {4.2.2, 4.5.4, 7.3.8}.  Decision-support tools can be particularly useful at each step of the road map, with more than 200 tools available for supporting nexus approaches related to biodiversity, water, food and climate change (although health is commonly not included in these tools) (well established) {4.6.1}.  Measures along the road map that can also assist with “course correction” are needed to enhance social and environmental outcomes and equity; examples include the use of free, prior and informed consent procedures and other human rights-based approaches (well established) {4.5.3.1, Box 4.11}.  These steps towards improved decision-making and governance can be incremental or more transformative, depending on how they are implemented (established but incomplete) {7.3} and could be used to inform decision-making across scales. For example, actors involved in subnational, national, regional or transboundary development planning processes could use steps of the road map, where relevant, when co-developing national biodiversity strategies and action plans, nationally determined contributions and national adaptation plans (Figure SPM.13)." ;
    ipbes:hasEstablishedIncomplete "Established_but_incomplete" ;
    dcterms:identifier "D4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Reforms to governance and economic systems can be facilitated by deliberate steps to identify existing challenges and contexts, increase actor engagement through coordination, knowledge co-production and strategic action and seek iterative, adaptive and scalable solutions (established but incomplete) {4.5, 5.6, 6.2, 7.3}. These steps can be visualized as a road map (Figure SPM.13) that encourages actors to work together to identify problems and solutions using tools and methods that can increase knowledge and improve cooperation and decision-making, aiming for just and sustainable futures (established but incomplete) {4.6, 7.3}. " .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/NXS24-D5> a ipbes:BackgroundMessage ;
    ipbes:Report <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/NXS24> ;
    ipbes:SubMessage <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-D5-SM0>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-D5-SM1>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-D5-SM10>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-D5-SM2>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-D5-SM3>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-D5-SM4>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-D5-SM5>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-D5-SM6>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-D5-SM7>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-D5-SM8>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-D5-SM9> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Capacity-strengthening is an important enabling condition recognized in many global conventions and initiatives (well established) {4.5.5, Table 4.11}.  Specific capacities to move towards improved nexus governance approaches include those which facilitate response option implementation; capacities to understand, leverage and mobilize equitable financial flows to support multiple co-benefits among nexus elements (well established) {4.5.4, 4.5.5, 6.2};  and capacities to scale and amplify options that show transformative potential (well established) {4.4, 4.5.4, 4.5.5, Table 4.12}.  For example, response options that generate new practices and innovations, shift values and views, or change structures and institutions show the most transformative potential (established but incomplete) {5.6.6}.  Important identified capacities to help actors achieve these aims include motivational capacities, which build awareness and desire for change (well established) {4.5.5};  analytical capacities, which enable actors to select, understand and use suitable decision-support tools and strengthen institutional and research capacities which can help address inequalities among countries (well established) {4.5.5, 4.6.1, Table 4.13, 7.3.3};  bridging capacities and promoting transdisciplinary research, including the ability to bring together different ways of knowing and doing through knowledge co-production processes (well established) {4.5.5, 4.6.2};  negotiation capacities to surface and navigate inevitable trade-offs among the values and interests of different actors and institutions, including those of Indigenous Peoples and local communities (well established) {3.1.2, 4.5.5, 7.3.7},  and deeper considerations of equity and justice which can facilitate a greater intersectional understanding of how power mediates governance processes (well established) {4.5.1, 4.5.3, Table 4.8, Box 4.12, 6.3.3.2};  and social networking capacities for facilitating co-learning opportunities by using knowledge and innovation brokers (Figure SPM.11) (established but incomplete) {4.5.5, Table 4.12, 7.5.5}.  While many capacity gaps still remain  (established but incomplete) {Appendix 3, 7.4.1}, there are available response options, such as reconnecting people with nature (B14), community water management (W15) and those response options that strengthen the capacities of women (W06), that both rely on and can build up actors’ and institutions’ capacities to help govern and manage across the nexus (well established) {5.1.3.14, 5.2.3.6, 5.2.3.15}." ;
    ipbes:hasEstablishedIncomplete "Established_but_incomplete" ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "D5" ;
    skos:prefLabel "While some actors and institutions across the globe, including Indigenous Peoples and local communities (established but incomplete) {1.2.2, 4.5.1, 4.5.2}, already possess capacities for nexus governance approaches, strengthening specific capacities for many other actors can improve outcomes (well established) {4.5.5}. " .

