<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.0.1> a ipbes:SubChapter ;
    ipbes:Chapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ch/NXS24ch5.0> ;
    ipbes:Reference <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/8GSB7D32> ;
    ipbes:Report <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/NXS24> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "This chapter focuses on response options available to actors within each of the five elements represented in the nexus – biodiversity, water, food, health and climate change. In this assessment, a response option is defined as actions or policies available to a range of actors that address specific challenges or opportunities in the governance and management of one or more elements of the nexus, with the goal of achieving positive and equitable outcomes across multiple nexus elements. Response options can include voluntary or mandatory policies, strategies, measures and interventions intended to change nexus elements, interlinkages between them and direct or indirect drivers shaping them (see Section 1.3 and Chapter 4). Effective response options account for a diversity of world views, behaviours, norms and values. Response options include actions (e.g., shifts to sustainable healthy diets), policies (e.g., repurposing public spending) and enablers (e.g., ensuring participation of Indigenous Peoples and local communities (IPLC) in resource decisions). Response options represent opportunities to address the issues reported earlier in this assessment (Figure 5.0.1). Chapters 1 to 3 lay out the conceptual basis for the nexus and assess past trends, current status and future trends in nexus interactions. Chapter 4 provides an overview of nexus governance, drawing on theoretical frameworks and tools to evaluate a broad range of policy and sociopolitical options that address the nexus challenges introduced in Chapter 1 and can accelerate a transition to sustainable futures. These previous chapters provide the conceptual foundations for the specific response options identified and assessed in this chapter. Figure 5.0.1. Organization of the Nexus Assessment chapters. This chapter builds on Chapters 1 to 4 to examine response options specific to each element of the nexus. This chapter builds upon Chapter 4 by focusing on the nexus from the perspective of actors within each of its component elements (i.e., biodiversity, water, food, health and climate change) through five subchapters that identify and assess the evidence for a range of response options. These response options are assessed and compared using a set of common criteria (Table 5.0.1), including their impacts on the other elements of the nexus. Critical actors, governance issues and equity dimensions are identified, and examples are provided of existing efforts to put the response options into action. This chapter is a resource for actors, particularly decision-makers and other stakeholders, within and across each of the five nexus elements. It addresses two of the policy-relevant questions of the overall assessment introduced in Chapter 1 (Section 1.1.3), which are decomposed here into five parts: * What response options are available to actors within each nexus element? For example, sustainable healthy diets, forest-based climate solutions and water-sensitive urban infrastructure are all response options originating within one or more nexus elements with co-benefits for other elements. * How do response options differ in the number and strength of their impacts on the nexus elements? For example, offshore wind power and reforestation can both reduce net greenhouse gas emissions (and thereby mitigate climate change). However, while offshore wind power is known to impact marine species (biodiversity) and fisheries (food), forest restoration is likely to impact threatened species (biodiversity), water quality and quantity (water), the availability of arable land (food) and vectors of infectious disease (health). * Which response options are complementary and synergistic with others, and which will involve trade-offs, in achieving pathways towards sustainable futures? For example, grassland restoration for biodiversity conservation may involve synergies with climate change by storing carbon and may be suitable for extensive meat production, but also risks trade-offs with food by removing land from crop production. * What attributes or enabling conditions are associated with response options that have nexus-wide benefits and potential for transformative change? For example, decisions that ensure equity among stakeholders may be more likely to lead to nexus-wide benefits and transformative change by ensuring buy-in and broad support. * How do response options support existing global policy frameworks and their targets and goals to achieve sustainable futures? The Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs), Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework and the Paris Agreement aim to accelerate and coordinate international efforts to conserve biodiversity, address climate change and chart a sustainable future. Response options that align with these frameworks can reinforce one or more global agreements already in place. Assessing the evidence supporting the answers to these questions is key to designing holistic policies and actions for managing socioecological systems and safeguarding nature and its contributions to people . While this assessment focuses on five specific nexus elements, the findings are applicable to consideration of other sectors and trends, such as transportation or urbanization, providing a general model for addressing nexus challenges." ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.0.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Role of this chapter" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.3.6> a ipbes:SubChapter ;
    ipbes:Chapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ch/NXS24ch5.3> ;
    ipbes:Reference <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/52FXRT2X> ;
    ipbes:Report <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/NXS24> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "The global food system faces many challenges associated with feeding a growing population, while addressing the environmental impacts of many conventional agricultural systems and tackling over-consumption, food loss and waste. The response options assessed demonstrate clear pathways through these challenges. The individual response options offer many nexus benefits, however, it is through synergistic interactions between response options that transformative change will occur (Box 5.3.16). Indeed, it is through the collective analysis of the response options against nexus elements that synergies and inconsistencies becomes apparent. Box. 5.3.16 Interactions and interdependencies among response options The FAO’s 10 Elements of Agroecology (Box 5.3.1) analytical framework is used to highlight and articulate interactions and interdependencies among the response options assessed. Successful transformation will entail the development and alignment of context-specific inclusive and innovative practices, policies, institutions and markets (Figure 5.3.20). Figure 5.3.20. Visual narrative highlighting interactions and interdependencies among food response options using the 10 Elements of Agroecology analytical framework. Adapted from FAO under licence CC BY-NC-SA 4.0. Food system transformation will require appropriate enabling environments and adaptation to specific contexts. Responsible governance embodies transparent, accountable and inclusive governance mechanisms. This is essential for creating an enabling environment for food system transformation that supports nexus governance through the recognition of multiple actors, values and knowledge systems and the co-production of possible solutions to address nexus challenges (Section 1.1.2 and Chapter 4). Reforming public spending (Section 5.3.3.13) will be needed as part of this, to support healthy food environments and consumption patterns, and encourage localized, sustainable production. A circular and solidarity economy can be promoted through access to natural resources and land (Section 5.3.3.16), which creates an environment that provides producers with the security and confidence to adopt sustainable practices with longer-term benefits. Adaptation to context ensures that response options are viewed as relevant, credible and legitimate by the actors involved. Achieving this requires the co-creation and sharing of knowledge from multiple knowledge systems, and the incorporation of human and social values, such as gender-inclusive approaches (Section 5.3.3.14) and culture and food traditions, such as the inclusion of Indigenous food systems (Section 5.3.3.15). Diversity at all levels is essential to food system transformation. Diversity of knowledge systems and the range of activities and livelihood options within food systems (Sections 5.3.3.15 and 5.3.3.16) will be key for local adaptation of response options. Diversity supports sustainable production through ecological intensification (Sections 5.3.3.4, 5.3.3.5 and 5.3.3.6), which aims to harness, maintain, enhance and restore biological and ecological processes in production systems. These include pollination, the regulation of pests and diseases, and restoring soil health (Section 5.3.3.2) through soil organic carbon accrual and nutrient cycling. Food diversity is key to sustainable consumption and shifting to sustainable healthy diets (Section 5.3.3.11) that are culturally appropriate. Dietary guidelines that emphasize consuming a wide variety of foods can generate demand for healthy and diversified food production. The concentrated food consumption of urban populations highlights city-region food systems (Section 5.3.3.12) as focal points for shifting demand for these diversified, healthy and safe food systems. Efficiency and resilience are emergent properties of diversified systems guided by co-created knowledge and co-designed to maximize synergies and minimize trade-offs. Recycling is key to reducing nutrient pollution (Section 5.3.3.7) and food loss and waste (Section 5.3.3.10), while ecological intensification (Sections 5.3.3.4, 5.3.3.5 and 5.3.3.6) and sustainable intensification (Section 5.3.3.3) can increase efficiency by reducing pollution from nutrients, pesticides (Section 5.3.3.8) and plastics (Section 5.3.3.9). This efficiency and resilience create further synergies through restoring soil health (Section 5.3.3.2) and halting the conversion of ecosystems of high ecological integrity (Section 5.3.3.1), both of which are essential for reversing biodiversity loss, mitigating climate change and providing a host of other nature’s contributions to people. The 16 response options described in Section 5.3.3 can be classified into five action categories: (i) actions that conserve natural ecosystems; (ii) actions that restore degraded lands and soils; (iii) actions that regenerate ecosystem functions in food production spaces, notably croplands, rangelands and aquatic systems; (iv) actions that reduce the impacts of food production and consumption on biodiversity, climate, water and health; and (v) actions that involve fundamental transformations in how food systems operate (Table 5.3.3). As noted in Box 5.3.16, food system transformation will require appropriate enabling environments and adapting response options to specific contexts. This will entail the development and alignment of context-specific, inclusive and innovative practices, policies, institutions and markets. Supportive policies that are coherent and aligned across nexus elements are particularly important but absent in many cases (Figures 5.3.17 and 5.3.19). Technologies and innovations are particularly important for production practices, including greater investment and innovations in practices that are compatible with both sustainability and health objectives; examples comprise no-till machinery, polyculture seeders and harvesters, machinery adapted to fruit, nut and vegetable cultivation, including adapted to small scale producers. Investments in breeding for environmental performance (e.g., belowground carbon capture) are also important, notably for currently under-consumed crops such as fruits, nuts, and vegetables. Most response options do not operate in isolation but rather there can be strong interdependencies between response options which are important to ensuring success (Figure 5.3.21). Bundling and aligning multiple response options can provide synergistic benefits and avoid trade-offs but will be strongly influenced by the nature and strength of interactions and interdependencies among response options. These in turn will be extremely context dependent. For example, halting conversion of ecosystems of high ecological integrity (Section 5.3.3.1) has a strong dependence on restoring soil health (Section 5.3.3.2), sustainable intensification (Section 5.3.3.3) and ecological intensification (Sections 5.3.3.4, 5.3.3.5 and 5.3.3.6). Without these response options operating, it becomes difficult if not impossible to halt conversion. Halting conversion, reducing pollution, dietary shifts and urban food systems were the response options which had the greatest dependencies on other actions. In contrast, reforming public funding enabled the greatest number of response options followed by gender-inclusive approaches and reducing pollution. Figure 5.3.21. Interdependencies between the response options. Interaction scores were characterized by the colour of the intersection box between response options in columns with response options in rows. When the colour of the intersection box is white, this means the interaction score is equal to zero, hence the two response options considered do not exhibit any dependency. On the contrary, when the intersection box colour is dark blue, this means that the interaction score is 3 indicating the highest level of interdependency among the two response options considered. The blue colour box (interaction score = 2) and the light blue colour box (interaction score = 1) respectively represent medium and low levels of dependency between response options. These strong interdependencies between response options are important to ensuring success. Such bundling and aligning of multiple response options can provide synergistic benefits and avoid trade-offs. For example, the substantial environmental impacts of agricultural expansion into natural habitats can be prevented by halting conversion of ecosystems of high ecological integrity (Section 5.3.3.1). However, halting conversion is only possible - or serious impacts on local food security can only be avoided - through the combined impacts of other response options that restore soils (Section 5.3.3.2), increase the efficiency and quantity of food production (Sections 5.3.3.3, 5.3.3.4, 5.3.3.5 and 5.3.3.6) and reduce its negative externalities (Sections 5.3.3.7, 5.3.3.8 and 5.3.3.9), as well as demand-facing response options such as reducing food loss and waste (Section 5.3.3.10) and encouraging dietary shifts to sustainable healthy diets (Section 5.3.3.11). Combining response options in this fashion will, in turn, only be possible using response options that enable the food system to be sustainably reorientated and reformed to benefit the nexus (Sections 5.3.3.12, 5.3.3.13, 5.3.3.14, 5.3.3.15 and 5.3.3.16). Of the individual response options, consumption-based approaches, including a focus on sustainable healthy diets and reducing food loss and waste, have potential to guide interventions with nexus-wide benefits – notably on climate, biodiversity and health. They can also bring important economic benefits. For these response options to gain traction, however, it is important to consider interactions and interdependencies influencing how, where and why they might face opposition, and how to overcome or circumvent such barriers. The special interests for whom any interruption to the status quo represents a threat need to be recognized and addressed. While the response options provide clear pathways forward, there is no one-size-fits-all approach for sustainable food systems (Figure 5.3.21). For production-facing options (Sections 5.3.3.3, 5.3.3.4, 5.3.3.5 and 5.3.3.6), global lands and waters cannot be treated homogenously. Some will need to be appropriated for food production, but others must be set-aside and used to safeguard other nexus elements. The variation in global lands and waters in their biodiversity and carbon stocks, their current and potential food production, and their vulnerability to different anthropogenic pressures means that not all response options will be appropriate in every location, and each will have to be designed for, or adapted to, local conditions. However, context-dependent evidence on achieving response options is often missing: for example, field-based trials rarely provide data across multiple nexus elements; global models struggle to integrate the diversity of plausible practices and fine resolution data; and farmers frequently adopt multiple practices to varying degrees based on local knowledge and capabilities that confound reductionist scientific methods. It is not possible to separate this biophysical variation from the human: the social, economic and cultural variability that will determine which approaches are appropriate or possible in different circumstances. Changing any system - let alone one as complex and interlinked as the global food system - risks worsening or entrenching inequities, even while attempting to reduce them. This is particularly true for the food system because of its fundamental importance to all human life. Almost everyone is directly affected by the global food system and the teleconnections of global trade can result in well-meaning interventions having disastrous unintended consequences for people’s health and livelihoods, or the global environment. While there is significant risk to food security, health and resilience to environmental change with one-size-fits-all approaches applied at scale, the sheer number of practices, interventions and proposals from multiple actors in the food system community is hugely encouraging. Many are local adaptations of global solutions driven by mixes of individual motivation and enabling environments. This diversity of practices, capabilities and innovations should be celebrated and encouraged and is perhaps our greatest asset in facing the challenges of the global food system. Aligning individual interests and capabilities with the goals of international frameworks (Figure 5.3.22),6 combined with specific actions and the equitable distributions of costs, risks and benefits, will be essential for achieving meaningful change. Food system response options are particularly important enablers of the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework Goal A targets, notably the response options halting conversion and loss of ecosystems of high ecological integrity (Section 5.3.3.1) and reducing different forms of pollution (Sections 5.3.3.7, 5.3.3.8 and 5.3.3.9). They are also a key means of achieving Goal B targets, notably sustainable production (target 10) and nature’s contributions to people (target 11) as food systems are fundamentally dependent on nature’s contributions to people. Reorienting food to sustainable healthy diets is a necessary precondition for achieving SDG2 and SDG3, which in turn are enablers of achieving SDG13, SDG14 and SDG15 as well as the Paris Agreement long-term global goals for mitigation and adaptation. Recognizing and leveraging the significant synergy between sustainable healthy diets and global goals has transformative potential. Figure 5.3.22. Food response options supporting achievement of global goals. The figure shows whether an individual response option contributes to the achievement of the Sustainable Development Goals, the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework targets and the long-term goals for mitigation and adaptation of the Paris Agreement. Response options listed in order by alphanumeric code. Shaded cells indicate support for a goal or a target. Response option titles in bold font indicate that the response option supports the achievement of more than five Sustainable Development Goals and more than five Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework targets." ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.3.6" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Synthesis and conclusions" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.4.5> a ipbes:SubChapter ;
    ipbes:Chapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ch/NXS24ch5.4> ;
    ipbes:Reference <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/H3EXMG7P> ;
    ipbes:Report <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/NXS24> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Several knowledge gaps were found during the course of assessing the health-related response options: 1. Evidence base: in general, health-related evidence for nexus elements is limited to specific case studies or dispersed across many studies. There are gaps in knowledge on the implementation and evaluation of the efficacy of nexus response options, particularly health-related policy measures. In addition, there are gaps in knowledge on the transferability of health nexus response options to other locations and populations, and on the impact and success of health nexus initiatives across the nexus elements. Furthermore, there is insufficient knowledge on how health-related response options can be used in a complementary manner, supporting each other, creating win-wins and avoiding trade-offs. 2. Evaluation: many health-focused response options lack the data with which to evaluate nexus impacts fully, or across multiple scales and contexts, including lack of data on economic costs and benefits. Similarly, there are gaps in information on specific evaluation of health-related impacts of biodiversity, climate change and water-related response options. There are also gaps in knowledge regarding value systems which include human health and well-being, and that accommodate multiple metrics to assess change . Gaps exist in knowledge on how the diversity of challenges faced by different populations, regions and scales can be taken into account in a transdisciplinary manner, recognizing that end-users will need to be able to determine what responses would work best in their specific health context based on key nexus assessment criteria (Chapter 5.0, Table 5.0.1). 3. Indigenous health knowledge: there is a lack of knowledge on how the gap between Indigenous and biomedical health systems can be bridged. There is also a lack of knowledge on why and when Indigenous patients get care from traditional healers versus biomedical providers, and on traditional ecological knowledge on plants and animals for traditional food and medicine. 4. Transformative potential: there are gaps in knowledge on how the potential for transformative change of health-related response options can be operationalized, and on how to do this in a way that avoids irreversible, ineffective pathways and allows for new insights to address unforeseen complexities. 5. Governance, finance and capacity-building: there are gaps in knowledge on how decision support systems (such as multi-criteria decision analysis) can be operationalized for health policy and governance actors to support their decision-making on health in the nexus. In addition, there is a lack of knowledge on how nexus governance frameworks including health can drive transformation at national and sub-national levels including perceptions, behaviour, programs and socioeconomic systems change. There are gaps in knowledge on how public finance management can be used for directing program funding for healthcare services in ways that are nexus focused, and on how financial flows for health have implications across the rest of the nexus. Finally, there is insufficient knowledge on how the capacity of government health actors to understand the importance of nexus approaches through education and training can be strengthened. This includes knowledge gaps on how best to organize shared training for professionals across the nexus, explicitly including across the health (human and veterinary) and environmental sectors but also in including IPLC input and capacity-building." ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.4.5" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Knowledge gaps" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.4.6> a ipbes:SubChapter ;
    ipbes:Chapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ch/NXS24ch5.4> ;
    ipbes:Reference <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/STXB9S8W> ;
    ipbes:Report <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/NXS24> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "What response options are available to actors at all levels within nexus elements? The 15 response options presented in this chapter are drawn from equitable, nexus-supportive health service provision, addressing environmental risks to health, promoting nature’s benefits to health and disease prevention, and nexus-integrative healthcare (Figure 5.4.3). The options are available to health actors from intergovernmental to community levels, although sometimes driven from opposite, or both, directions (i.e., bottom-up, top-down or where intergovernmental standards are taken up by community groups ahead of national endorsement). However, as each response option represents multiple case studies, an individual case may vary in context and actors. How do response options differ in their impacts across the nexus? The response options were chosen for their diversity, complementarity and nexus impacts, which are predominantly positive, if not strongly positive, as shown in Figure 5.4.5. However, as human health is their primary purpose there is an anthropocentric bias that makes some trade-offs likely. For example, controlling mosquitoes with biocides, including the synthetic insecticide dichlorodiphenyltrichloroethane (DDT) where there are no alternatives, may have unintended negative impacts on biodiversity. Such trade-offs or unintended consequences of the response options may be exacerbated by the varied extent to which nexus impacts are documented. For example, the water available for ecosystems may be unknown whereas the availability of water for drinking, hygiene and sanitation and food production is well documented (e.g., Section 5.4.3.1, H01, Universal health coverage). Likewise, H02, Intercultural health services (Section 5.4.3.2) benefit from access to culturally and biodiverse traditional lands but impacts of such services on biodiversity or water may not be reported. Similarly, biodiversity and food system drivers of zoonotic disease may be better understood than the impacts of zoonoses on biodiversity (see Section 5.4.3.11, H11, Biodiversity management for zoonoses; Section 5.4.3.14, H14, the One Health approach). Concern for future health (i.e., multigenerational health equity) has catalysed a greater understanding of the impacts of human activity on the environment and climate change. Major causes of pollution (see Section 5.4.3.7, H07, Pollution prevention) and unhealthy diets (see Section 5.4.3.6, H06, Reduce meat overconsumption) are well studied examples for which impacts have been measured across multiple nexus elements and aggregated. Purposeful data collection on impacts across multiple environmental and social criteria in on-the-ground projects, such as Health in Harmony in Borneo (Box 5.4.3) and Watershed Interventions for Systems Health in Fiji (WISH Fiji) (Box 5.4.10), are not common but provide valuable evidence collected in the field. In many countries, health sectors are developing measures of full life-course impacts and endeavouring to transform themselves in recognition of the enormity of climate action required to protect health (e.g., Section 5.4.3.3, H03, Net-zero sustainable healthcare) and the need to catalyse societal and other sector change. In this assessment, metrics specific for each nexus element were used (Chapter 5.0, Table 5.0.1) which is an important contribution to cross-sector communication but also a reason for missing data as this is a novel approach (see Section 5.4.5). Technical feasibility is high for the response options and the potential for transformation exists for all. In addition, most response options can be governed for successful implementation at various temporal and spatial scales (Figure 5.4.7). H02, Intercultural health services (Section 5.4.3.2); H12, Integrated watershed-health interventions (Section 5.4.3.12) and H13, Health impact assessments (Section 5.4.3.13) are practised at local and sub-national scales, emphasizing the importance of population-place relationships. The economic benefits from implementation are positive and tend to be substantial, with the corresponding individual intervention-based costs not exceeding the benefit scores for any of the response options (Figure 5.4.6). Individuals in society stand to gain with the adoption of these nexus-oriented approaches to good health and well-being. Gross economic costs that may arise from the scaling out of response options are mostly low to very low (e.g., Section 5.4.3.1, H01, Universal health coverage; Section 5.4.3.3, H03, Net-zero sustainable healthcare; Section 5.4.3.5, H05, Nature on prescription; Section 5.4.3.6, H06, Reduce meat overconsumption; Section 5.4.3.7, H07,Pollution prevention; section 5.4.3.10, H10, Forest Conservation for health; Section 5.4.3.13, H13, Health impact assessments), indicating an overall cost-based rationale for scaling and implementation in alignment with social cost-effectiveness. The cost implications for H15, Integrated health education (Section 5.4.3.15) and H04, Sustainable use of medicinal plants (Section 5.4.3.4) lacks evidence, and significant gross economic costs could be associated with scaling up H05, Nature on prescription (Section 5.4.3.5) and H12, Integrated watershed-health interventions (Section 5.4.3.12), although costs could be largely context dependent. This is also the case for implementing H14, the One Health approach (Section 5.4.3.14). However, there is relatively weaker understanding on economic risks associated with the implementation of some of the response options, such as H05, Nature on prescription (Section 5.4.3.5); H10, Forest conservation for health (Section 5.4.3.10); H14, the One Health approach (Section 5.4.3.14) and H15, Integrated health education (Section 5.4.3.15) (for details see Figure 5.4.6). Available documentation of experiences with implementing response options indicates variations in terms of equity considerations (Figure 5.4.6). For instance, distributional equity continues to be neglected in the case of H04, Sustainable use of medicinal plants (Section 5.4.3.4), with traditional knowledge holders facing persistent inequities in benefit sharing and access to resources . Stringent implementation of the Nagoya Protocol on Access to Genetic Resources and the Fair and Equitable Sharing of Benefits Arising from their Utilization, focusing on capacity-building of stakeholders and strengthening institutional mechanisms to protect traditional knowledge and the interests of IPLC actors, can assist (Wynberg 2017; Pisupati and Bavikatte 2014). Recognitional equity and inclusion are mostly well considered by the response options, though for H05, Nature on prescription (Section 5.4.3.5) and H14, the One Health approach (Section 5.4.3.14), there is potential for strengthening this dimension of equity. Procedural equity considerations are less evident with several response options associated with top-down decision-making or limited engagement with stakeholders. However, in the case of seven response options, nexus impacts are achievable with the highest levels of engagement across stakeholders, with communities acting together and driving decision-making, including H14, the One Health approach (Section 5.4.3.14). Other response options advancing procedural equity in the nexus are H02, Intercultural health services (Section 5.4.3.2); H07, Pollution prevention (Section 5.4.3.7); H09, Urban green infrastructure (Section 5.4.3.9); H10, Forest conservation for health (Section 5.4.3.10); H12, Integrated watershed-health interventions (Section 5.4.3.12) and H13, Health impact assessments (Section 5.4.3.13). How do response options create synergies or trade-offs with others, and how can response options be bundled or sequenced to create greater nexus-wide benefits? The response options presented in this subchapter can be bundled and sequenced to address multiple health concerns. Where access to quality primary health services is limited, addressing access is fundamental and a priority (i.e., Section 5.4.3.1, H01, Universal health coverage). Response options that ensure health services are inclusive of cultural values and priorities (e.g., section 5.4.3.2, H02, Intercultural health services) and sustainable and resilient, including H03, Net-zero sustainable healthcare (Section 5.4.3.3) and H04, Sustainable use of medicinal plants (Section 5.4.3.4), can be bundled dependant on a country or population’s context (Figure 5.4.18A). Such bundling addresses potential trade-offs against the rights of culturally diverse and IPLC communities and against the environment where healthcare is resource and energy intensive and polluting. Further bundling can address the right to health and the right to a clean, healthy and sustainable environment (Figure 5.4.18B). Many countries face escalating environmental health risks from poorly regulated pollution (see Section 5.4.3.7, H07, Pollution prevention), environmental degradation and vulnerability to extreme weather events (Section 5.4.3.8, H08, Mangrove conservation and restoration for health; Section 5.4.3.10, H10, Forest conservation for health) and from zoonotic and neglected tropical diseases (Section 5.4.3.11, H11, Biodiversity management for zoonoses) and therefore may prioritize and possibly bundle these response options. Reducing these risks may be associated with real or perceived trade-offs against economic development, some livelihoods and potentially poverty reduction in the short-term. Non-communicable diseases are the major burden of disease globally and may occur in parallel with a significant burden of communicable diseases. In Figure 5.4.18, the bundling examples shown in panels A, C and D are indicative of the range of health concerns that may be relevant to consider. Health actors trained in One Health or other integrated approaches, and/or bringing IPLC world views, can enhance nexus inclusive approaches (Figure 5.4.18E) to address trade-offs against the health and well-being of other species, i.e., directly as biodiversity loss, indirectly through greenhouse gas emissions, water and food production, wherever the needs of people are in competition with good environmental management. The nexus response options presented here are also vulnerable to such trade-offs. For example, nature-based health interventions such as H05, Nature on prescription (Section 5.4.3.5) may increase human pressure on nature, while H09, Urban green infrastructure (Section 5.4.3.9) may create habitat that puts wildlife in conflict with people and creates opportunities for zoonotic disease. Figure 5.4.18. Illustrative examples of bundling and sequencing the health response options presented. Response options may be more relevant to a context depending on burden of disease, location and economic development of a country. However, enabling the right to health services (A) and addressing specific disease risks or enablers of a clean, healthy and sustainable environment (B) are both required (note that in B, other options may also be required). Response options may be bundled to address one or more communicable (C) or non-communicable diseases (D), in association with healthcare services (A). One Health and other integrated approaches to education, training and governance and/or Indigenous Peoples’ and local communities’ values can enhance the acceptability and capacity to address nexus challenges (E). Importantly, while the figure focuses on the health-related response options featured in this subchapter, synergies with similar response options from other sectors will strengthen the nexus, e.g., in panel D, F11/C15, Sustainable healthy diets (Sections 5.3.3.11 and 5.5.3.15) and in panel E, B10, Rights-based approaches (Section 5.1.3.10). See also Section 5.6.4, Figure 5.6.5 and Table 5.6.3. These response options do not represent a comprehensive coverage of all health issues, but rather a selection of 15 for which there are benefits across some or all the nexus elements. These response option priorities must relate to specific burdens of disease in a population and their vulnerability to environmental and other impacts (Figures 5.4.5, 5.4.6 and 5.4.7). Acknowledgement of trade-offs is relative to timescales of interest. Investments in integrated, enabling response options (i.e., H11 to H15) may not always produce immediate short-term benefits but the medium- and long-term payoffs are likely to be high. Decision-support tools (Section 4.6) can assist in addressing economic costs and benefits, social and political support, equity feasibility and other nexus priorities specific to local conditions. What enabling conditions are important for response options to have nexus-wide benefits and realize their potential for transformative change? Upholding the rights to health and the right to a clean, healthy and sustainable environment creates enabling conditions for implementing the health-related response options. In addition to creating equitable and appropriate access to health services, two other response options are important enablers. Integrated health education such as Planetary Health and One Health (Section 5.4.3.15) builds knowledge and capacity in current and future actors. This education and training enable actors to address the immediate needs of a growing human population within a framework of action to address the longer-term health risks of environmental and climate degradation. Operationalising H14, the One Health approach (Section 5.4.3.14) builds on the significant progress in governance represented by the Quadripartite Alliance of WHO, FAO, WOAH and UNEP at the international level and its national equivalents. It is noteworthy that policy design and implementation, equity and institutional capacities are consistently strong enablers across the 15 response options (Figure 5.4.14). The extent to which current sectoral approaches can translate to integrated nexus-wide approaches relies on political will and institutional capacities to adapt governance and finance to support investment in multi-sectoral, nexus-oriented programs and policies. Most of the response options demonstrate high levels of technical and economic feasibility, and are associated with low economic risks and low costs of scaling. These conditions should favour the adoption of nexus governance for improving societal health and well-being. Where specific trade-offs exist, such as the possibility of occurrence of economic risks, these may be context dependent and operate over specific periods of time. Special governance mechanisms are called for to manage such situations to harness the long-term and wide spectrum of nexus co-benefits, including advancing human health. Expanding nexus approaches and promoting response options requires education, and support from nations and civil society through value systems that maximize and invest in well-being. This may be through legislation or in minimizing well-being costs (environmental, social, economic), or through greater participation of communities and individuals in decision-making processes. Furthermore, in a changing world where uncertainty, complexity and vulnerability are dominant, a cautious step-by-step implementation approach not only avoids following irreversible, ineffective pathways but also serves as feedback to improve health and sustainability. How do response options advance existing global frameworks such as the SDGs, the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework and Paris Agreement? All 15 response options are aligned with the 2030 Agenda for Sustainable Development, the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework and the Paris Agreement (Figure 5.4.19).8 The response options presented here collectively support the achievement of all SDGs with some specifically referenced in SDG targets (e.g., H01, Universal health coverage in SDG targets 3.8 and 5.5; H09, Urban green infrastructure in SDG target 11.7). All the response options address multiple goals (Figure 5.4.19) which is an important attribute of nexus response options and the intention of the 2030 Agenda framework. All but one of the 23 Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework targets are addressed. The Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework target 6 on invasive alien species is not specifically addressed but could be in more targeted responses aimed at controlling hosts of vector-borne and zoonotic diseases. Only H01, Universal health coverage (Section 5.4.3.1) was not linked to any of the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework targets. All other response options were linked to multiple targets; for example, H05, Nature on prescription (16 targets); H12, Integrated watershed-health interventions (10 targets); H10, Forest conservation for health (9 targets); H08, Mangrove conservation and restoration for health (8 targets); H14, the One Health approach (8 targets); H11, Biodiversity management for zoonoses (7 targets); H02, Intercultural health services, H04, Sustainable use of medicinal plants and H13, Health impact assessment (6 targets each). Aligning with these goals and targets is important but achieving the intended outcome can be difficult. All response options require further support through knowledge dissemination and communication, as well as structural change, and suitable and enforceable governance mechanisms to be truly effective in this way. The response options featured in this subchapter are technically feasible and extensively support achieving the SDGs, the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework targets and the Paris Agreement long-term global goals for mitigation and adaptation. Although they cannot address all diseases (as a selection of 15 response options out of many), they do support disease prevention and the attainment of good health, which long-term, requires positive outcomes for food, water, climate and biodiversity, equitable access to health services and a health sector committed to sustainability. Health governance for genuine cross-sectoral collaboration (such as One Health) exists at international and at many national scales but requires further support to have capacity to increase, bundle and upscale a diverse range of nexus responses. Producing more decision-supportive evidence for these and other potential responses is required. More data on interlinkages between health and other nexus elements and evidence-rich case studies are recommended, as extensive data collection of multisector-relevant metrics across the nexus are not yet regularly undertaken. Applying a nexus approach to health sector activities supports further multisector-sectoral collaboration to address environmental health risks, promote natures contributions to health, respect and support culturally diverse views on health and the ecosystems in which people live, and to do this with emphasis on disease prevention and a reduction of the environmental impact of modern healthcare. Education and training of health actors to IPLC values of health and nature, and contemporary developments in health that promote optimal outcomes for humans, animals and the environment enhance support for a nexus approach. Figure 5.4.19. Health response options supporting achievement of global goals. The figure shows whether an individual response option contributes to the achievement of the Sustainable Development Goals, the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework targets and the long-term goals for mitigation and adaptation of the Paris Agreement. Response options listed in order by alphanumeric code. Shaded cells indicate support for a goal or a target. Response option titles in bold font indicate that the response option supports the achievement of more than five Sustainable Development Goals and more than five Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework targets." ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.4.6" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Synthesis and conclusions" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-7.5> a ipbes:SubChapter ;
    ipbes:Chapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ch/NXS24ch7> ;
    ipbes:Report <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/NXS24> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "This concluding chapter has focused on the why, how and who of action to address nexus challenges that have been the focus of the entire assessment. Ensuring that future pathways and options for sustainability remain open by acting now across key governance actors in tackling multiple systemic, compounding and cascading crises can be enabled through the processes and steps outlined in the chapter. Selecting, negotiating and implementing response options, such as those identified in Chapters 4, 5 and 6, are key actions that can help actors achieve more synergistic, coordinated and enhanced outcomes. Yet as the entire assessment has noted, many interlinkages continue to be unaddressed by policies, actions and options, and a range of actors and institutions not represented in decision-making, due in part to the persistence of key nexus challenges around complexity, governance, values, scaling and finance as well as lack of capacities. Addressing these problems head-on can lead to both important incremental steps forward as well as facilitating shifts towards transformative change, which will involve transforming the way new structures, practices and values are reflected in response options and governance that underpins decision-making. Such transformative change is both an appropriate response and a future target, given the large costs of inaction in not resolving existing crises, including very large amounts of monetary and non-monetary damages from current and projected climate change to nature and people and rising welfare impacts of disasters, biodiversity loss and ecosystem degradation. The costs of not acting are creating not only current problems and foreclosing future decisions, but also ultimately will require larger amounts of money to fix at a later time point if deferred. The road map for nexus action has provided a use-inspired process and set of sets that can help actors from many different sectors, regions and backgrounds to approach nexus challenges in a consistent, iterative and inclusive manner, consistent with the components of nexus governance needs that the assessment (and particularly Chapter 4) have identified. Clusters of steps cover exploration of the contexts of nexus problems, coordination and strategic action to address problems using a nexus approach, and implementation and scaling of solutions, with decision-support tools available along the way. Other steps in the road map aim to shift power dynamics and vested interests blocking transitions and transformations to just and sustainable futures, including through expanding enablers for nexus approaches and response options. Decisions about what response options to pursue and how to do so can be enabled by co-design with diverse actors and institutions through transparent and equitable processes and approaches, which can be enabled by structured decision-making through the road map, and which are likely to lead to more successful implementation. A road map process can also help facilitate acting with more attention and understanding of other sectors through collaborative and coordinated action across multiple sectors and actors. Building shared visions of alternative futures is a key goal of road map use, with many processes and models available to envision sustainable transition pathways that recognize complexity and uncertainty and help find futures through these. Drawing attention to enabling conditions, such as institutional capacities, financial and economic enablers, changes in behaviours and values, technological readiness and material endowments can help ensure better response option choice and implementation. Many response options are highly cost-effective or provide livelihood and income opportunities, and others pay for themselves in cost savings and higher efficiencies. While no response option is likely to be a simple win-win, the road map steps can help with understanding of the limitations, trade-offs and potential inequities involved in any policy choice. Additionally, appropriate scaling of response options accelerates their adoption elsewhere. These processes can be helped by a focus on improved capacities for actors using the road map, including helping developed more transformative capacities of stewarding, unlocking, innovation and orchestrating. The assessment has also revealed a number of knowledge gaps across key areas of nexus interlinkages; data availability and access; assessment methods, tools, scenarios and models; ILK/IPLC issues; nexus response options; nexus governance; and nexus financing. There are also capacity gaps related to enhanced learning and engagement; facilitating expertise and information; and the need for strengthened national and regional capabilities. Technology gaps (e.g., effectiveness of emerging technologies in delivering outcomes across the nexus) exist and need attention, particularly around creating enabling conditions and policies; the importance of identifying pre-emergent technologies; assessments of technology-equity links; and attention to the emerging digital technology challenges of complexity, consistency and need for open science approaches. However, on-going research is filling many of these gaps, and in many cases, better understanding of these gaps can be accomplished through active co-production of knowledge while simultaneously taking action (e.g., through iterative experimentation), providing further evidence for the reasons for acting now." ;
    dcterms:identifier "7.5" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Conclusion" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-A1-SM0> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:Illustration <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/NXS24-FigureSPM.3> ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-2.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Ten out of twelve indicators across five categories of indirect drivers (economic, demographic, institutional, cultural and technology) have increased since 2001 (established but incomplete) {2.3.2} (Figure SPM.3). " ;
    ipbes:hasEstablishedIncomplete "Established_but_incomplete" ;
    dcterms:identifier "2.3.2" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-A1-SM1> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:Illustration <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/NXS24-FigureSPM.3> ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-2.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Research and innovation (i.e., technological development), education, poverty reduction and some environmental regulations have led to improved trends in nexus elements (established but incomplete) {2.3.2.1, 2.3.2.2, 2.3.2.3, 2.3.2.4, 2.3.2.5} (Figure SPM.3). " ;
    ipbes:hasEstablishedIncomplete "Established_but_incomplete" ;
    dcterms:identifier "2.3.2.1, 2.3.2.2, 2.3.2.3, 2.3.2.4, 2.3.2.5" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-A1-SM2> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:Illustration <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/NXS24-FiguresSPM.2andSPM.3> ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-2.3>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-2.4> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "However, negative effects from other indirect drivers, such as growth in gross domestic product, trade, population and urbanization, as well as high per capita consumption in high-income countries and increased world per capita consumption, have intensified direct drivers, although the rate of change differs among regions and countries, resulting in cascading negative effects on nexus elements (well established) {2.3.1, 2.4} (Figures SPM.2 and SPM.3). " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "2.3.1, 2.4" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-A1-SM3> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-1.1>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-1.3>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-4.2>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-4.5>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-7.2> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Governments and other actors have often failed to address these challenges, particularly as sectoral policies often do not take into account indirect drivers and remain fragmented across different institutions and actors; this results in governance gaps, conflicting objectives and incentives and leads to unintended consequences and inefficient use of resources (well established) {1.1.2, 1.3.4, 4.2.2, 4.5.4, 7.2.5}. " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "1.1.2, 1.3.4, 4.2.2, 4.5.4, 7.2.5" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-A1-SM4> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-2.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """Armed conflicts have increased in number since 2010 (well established) {2.3.2.3}. Conflicts have intensified some direct drivers and, in addition to loss of human life, may damage or destroy biodiversity, agricultural lands and water supply and impact human well-being. Armed conflicts also create barriers to collaboration, severely delaying collective and transformative action in support of sustainable development (well established) {2.3.2.3}.
""" ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "2.3.2.3" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-A2-SM0> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-2.3>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-2.4>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-2.5> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription " Freshwater and marine coastal ecosystems are particularly sensitive because they accumulate anthropogenic stressors, such as pollutants and sediments, across ecosystem and watershed boundaries (well established) {2.3.1.1, 2.3.1.3, 2.4.1, 2.5.2}. " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "2.3.1.1, 2.3.1.3, 2.4.1, 2.5.2" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-A2-SM1> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-2.5> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Water extraction for food production is responsible for approximately 80 per cent of humanity’s water demand (well established) {2.5.2.1}. " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "2.5.2.1" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-A2-SM2> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:Illustration <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/NXS24-FigureSPM.4> ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-2.3>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-2.4>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-2.5> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "The water cycle is regulated by ecosystem and geophysical processes that support biodiversity and provide nature’s contributions to people that are essential to human health and well-being (Figure SPM.4). Wetlands and inland water bodies cover just 2.6 per cent of the Earth’s terrestrial surface but play a significant role in water regulation and climate change mitigation and adaptation. They are also the water bodies most affected by human activities and climate change, with a substantial proportion having been degraded or lost over past centuries (well established) {2.3.3, 2.4.1, 2.5.1, 2.5.2}. " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "2.3.3, 2.4.1, 2.5.1, 2.5.2" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-A2-SM3> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-2.4> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Forests are also crucially important for water regulation and provision; they capture, filter and regulate water through their vegetation and soils, ensuring clean and accessible fresh water for up to 75 per cent of the world’s population (in 2005) (well established) {2.4.1}. " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "2.4.1" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-A2-SM4> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-2.5> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Consequently, loss of forest cover decreases water regulation, quality and availability, resulting in increasing water treatment costs and negative health outcomes (well established) {2.4.1}. At least 50 diseases are attributable to poor water supply, water quality and sanitation (well established) {2.5.2.3}. " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "2.4.1",
        "2.5.2.3" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-A2-SM5> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-2.3>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-2.5> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Among marine ecosystems, coral reefs are under combined threats from unsustainable fishing, land-based pollution, climate change and ocean acidification, with approximately a third of reef-building coral species already at high risk of extinction (well established) {2.3.1, 2.5.2}." ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "2.3.1, 2.5.2" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-A2-SM6> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-2.4>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-2.5> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Coral reefs are the most endangered ecosystems and may disappear globally in the next 10 to 50 years {2.4.1}. These impacts potentially affect nearly 1 billion people who live within 100 km of coral reefs (some 13 per cent of the global population) and who benefit from them in terms of food, medicine, protection from coastal storms and erosion, tourism, recreation and livelihoods (well established) {2.4.1, 2.5.2}." ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "2.4.1",
        "2.4.1, 2.5.2" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-A3-SM0> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-2.4>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-2.5> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Negative impacts of food systems on the nexus elements from both land conversion and unsustainable agricultural practices have decreased biodiversity and consequently many of nature’s contributions to people, particularly through diminished regulating contributions (e.g., regulation of water quality and climate) (well established) {2.4.2, 2.5} " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "2.4.2, 2.5" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-A3-SM1> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-2.4>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-2.5> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "and increased non-communicable disease risks (well established) {2.4.3, 2.5}, " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "2.4.3, 2.5" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-A3-SM10> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-2.4>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-2.5> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Global malnutrition and inequalities in food security persist despite a decline in the number of undernourished people (well established) {2.4.2, 2.5}. " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "2.4.2, 2.5" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-A3-SM11> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-2.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Eighty per cent of undernourished people are in developing countries and primarily live in rural areas (well established) {2.3.3}. " ;
    dcterms:identifier "2.3.3" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-A3-SM12> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-2.4>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-2.5> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "The costs of healthy diets can be high, particularly in developing countries, and, consequently, inaccessible to many: 42 per cent of the global population, 86 per cent of low-income country populations and 70 per cent of lower-middle-income country populations could not afford healthy diets in 2021 (well established) {2.4.2, 2.5}. " ;
    dcterms:identifier "2.4.2, 2.5" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-A3-SM13> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-2.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Food insecurity affects over 800 million people in Asia and Africa and, globally in 2017, nearly 3 million deaths were associated with diets low in whole grains (established but incomplete) {2.3.1}." ;
    ipbes:hasEstablishedIncomplete "Established_but_incomplete" ;
    dcterms:identifier "2.3.1" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-A3-SM14> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-2.4>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-2.5> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription " Unsustainable exploitation and pollution of freshwater and marine ecosystems impact millions of people, including those highly dependent on protein-rich food obtained from these ecosystems, such as Indigenous Peoples and local communities (well established) {2.4.2, 2.5}." ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "2.4.2, 2.5" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-A3-SM2> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-2.4>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-2.5> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "emerging infectious diseases (established but incomplete) {2.4.3, 2.5}" ;
    ipbes:hasEstablishedIncomplete "Established_but_incomplete" ;
    dcterms:identifier "2.4.3, 2.5" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-A3-SM3> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-2.5> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription " and global temperatures and other climatic changes (well established) {2.5.2.2}. " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "2.5.2.2" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-A3-SM4> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-2.3>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-2.4> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Rising global food demand, driven by affluence in particular, has led to an increase in agricultural production. This has been partially achieved through unsustainable agricultural practices that have led to unsustainable use of water and synthetic chemical substances, such as mineral fertilizers and pesticides, and that also contribute to climate change and affect the other nexus elements, through increasing air, water and land pollution and loss of biodiversity in terrestrial, freshwater and marine ecosystems (well established) {2.3.1, 2.4.2}. " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "2.3.1, 2.4.2" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-A3-SM5> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-2.4> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Climate change has also slowed growth in agricultural productivity in recent decades (well established) {2.4.4}. " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "2.4.4" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-A3-SM6> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-2.4>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.4> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Global agrobiodiversity, including genetic resources for food and agriculture, is declining (well established) {2.4.2, 5.4.3.3}, " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "2.4.2, 5.4.3.3" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-A3-SM7> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-2.4> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "with global food production heavily dependent on just nine crop species that contribute to 65 per cent of the world’s crop production (well established) {2.4.2}. " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "2.4.2" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-A3-SM8> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-2.4> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "This impacts ecosystem functioning, food system resilience, food security and nutrition, and social (employment, health) and economic (income, productivity) systems (well established) {2.4.2}. " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "2.4.2" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-A3-SM9> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-2.4> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "The increased quantity of food production has not been matched by improved nutritional quality (well established) {2.4.2}. " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "2.4.2" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-A4-SM0> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:Illustration <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/NXS24-FigureSPM.2> ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-2.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Life expectancy has increased dramatically across the globe in recent centuries, particularly due to improvements in food security and healthcare, including medicines from biodiversity, but average life expectancy varies by as much as 20 years among regions (Figure SPM.2). Child mortality rates are 10 times higher in least developed countries than in high-income countries (well established) {2.3.3}. " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "2.3.3" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-A4-SM1> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-2.3>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-2.5> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Unhealthy diets have become one of the most important drivers of global mortality, accounting for nearly 11 million adult deaths in 2017 and 255 million disability-adjusted life years (15 per cent of all disability-adjusted life years among adults) (well established) {2.3.3, 2.5}. " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "2.3.3, 2.5" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-A4-SM2> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-2.4>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-2.5> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Unsustainable farming systems contribute to biodiversity loss, excessive water use, pollution and climate change, which further exacerbate health problems. Increased air and water pollution caused an estimated 9 million premature deaths in 2019 (16 per cent of all deaths worldwide) (well established) {2.4.3, 2.5} through diseases such as respiratory disease, cancer, allergies, birth defects, neurodegenerative disease and impaired cognitive development. " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "2.4.3, 2.5" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-A4-SM3> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-2.5> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Emerging and re-emerging infectious disease events have been rising, with half of these driven by changes in land use, agricultural practices and activities that encroach on natural habitats and lead to increased contact between wildlife, domestic animals and humans (established but incomplete) {2.5}. " ;
    ipbes:hasEstablishedIncomplete "Established_but_incomplete" ;
    dcterms:identifier "2.5" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-A4-SM4> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-2.4> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "In addition, the loss of Indigenous and local knowledge of biodiversity has resulted in declines in traditional medicine use by many Indigenous Peoples and local communities {Box 2.7}. Nature is also integral to physical, emotional and mental health and well-being, as more biodiverse environments and access to them facilitate recovery from stress, depression and other health-related conditions (well established) {2.4.3}." ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "2.4.3",
        "Box 2.7" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-A5-SM0> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-2.5> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Under current trends, climate change leads to irreversible loss of marine biodiversity, such as coral reefs, and negative effects on coastal fisheries, both of which provide diets that prevent malnutrition, stunted child growth and other conditions (well established) {2.5.2.2}. " ;
    dcterms:identifier "2.5.2.2" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-A5-SM1> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-2.5> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Climate change also impacts terrestrial food production through numerous pathways (e.g., heat-stress effects on crop yield, water availability and quality) with consequences for human health and wellbeing, including exacerbating food insecurity for vulnerable populations. Other direct health impacts include extreme weather events such as heatwaves, flooding, droughts and wildfires, and increased dispersal of pathogens and pollutants (such as untreated wastewater, fertilizers, pesticides, sediments and air pollutants) that can be transboundary. Currently, 58 per cent (218 out of 375) of known human infectious diseases are likely to worsen owing to climate change (established but incomplete) {2.5.2.2}. " ;
    ipbes:hasEstablishedIncomplete "Established_but_incomplete" ;
    dcterms:identifier "2.5.2.2" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-A5-SM2> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-2.5> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Climate change directly contributed to 62,000 heat-related deaths in Europe in 2022, more than 1,500 in the United States in 2023 and between 12,000 and 19,000 heat-related deaths in children in Africa between 2011 and 2020 (well established) {2.5.2.2}. " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "2.5.2.2" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-A5-SM3> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-2.5> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "In the past 50 years, extreme weather-, climate- and water-related events have caused nearly 12,000 disasters, leading to 2 million human deaths (90 per cent in low- and lower-middle-income countries) and $4.3 trillion in total costs globally (well established) {2.5}." ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "2.5" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-A5-SM4> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-2.5> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Land-use change associated with food production was responsible for an estimated 21 per cent of global carbon dioxide equivalent emissions in 2018 (well established) {2.5.2.1}. " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "2.5.2.1" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-A5-SM5> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.5> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Functioning and resilient ecosystems make critical contributions to climate change mitigation and adaptation (well established) {5.5.3}, " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.5.3" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-A5-SM6> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-2.4>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-2.5>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.5> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "such as by buffering extreme weather events and acting as a carbon sink. For example, coastal ecosystems contribute to more than 50 per cent of carbon sequestration in the oceans and provide protection from flooding (well established) {2.4.1, 2.5, 5.5.3.13}." ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "2.4.1, 2.5, 5.5.3.13" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-A6-SM0> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-6.1> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Existing policies and international agreements have shown limited success in controlling the impacts of economic pressures on nexus elements, despite estimates that over half of global gross domestic product ($58 trillion of economic activity in 2023) is generated in sectors that are moderately to highly dependent on nature (established but incomplete) {6.1.3}. " ;
    ipbes:hasEstablishedIncomplete "Established_but_incomplete" ;
    dcterms:identifier "6.1.3" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-A6-SM1> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-6.1>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-6.2>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-7.2> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "For example, negative externalities (costs not considered as part of decision-making processes) across the fossil fuel, agriculture and fisheries sectors are currently estimated in the range of $10 trillion to $25 trillion per year, reflecting the negative impacts of production and consumption in these sectors on biodiversity, water, health and climate change (established but incomplete) {6.1.3, 6.2.3, 7.2.3}. " ;
    ipbes:hasEstablishedIncomplete "Established_but_incomplete" ;
    dcterms:identifier "6.1.3, 6.2.3, 7.2.3" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-A6-SM2> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:Illustration <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/NXS24-FigureSPM.12> ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-6.2> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Private sector financial flows that are directly damaging to biodiversity are estimated at $5.3 trillion, and public subsidies incentivizing such activities, distorting trade and increasing pressure on natural resources are estimated at approximately $1.7 trillion per year (Figure SPM.12) (established but incomplete) {6.2.3}. " ;
    ipbes:hasEstablishedIncomplete "Established_but_incomplete" ;
    dcterms:identifier "6.2.3" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-A6-SM3> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-6.2> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Additionally, illegal resource extraction activities, including in the wildlife, timber and fish trades, are valued at $100 billion to $300 billion per year or more (established but incomplete) {6.2.3}. " ;
    ipbes:hasEstablishedIncomplete "Established_but_incomplete" ;
    dcterms:identifier "6.2.3" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-A6-SM4> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:Illustration <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/NXS24-FigureSPM.12> ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-6.2> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "In contrast, expenditures aimed at improving the status of biodiversity amount to significantly less than 1 per cent of global gross domestic product (these positive flows are estimated at $200 billion per year) (Figure SPM.12) (well established) {6.2.2}." ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "6.2.2" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-A6-SM5> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-6.1>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-6.2> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "The economic impacts of biodiversity loss vary between countries and regions, with higher relative impacts in developing countries where there are also higher barriers to mobilizing sustainable financial flows (exacerbated in some cases by burdens of high debt) (well established) {6.1.4, 6.2.5}." ;
    dcterms:identifier "6.1.4, 6.2.5" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-A7-SM0> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-2.5> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Sixty-five per cent of the world’s population lives in areas where at least one nexus element is in relatively positive condition, which predominantly stems from 50 per cent of people living in areas with high food provisioning (established but incomplete) {2.5.3.1}. " ;
    ipbes:hasEstablishedIncomplete "Established_but_incomplete" ;
    dcterms:identifier "2.5.3.1" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-A7-SM1> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-2.5> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Fiftytwo per cent of people live in areas showing degradation of at least one nexus element. For example, 41 per cent of the world’s population lives in areas that experienced an extremely strong decline in biodiversity between 2000 and 2010, 9 per cent in areas that have experienced very high health burdens (high disability-adjusted life years) and 5 per cent in areas that have experienced high levels of malnutrition (well established) {2.5.3.1}." ;
    dcterms:identifier "2.5.3.1" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-A7-SM10> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-3.7> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "1. Nature-oriented nexus: This archetype exhibits significant positive impacts on biodiversity and broadly positive impacts on the other nexus elements, although impacts on food and human health are slightly lower, reflecting competition for land. Nature-oriented nexus scenarios focus on protected areas, especially in marine systems, with high levels of protection effectiveness and broadly ambitious climate action. They are characterized by strong environmental regulation, sustainable agricultural practices, lower rates of global per capita consumption and strong development of green technologies." .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-A7-SM11> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-3.7> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "2. Balanced nexus: Scenarios in this archetype have broadly positive impacts across all nexus elements, but with less positive impacts on biodiversity, water and climate and slightly more positive impacts for food and human health compared with the natureoriented nexus. Balanced nexus scenarios are characterized by stronger environmental regulation than natureoriented nexus scenarios and less reliance on technologies. Besides biodiversity conservation, this archetype also focuses heavily on the restoration and sustainable use of natural resources and on sustainable agricultural practices. Like the nature-oriented nexus archetype, it is characterized by sustainable lifestyles and consumption changes." .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-A7-SM12> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-3.7> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "3. Conservation first: This archetype prioritizes positive outcomes for biodiversity through area-based conservation but fails to improve conservation effectiveness or to set up a sufficiently holistic and reinforcing system of sustainable management across all nexus elements. Thus, unintended consequences can arise from the need to increase food production outside of protected areas, which may also lead to increases in food prices and to food insecurity. This results in moderately positive impacts on biodiversity in general, slightly positive impacts on climate, but moderately negative impacts on food and variable impacts on water and human health. The archetype includes higher economic growth than the nature-oriented nexus and the balanced nexus archetypes and fails to stabilize global aggregate consumption levels. Hence, the overall biodiversity benefits are less than in the nature-oriented nexus scenarios." .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-A7-SM13> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-3.7> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "4. Climate first: Scenarios in the climate first archetype prioritize positive impacts on climate but result in negative impacts on biodiversity and food and variable effects on water. The scenarios in this archetype assume competition for land and other resources between climate change mitigation actions and the other nexus elements if actions are not planned in an integrated way. This archetype relies very heavily on technological innovation and solutions and on stabilizing per capita consumption." .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-A7-SM14> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-3.7> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "5. Food first: In contrast to the nature-oriented nexus and balanced nexus archetypes, which focus on sustainable agriculture, the food first archetype focuses on unsustainable agriculture. This set of scenarios prioritizes food production, with positive impacts on nutritional health arising from unsustainable intensification of production and increased per capita consumption. Scenarios in the food first archetype have negative impacts on biodiversity due to land conversion for agricultural areas, unsustainable agricultural and fishing practices and pollution. They also have negative impacts on water because of increasing agricultural irrigation and negative impacts on climate change because of increases in greenhouse gas emissions from agriculture." .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-A7-SM15> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-3.7> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "6. Nature overexploitation: This set of scenarios has negative impacts on biodiversity, food, human health and climate, and variable impacts on water. Nature overexploitation scenarios are characterized by overconsumption of natural resources, especially in marine ecosystems, by unsustainable energy demand, including but not limited to demand for fossil fuels, and by weak environmental regulation exacerbated by delayed action." .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-A7-SM2> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-2.5> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription " These burdens disproportionately affect developing countries, including small island developing States, and those people in vulnerable situations in high-income countries and Indigenous Peoples (well established) {2.5.3.1, 2.5.4.1, 2.5.4.2}. " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "2.5.3.1, 2.5.4.1, 2.5.4.2" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-A7-SM3> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-1.2>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-2.5>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-4.5>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-6.2> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Pressures on Indigenous lands, such as those driven by illegal and unregulated resource extraction, have also caused serious impacts across nexus elements that are essential to livelihoods, including alarming levels of mercury found in sediments, fish and water near mining sites {Box 2.14}. Compounding these problems are losses of language and culture among some Indigenous Peoples and local communities, and their exclusion from research, decision-making and funding (well established) {1.2, 2.5.4, 4.5.2, 6.2.4}. " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "1.2, 2.5.4, 4.5.2, 6.2.4",
        "Box 2.14" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-A7-SM4> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-2.5> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Successful cases show that management of conserved areas {4.5.2, Box 5.1.3} and food systems {5.3.3.15, 7.1} by Indigenous Peoples and local communities, when recognized and supported, delivers nexus-wide benefits (well established) {2.5.4.1, 2.5.4.2}. " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "2.5.4.1, 2.5.4.2",
        "4.5.2, Box 5.1.3",
        "5.3.3.15, 7.1" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-A7-SM5> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-1.2>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-4.5> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "These promising outcomes reflect the often high dependence of Indigenous Peoples and local communities on biodiversity for their livelihoods, and the holistic approaches and world views that shape how they relate to nature and sustainably manage the nexus elements (well established) {1.2.2, 4.5.2}." ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "1.2.2, 4.5.2" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-A7-SM6> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-1.2>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-4.5> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "B. Future nexus interactions" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-A7-SM7> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-1.2>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-4.5> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Box SPM.1. Nexus scenario archetypes: implications for biodiversity and the other nexus elements" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-A7-SM8> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-3.1>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-3.7> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Nexus scenario archetypes have been created on the basis of the assessment of 186 individual scenarios from 52 studies examining interactions among at least three nexus elements {3.1, 3.7}. These scenarios covered multiple time periods, mostly ending between 2050 and 2100, although some scenarios were not tied to a specific time frame or period (e.g., scenarios of protected areas). Approximately 60 per cent of the scenarios addressed the role of indirect drivers and 12 per cent were based on stakeholder engagement, but only 8 per cent included Indigenous Peoples’ and local communities’ knowledge systems. Twelve per cent of the studies were qualitative, while around 88 per cent of the studies were quantitative. The scenarios covered the terrestrial and freshwater realms (59 per cent) and the marine realm (41 per cent). Fifty-seven per cent of the scenarios focused on the global scale, while 27 per cent covered the regional scale, 6 per cent the national scale and 10 per cent the local scale. Local and regional scenarios focused mainly on the IPBES regions of Europe and Central Asia, followed by Asia-Pacific and the Americas." ;
    dcterms:identifier "3.1, 3.7" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-A7-SM9> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-3.7> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """The scenarios were clustered statistically into six nexus scenario archetypes based on an analysis of the positive and negative outcomes for biodiversity, water, food, human health and climate change {3.7.1}. The analysed scenarios focused exclusively on human health. The archetypes represent different, plausible outcomes for the nexus elements and the interlinkages among them. Scenario archetypes 1 and 2 represent different types of sustainability scenarios; 
archetypes 3 to 5 prioritize a specific nexus element; and archetype 6 represents scenarios with little or no concern for environmental challenges. Business-as-usual scenarios, which represent the continuation of current trends, fall into both archetypes 5 and 6.""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "3.7.1" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-B1-SM0> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:Illustration <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/NXS24-BoxSPM.1,FigureSPM.5> ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-3.2>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-3.3>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-3.4>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-3.5>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-3.6>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-3.7> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Scenario studies (Box SPM.1, Figure SPM.5), while noting that there are regional differences, suggest that business-as-usual scenarios (nature overexploitation and food first) that include lifestyles intensive in material and energy consumption, increased greenhouse gas emissions, intensive land use and unsustainable exploitation of natural resources result in negative impacts on biodiversity and the other nexus elements (established but incomplete) {3.2.1, 3.3.1, 3.4.1, 3.5.1, 3.6.1, 3.7.1}. " ;
    ipbes:hasEstablishedIncomplete "Established_but_incomplete" ;
    dcterms:identifier "3.2.1, 3.3.1, 3.4.1, 3.5.1, 3.6.1, 3.7.1" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-B1-SM1> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-3.7> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Scenarios in which there is a failure to implement strong and integrated environmental regulations while emphasizing unsustainable and inequitable economic growth (conservation first, climate first and food first) result in severe trade-offs among the nexus elements (well established) {3.7.1, 3.7.2}. " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "3.7.1, 3.7.2" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-B1-SM2> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-3.7> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "In contrast, sustainability scenarios (nature-oriented nexus and balanced nexus) are associated with sustainable consumption and production and lifestyles, sustainable healthy diets, reduced food loss and waste and reduction of water use. They are also characterized by more equal distribution of benefits from economic growth, as well as by policies enabling behavioural change and pro-sustainability regulations (established but incomplete) {3.7.1, 3.7.2}. " ;
    ipbes:hasEstablishedIncomplete "Established_but_incomplete" ;
    dcterms:identifier "3.7.1, 3.7.2" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-B1-SM3> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-3.7> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Such scenarios often consider multiple actors and the associated values and knowledge systems, including those of Indigenous Peoples and local communities (established but incomplete) {3.7.3}. " ;
    ipbes:hasEstablishedIncomplete "Established_but_incomplete" ;
    dcterms:identifier "3.7.3" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-B1-SM4> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-3.7> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Changes in indirect drivers in future scenarios, such as institutional drivers (e.g., governance, power relations), cultural drivers (e.g., lifestyles) and technology, influence direct drivers and have strong individual impacts on biodiversity, water, food, human health and climate change and on the interconnections among them (well established) {3.7.1, 3.7.2, 3.7.3}. " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "3.7.1, 3.7.2, 3.7.3" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-B1-SM5> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-3.6>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-3.7> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Scenarios show that timely implementation of response options is critical, as options implemented early are associated with positive outcomes for biodiversity, food, water, human health and climate change (established but incomplete) {3.6.3, 3.7.1}, " ;
    ipbes:hasEstablishedIncomplete "Established_but_incomplete" ;
    dcterms:identifier "3.6.3, 3.7.1" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-B1-SM6> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-7.2> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "with increasing evidence of the greater effectiveness of investing in actions now rather than later (established but incomplete) {7.2.4}." ;
    ipbes:hasEstablishedIncomplete "Established_but_incomplete" ;
    dcterms:identifier "7.2.4" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-B2-SM0> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-3.2>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-3.7> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Nature overexploitation and food first scenarios show declining outcomes for biodiversity, mainly driven by unsustainable food production and resource extraction, as well as by climate change (well established) {3.2, 3.7.1}. " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "3.2, 3.7.1" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-B2-SM1> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:Illustration <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/NXS24-FigureSPM.6> ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-3.2>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-3.7> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Nature-oriented nexus and balanced nexus scenarios that include integrated approaches combining enhanced conservation, restoration and sustainable use and climate change mitigation actions with measures targeting the drivers of habitat conversion and degradation, such as sustainable production and consumption interventions, succeed in reversing biodiversity loss while achieving multiple benefits for water, food, human health and climate change mitigation and adaptation (Figure SPM.6) (established but incomplete) {3.2.2, 3.2.3, 3.7.1}. " ;
    ipbes:hasEstablishedIncomplete "Established_but_incomplete" ;
    dcterms:identifier "3.2.2, 3.2.3, 3.7.1" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-B2-SM2> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-3.5>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-3.6>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-3.7> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Such scenarios project positive long-term outcomes across nexus elements by supporting socioecological processes that are essential for clean water (e.g., filtration), food production (e.g., pollination, soil formation and maintenance, and pest control), human health and quality of life (e.g., air quality and nature’s contributions to people related to positive physical and mental health), and adapting to and mitigating climate change (e.g., carbon sequestration) and its impacts (e.g., flood mitigation) (established but incomplete) {3.5.3, 3.6.3, 3.6.4, 3.7.1, 3.7.2}. " ;
    ipbes:hasEstablishedIncomplete "Established_but_incomplete" ;
    dcterms:identifier "3.5.3, 3.6.3, 3.6.4, 3.7.1, 3.7.2" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-B2-SM3> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-3.2> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Nature-oriented nexus scenarios aim to increase and improve the effectiveness of protected areas, conservation of key biodiversity areas and other effective area-based conservation measures, and highlight the importance of integrated spatial planning and deliberate efforts to address existing and emerging injustices and inequality (established but incomplete) {3.2.3}. " ;
    ipbes:hasEstablishedIncomplete "Established_but_incomplete" ;
    dcterms:identifier "3.2.3" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-B2-SM4> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-3.2>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-3.7> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Evidence from scenarios shows that protecting up to 30 per cent of terrestrial, freshwater and marine areas can provide nexus-wide benefits if these are effectively managed for nature and people (established but incomplete) {3.2.3, 3.7.1}. " ;
    ipbes:hasEstablishedIncomplete "Established_but_incomplete" ;
    dcterms:identifier "3.2.3, 3.7.1" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-B2-SM5> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-3.2> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Higher levels of protection in terrestrial systems beyond 30 per cent would have greater biodiversity benefits, but can lead to trade-offs for food production, food security and nutritional health, including increases in food prices (established but incomplete) {3.2.3.1}. " ;
    ipbes:hasEstablishedIncomplete "Established_but_incomplete" ;
    dcterms:identifier "3.2.3.1" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-B2-SM6> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-3.2>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-Box3.5> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "In marine ecosystems, nature protection, if managed effectively, could deliver synergies across all the nexus elements (established but incomplete) {3.2.3, Box 3.5}." ;
    ipbes:hasEstablishedIncomplete "Established_but_incomplete" ;
    dcterms:identifier "3.2.3, Box 3.5" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-B3-SM0> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-3.4> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Globally, the environmental impacts of food systems in terms of greenhouse gas emissions, land-use change, water use, and nitrate and phosphorus pollution are projected to increase under food first scenarios, which prioritize unsustainable food production and consumption over other nexus elements (well established) {3.4}. " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "3.4" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-B3-SM1> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-3.4> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Such scenarios also project significant negative impacts on biodiversity through degradation of ecosystems, habitat loss and habitat fragmentation (well established) {3.4}. " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "3.4" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-B3-SM2> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-3.4>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-Table3.2> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Transforming food systems would produce nexus-wide benefits, including reducing pressures for land conversion, greenhouse gas emissions and water pollution (well established) {3.4, Table 3.2}. " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "3.4, Table 3.2" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-B3-SM3> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-3.4> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Food system transformations assessed in nature-oriented nexus scenarios combine a range of response options comprising sustainable agricultural practices (such as improving nitrogen use efficiency, integrated pest management, agroecology, agroforestry and sustainable intensification), reductions in food losses and waste, adoption of novel food and feed sources (e.g., macroalgae, microbial protein) and sustainable healthy diets {3.4}. Such transformations would enable the current agricultural land area to meet the calorific and nutritional needs of future generations in the medium to long term (e.g., through improved productivity), enabling positive outcomes for human health and for biodiversity as well as sustainability (well established) {3.4}. " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "3.4" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-B3-SM4> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-3.4>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.3>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.5> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Scenarios show that sustainable healthy diets and the reduction of food loss and waste decrease greenhouse gas emissions and benefit other nexus elements; in addition, sustainable healthy diets also reduce human deaths (well established) {3.4.2, 5.3.3, 5.5.3}. " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "3.4.2, 5.3.3, 5.5.3" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-B3-SM5> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-3.4> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "If sustainably managed, aquatic ecosystems can also contribute to biodiversity conservation and health. Scenarios based on sustainable and inclusive food production from the oceans and sustainable aquaculture have nexus-wide benefits and include managing fisheries sustainably, favouring low-impact fishing techniques that reduce discard, bycatch and the destruction of habitat, shifting towards sustainable healthy diets that are less resource-intensive and distributing food more equitably (well established) {3.4.2, 3.4.3}. " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "3.4.2, 3.4.3" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-B3-SM6> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-3.7> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Such transformative scenarios are based on proactive policies, such as marine protected areas, and are inclusive of the practices of Indigenous Peoples and local communities. They also show the importance of connecting biodiversity, water, food, human health and climate change policy to reduce tradeoffs (established but incomplete) {3.7.1, 3.7.2, 3.7.3}." ;
    ipbes:hasEstablishedIncomplete "Established_but_incomplete" ;
    dcterms:identifier "3.7.1, 3.7.2, 3.7.3" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-B4-SM0> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-3.6> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Exposure to risks from climate change across multiple sectors is projected to double in the case of global warming levels between 1.5°C and 2°C (well established) and to double again in the case of warming levels between 2°C and 3°C (established but incomplete) {3.6, 3.6.2}. " ;
    ipbes:hasEstablishedIncomplete "Established_but_incomplete" ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "3.6, 3.6.2" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-B4-SM1> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-3.5>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-3.6> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "This presents a growing challenge to biodiversity and the integrity and functioning of ecosystems in terrestrial, freshwater and marine environments. Intensifying climate change will also stress water resources and undermine agricultural productivity and food productivity in food production systems, exacerbate droughts and flooding, cause increased mortality from heatwaves and expand the epidemic belt for vector-borne diseases towards higher latitudes and altitudes (well established) {3.5.1, 3.5.2, 3.6.2, 3.6.3}. " ;
    dcterms:identifier "3.5.1, 3.5.2, 3.6.2, 3.6.3" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-B4-SM2> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-3.6> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "The impacts of climate change can also interact with other drivers, such as land-use change, potentially leading to tipping points (established but incomplete) {3.6.1}. " ;
    ipbes:hasEstablishedIncomplete "Established_but_incomplete" ;
    dcterms:identifier "3.6.1" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-B4-SM3> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-3.6> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Scenarios have explored the potential implications of delayed mitigation action for future large-scale implementation of land- and ocean-based carbon dioxide removal options to achieve the Paris Agreement long-term temperature goals. If not planned in an integrated way and accompanied by ambitious emissions reduction strategies, these scenarios show that there could be adverse impacts on terrestrial biodiversity, water and food due to increased competition for land (established but incomplete) {3.6.3}. " ;
    ipbes:hasEstablishedIncomplete "Established_but_incomplete" ;
    dcterms:identifier "3.6.3" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-B4-SM4> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-3.6> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Conversely, marine scenarios show that climate change mitigation options, such as restoring seagrass meadows and mangroves, can have multiple benefits across the nexus (well established) {3.6.3}. " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "3.6.3" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-B4-SM5> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-3.6> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "However, climate change mitigation solutions that focus on the placement of excess carbon in deep water are still far from implementation, and knowledge is lacking about potential impacts on biodiversity and food (established but incomplete) {3.6.3.2}. " ;
    ipbes:hasEstablishedIncomplete "Established_but_incomplete" ;
    dcterms:identifier "3.6.3.2" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-B4-SM6> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-3.6>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-3.7> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Scenarios that are characterized by integrated climate actions such as conserving and restoring ecosystems for carbon sequestration have nexus-wide benefits (well established) {3.6.3, 3.7.1, 3.7.2}." ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "3.6.3, 3.7.1, 3.7.2" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-B4-SM7> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-3.6> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription " For example, many synergies across the nexus elements are possible by conserving coastal and marine systems that contribute to carbon sequestration and to adaptation to the impacts of climate change (well established) {3.2.4}. Scenarios show that climate change adaptation is urgently needed and can have multiple benefits for other nexus elements (well established) {3.6.4}. " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "3.2.4",
        "3.6.4" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-B4-SM8> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-3.6>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-Box3.8> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Inclusive and integrated planning of adaptation actions (e.g., urban blue-green infrastructure {3.6.4.1, 5.4.3.9}) can help to avoid unintended consequences (or maladaptation), such as the risks of harm to the livelihoods of Indigenous Peoples and local communities (established but incomplete) {3.6.2, 3.6.4, Box 3.8}." ;
    ipbes:hasEstablishedIncomplete "Established_but_incomplete" ;
    dcterms:identifier "3.6.2, 3.6.4, Box 3.8",
        "3.6.4.1, 5.4.3.9" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-B5-SM0> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-3.7> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Nature-oriented nexus and balanced nexus scenarios support the achievement of most of the Sustainable Development Goals, whereas food first and nature overexploitation scenarios support the achievement of the fewest Goals (established but incomplete) {3.7.2}. " ;
    ipbes:hasEstablishedIncomplete "Established_but_incomplete" ;
    dcterms:identifier "3.7.2" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-B5-SM1> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-3.7> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "The nexus scenario archetypes show that maximizing all nexus elements simultaneously is unlikely to be possible, but achieving balance across policy goals is likely to lead to beneficial outcomes for nature and people. Nature-oriented nexus and balanced nexus scenarios have the largest benefits for Goals 14 (life below water) and 15 (life on land). Scenarios with the least benefits for multiple policy goals (food first and nature overexploitation) contain many marine scenarios associated with unsustainable fishing. Nexus scenarios rarely assess Goals 1, 5, 7, 10, 16 and 17, which represents an important knowledge gap (well established) {3.7.2}. " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "3.7.2" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-B5-SM2> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-6.1>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-7.2> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Scenarios, including those under the nature-oriented nexus and balanced nexus archetypes, do not adequately consider the complex issues of poverty and social inequalities; those that do consider these issues use simplified metrics that capture distributional impacts only inadequately. Delaying action to meet policy goals will likely increase costs over time. For example, some estimates indicate that delaying action on biodiversity policy goals to 2030 could double the eventual costs of action, while also increasing the probability of irreplaceable losses, such as species extinctions (established but incomplete) {6.1.2.4, 7.2.4}. " ;
    ipbes:hasEstablishedIncomplete "Established_but_incomplete" ;
    dcterms:identifier "6.1.2.4, 7.2.4" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-B5-SM3> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-7.2> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Similarly, it has been estimated that delaying action to meet climate goals could increase the costs of adaptation and mitigation by a minimum of approximately $500 billion per year (established but incomplete) {7.2.4, 7.2.5}." ;
    ipbes:hasEstablishedIncomplete "Established_but_incomplete" ;
    dcterms:identifier "7.2.4, 7.2.5" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-B5-SM4> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-7.2> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "C. Response options that address nexus interactions" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-B5-SM5> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:Illustration <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/NXS24-FigureSPM.7> ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-6.2> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "The response options considered here are actions or policies that can help advance governance and sustainable management of one or more elements of the nexus. The 71 response options assessed in depth in Chapter 5 {5.1, 5.2, 5.3, 5.4, 5.5. 5.6} were clustered into 10 categories that form the structure for the background messages in section C (Figure SPM.7): conserve or halt conversion of ecosystems of high ecological integrity {C1}, restore natural and semi-natural ecosystems {C2}, manage ecosystems in human-exploited lands and waters {C3}, consume sustainably {C4}, reduce pollution and waste {C5}, integrate planning and governance {C6}, manage risk {C7}, ensure rights and equity {C8}, align financing {C9} and a tenth category for other options. The response options are not meant to be an exhaustive list, but rather to represent a range of evidence-based options involving different actors and sectors, spatial and temporal scales, and feasibility levels that can be adapted to different national and local circumstances. They represent a menu of options that can be applied in different contexts. Some response options may not be appropriate in all countries, and all would be implemented in accordance with national legislation and sovereignty and in accordance with relevant international obligations. Even within countries, effectiveness and acceptability depend critically on political, social and ecological contexts. This important point is emphasized here, as it applies to all the response options summarized below. These categories are neither exhaustive nor exclusive; for example, response options that elsewhere might be considered types of “nature-based solutions” and “ecosystem-based approaches” are categorized here by their primary goals (e.g., reducing disaster risks or managing ecosystem functions such as carbon sequestration). In addition to the 71 response options, Chapter 6 includes several financial response options assessed using a different methodology {6.2} than that used in Chapter 5; some of these options are discussed in background message C9." ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.1, 5.2, 5.3, 5.4, 5.5. 5.6",
        "6.2",
        "C1",
        "C2",
        "C3",
        "C4",
        "C5",
        "C6",
        "C7",
        "C8",
        "C9" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C1-SM0> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.1>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.3>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.4> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Many successful examples of area-based conservation (B01) exist in different parts of the world that directly support target 3 of the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework, to effectively conserve at least 30 per cent of terrestrial, inland water and coastal and marine areas by 2030; these include the creation of public and private natural reserves, national parks and conservation of areas and other effective area-based conservation measures of special interest. Successful establishment and management are dependent on aligning the values of multiple actors, such as through ensuring the full and effective participation of Indigenous Peoples and local communities in processes from co-design to governance (e.g., through rights-based approaches, among others, in accordance with national legislation and international instruments) and sharing the multiple benefits of conservation across public and private stakeholders and rights holders (well established) {5.1.3.1, 5.3.3.1, 5.4.3.10}. " ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.1.3.1, 5.3.3.1, 5.4.3.10" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C1-SM1> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.1> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "For example, marine protected areas in Australia and Chile have led to increases in biodiversity, greater abundance of fish for human consumption and improved incomes for local communities and, in the case of Australia, increased tourism revenues (B01) (well established) {5.1.3.1}. " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.1.3.1" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C1-SM2> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Indirect actions such as improving the sustainable production and consumption of certain foods and increasing resource use efficiency can complement and enable ecosystem conservation and adaptation by reducing pressure on land and aquatic resources (F01) (established but incomplete) {5.3.3.1}. " ;
    ipbes:hasEstablishedIncomplete "Established_but_incomplete" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.3.3.1" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C1-SM3> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "In some cases, commodity-wide and private industry commitments, such as the Amazon Soy Moratorium in Brazil, have reduced pressures on important ecosystems through better monitoring and more transparent efforts (F01) (well established) {5.3.3.1}. " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.3.3.1" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C1-SM4> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:Illustration <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/NXS24-FigureSPM.8> ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.4> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Conserving or halting conversion of forests and other ecosystems protects human health and well-being by combating climate change, reducing the impact of extreme weather events, such as storms, droughts and landslides, increasing water and air quality and reducing disease risk (H08, H10) (Figure SPM.8) (well established) {5.4.3.8, 5.4.3.10}." ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.4.3.8, 5.4.3.10" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C10-SM0> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.6>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-7.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Current approaches to managing nexus elements have failed to harness the full potential of nexus-wide benefits because they have been designed and implemented in isolation, at more limited scales or without adequate consideration of the interdependencies and interconnections among nexus elements and among response options (well established) {5.6, 7.3}. " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.6, 7.3" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C10-SM1> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-4.4>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.6>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-7.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Coordinated implementation and scaling of multiple response options are likely to increase their cumulative impacts and potential for transformative change but will require effective governance by and collaboration among disparate actors and reliable sources of finance (established but incomplete) {4.4, 5.6, 7.3}. " ;
    ipbes:hasEstablishedIncomplete "Established_but_incomplete" ;
    dcterms:identifier "4.4, 5.6, 7.3" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C10-SM10> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:Illustration <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/NXS24-FigureSPM.10-C>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/NXS24-FigureSPM.7> ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.6> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "The capacity to contribute to multiple goals and targets simultaneously is a common and powerful feature of nexus approaches. These response options are therefore a promising mechanism for integrating efforts and accelerating progress towards multiple policy goals and frameworks. Response options in each of the 10 categories (Figure SPM.7) align with both frameworks discussed above and with the Paris Agreement (Figure SPM.10-C). Each category supports between 7 and 12 Sustainable Development Goals, between 9 and 19 Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework targets, and the long-term goals for mitigation and adaptation of the Paris Agreement. Individual response options also can improve implementation of global frameworks. Twenty-four response options simultaneously advance more than five Sustainable Development Goals and more than five Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework targets: B01, Area-based conservation; B02/C14, Urban nature-based solutions; B03/C11, Agroecology; B04, Ecosystem-based adaptation in rural landscapes; B09, Integrated landscape and seascape approaches; B10, Rightsbased approaches; B11, Multilateral environmental agreements; B12, Land and sea planning; B14, Reconnecting people with nature; W08, Transboundary water cooperation; W13, Water-sensitive urban infrastructure; F02, Restore soil health; F03/C02, Sustainable intensification; F04, Ecological intensification – croplands; F06, Ecological intensification – aquatic foods; F11/C15, Sustainable healthy diets; F13, Reform public spending; F15, Indigenous food systems; H09, Urban green infrastructure; H10, Forest conservation for health; H12, Integrated watershed-health interventions; C07, Sustainable bioeconomy; C12, Forest-based practices to address climate change; and C13, Restoration of coastal and marine ecosystems for carbon sequestration." .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C10-SM11> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-7.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "These response options can help overcome gaps within existing frameworks; for example, a spatial disconnect exists between the national to global scales at which progress towards goals is monitored and the more local scales at which many nexus elements and systems are managed. To facilitate nexus governance and approaches, new types of indicators, data and processes may need to be put in place to reflect nexus interlinkages and monitor them over time (established but incomplete) {7.3.8}." ;
    ipbes:hasEstablishedIncomplete "Established_but_incomplete" ;
    dcterms:identifier "7.3.8" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C10-SM12> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-7.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "D. Governing the nexus for achieving just and sustainable futures" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C10-SM2> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-7.2> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Well-coordinated implementation of response options can also result in cost savings compared with siloed and duplicative policies (established but incomplete) {7.2.5}. " ;
    ipbes:hasEstablishedIncomplete "Established_but_incomplete" ;
    dcterms:identifier "7.2.5" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C10-SM3> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:Illustration <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/NXS24-BoxSPM.2,FigureSPM.10> ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.6> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Bundling or sequencing response options would support the achievement of the global policy frameworks set out under the 2030 Agenda for Sustainable Development, the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework and the Paris Agreement (Box SPM.2, Figure SPM.10) (well established) {5.6}. " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.6" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C10-SM4> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.6>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-7.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "The goals and targets of these policy frameworks interrelate strongly, and response options that address challenges or opportunities associated with multiple nexus elements can simultaneously support these global policy frameworks. To be effective, response options need to be implemented in context-specific ways that are appropriate to specific regional, national and local circumstances, as there is no onesize-fits-all approach (well established) {5.6, 7.3.5}. " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.6, 7.3.5" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C10-SM5> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-2.5>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-3.6> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Importantly, however, many response options will be less effective or impossible to implement if climate change is not urgently addressed (well established) {2.5.2.2, 3.6.2}." ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "2.5.2.2, 3.6.2" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C10-SM6> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-2.5>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-3.6> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Box SPM.2. Contribution of response options to global policy frameworks " .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C10-SM7> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:Illustration <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/NXS24-Figure SPM.10> ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.6> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Response options contribute to the implementation and achievement of the Sustainable Development Goals, the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework and the Paris Agreement (well established) {5.6.7} (Figure SPM.10). Many response options support all three frameworks, thereby offering valuable mechanisms for addressing challenges and priorities in an integrated manner and improving implementation across global policy goals (well established) {5.6.7}. Mapping response options to the specific goals and targets of each framework illustrates their alignment. " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.6.7" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C10-SM8> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:Illustration <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/NXS24-FigureSPM.10-A> ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.6> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Each of the 17 Sustainable Development Goals is supported by at least three response options, and 11 Goals are supported by 10 or more response options (Figure SPM.10-A). As expected, Goals that focus on the nexus elements (life on land, life below water, clean water, zero hunger, health and well-being and climate action) are most closely aligned with response options (well established) {5.6.7}. However, response options align substantially with all the other Goals, illustrating broad support for the global agenda for just and sustainable futures. " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.6.7" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C10-SM9> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:Illustration <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/NXS24-FigureSPM.10-B> ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.6> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "The Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework is similarly well supported by response options. Each of the 23 targets is supported by at least three response options, and 16 are supported by 10 or more response options (Figure SPM.10-B). Targets advanced by a large number of response options include those focused on habitat conservation, pollution reduction, climate change adaptation, biodiversity within food systems, and nature’s contributions to people. As with the Sustainable Development Goals, response options also provide substantial support to targets of the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework that are less directly related to the five nexus elements. " .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C2-SM0> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:Illustration <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/NXS24-FigureSPM.8> ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.1> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Restoring ecosystems supports targets 2, 8, 10 and 11 of the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework. Restoring ecosystems provides multiple benefits by increasing the capacity of degraded lands and waters to provide habitat functions, to improve water quality and availability and to restore productive capacity (Figure SPM.8) (well established) {5.1.3.5, 5.1.3.6, 5.1.3.7}. " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.1.3.5, 5.1.3.6, 5.1.3.7" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C2-SM1> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.1>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.4>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.5> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Restoration contributes to climate change adaptation and socioecological resilience and can also contribute to climate change mitigation when it targets carbon storage in forests, peatlands, seagrass beds, salt marshes and marine and coastal ecosystems that contribute to carbon sequestration (B05, B07, H08, C04, C12, C13) (well established) {5.1.3.5, 5.1.3.7, 5.4.3.8, 5.5.3.4, 5.5.3.12, 5.5.3.13}. " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.1.3.5, 5.1.3.7, 5.4.3.8, 5.5.3.4, 5.5.3.12, 5.5.3.13" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C2-SM2> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "For example, mangrove restoration in Senegal (C13) has resulted in significant carbon sequestration, increases in biodiversity, reductions in coastal erosion and improved water quality, food security, livelihoods and health for local populations {Box 5.5.3}. Likewise, soil restoration improves soil health, enhancing water regulation and food production potential (F02) (well established) {5.3.3.2} " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.3.3.2",
        "Box 5.5.3" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C2-SM3> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.1>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "and supporting climate change mitigation through increasing carbon storage as well as providing adaptation benefits (well established) {5.5.3.1}. When targeting areas of biodiversity concern, restoration can protect endangered species and preserve culturally important food sources and practices (B08, F15) (well established) {5.1.3.8, 5.3.3.15}. " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.1.3.8, 5.3.3.15",
        "5.5.3.1" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C2-SM4> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.1>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Restoration is most effective when coordinated across actors (e.g., among Indigenous Peoples and local communities, government actors and other stakeholders and rights holders) and implemented at large scales (B05, B06, B07, F02) (well established) {5.1.3.5, 5.1.3.6, 5.1.3.7, 5.3.3.2}. " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.1.3.5, 5.1.3.6, 5.1.3.7, 5.3.3.2" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C2-SM5> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.1> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "However, such broad efforts can face challenges stemming from their long-term nature, financial requirements and dependence on enduring and resilient partnerships (well established) {5.1.3.5, 5.1.3.6}. " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.1.3.5, 5.1.3.6" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C2-SM6> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.3>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-Box5.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Successful projects often address multiple stakeholder concerns, ensure equity in decision-making, implement systematic planning and monitoring and have secure financing (well established) {Box 5.3.1, 5.3.3.2, Box 5.3.2, 5.3.4}. For example, in south-central Niger, the co-development of farmer-managed natural regeneration has empowered local farmers through widely applicable and low-cost efforts and led to the regreening of 5 million ha with native trees and agroforestry systems, thus enhancing soil health and biodiversity and increasing cereal yields by 30 per cent (F02) {5.3.3.2, Box 5.3.3}." ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.3.3.2, Box 5.3.3",
        "Box 5.3.1, 5.3.3.2, Box 5.3.2, 5.3.4" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C3-SM0> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-2.5>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Several response options can directly improve the sustainable use and management of ecosystems and support targets 10, 11 and 12 of the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework. This is particularly important in agricultural systems, as the way food is produced, what foods are produced and consumed, where they are produced and how much food is lost and wasted impact both nature and people (well established) {2.5.2.1, 5.3.1}. " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "2.5.2.1, 5.3.1" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C3-SM1> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.1>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.3>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.5> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Agroecology represents a shift to production systems where equitable access to land and a blend of scientific and Indigenous and local knowledges guide the sustainable management of biodiversity, crops and other resources (B03, F04, F05, F06, C11) (well established) {5.1.3.3, 5.3.3.4, 5.3.3.5, 5.3.3.6, 5.5.3.11}. " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.1.3.3, 5.3.3.4, 5.3.3.5, 5.3.3.6, 5.5.3.11" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C3-SM2> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.2> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Ecological intensification of croplands and rangelands uses ecological processes and reduces external inputs, creating habitats and connectivity for biodiversity (B03, F04, F05) (well established) {5.1.3.3, 5.3.3.4, 5.3.3.5} while enhancing water retention (W04) (established but incomplete) {5.2.3.4}, " ;
    ipbes:hasEstablishedIncomplete "Established_but_incomplete" ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.1.3.3, 5.3.3.4, 5.3.3.5",
        "5.2.3.4" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C3-SM3> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:Illustration <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/NXS24-FigureSPM.8> ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.3>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.5> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "crop productivity (F04) (well established) {5.3.3.4} and soil health (F02), including soil organic content (C01) (Figure SPM.8) (well established) {5.3.3.2, 5.5.3.1}. " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.3.3.2, 5.5.3.1",
        "5.3.3.4" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C3-SM4> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Ecological intensification benefits from creating and supporting markets for sustainable products, payments for ecosystem services and other positive incentives (well established) {5.3.3.3} " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.3.3.3" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C3-SM5> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.2>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.3>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.5> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "when in accordance with relevant international trade obligations. In aquatic ecosystems, ecological intensification of aquatic food production (F06), sustainable inland fisheries management (W05) and integrated multitrophic aquaculture (C03) have positive impacts on food production, nutrition, biodiversity, climate change adaptation and livelihoods (established but incomplete) {5.2.3.5, 5.3.3.6, 5.5.3.3}. " ;
    ipbes:hasEstablishedIncomplete "Established_but_incomplete" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.2.3.5, 5.3.3.6, 5.5.3.3" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C3-SM6> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.3>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.5> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Sustainable intensification can lead to land sparing and is a globally applicable response option for increasing agricultural production efficiency and overall food production, while reducing land conversion and social, environmental and some health impacts (well established) {5.3.3.3, 5.5.3.2}. " ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.3.3.3, 5.5.3.2" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C3-SM7> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.1>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.5> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Different response options acknowledge the importance of combining land-sparing and land-sharing practices in a context-specific manner. Managing ecosystems in other settings, such as in forests through forest-based practices to address climate change (C12) and in cities through urban nature-based solutions (B02, C14), and/or ecosystem-based approaches can have multiple benefits, including for climate regulation, water availability and mental and physical health (well established) {5.1.3.2, 5.5.3.12, 5.5.3.14}." ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.1.3.2, 5.5.3.12, 5.5.3.14" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C4-SM0> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.3>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.4>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.5> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Response options can enable and encourage sustainable consumption and support target 16 of the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework. These include shifting to sustainable healthy diets (F11, H06, C15) and reducing food waste (F10), which together benefit food security and health, reduce greenhouse gas emissions and could free up land, providing in a range of cases co-benefits for nexus elements, such as biodiversity conservation and carbon sinks (well established) {5.3.3.10, 5.3.3.11, 5.4.3.6, 5.5.3.15}. " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.3.3.10, 5.3.3.11, 5.4.3.6, 5.5.3.15" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C4-SM1> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.2> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Other options include improving the efficiency of water use in agriculture (W04), which can benefit food production and water conservation (well established) {5.2.3.4}, " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.2.3.4" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C4-SM2> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:Illustration <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/NXS24-FigureSPM.8> ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.5> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "and sustainable bioeconomy (C07), which benefits all nexus elements (well established) {5.5.3.7} (Figure SPM.8). " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.5.3.7" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C4-SM3> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.5> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "The adoption of new and renewable energy technologies, such as solar and wind power, supports a rapid transition to renewable energy (C05, C06) {5.5.3.5, 5.5.3.6}, helping to mitigate climate change and its negative impacts on all nexus elements (well established) {5.5.1}, " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.5.1",
        "5.5.3.5, 5.5.3.6" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C4-SM4> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.5> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "but environmental assessments and appropriate policies would be needed to avoid trade-offs, particularly for biodiversity and food systems (well established) {5.5.3.5, 5.5.3.6}. " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.5.3.5, 5.5.3.6" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C4-SM5> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.5> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Behaviour change will be necessary to shift consumption practices and can be enabled by increasing accessibility and desirability, taking into account cultural acceptance of sustainable healthy diets (F11, H06, C15) (well established) {5.3.3.11, 5.4.3.6, 5.5.3.15} and making sustainable energy and water consumption default options (C07) (well established) {5.5.3.7}. " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.3.3.11, 5.4.3.6, 5.5.3.15",
        "5.5.3.7" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C4-SM6> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.3>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-Box5.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "For example, implementing food-based dietary guidelines in public food procurement, particularly targeting public school feeding programmes, can create structured demand for healthy food in combination with increased opportunities for on-farm diversification aimed at increasing the supply and consumption of local seasonal foods (well established) {5.3.3.11, Box 5.3.8, Box 5.3.9}. " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.3.3.11, Box 5.3.8, Box 5.3.9" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C4-SM7> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.4> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Protecting the diversity and availability of medicinal plants can also promote their sustainable consumption (H04) (well established) {5.4.3.4}." ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.4.3.4" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C5-SM0> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:Illustration <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/NXS24-FigureSPM.8> ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.2>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.3>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.5> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Response options can reduce pollution directly through regulations and incentives (W12, F07, F08, F09), as well as indirectly through reduced and more efficient use of fertilizers, improved waste management and reduced use of pesticides (F03, C02) (well established) {5.2.3.12, 5.3.3.3, 5.3.3.7, 5.3.3.8, 5.3.3.9, 5.5.3.2}, leading to improved freshwater quality, air quality, ocean quality and soil health (Figure SPM.8). " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.2.3.12, 5.3.3.3, 5.3.3.7, 5.3.3.8, 5.3.3.9, 5.5.3.2" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C5-SM1> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-6.2> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "These response options support target 7 of the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework. Some countries have successfully implemented pollution controls in the form of reduced subsidies to agricultural production systems (e.g., leading to declines in nitrogen pollution in Denmark) {Box 6.2}. However, developing countries may face multiple barriers in the reduction of such subsidies (well established) {6.2.3}." ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "6.2.3",
        "Box 6.2" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C5-SM2> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.4>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.5> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Levels of pollution, such as air contaminants, that are hazardous to health can be reduced through international and national standards and regulations (C08), including emissions standards for motor vehicles and power plants (H07), which also improve environmental protection more broadly (well established) {5.4.3.7, 5.5.3.8}. " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.4.3.7, 5.5.3.8" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C5-SM3> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.2>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.3>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.4>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.5> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Reducing pollution from all sources is particularly significant for people in developing countries. For example, 90 per cent of premature deaths from pollution occur in low- and middle-income countries, with air pollution being the major cause of those deaths {5.4.3.7}, and the benefits to humans and nature are often greater than the costs of such policies. Furthermore, access to adequate sanitation services and domestic wastewater treatment is a critical issue in African, Asian and Latin American and Caribbean countries (well established) {5.2.3.12, 5.3.3.7, 5.3.3.8, 5.3.3.9, 5.4.3.7, 5.5.3.2}. " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.2.3.12, 5.3.3.7, 5.3.3.8, 5.3.3.9, 5.4.3.7, 5.5.3.2",
        "5.4.3.7" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C5-SM4> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Reducing plastic pollution (F09) has led to increased water quality and wildlife protection, fewer floods, and reductions in incidence of associated water-borne diseases; nevertheless, some measures to reduce plastic pollution have not been effective in some countries, and subsidizing recycling often requires costly government intervention (established but incomplete) {5.3.3.9}." ;
    ipbes:hasEstablishedIncomplete "Established_but_incomplete" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.3.3.9" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C6-SM0> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.1>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.3>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.4> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Response options involving mainstreaming biodiversity across sectors primarily target biodiversity, but they also have considerable potential to benefit other nexus elements and thereby support important policy goals, including the Sustainable Development Goals, the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework and the Paris Agreement (well established) {5.1.1.4, 5.1.3.9, 5.1.3.12, 5.3.3.12, 5.4.3.12}. " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.1.1.4, 5.1.3.9, 5.1.3.12, 5.3.3.12, 5.4.3.12" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C6-SM1> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:Illustration <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/NXS24-FigureSPM.9> ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.1>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Response options that integrate across landscapes and seascapes (B09) and that involve strategic land and sea planning (B12, F12) produce nexus-wide benefits by implementing several actions either together (i.e., in bundles) or sequentially to take advantage of synergies (Figure SPM.9) (well established) {5.1.3.9, 5.1.3.12, 5.3.3.12}. " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.1.3.9, 5.1.3.12, 5.3.3.12" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C6-SM2> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.1>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Nexus-wide benefits of such integrated approaches include conservation of both terrestrial and marine biodiversity, improved water quality in both freshwater and coastal zones, strengthened natural infrastructure (e.g., mangroves, riverside forests) to buffer climate extremes and more equitable sharing of these benefits resulting from the involvement of Indigenous Peoples and local communities in decision-making, development and implementation (well established) {5.1.3.9, 5.1.3.12, 5.3.3.12}. " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.1.3.9, 5.1.3.12, 5.3.3.12" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C6-SM3> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.2> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "These response options support targets 1 and 12 of the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework. Transboundary water cooperation facilitates sustainable management of resources at the basin scale and better collaboration between sectors and stakeholders (W08) (well established) {5.2.3.8}. " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.2.3.8" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C6-SM4> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.2> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Improving groundwater governance (W09) through cooperation across scales, where appropriate, and support for community water management (W15) increase benefits across the nexus elements, while integrated water infrastructure (W02) and water-sensitive urban infrastructure (W13), which take advantage of natural systems to reduce risks from floods and other hazards, deliver benefits for food production and contribute to climate change mitigation and adaptation (well established) {5.2.3.2, 5.2.3.9, 5.2.3.13, 5.2.3.15}. " ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.2.3.2, 5.2.3.9, 5.2.3.13, 5.2.3.15" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C6-SM5> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.4> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Integrating disease management within landscapes, including inland water bodies, and seascapes can reduce risks of waterborne and other diseases and provide wider benefits from protecting water quality and biodiversity (H12) (well established) {5.4.3.12}. " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.4.3.12" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C6-SM6> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:Illustration <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/NXS24-FigureSPM.9> ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.2>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-Box5.2> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Local solutions often emerge from coordinated networks by drawing on social knowledge and integrating actions across sectors by increasing collaboration among diverse actors (well established) {5.1.3.9, 5.1.3.12, 5.2.3.8, 5.2.3.15, 5.3.1, 5.4.3.12, 5.5.4.1} (Figure SPM.9). For example, in the lower Mekong basin, multisectoral development agreements and investments, alongside integrated governance from the local to the watershed level, were critical for implementing a long-term intergovernmental sustainable river management plan {5.2.3.8, 5.2.4, Box 5.2.10}." ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.1.3.9, 5.1.3.12, 5.2.3.8, 5.2.3.15, 5.3.1, 5.4.3.12, 5.5.4.1",
        "5.2.3.8, 5.2.4, Box 5.2.10" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C7-SM0> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:Illustration <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/NXS24-FigureSPM.8> ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.1>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.4>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.5> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Response options can be direct actions to limit climate and health risks or contribute indirectly to risk reduction and can support targets 8, 11 and 12 of the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework. Direct actions may include urban nature-based solutions and ecosystem-based approaches (B02, H09, C14) that increase urban green and blue space to manage heat island effects, improve water quality and availability and reduce air pollution as well as allergens and zoonotic disease risk (Figure SPM.8) (well established) {5.1.3.2, 5.4.3.9, 5.5.3.14}." ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.1.3.2, 5.4.3.9, 5.5.3.14" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C7-SM1> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.1> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription " In rural landscapes, conserving and restoring natural or semi-natural ecosystems (B04), including coastal vegetation and mangroves, reduces risks from flooding and other climate extremes (well established) {5.1.3.4}. " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.1.3.4" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C7-SM2> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.5> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Indirect approaches include systems for early warning and risk communication (C09), which allow communities to respond quickly to extreme weather events and to make longer-term adaptation decisions, with many successful examples including weather forecasts and index-based crop insurance (well established) {5.5.3.9}. " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.5.3.9" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C7-SM3> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.4> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "There are opportunities for countries with resource-intensive healthcare sectors to reduce negative impacts across the nexus elements by increasing investments in disease prevention and by reducing pollution, waste and greenhouse gas emissions (H03) (well established) {5.4.3.3}. " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.4.3.3" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C7-SM4> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.4> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Increasing investments in risk identification, disease prevention and health promotion, including as a community-led process, provides multiple nexus benefits (H13) (well established) {5.4.3.13}. " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.4.3.13" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C7-SM5> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.4> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "The One Health approach (H14) supports integrating biodiversity and food system management with local health services to reduce risks from zoonotic pathogen emergence and spillover at source (H11), malnutrition and other risks such as those to wildlife health, food production and ecosystems (established but incomplete) {5.4.3.11, 5.4.3.14}. " ;
    ipbes:hasEstablishedIncomplete "Established_but_incomplete" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.4.3.11, 5.4.3.14" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C7-SM6> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.4> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Successful examples, such as the Unified Health System in Brazil, have involved human health professionals, veterinarians and environmental health practitioners working together with farmers and policymakers to jointly design holistic practices aimed at addressing social and environmental determinants of health and contributing to preventing pathogen emergence, thus reducing disease outbreaks for both people and animals (well established) {5.4.3.11}." ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.4.3.11" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C8-SM0> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:Illustration <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/NXS24-FigureSPM.7> ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.1>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.2>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Several response options emphasize rights to water, food, health, land and a clean, healthy and sustainable environment and provide possible ways of recognizing and implementing these human rights for all, including Indigenous Peoples and local communities and women (B10, W01, W06, W07, W14, F14, F15) (Figure SPM.7) (well established) {5.1.3.10, 5.2.3.1, 5.2.3.6, 5.2.3.7, 5.2.3.14, 5.3.3.14, 5.3.3.15}. " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.1.3.10, 5.2.3.1, 5.2.3.6, 5.2.3.7, 5.2.3.14, 5.3.3.14, 5.3.3.15" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C8-SM1> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.2> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "These options can support targets 22 and 23 of the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework. Response options that focus on gender equality, such as addressing the gendered burdens of water collection (W14) and inclusive water management (W06), can improve access to and availability of clean and safe water and improve mental and physical health, leading to enduring and transformative social outcomes (well established) {5.2.3.6, 5.2.3.14}. " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.2.3.6, 5.2.3.14" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C8-SM2> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Socially just and gender-inclusive tenure systems for food are explicitly supported through agroecological practices (F14, F16) (well established) {5.3.1, 5.3.3.14, 5.3.3.16}. " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.3.1, 5.3.3.14, 5.3.3.16" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C8-SM3> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.3>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.5>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-7.1> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Indigenous food systems (F15), grounded in reciprocal world views and values regarding people and nature in balance and the sustainable use of biodiversity, are supplying sustainable and healthy foods from lands, inland waters and oceans while also contributing to biodiversity conservation and climate change mitigation and adaptation (well established) {5.3.3.15, 5.5.4.3, 7.1}. " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.3.3.15, 5.5.4.3, 7.1" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C8-SM4> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-7.1> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "However, further support and recognition would help counter pressures from unsustainable agricultural practices, loss of land and declines in interest in Indigenous foods among young people (established but incomplete) { 7.1}. " ;
    ipbes:hasEstablishedIncomplete "Established_but_incomplete" ;
    dcterms:identifier " 7.1" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C8-SM5> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.4> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Universal health coverage (H01) increases access to primary healthcare services and women’s sexual and reproductive health and rights, while intercultural health services (H02) also uphold the right to health and recognize nature’s contributions to human well-being (well established) {5.4.3.1, 5.4.3.2}. " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.4.3.1, 5.4.3.2" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C8-SM6> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.1> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Rights-based approaches to conservation are grounded in rights related to access to and management of natural resources, including land tenure and resource rights, as well as in the recognition of the rights of nature (B10, W07, F16), in accordance with national legislation and international principles of national sovereignty over natural resources (well established) {Box 4.11, 5.1.3.10, 5.2.3.7, 5.3.3.16}. These approaches show strong effectiveness in improving nexus elements; for example, in Brazil, formalizing and enforcing tenure rights to territories of Indigenous Peoples and local communities resulted in decreases in deforestation and increases in forest restoration (established but incomplete) {5.1.3.10}." ;
    ipbes:hasEstablishedIncomplete "Established_but_incomplete" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.1.3.10",
        "Box 4.11, 5.1.3.10, 5.2.3.7, 5.3.3.16" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C9-SM0> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.6>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-6.2> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Multiple financial response options, instruments and approaches exist which can support targets 15, 18 and 19 of the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework, but these options face implementation, accessibility and scale challenges (well established) {5.6.4, 6.2.4.1}. " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.6.4, 6.2.4.1" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C9-SM1> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.5>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-6.2> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "For example, some instruments (e.g., green and blue bonds) can be used to raise funds to explicitly target multiple nexus benefits, but their implementation is limited and scaling up has been slow (established but incomplete) {6.2.2.1}. Other promising response options aim to increase financing and improve access to it; examples include payments for ecosystem services, which have mobilized up to $42 billion per year from both public and private sources (well established) {5.5.3.12, 6.2.6.2}, a" ;
    ipbes:hasEstablishedIncomplete "Established_but_incomplete" ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.5.3.12, 6.2.6.2",
        "6.2.2.1" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C9-SM2> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "nd microfinance, which is currently at low levels for nexus approaches such as agroecology (established but incomplete) {6.2.6.2, Box 6.12}. Response options can also shift enabling environments, such as internalizing costs of environmental degradation through water pricing (W10) (established but incomplete) {5.3.2.10} " ;
    ipbes:hasEstablishedIncomplete "Established_but_incomplete" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.3.2.10",
        "6.2.6.2, Box 6.12" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C9-SM3> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.1> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "and natural capital accounting (B13), which identifies and values natural assets (well established) {5.1.3.13}. " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.1.3.13" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C9-SM4> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.3>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-6.2>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-6.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Implementation of response options that incentivize trade in sustainable products, in accordance with relevant international obligations, has shown positive impacts on nexus elements (established but incomplete) {5.3.3.1, 6.2.5.1, 6.3.3}. " ;
    ipbes:hasEstablishedIncomplete "Established_but_incomplete" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.3.3.1, 6.2.5.1, 6.3.3" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C9-SM5> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.5>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-6.2> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Global cooperation (C10) on meeting financing needs and access to technological innovations also remain crucial, in particular for supporting developing countries (well established) {5.5.3.10, 6.2.2}. " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.5.3.10, 6.2.2" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C9-SM6> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Eliminating, phasing out or reforming subsidies that damage nexus elements, if implemented in accordance with international obligations, would contribute to shifting business models towards sustainability and recognize the benefits of and reduce pressures on biodiversity, providing benefits for biodiversity and its contribution to nexus elements (well established) {5.3.3.13}, " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.3.3.13" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-C9-SM7> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.1>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.4> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "taking note of the differing needs of developing countries. Some response options, such as natural capital accounting (B13) and integrated watershed-health interventions (H12), can help align the interests of financial and other stakeholders (established but incomplete) {5.1.3.13, 5.4.3.12}." ;
    ipbes:hasEstablishedIncomplete "Established_but_incomplete" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.1.3.13, 5.4.3.12" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-D1-SM0> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-1.1>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-4.2>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-4.5>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-7.1> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Challenges for governing the nexus include navigating socioecological complexity to address fragmented and sectoral decision-making; multiple and diverse values; insufficient, inaccessible and unpredictable finance; and inadequate and inappropriate scaling of actions (established but incomplete) {1.1.2, 4.2, 4.5, 7.1}. " ;
    ipbes:hasEstablishedIncomplete "Established_but_incomplete" ;
    dcterms:identifier "1.1.2, 4.2, 4.5, 7.1" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-D1-SM1> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:Illustration <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/NXS24-FigureSPM.11> ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-4.5> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "These challenges can be addressed through improved and reoriented “nexus governance approaches” (Figure SPM.11), defined as the development and use of coordinating structures and processes that enhance the engagement of multiple actors through horizontal (e.g., across various nexus elements and associated sectors) and vertical (e.g., cross-scale connectivity or multilevel governance) channels to address nexus challenges, identify policy and sociopolitical options and manage their implementation (established but incomplete) {4.5}. " ;
    ipbes:hasEstablishedIncomplete "Established_but_incomplete" ;
    dcterms:identifier "4.5" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-D1-SM2> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:Illustration <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/NXS24-FigureSPM.11> ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-4.5> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Nexus governance approaches, building on and in line with different national and international obligations, can provide an alternative to current siloed approaches and address indirect drivers through: (a) integrative, holistic and transdisciplinary framings of problems and solutions; (b) inclusive approaches that bring about enhanced opportunities for diverse actor engagement; (c) considerations of equity and justice, alongside accountability; (d) enhanced mechanisms and processes for collaboration and coordination across scales and sectors; and (e) adaptive, reflexive and experimental approaches to learn from successes and to scale these solutions (established but incomplete) {4.5.4} (Figure SPM.11). " ;
    ipbes:hasEstablishedIncomplete "Established_but_incomplete" ;
    dcterms:identifier "4.5.4" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-D1-SM3> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-4.4> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Attention to scaling can also assist in accelerating the adoption, implementation and amplification of response options over wider regions and longer time frames. Scaling opportunities include scaling up (institutionalizing at the level of policy, rules and laws), scaling out (applying response options to new places), scaling down (localizing response options that are highly adaptive) and scaling deep (changing relationships, unsustainable and unjust world views, mindsets or beliefs) (well established) {4.4.1}. " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "4.4.1" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-D1-SM4> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-4.4>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-4.5>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-7.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "When combined with capacity-strengthening processes {D5}, scaling can enable transformative change (established but incomplete) {4.4, 4.5.5, 7.3}." ;
    ipbes:hasEstablishedIncomplete "Established_but_incomplete" ;
    dcterms:identifier "4.4, 4.5.5, 7.3",
        "D5" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-D2-SM0> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-4.5>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.6>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-7.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "The intertwined nature of nexus elements means that nearly all actors (local to global, informal and formal, public and private, individual and collective) have a potential role to play. Nexus governance approaches can help align actors across sectors, foster a shared understanding of challenges and opportunities, reduce tensions arising over trade-offs, improve effectiveness, potentially reduce costs of duplication, and support and incentivize collective action and collaboration (established but incomplete) {4.5, 5.6.4, 7.3}. " ;
    ipbes:hasEstablishedIncomplete "Established_but_incomplete" ;
    dcterms:identifier "4.5, 5.6.4, 7.3" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-D2-SM1> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:Illustration <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/NXS24-FigureSPM.11> ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-4.5>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.1>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.6>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-7.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Transdisciplinary engagement of all actors, especially those historically and currently marginalized, in the collaborative design, implementation and monitoring of response options is important. This increases acceptability, transparency and effectiveness, as it incentivizes cooperation and fosters co-learning, especially among diverse actors who may have conflicting values and objectives (Figure SPM.11) (established but incomplete) {4.5, 5.1.5, 5.6.4, 7.3}. " ;
    ipbes:hasEstablishedIncomplete "Established_but_incomplete" ;
    dcterms:identifier "4.5, 5.1.5, 5.6.4, 7.3" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-D2-SM2> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-4.5>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.6> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Ensuring both procedural and distributional equity also improves outcomes: response options assessed to have higher equity impacts also provide greater potential benefits across the nexus elements (established but incomplete) {4.5.3, 5.6.5.2}. " ;
    ipbes:hasEstablishedIncomplete "Established_but_incomplete" ;
    dcterms:identifier "4.5.3, 5.6.5.2" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-D2-SM3> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-4.3>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-4.5> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Response options not co-created with relevant actors can lack credibility and legitimacy, leading to poor performance, low adoption rates, protest, resistance and poor equity outcomes (well established) {4.3.5, 4.5.1.1, 4.5.3}. " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "4.3.5, 4.5.1.1, 4.5.3" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-D2-SM4> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-4.5>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-6.3>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-7.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "However, because not all actors and institutions have power, agency and resources to act, strengthening their capacities and skills and improving enabling conditions, including supportive and inclusive finance and incentive mechanisms and transparent monitoring and accountability, are foundational to changing trajectories (established but incomplete) {4.5, 6.3.3.2, 7.3.7}." ;
    ipbes:hasEstablishedIncomplete "Established_but_incomplete" ;
    dcterms:identifier "4.5, 6.3.3.2, 7.3.7" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-D3-SM0> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-6.1>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-7.2> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Nature-related risks to economic and financial systems, estimated in the trillions of dollars and mutually reinforcing with risks from climate change, are increasing interest in and opportunities for reforming the relationship between the economy and nature (established but incomplete) {6.1.4, 7.2}. " ;
    ipbes:hasEstablishedIncomplete "Established_but_incomplete" ;
    dcterms:identifier "6.1.4, 7.2" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-D3-SM1> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:Illustration <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/NXS24-Figure SPM.12> ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-6.2> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Nevertheless, and despite the fact that financial resources flowing to biodiversity (particularly from public sources) have increased in the past decade, progress has been inadequate, and gaps remain to meet resource needs for biodiversity, which are estimated in the range of $0.3 trillion to $1 trillion per year (Figure SPM.12) (established but incomplete) {6.2.2}. " ;
    ipbes:hasEstablishedIncomplete "Established_but_incomplete" ;
    dcterms:identifier "6.2.2" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-D3-SM2> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-6.2> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Gaps in financing to meet the Sustainable Development Goals related to nexus outcomes beyond biodiversity add at least $4 trillion to these investment needs each year (established but incomplete) {6.2.1}. " ;
    ipbes:hasEstablishedIncomplete "Established_but_incomplete" ;
    dcterms:identifier "6.2.1" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-D3-SM3> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:Illustration <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/NXS24-FigureSPM.12> ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-1.1>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-6.2> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "While some mechanisms and financial flows for biodiversity aim to take advantage of nexus interactions by promoting effectiveness and multiple co-benefits, identifying additional nexus finance options is important to deliver nexus solutions to the degree required for just and sustainable futures (Figure SPM.12) (well established) {1.1.2.5, 6.2.2}. " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "1.1.2.5, 6.2.2" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-D3-SM4> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-6.2> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Three complementary categories of response options were identified which could collectively help align economic and financial systems with biodiversity and/or help direct increased financial resources towards biodiversity and other nexus elements: (a) measures to improve the accessibility, availability and use of information, including information related to the diverse values of nature by economic and financial decision makers. Examples in this category include the adoption and use of metrics beyond gross domestic product, the incorporation of nexus-related information in the appraisal of public spending, and transparency and reporting requirements in the private sector; (b) options aimed at improving access to and the availability of financial resources through the use of financial and economic instruments. Examples in this category include green bonds, microfinance and payments for ecosystem services, as well as tax policies to increase the availability of public funds; and (c) options to reduce negative incentives that drive damage to biodiversity and nexus elements. Examples in this category include improving safeguards and standards for investments and, where contextually appropriate and in accordance with international obligations, tackling negative incentives such as harmful subsidies (well established) {6.2.6}. " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "6.2.6" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-D3-SM5> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-6.2> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Available evidence shows a clear bias in the current distribution of biodiversity finance, with absolute levels of domestic public spending concentrated in countries of North America and Europe and in China. However, only 5 per cent of private financial flows globally for biodiversity are allocated to least developed countries and other low-income countries (well established) {6.2.2.3}. " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "6.2.2.3" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-D3-SM6> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-6.2> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "This highlights the challenges faced by all developing countries, including those already devoting much of their public funding to biodiversity, in mobilizing resources from all sources, recognizes that developing countries may not be able to dedicate sufficient resources to the nexus elements and recognizes the need to strengthen the capacity to implement economic and financial response options. It also highlights the importance of complementary reforms to the economic and financial system, including to tackle existing debt concerns and the cost of finance linked to perceived investment risks, and can help ensure adequate, accessible and affordable finance in developing countries and help finance just and equitable transitions (well established) {6.2.1, 6.2.5.4}. " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "6.2.1, 6.2.5.4" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-D3-SM7> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-6.1>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-6.2> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Indigenous Peoples and local communities face particular challenges in accessing finance and funding across scales, indicating a further area for action (well established) {6.1.3, 6.2.4}. " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "6.1.3, 6.2.4" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-D3-SM8> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-6.1>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-6.2> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Wider transition risks related to the uncertain costs of adapting to changes in operations can be reduced through providing policy certainty and improving business awareness of impacts and dependencies on nature and of the opportunities created by innovative business models that contribute to the conservation and sustainable use of biodiversity, and through working with nature (established but incomplete) {6.1.4, 6.2.6}." ;
    ipbes:hasEstablishedIncomplete "Established_but_incomplete" ;
    dcterms:identifier "6.1.4, 6.2.6" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-D4-SM0> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-7.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Key steps towards improved and holistic decision-making may include: characterizing underlying causes of nexus challenges and impacts of direct and indirect drivers on nexus elements (well established) {7.3.1}; " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "7.3.1" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-D4-SM1> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-7.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "identifying and convening governance actors who may currently not work collaboratively across scales (well established) {7.3.2}; understanding nexus elements and the interactions among them (well established) {7.3.3}; " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "7.3.2",
        "7.3.3" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-D4-SM2> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-4.5>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-7.2>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-7.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "co-creating visions for just and sustainable outcomes of interventions and surfacing and aligning values, which can broaden spaces for dialogue, shift power dynamics, increase inclusion and participation and create greater support and legitimacy for response options (well established) {4.5.1, 4.5.3, 7.2, 7.3.4}; " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "4.5.1, 4.5.3, 7.2, 7.3.4" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-D4-SM3> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-4.2>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-7.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "identifying response options and assessing trade-offs and synergies (well established) {7.3.5}; assessing enabling conditions and overcoming barriers, which can include considerations of policy design and implementation, equity and diversity, institutional capacity, behaviour and lifestyles, technology and material endowments (well established) {4.2.5, 7.3.6}; " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "4.2.5, 7.3.6",
        "7.3.5" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-D4-SM4> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-7.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "negotiating implementation and strengthening transformative outcomes of response options, including through scaling response options and strengthening the capacities of actors (well established) {7.3.6}; " ;
    dcterms:identifier "7.3.6" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-D4-SM5> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-4.2>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-4.5>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-7.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "and embedding experimentation, evaluation, reflection and learning through monitoring to foster adaptive governance (well established) {4.2.2, 4.5.4, 7.3.8}. " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "4.2.2, 4.5.4, 7.3.8" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-D4-SM6> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-4.6> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Decision-support tools can be particularly useful at each step of the road map, with more than 200 tools available for supporting nexus approaches related to biodiversity, water, food and climate change (although health is commonly not included in these tools) (well established) {4.6.1}. " ;
    dcterms:identifier "4.6.1" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-D4-SM7> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-4.5>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-Box4.11> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Measures along the road map that can also assist with “course correction” are needed to enhance social and environmental outcomes and equity; examples include the use of free, prior and informed consent procedures and other human rights-based approaches (well established) {4.5.3.1, Box 4.11}. " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "4.5.3.1, Box 4.11" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-D4-SM8> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:Illustration <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/NXS24-FigureSPM.13> ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-7.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "These steps towards improved decision-making and governance can be incremental or more transformative, depending on how they are implemented (established but incomplete) {7.3} and could be used to inform decision-making across scales. For example, actors involved in subnational, national, regional or transboundary development planning processes could use steps of the road map, where relevant, when co-developing national biodiversity strategies and action plans, nationally determined contributions and national adaptation plans (Figure SPM.13)." ;
    ipbes:hasEstablishedIncomplete "Established_but_incomplete" ;
    dcterms:identifier "7.3" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-D5-SM0> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-4.5>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-Table4.11> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Capacity-strengthening is an important enabling condition recognized in many global conventions and initiatives (well established) {4.5.5, Table 4.11}. " ;
    dcterms:identifier "4.5.5, Table 4.11" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-D5-SM1> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-4.5>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-6.2> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Specific capacities to move towards improved nexus governance approaches include those which facilitate response option implementation; capacities to understand, leverage and mobilize equitable financial flows to support multiple co-benefits among nexus elements (well established) {4.5.4, 4.5.5, 6.2}; " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "4.5.4, 4.5.5, 6.2" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-D5-SM10> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.1>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.2> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "While many capacity gaps still remain  (established but incomplete) {Appendix 3, 7.4.1}, there are available response options, such as reconnecting people with nature (B14), community water management (W15) and those response options that strengthen the capacities of women (W06), that both rely on and can build up actors’ and institutions’ capacities to help govern and manage across the nexus (well established) {5.1.3.14, 5.2.3.6, 5.2.3.15}." ;
    ipbes:hasEstablishedIncomplete "Established_but_incomplete" ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.1.3.14, 5.2.3.6, 5.2.3.15",
        "Appendix 3, 7.4.1" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-D5-SM2> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-4.4>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-4.5>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-Table4.12> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "and capacities to scale and amplify options that show transformative potential (well established) {4.4, 4.5.4, 4.5.5, Table 4.12}. " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "4.4, 4.5.4, 4.5.5, Table 4.12" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-D5-SM3> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.6> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "For example, response options that generate new practices and innovations, shift values and views, or change structures and institutions show the most transformative potential (established but incomplete) {5.6.6}. " ;
    ipbes:hasEstablishedIncomplete "Established_but_incomplete" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.6.6" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-D5-SM4> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-4.5> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Important identified capacities to help actors achieve these aims include motivational capacities, which build awareness and desire for change (well established) {4.5.5}; " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "4.5.5" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-D5-SM5> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-4.5>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-4.6>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-7.3>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-Table4.13> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "analytical capacities, which enable actors to select, understand and use suitable decision-support tools and strengthen institutional and research capacities which can help address inequalities among countries (well established) {4.5.5, 4.6.1, Table 4.13, 7.3.3}; " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "4.5.5, 4.6.1, Table 4.13, 7.3.3" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-D5-SM6> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-4.5>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-4.6> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "bridging capacities and promoting transdisciplinary research, including the ability to bring together different ways of knowing and doing through knowledge co-production processes (well established) {4.5.5, 4.6.2}; " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "4.5.5, 4.6.2" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-D5-SM7> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-3.1>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-4.5>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-7.3> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "negotiation capacities to surface and navigate inevitable trade-offs among the values and interests of different actors and institutions, including those of Indigenous Peoples and local communities (well established) {3.1.2, 4.5.5, 7.3.7}, " ;
    ipbes:hasWellestablished "Well-established" ;
    dcterms:identifier "3.1.2, 4.5.5, 7.3.7" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/subm/NXS24-D5-SM8> a ipbes:SubMessage ;
    ipbes:SubChapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-4.5>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-6.3>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-Box4.12>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-Table4.8> ;
