<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.3.5> a ipbes:SubChapter ;
    ipbes:Chapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ch/NXS24ch5.3> ;
    ipbes:Reference <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/863R5XCY>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/ELVVW6AS>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/N5W54F83>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/SSJBGQ3B>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/XRHC7PET> ;
    ipbes:Report <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/NXS24> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Several knowledge gaps were found in the course of assessing the food-related response options. Many of the key knowledge gaps for food systems are closely linked: 1. Synergies and trade-offs across both response options and nexus elements. Food system actors rarely adopt single response options but apply and adapt multiple response options while seeking impact across nexus elements. This contrasts with most scientific evidence, which assesses single response options and one or few nexus elements. More generally, there is a knowledge gap around how to promote the alignment of different interest groups, industries and policymaking bodies to minimise trade-offs and maximise synergies. 2. Engagement, implementation and behavioural change. There is a lack of sufficient knowledge on how to engage all relevant actors, integrate action across actors and sectors, implement response options and translate knowledge into useful forms. Food system response options are contingent on behavioural change but major gaps remain in understanding effective behavioural change mechanisms to move production, consumption and purchasing decisions toward options that have more nexus-wide benefits. 3. Cross-scale issues and international trade. Identifying and managing trade-offs while leveraging synergies is relatively well documented at landscapes scales, but not at national and international scales. How international trade affects the magnitude and distribution of the costs and benefits of food production, and the efficacy of response options remains a critical gap, particularly with the potential of leakage and the displacement of negative nexus impacts to other regions. 4. Justice implications of response options are largely missing from the literature, including impacts of change on communities, notably food producing communities, but also the justice dimensions of food over-consumption and displaced environmental impacts on populations least responsible for that impact. There is also a lack of knowledge on gender input to climate change mitigation and adaptation policies (Section 5.3.3.14). Issues of equity beyond gender and youth are not systematically incorporated into research and innovation, and <10 per cent of 1.3 million agri-food research publications reported any social inclusion or human development outcomes . There is a need to address specific questions around how to ensure the benefits and costs of yield-increasing (Sections 5.3.3.3, 5.3.3.4, 5.3.3.5 and 5.3.3.6) or nature-restoring response options (Sections 5.3.3.1 and 5.3.3.2) are equitably distributed, notably across countries and in terms of economics, gender and IPLC. 5. Unbiased knowledge. There are geographic and sociocultural biases in the availability of evidence, with most evidence from high-income countries. Literature that focuses on smallholder farming systems is more representative of the Global South and has high relevance. While known to be important, diverse and adapted to local environments, ILK food systems are poorly documented. There are also biases in which world view and knowledge system is acknowledged as valid evidence, with ILK systems frequently neglected or marginalized. 6. Clear terminology. Terms such as agroecology, diversification, regenerative, conservation tillage, ecological or sustainable intensification, and organic are used varyingly as knowledge systems, sets of practices, specific practices and outcome measures. This limits the effectiveness of evidence synthesis and can have unintended sociopolitical implications such as the promotion of a biased or suboptimal suite of interventions. 7. Microbiomes as drivers of human and soil metabolism. While much focus has been given to macro-biodiversity there is growing recognition that microbiomes play key roles in metabolic processes, notably in soils and in and on humans. Soil and gut microbiomes are gaining increasing attention thanks to the emergence of increasingly accurate microbial tracing technologies. While soil and human microbiomes share some overlap if individuals are exposed to soils at an early age, the microbiomes remain quite distinct. However, there is growing evidence that modern practices, both in food production and food consumption have similar effects in altering the diversity, composition and function of soil and gut microbiomes with effects on both soil and human health. Low input diversity (crop diversity/food diversity); regular exposure to biocides (pesticides, herbicides, antibiotics); and high turnover inputs (nitrogen fertilizer/sugar consumption) have all been correlated with important changes leading to lower microbiome diversity in soils and human guts. To what degree this loss impacts important soil and human health functions, including possible soil/human gut microbiome interrelations, remains poorly understood (see also Chapter 5.4, Box 5.4.6). To overcome these knowledge gaps, food systems research would benefit from the co-development of research and implementation plans with a range of food system actors. In addition, it should focus on achieving change, rather than just documenting unsatisfactory conditions . Broadening evidence reviews and recognizing diverse knowledge systems (particularly place-based ILK systems), clearly identifying responsibilities, and fostering collaboration and open participation will all be needed to increase fairness, justice and impact in food system change ." ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.3.5" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Knowledge gaps" .

