<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.2.5> a ipbes:SubChapter ;
    ipbes:Chapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ch/NXS24ch5.2> ;
    ipbes:Reference <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/D5ETWHGQ>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/F88V62D2>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/HCSPD4KL>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/PPCVRYKV>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/UXH92RPZ>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/VLRRA4I3>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/YMIWNACU> ;
    ipbes:Report <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/NXS24> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Several knowledge gaps were found when reviewing primary policy documents to identify a first set of 110 response options that were then clustered into the 15 response options described in this chapter . The list of 110 response options was inspired by existing policy documents, so ‘novel’ response options and the methods and tools to implement these options may not be included. However, the 15 response option clusters captured numerous specific actions at multiple temporal and spatial scales. Nevertheless, there are limitations due to lack of literature on the extent to which these are generalizable to many geographies, as well as their relation to different cosmovisions of diverse communities. For many response options, there was limited information from which to derive quantitative scores for the different criteria used to evaluate the options. As response options were chosen that covered several nexus elements, the majority of scores across response options were positive. However, these positive scores could, for example, indicate only potential synergies as robust data on the applicability of the response option was lacking. In particular, empirical data on the implementation of response options are limited to a few examples that are difficult to generalize from, given the context dependency and scale of many of the options. Furthermore, publication biases against publishing negative results can play a role in the findings and conclusions drawn. In addition, there are regional biases in evidence, as for example the literature on integrated water infrastructure and dam operations is mostly from North America, Oceania and South Africa, while there are limited insights on the application of most response options in island countries. Most literature is also in the English language. This effects access to evidence in different languages, including those used by IPLC, and to key grey literature which may not be in the written form but shared orally or in other ways. To address this gap, scenarios that better account for the visions embedded in ILK and include the participation of IPLC would improve understanding of rights and values in determining choice of response options. Response options may be mentioned implicitly in the literature rather than directly using the terminology “response option”. For example, nature-based solutions were not included as a response option group as they were embedded within several of the 15 but often described using a variety of different terms synonymous with nature-based solutions. As such there is a general lack of studies synthesizing the social, economic and environmental goals of specific types of nature-based solutions and reporting both their positive and negative interactions (i.e., synergies and trade-offs) with the nexus elements . There is also a knowledge gap on cases of imperfect implementation or failures in planning and implementation, and comparison to other non-nature-based alternatives. Some knowledge gaps can appear where there is limited access to public information about water management, regulations and policies. In addition, there is limited information on the rights of nature approach in public policies and regulations on water quality and quantity, as it is a new paradigm that has been criticized by some within legal doctrines. Four other key general knowledge gaps have been identified through the process of conducting this assessment. First, data and indicators on the status of, and trends in, the nexus elements (biodiversity, water, food, health and climate change) and, in particular, their interactions spanning space and time scales is largely missing. The nexus element of water has traditionally received attention in nexus approaches, however, studies tend to be restricted to local case studies and available data is yet to be collected following consistent protocols and with the ability to be scaled up, although some exemplary initiatives are emerging (see . Data covering not only indicators of water quantity or quality are necessary, but also how water relates to the other nexus elements, such as biodiversity, food and health. Some initiatives for virtual water calculations are emerging that reflect the water used for food systems and for ecosystem flows, but much greater effort is needed especially in the most data poor and more water scarce regions of the world. Second, additional knowledge is needed on the impact of the response options on the nexus elements, with particular emphasis on quantifying trade-offs and synergies. Few studies exist on higher-order nexus interactions involving three or more nexus elements, particularly studies involving health. Studies that link water with multiple nexus elements such as health (access to quality healthcare), food (environmental performance of food), and climate change (mitigation impact) are lacking. Between 4 to 5 of the 15 response options had inconclusive evidence for the factors above, whereas 2 of the 15 had no evidence. In addition, evidence on how the rights of nature response option (W07) is linked to the nexus elements is primarily inconclusive and in cases an extrapolation based on expert opinion. Third, studies on nexus interactions spanning across the terrestrial, freshwater and marine realms were also limited. Although this chapter focuses on the freshwater domain, there are clear dependencies across these domains. The widely diverse water surfaces enable a range of (mostly) freshwater limnological systems such as wetlands, peatlands, lakes, reservoirs, ponds, rivers, springs, streams and groundwater, which in turn feed into coastal and marine environments through flows of water, sediments, nutrients as well as pollution and other materials, but also through effects such as saline intrusion and interventions such as desalination to provide water security. Thus, the interconnected nature of water systems requires further studies that establish the flows, telecoupling of processes and impacts. Moreover, additional studies that not only focus on flows of water on the Earth surface but also in the atmosphere are missing or few in number, but fundamental for shifting perspectives towards precipitation-based water resources, (e.g., through moisture recycling or precipitation management under climate change, but also to elucidate land-atmosphere feedbacks). Fourth, many of our response options could be implemented across temporal and spatial scales, including freshwater and marine environments. However, evaluation methods and tools supporting implementation are limited, which may lead to missed nexus opportunities. Many response options had evidence from site-specific implementation, and the ability of that response option to affect other nexus elements and broader scales was difficult to quantify. There is a need for novel methods, models and decision support tools for assessing interactions among three or more nexus elements and actors in the implementation of response options, including methods focused on spatial/temporal dynamics and scaling up, out, down and deep of response options and their long-term outcomes for the nexus. Studies that evaluate the impacts of response options on multiple nexus elements before and after implementation to understand synergies and trade-offs and how these are influenced by the implementation process and good governance, including across multiple scales and contexts would also be of value to stakeholders. Finally, studies to improve understanding of IPLC managed systems, their importance, monetary and non-monetary value, and potential to upscale, including consideration of contested property rights and traditional rights are largely missing, but already some promising examples exist (see boxes in the previous sections). Examples of such studies may include, but not be restricted to, scenarios that better account for the visions embedded in ILK and include the participation of IPLC. In addition, cases studies in the Global South are a particular knowledge gap. There is a general gap in understanding of alternative and innovative approaches to nexus governance, which could improve the performance of the response options." ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.2.5" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Knowledge gaps?" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.4.2> a ipbes:SubChapter ;
    ipbes:Chapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ch/NXS24ch5.4> ;
    ipbes:Reference <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/2QADVN5B>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/83LTYJQM>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/ED4JVTS9>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/I9XVIKUV>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/IJNUXL33>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/L78FYVHL>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/L7WPFQPV> ;
    ipbes:Report <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/NXS24> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Moving from a 20th century Western and biomedical healthcare perspective to a more integrative one requires bringing together multiple actors and institutions. Discussions on the role of diverse actors with direct implications for healthcare provisioning are increasingly seen at various platforms, especially global ones. Examples include WHO initiatives which focus on the role of Indigenous Peoples and Indigenous knowledge in healthcare , such as the co-creation of a Global Action Plan and the Strategy and Action Plan on Ethnicity and Health for the Americas . The success of an integrative approach towards health requires concerted action from actors conventionally placed within the human health sector (patients, doctors, nurses, allied health practitioners, paramedical staff, pharmacists, managers, health education institutions, regulatory agencies) as well as outside actors who engage with the health sector in various capacities (including international health agencies, public and private sector researchers, biologists, veterinarians, biochemists, urban planners, environmental managers, environmental and (bio)hazard engineers, energy and infrastructure providers, networks and associations - community and civil society organizations and funders). WHO for example, places importance on the need for actors to be trained and capacity strengthened at the institutional level so that all actors and institutions are able to play their roles in delivering on One Health (see also Section 5.4.4). Interactions among constellations of actors across different categories within the health sector (Table 5.4.1) has significantly increased, especially in low- or middle-income country contexts. The WHO, for example, collaborates with national governments, who may in turn hire (some of the) human resources from a third party (as in national polio surveillance projects). Several health insurance schemes aimed at achieving Universal Health Coverage in low- or middle-income countries actively engage public and private sector health actors, encouraging shared goals and approaches (e.g., India’s Pradhan Mantri Jan Arogya Yojana, National Health Authority , Kenya’s National Hospital Insurance Fund ). Actors that can promote an integrative approach, operate at multiple scales and provide an interdisciplinary perspective towards operationalization of response options across the nexus while advancing health are listed in Table 5.4.1, categorized broadly into government, private, civil society and IPLC actors. The potential and urgency for collaborations with actors from other nexus elements is increasingly recognized, especially post COVID-19, across the globe. The scope and operationalization of the potential for these collaborations varies across actor categories, is still evolving in many instances and, yet, as almost all the response options demonstrate (see Section 5.4.3), there are nexus actions which can be upscaled and replicated in far greater measure than currently practiced. Although the success varies by country, evidence indicates that multi-sector collaboration does improve health sector coordination and health outcomes (e.g., for universal health coverage in Sub-Saharan Africa ). Table 5.4.1. Examples of health sector actors involved in the implementation of health response options. See Section 1.3.3 and Table 1.1 for actor category descriptions. Actor category Actors Example actors Global and regional institutions Multilateral agencies and intergovernmental organizations World Health Organization (WHO), United Nations Children’s Fund (UNICEF), United Nations Development Programme (UNDP), United Nations Environment Programme (UNEP), UN Women, United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change (UNFCCC), World Organization for Animal Health (WOAH), the Quadripartite Alliance for OH (WOAH-FAO-WHO-UNEP), Pan American Health Organisation (PAHO), Global Centre for Traditional Medicine Bilateral agencies/ organizations (including donors/ official development and co-operation agencies with bilateral arrangements) Country-based organizations for international cooperation and development such as: the Swedish International Development Cooperation Agency (SIDA), German Agency for International Cooperation (GIZ), United States Agency for International Development (USAID), Australian Agency for International Development (AusAID), Norwegian Agency for Development Cooperation (NORAD), UK Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office (FCDO). Regional agencies and organizations Asia Pacific Health Observatory, Association of Southeast Asian Nations (ASEAN), African Centre for Disease Control and prevention (CDC), European Centre for Disease Prevention and Control, US Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) National governments, regional and local governments National, sub-national and local government bodies Ministries, departments, and line agencies for human health, animal health and environment, Agencies/bodies for Indigenous health and wildlife conservation Knowledge and educational communities Knowledge generation actors (international) Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change (IPCC), IPBES, consortia and networks such as One Health High-level Expert Panel (OHHLEP), Future Earth-Health Knowledge Action Network) Knowledge generation actors (national) Health researchers (laboratories, research institutions), funding agencies (e.g., New Frontiers Research Fund Canada) Educational institutions (public) Higher education sector (medicine, nursing, pharmacy, dentistry, veterinary), schools (integrative approach in health literacy); awareness creation programs Educational institutions (private) As above Health professional and regulatory associations (public and private) World Medical Association (WMA), National Medical Associations, Nursing and Allied Health Associations, World Organization of National Colleagues, Academies and Academic Associations of General Practitioners and Family Physicians (WONCA), Occupational Health, Disability, Health-related Rehabilitation and Resettlement agencies (e.g., for Indigenous communities, displaced persons, migrants), World and National Veterinary Associations and other animal health NGOs and associations Civil society Community and consumer-based individuals / organizations Civil society-based organizations, such as Rotary International (country chapters), patient organizations, individuals (e.g., village leaders, champions, health volunteers, village/ community health workers, livestock owners and volunteers, village liaisons, champions of traditional medicine and practices, health activists) Community based organizations Allied social organizations Sociocultural organizations, vulnerable groups organizations, youth organizations, sports organizations Indigenous Peoples and local communities (IPLC) Institutions / systems Practitioners of Ayurveda, Unani, African herbalism, Sowa-Rigpa, Siddha, Callahuayas, naturopathy Practitioners Traditional healers, shamans, traditional medicine practitioners, midwives Community support Elders, women’s organizations, community health workers Private sector and business organizations Not-for-profit: International non-governmental organizations (NGOs) NGOs in synergy with national and international agreed priorities on health (e.g., OXFAM, Médecins Sans Frontières, Comité International de la Croix Rouge, Healthcare Without Harm National/ sub-national NGOs Playing a critical role especially in low- and middle-income countries Co-operative/for profit institutions: Individual health professionals Broadly defined, includes professionals involved in health promotion, prevention, diagnosis and treatment of disease, rehabilitation for chronic conditions, including: * Diversity of practitioners and facilitators for healthcare across countries, differentiated by urban/rural, gender, socioeconomic status, indigeneity, formal/informal, geography. * Supply and value-addition actors: medicinal plant gatherers and traders of medicinal (wild) and farmed animals, local medicine makers (traditional, informal sector) Private commercial / businesses related to human health, animal health, traditional and Indigenous practices Food production and retail sector, pharmacists and pharmaceutical companies, national/sub-national companies (e.g., healthcare, insurance), livestock breeders, farmers Science-policy interfaces Multilateral and bilateral agencies, intergovernmental organizations, national and sub-national government and regulatory bodies Agencies and organizations (see examples cited above) that support the translation of science into policy and assist in implementation: can engage public sector actors across nexus elements, or a mix of public and private actors, including local communities Knowledge generation actors (international, national) Actors spanning both private and public sector (see examples cited above) that serve to bridge the science-policy gap through their actions Financing institutions Financial and insurance institutions (global/ regional/ national) Health-related insurance companies, development banks (e.g., World Bank, Asian Development Bank, African Development Bank Group) Program funding agencies (private sector, not-for-profit) Wellcome Trust, Bill and Melinda Gates Foundation, other health philanthropies Program funding institutions (international / national) National: US National Institute of Health, UK Medical Research Council Media and the arts Multilateral and bilateral agencies, intergovernmental organizations, national and sub-national government Actors engaging with health promotional campaigns and advocacy (e.g., UNICEF). Government health ministries, departments working independnelty and in collaboration with private companies for mass media campaigns and outreach Practitioners, allied social organizations (IPLC, CBOs, CSOs) Actors that engage with communicators, awareness creation and dissemination activities in multiple ways including visual and performing arts, health promotion campaigns, cultural instruction and storytelling for advancing integrative approaches and recognition of the nexus. Individuals, communities and formal organizations are involved. Educational institutions (public, private) Actors that advance integrative approaches through curricula, health promotion, health literacy and other awareness creation programs through schools and higher education sectors" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.4.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Key actors and institutions" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.6.5> a ipbes:SubChapter ;
    ipbes:Chapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ch/NXS24ch5.6> ;
    ipbes:Reference <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/4462ER4R>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/7MG5FEEL>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/BFY39PRS>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/CLXM3W9N>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/PULFZYFJ>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/QJJSZY6D>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/XWWZGFAT> ;
    ipbes:Report <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/NXS24> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Key question: What attributes or enabling conditions are associated with response options that have nexus-wide benefits and potential for transformative change? Response options are not pursued in a vacuum. Broader enabling conditions (e.g., relevant systems of policies and rules, local context and norms) can influence the likelihood of nexus-wide benefits. Attributes of the response option itself (e.g., equity in participation, economic costs) can also determine the likelihood of its success and impact across nexus elements. Enabling conditions and attributes often interact to determine success, and as noted in section 5.6.4 some response options may be enablers for others. 5.6.5.1 Broader enabling conditions Enabling conditions identified in this chapter correspond to those first identified and highlighted in Chapter 4: policy design and implementation, equity and diversity, institutional capacity, finance and economics, behaviour and lifestyle, technology, and material endowments. As determined by subchapter teams (see Figures 5.1.11, 5.2.8, 5.3.19, 5.4.14, 5.5.15), policy design together with institutional capacity for implementation is the most frequent enabling condition across response options, both overall (Figure 5.6.6) and for each individual nexus element. This repeated pattern emphasizes that the right policy framings are a necessary condition for implementing response options. Behaviour and lifestyle and equity and diversity also appeared frequently and are essential to successful policy design and implementation, particularly for response options that can be enabled by changes in individual choices and values (Figure 5.6.6). In contrast, finance and economics and material endowments accounted for the most frequent barriers (Figure 5.6.6), indicating that financial and other resources are a common impediment to response option success and impact. While technology can sometimes be a barrier for a few response options (e.g., expanded use of renewables like wind and solar), it was only noted by the climate change subchapter, indicating that other sectors are not prevented from moving forward on many response options due to a lack of technology. Encouragingly, subchapter teams identified many more potential enablers than outright barriers for the response options assessed. Figure 5.6.6. Enablers and barriers affecting implementation of the response options assessed in Chapter 5. For seven types of enablers/barriers, dots indicate the number of response options for which successful implementation is enabled (enabler) or hindered (barrier) or for which the role of the enabler/barrier is unclear or where the role cannot be determined. A given enabler/barrier can be an enabler for some response options, a barrier for others and both an enabler and barrier for the same response option. This figure presents pooled information across all elements; for analogous figures for each nexus element, see the online Annex 5.6.1, Figure A.5.6.1. See Chapter 4, Table 4.6 for descriptions of the enablers and barriers.5 Importantly, enabling conditions vary widely with local context and conditions. For example, water-efficient agriculture (W06) is more likely to have impact in regions with high water demands, water scarcity and regional water management institutions. Sustainable healthy diets (F11 and C15) are more likely to succeed in cultures with stronger educational and health care institutions. In general, the success, impact and equity of any response option depend as much on local context as on the response option itself, as the same response option may have large and equitable impacts in one setting and not in another. Response options in all nexus elements share a need for broader nexus governance (e.g., support for integrative, inclusive, equitable, coordinated and adaptive approaches) to facilitate their design and implementation (Section 1.3; Section 4.2). For example, W15, Community water management (Section 5.2.3.15) and other community-based response options are more likely to succeed when implemented in governance environments in which diverse knowledge sources are already valued and democratic decision-making is strong. Response options vary in terms of how flexibly they can draw on multiple knowledge systems, including Indigenous and local knowledge (ILK). Some response options are fundamentally rooted in ILK systems, including W07, Rights of nature (Section 5.2.3.7), F15, Indigenous food systems (Section 5.3.3.15) and H02, Intercultural health services (Section 5.4.3.2). Other options derive from Western knowledge systems or legal frameworks (e.g., B10, Rights-based approaches (Section 5.1.3.10)) but can be mobilized for strong support for ILK in the process of implementation. Some response options themselves are enablers of others; for example, H01, Universal health coverage (Section 5.4.3.1) can enable other response options such as H05, Nature on prescription (Section 5.4.3.5) and H13, Health impact assessments (Section 5.4.3.13) by providing means of funding and institutional support. The assessment makes clear that collaborations among actors are crucial in understanding and solving the nexus challenges laid out in Chapter 1, Section 1.1.2. Based on actor tables within the Chapter 5 subchapters, nearly all response options require actors from multiple categories as key implementors. For example, in the health sector, preventing disease requires multidisciplinary approaches that involve community organizations, the private sector and government in complex partnerships (Section 5.4.4). Many response options also integrate actors from multiple nexus elements. For example, maximizing the benefits of B02/C14, Urban nature-based solutions (Section 5.1.3.2; Section 5.5.3.14) integrates biodiversity experts, water managers, public health professionals, and others. In fact, in 7 of the 15 response options in the health subchapter, actors from all five nexus elements are required. On the other hand, power dynamics among actors, both within and among nexus elements, can also create barriers as more powerful actors block nexus response options that reduce their advantage. For example, a key barrier for transitioning towards healthier diets is the consolidated power of large agribusinesses that control food manufacturing, distribution and product placement in the retail sector. These companies provide consumers with cheap, often highly processed food of low nutritional value and continue to resist efforts to transition toward sustainable healthy diets. Collaborations among actors from different categories and nexus elements require improved governance mechanisms (see Chapters 4 and 7). Multi-actor and multi-stakeholder platforms and organizations, alongside more democratic approaches, can create conditions under which people can work together to address nexus challenges and resolve issues involving one or more nexus elements. Although there may be high transaction costs for initial set up, these facilitating mechanisms contribute to more sustained impacts over time (see Sections 5.X.4 in each Chapter 5 subchapter). 5.6.5.2 Attributes of response options In addition to enabling conditions, the likelihood of a response option’s success in generating positive nexus impacts also depends on attributes of the response option itself. These intrinsic attributes include social acceptability, economic costs and benefits, and distributional equity of benefits among social groups. Figure 5.6.7 summarizes these and other key attributes for the 24 focal response options assessed in detail in this chapter. Below, three attributes are highlighted and discussed in more detail. Figure 5.6.7. Key attributes of focal response options assessed in Chapter 5. This figure presents six main criteria relevant to key attributes, out of the 10 total criteria used by subchapters to assess response options. Each main criterion has two to three indicators (columns). As in the original subchapter tables from which this table is assembled, blue shades indicate positive impacts and red shades indicate negative impacts. Intensity of colour represents the magnitude of impact (light = small; medium = medium impact; dark = large;). Grey indicates a score of 0. White indicates either inconclusive evidence (INC) or no evidence (NE). Where impacts differ depending on context, impact ranges are shown by multiple colours within a cell. Note the United Nations Food Systems Pathways were only assessed by the subchapter focused on the nexus element of food (Section 5.3). Equity Three dimensions of equity were assessed for each response option. Distributional equity ensures fair sharing of costs and benefits, recognitional equity recognizes the need for representation of multiple identities and world views, and procedural equity promotes processes that are inclusive and participatory. All three dimensions of equity are essential for ensuring just and sustainable outcomes. For example, when prescribing time spent in nature (H05), inequalities in access to these prescription programs can exclude those who would benefit the most (e.g., low-income urban dwellers who live further from green space already). While many such programs often target people from disadvantaged neighbourhoods, they themselves may not be empowered to change the quantity of greenspace in their locality (Section 5.4.3). Equity can be both an enabling condition and an attribute of response options. Broader enabling conditions include governance systems that recognize and empower diverse stakeholders and values, especially those from vulnerable populations and IPLC (Chapter 4). For example, decentralized governance approaches have supported effective local area-based conservation (Section 5.1.3.1) with positive benefits for both people and nature. Impact on equity is also an intrinsic attribute of response options that varies among them, especially for distributional equity (Figure 5.6.7). Many response options focus directly on increasing equity and inclusion. For example, B10, Rights-based approaches (Section 5.1.3.10), W14, Addressing gendered burdens of water collection (Section 5.2.3.14), F15, Indigenous food systems (Section 5.3.3.15) and H01, Universal health coverage (Section 5.4.3.1) are all responses that specifically target equity issues and thus can contribute to more effective nexus governance. These response options are likely to result in more just outcomes, as well as providing enabling conditions for the success and equity of others. Tools to promote equity (e.g., participatory processes, recognition of rights, knowledge co-production) have the potential to challenge and overcome actor power imbalances, contributing to transformative change (Section 4.2.5.2). Case studies included in each subchapter (Figure 5.6.2) suggest that successful response options often incorporate rights-based approaches, gender aspects, awareness and education and the participation of IPLC in their plans and thus help to advance different components of equity. The connection between equity and nexus-wide impacts is evident in the scores assigned to response options by subchapter teams (Figure 5.6.8). Overall and for most elements, response options with higher distributional equity scores tend to have higher potential benefits across the nexus. For example, B10, Rights-based approaches (Section 5.1.3.10) and B03/C11, Agroecology (Section 5.1.3.3; Section 5.5.3.11) both have relatively high positive scores for both nexus impacts and distributional equity, while W03, Dam operation (Section 5.2.3.3) and F06, Ecological intensification - aquatic foods (Section 5.3.3.6) both have relatively low scores for both. While overall scores are helpful in assessing the general potential for response options to advance equity, actual equity impacts depend strongly on how response options are implemented. The context-specificity is evident in the large number of split cells in Figure 5.6.7, showing a wide range of expected equity impacts depending on context and implementation. For example, payment schemes for ecosystem services (e.g., Reducing emissions from Deforestation and Forest Degradation in Developing Countries and the conservation of and sustainable management of forests and enhancement of forest carbon stocks (REDD+)) have been widely criticized for inequitable implementation if payment sizes and types are not equitable or if some potential actors (such as IPLC) are excluded from decision-making (Section 5.1.4). The generally high equity scores assigned may thus mask implementation issues that depend on local conditions, enablers and interactions among actors. Figure 5.6.8. Relationships between nexus-wide impacts and three intrinsic attributes of response options. Panels depict nexus impact score versus A) distributional equity, B) technical feasibility and C) gross economic costs. In each panel, points represent each of the response options assessed in this chapter (Table 5.6.1). Colours denote the nexus element of origin. All scores are based on assessed criteria in subchapters for each nexus element (Chapter 5.0.3, Table 5.0.1).6 Criteria scored as minimum/maximum ranges due to uncertainty are represented here as the average of the minimum and maximum scores. Labelled points indicate response options noted in the text as examples. Technical feasibility Technical feasibility influences how quickly and broadly a response option can be implemented as well as its likelihood of success. It is important to note that some response options are more feasible when implemented over small scales and with narrow sectoral goals. Furthermore, there is a logical concern that nexus responses options, with impacts across multiple nexus elements, will tend to be complex, expensive and less feasible than siloed approaches. This assessment suggests otherwise. Based on evidence-based scoring from subchapters, there is no general relationship between potential nexus impacts and technical feasibility (Figure 5.6.8). Most response options assessed in this chapter appear to be relatively feasible, and they represent a range from low to high potential nexus impacts. For example, C05, Offshore wind power (Section 5.5.3.5) is highly feasible and already being implemented, but its nexus-wide impacts beyond renewable energy production are slightly negative (Figure 5.6.3). In contrast, H08, Mangrove conservation and restoration for health (Section 5.4.3.8) is also highly feasible and scores highly on nexus impacts through its benefits for species habitats (biodiversity), water quality (water) and carbon storage (climate change). Economic benefits and costs Response options assessed in this report aim to advance pathways to long-term sustainability rather than short-term economic considerations. However, the costs and benefits of a response option, especially in the context of its potential benefits, can affect the degree of political support and public resources it receives. While economic benefits are often included in global assessments such as those produced by IPBES, costs are more rarely considered but are equally important to feasibility and impact. The assessment of response options and resulting scores reveal no overall relationship between nexus impacts and economic costs (Figure 5.6.8). In other words, it is not the case in general that more widely impactful response options are those that are more costly to implement. There are response options with nexus-wide benefits available to actors at a range of costs. While costs of some response options can be substantial, several policy actions could free up funding immediately for use in enabling other response options. For example, cost-savings from F13, Reform public spending (Section 5.3.3.13) could support more investments in a costlier option with stronger nexus-wide benefits such as F01, Halting conversion of ecosystems of high ecological integrity (Section 5.3.3.1), F02, Restore soil health (Section 5.3.3.2) or C01, Increase soil organic carbon content (Section 5.5.3.2) that require widespread monitoring. Other response options pay for themselves in savings across nexus elements. For example, cost-savings from the health benefits of H06, Reduce meat overconsumption (Section 5.4.3.6) and F11/C15, Sustainable healthy diets (Section 5.3.3.11; Section 5.5.3.15) are enormous, as current dietary patterns carry hidden costs of roughly $9 trillion (in 2020 purchasing power parities dollars) due to obesity and disease ." ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.6.5" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Enabling conditions and attributes for nexus-wide impacts" .

