<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.5.6> a ipbes:SubChapter ;
    ipbes:Chapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ch/NXS24ch5.5> ;
    ipbes:Reference <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/2JBGM8SC>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/9KLYKL34>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/B998KG5F>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/BB9TQNAY>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/C66Y55Y5>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/CGUVC8PV>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/LTW6X2EU>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/LV9ERJ25>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/NDYV49ZY>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/PFZRXW3Z>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/PKAM3M4J>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/Q7Q7SL9T>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/TIQUPJSU>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/TJMFJYMV>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/XV8XQ8GX> ;
    ipbes:Report <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/NXS24> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Addressing the multi-dimensional challenges posed by climate change requires a nuanced understanding of the complex interrelationships among diverse nexus elements. While some climate change response options can deliver mutual benefits across the nexus, such as water, energy and food security (see Figure 5.5.3 and Section 5.5.3), others may have unintended consequences, impeding aspects of sustainable development2 (Figure 5.5.4). Failures to account for nexus interactions have serious consequences in the climate sector. Response options designed solely for climate change mitigation, such as large-scale afforestation with non-native species in ecologically incongruent regions, tend to have more pronounced negative impacts. These may lead to resource competition or ecological imbalances2. In contrast, strategies devised with a comprehensive nexus approach can maximize synergies. For instance, F11/C15,Sustainable healthy diets (Section 5.5.3.15; Section 5.3.3.11) can reduce GHG emissions, alleviate pressure on land and water resources, and lower public health costs associated with poor nutrition, thereby acting as an enabler for other response options to be implemented successfully. When conflicts arise within the nexus, tailored management strategies, such as incorporating new woodlands into sustainably managed landscapes in ways that benefit local populations, can mitigate adverse effects. Some response options enable the successful implementation of others, highlighting that bundling and sequencing of response options can help deliver transformative change. For example, the reduced pressure on land arising from shifting to F11/C15, Sustainable healthy diets (Section 5.5.3.15; Section 5.3.3.11) in overconsuming regions and/or through C02/F02, Sustainable intensification (Section 5.5.3.2; Section 5.3.3.3) can enable more sustainable forms of farming such as B03/C11, Agroecology (Section 5.1.3.3; Section 5.5.3.11). This in turn can free up land for other land-based climate change response options, such as reforestation, as part of C12, Forest-based practices to address climate change (Section 5.5.3.12); C04, Wetland conservation and restoration (Section 5.5.3.4) and land-based renewable energy, such as C06, Solar photovoltaics on land (Section 5.5.3.6). The rapid transformation, through systems transitions, of multiple sectors including energy, land-use, urban planning, industrial activities and societal behaviours, is necessary for holistic climate change mitigation, adaptation and sustainable development. Achieving this systemic shift necessitates harmonizing various enabling conditions - financial, social, economic, institutional and political. The governance of these sectors is influenced not just by formal institutions but also by a plethora of actors and networks, both of which can either facilitate or obstruct effective solutions. The potential for transformative change is often optimized when multiple response options are integrated. For example, the additional land demands that might arise from employing B03/C11, Agroecology (Section 5.1.3.3; Section 5.5.3.11) can be offset by a parallel shift to F11/C15, Sustainable heathy diets (Section 5.3.3.11; Section 5.5.3.15) and F10, Reducing food loss and waste (Section 5.3.3.10). Cities that epitomize low-carbon resilience through implementing integrated B02/C14, Urban nature-based solutions (Section 5.1.3.2; Section 5.5.3.14) are pivotal in driving these interlinked positive outcomes. Implementation of all response options supports the achievement of international policy frameworks, such as the SDGs, the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework and Paris Agreement (Figure 5.5.4; Figure 5.5.16).8 All response options support the delivery of at least two SDGs with some contributing to eight and all contribute to at least two of the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework targets, with some contributing to up to fourteen, and all contribute towards the Paris Agreement long-term global goals for climate change mitigation and adaptation. Examples of response options contributing to the implementation of the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework are C01, Increased soil organic carbon (Section 5.5.3.1); C04, Wetland conservation and restoration (Section 5.5.3.4); C07, Sustainable bioeconomy (Section 5.5.3.7), and C10, Global cooperation for finance and technology (Section 5.5.3.10) . Others, despite their importance for climate action, e.g., C09, Multi-hazard early warning systems (Section 5.5.3.9), have limited representation in the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework, potentially due to the framing of biodiversity challenges . Similar response options contribute to multiple SDGs, i.e., C01, Increase soil organic carbon (Section 5.5.3.1) ; C04, Wetland conservation and restoration (Section 5.5.3.4) which contributes positively to 12 of the 17 SDGs and C07, Sustainable bioeconomy (Section 5.5.3.7). In addition, several response options are closely aligned with the United Nations Food Systems pathways, such as C01, Increased soil organic carbon (Section 5.5.3.1) ; C02/F02, Sustainable intensification (Section 5.5.3.2; section 5.3.3.3) ; C03, Integrated multitrophic aquaculture (Section 5.5.3.3) and B03/C11, Agroecology (Section 5.1.3.3; Section 5.5.3.11) . Figure 5.5.16. Climate change response options supporting achievement of global goals. The figure shows whether an individual response option contributes to the achievement of the Sustainable Development Goals, the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework targets and the long-term goals for mitigation and adaptation of the Paris Agreement. Response options listed in order by alphanumeric code. Shaded cells indicate support for a goal or a target. Response option titles in bold font indicate that the response option supports the achievement of more than five Sustainable Development Goals and more than five Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework targets. Implementing individual, bundles or sequences of response options is enabled by nexus governance. Consideration of ethics, values and world views is fundamental to such governance as it dictates the inclusivity and effectiveness of climate strategies. Equity and justice are not just normative principles but operational necessities for resilient and sustainable community well-being. ILK contributes to this by offering contextual, nuanced strategies that simultaneously address climate action and broader sustainable development goals, such as biodiversity conservation and food security. The weaving of ILK into scientific frameworks enriches climate policy toolkits, embedding equity and inclusivity into the resulting strategies. To strengthen climate actions effectively, creating strong policies through governance systems that connect and improve the nexus elements of biodiversity, water, food and health across levels from local to global is essential. Effective nexus governance for climate is most likely realized through a hybrid model, which combines the structured predictability of formal mechanisms with the adaptive capacity of informal systems and networks of actors. This complementarity contributes to the resilience and robustness of climate strategies, fulfilling broader environmental and human well-being objectives. When strategically aligned with nexus elements, integrating diverse options for climate actions presents an unparalleled opportunity to drive transformative change. Through coordinated efforts, inclusive governance and the empowerment of local communities, resilience, equity and sustainability can be fostered across all nexus dimensions. By embracing holistic and innovative approaches, the global community can effectively navigate the complexities of climate action, ensuring a balanced and equitable future for all. This comprehensive approach addresses the immediate impacts of climate change and lays the groundwork for long-term sustainability and resilience. Collaboration of policymakers, communities and other stakeholders leverages scientific insights and local knowledge to implement effective and equitable climate change response options. The successful realization of these goals will require sustained commitment, robust governance frameworks and adaptive capacity to respond to emerging challenges and opportunities. As society moves forward, the lessons learned and best practices from diverse contexts must be shared and scaled to foster a global response that is both inclusive and impactful." ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.5.6" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Synthesis and conclusions" .

