        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/W8H2YPSJ>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/WBAIRUHK>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/Y2UK8BN2>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/Y4L27UEH>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/Z7K6NKVT>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/ZUSRHGXC> ;
    ipbes:Report <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/NXS24> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "The 14 assessed biodiversity response options all have the potential to benefit at least four nexus elements, with only two not positively impacting all five elements (Figure 5.1.16). They involve several actors at all levels with different arrangements for interaction (see Section 5.1.4). The nexus-wide outcomes vary across the biodiversity response options. Some response options are highly multifunctional providing benefits to several nexus elements (e.g., Section 5.1.3.4, B04 Ecosystem-based adaptation in rural landscapes; Section 5.1.3.9, B09 Integrated landscape and seascape approaches; Section 5.1.3.12, B012 Land and sea planning), while others are targeted to fewer nexus elements in addition to biodiversity (e.g., Section 5.1.3.2, B02, Urban nature-based solutions; Section 5.1.3.5, B05, Forest landscape restoration; Section 5.1.3.6, B06, Restoration of coastal and marine systems). Some response options can comprise trade-offs especially those reducing production intensity (e.g., Section 5.1.3.1, B01, Area-based conservation; Section 5.1.3.3, B03, Agroecology) or changing land use (e.g., Section 5.1.3.5, B05, Forest landscape restoration; Section 5.1.3.8, B08, Rewilding). Overall, more trade-offs were reported with food and to a lesser extent with health, water and climate change. Figure 5.1.16. Benefits and trade-offs of identified response options with respect to nexus elements.10 The number of nexus elements per response option that contain a negative score for any of their scores for the impacts on nexus elements represented in Figure 5.1.2 (left); and the number of elements with a median positive impact score (right). The size of the end points indicates the number of sources supporting a response option. Biodiversity response options primarily target biodiversity protection to maintain current state, management of its use and extraction, and restoration of degraded structure and function. In addition, they primarily benefit water for ecosystems and people and climate change adaptation and mitigation, although quantitative evidence for climate change mitigation by specific response option is incomplete (Figure 5.1.2). Several response options address both climate change mitigation and adaptation: B03, Agroecology (Section 5.1.3.3); B04, Ecosystem-based adaptation in rural landscapes (Section 5.1.3.4) and B08, Rewilding (Section 5.1.3.8). Other response options favour either mitigation (B05, Forest landscape restoration, Section 5.1.3.5) or adaptation (B02, Urban nature-based solutions, Section 5.1.3.2). Response options for biodiversity have substantial co-benefits for health, especially in terms of well-being, with B03, Agroecology (Section 5.1.3.3) and B14, Reconnecting people with nature (Section 5.1.3.14) showing the greatest benefits. There are also some potential trade-offs with disease vectors when pathogens, parasites or predators are favoured, for example, when B02, Urban nature-based solutions (Section 5.1.3.2) , B05, Forest restoration (Section 5.1.3.5) or B08, Rewilding (Section 5.1.3.8) favour harmful biota in proximity to vulnerable human populations . Some response options include food as a primary target, such as B03, Agroecology (Section 5.1.3.3). B04, Ecosystem-based adaptation in rural landscapes (Section 5.1.3.4) and B09, Integrated landscape and seascape approaches (Section 5.1.3.9). However, trade-offs with food can occur in the case of B05, Forest landscape restoration (Section 5.1.3.5) or B08, Rewilding (Section 5.1.3.8) when food production is reduced in favour of return to natural systems, and during transitions to agroecology . The varying performance and trade-offs associated with the response options can be overcome by combining response options in bundles or sequences at the landscape to regional scale (Section 5.6.5). This complementarity between response options, where implementation of a response option can strengthen or enable the performance of other options, is recognized in B09, integrated landscape and seascape approaches (Section 5.1.3.9), B12, land and sea planning (Section 5.1.3.12), and in some B10, Rights-based approaches in landscapes and seascapes managed by IPLC (Section 5.1.3.10). For example, an integrated landscape approach and/or land planning can deal with the trade-offs with food for B05, Forest restoration (Section 5.1.3.5) or B08, Rewilding (Section 5.1.3.8) by combining these actions with others that have a positive impact on food production, such as sustainable intensification (see Section 5.3.3.3 (F03); Section 5.5.3.2 (C02)), fostering synergies in the landscape. In addition, when deforested land used for agricultural crops or pastures reverts to natural forest the restored biodiversity may provide a wide array of nature’s contributions to people, supporting rural economies and livelihoods . Furthermore, it may increase resilience to climate variability, and when integrated with B03, Agroecology (Section 5.1.3.3), complementarities among crop rotations may offset yield losses when considering longer-term (e.g. decadal) production, especially under climate change . Thus, while forest restoration and rewilding are inherently associated with decreased food production where there is competition for land, they can contribute to increasing the provision of, and income from, other essential foods, products and medicine (game, fish and seafood, wild foods and medicinal plants), particularly to local people . Bundling response options can also deliver reciprocal benefits for mental health and the conservation or restoration of natural spaces. For example, protected areas (e.g., B01, Area-based conservation (Section 5.1.3.1), B02, Urban nature-based solutions (Section 5.1.3.2) or B08, Rewilding (Section 5.1.3.8) combined with adequate land-use configuration (e.g., B12, Land and sea planning (Section 5.1.3.12)) can be an enabler for B14, Reconnecting people with nature (Section 5.1.3.14) by overcoming inequities in physical access to nature and reducing the footprint of travelling to natural areas. In turn, reconnection with nature can lead to behavioural change that benefits biodiversity conservation, restoration and sustainable use . Procedural equity is an enabling condition for biodiversity response options to have nexus-wide benefits and positive socioeconomic impacts. Negative outcomes, if reported, tend to fall either in the lower categories of participation or in cases where there was no information on the participation level (Figure 5.1.17). Higher levels of participation were associated with a lower proportion of trade-offs for nexus elements. Conversely, inadequate institutional capacity is one of the strongest barriers to implementation of response options that may lead to negative impacts across all elements of the nexus, as was reported in some cases of B12, Land and sea planning (Section 5.1.3.12; Figure 5.1.13). Legal instruments that recognize the legitimacy of governance mechanisms based on customary systems and traditional knowledge, and social and information-based instruments enable other response options by boosting equity and driving social and behavioural changes (B10, Rights-based approaches (Section 5.1.3.10); B14, Reconnecting people with nature (Section 5.1.3.14)). Finally, social acceptability and technological feasibility are also enabling factors in the implementation of biodiversity response options, while economic cost is the most frequent barrier in the examined examples of implementation of response options. Figure 5.1.17. Social effectiveness, economic effectiveness and distributional equity scores for different participation levels.7 Participation increases from informing, the lowest level of participation, to supporting community interests. Points are coloured based on whether the response option being scored benefits more than three nexus elements (median positive score) with no negative scores at all or incurs possible trade-offs. A response option was assumed to incur a trade-off if any of the indicator scores for impacts on each nexus element were negative (see Chapter 5.0, Table 5.0.1). Besides these trends in enablers and barriers, successful response options require a mix of top-down and bottom-up processes. This includes tailoring response option implementation to context, taking into account the local state of enabling conditions and the incidence of direct and indirect drivers that are addressed by the biodiversity response options (Figure 5.1.18). The most desirable mix of top-down and bottom-up processes varies across response options and places, with the best combination depending on context. Exemplary cases, for example, for B04, Ecosystem-based climate adaptation in rural landscapes (Section 5.1.3.4) or B09, Integrated landscape and seascape approaches (Section 5.1.3.9; Box 5.1.4), highlight successful processes and their combinations of actors, institutions and sociopolitical options. Knowledge synthesis and sharing of successes and failures according to standardized criteria in databases will support scaling out and scaling up of such exemplary cases. Capacity strengthening and transformative social learning across actors and institutions are also essential to support these knowledge exchange and scaling processes, and to build capacity for multi-actor governance (Section 7.4.1). Knowledge exchange and networking across on-the-ground actions that are locally led and managed by IPLC and private actors is essential for their scaling out to more numerous locations. The scaling out of successful response options will support mainstreaming biodiversity into all sectors of society. The roles of intermediary institutions and change leaders in communities also need to be considered, along with regulations and institutionalization (scaling up) and behavioural change processes (scaling deep) (Section 4.4). Figure 5.1.18. Geographical spread of literature sources supporting biodiversity response options.7 A) Centroids of countries with the number of sources originating from the country. B) Direct drivers targeted by response options per region (Africa: AFR; Americas: AME; Asia and the Pacific: ASP; Europe and Central Asia: ECA). Drivers: habitat loss and degradation (HAB); exploitation of wild populations (EXP); climate change (CLM); pollution of soil, water and air (POLL); species introductions (SPE); other (OTH); and no info (MSG). (C) Indirect drivers targeted by response options per region: economic (ECO); demographic (DEM); governance (GOV); values (VAL); technological (TEC); no info (MSG). The drivers behind biodiversity degradation are complex and multifaceted, with direct and indirect factors playing significant roles (Chapter 2). Analysis shows that the selected response options for addressing biodiversity concerns align with global assessments of threats, such as those outlined previously . Among the direct drivers targeted by the biodiversity response options, habitat conversion and degradation emerge as strongly predominant, accounting for 48-65 per cent of cases across regions (Figure 5.1.18). This is followed by climate change, pollution and exploitation of wild populations, each with similar levels. Species introductions are addressed to a much lesser extent. There is more regional variation in how the response options target the indirect drivers that underpin the direct drivers (Chapter 2, Figure 2.8). The top four - demography, institutional, technology and economic - comprise 80 per cent of the indirect drivers addressed globally, with some regional variation in their representation across the database. In line with the drivers addressed by the biodiversity response options, each response option contributes to the achievement of several SDGs (Figure 5.1.19)11, exemplifying different and complementary actions to support sustainable development though biodiversity conservation, restoration and sustainable use. The response options contribute to between three and nine SDGs. More response options support those SDGs that are directly related to the nexus elements (e.g., biodiversity – SDGs 14 and 15, water – SDG6, food – SDG2 and climate change – SDG13), probably reflecting the selection criteria. The exception is health (SDG3) since most response options contribute indirectly to health and well-being, through multiple nature’s contributions to people or people connecting with nature. The response options also strongly contribute to the targets of the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework, supporting between 4 and 15 targets. In particular, response options support targets 1 to 3, 11, 14 and 22 as these align with nexus approaches that aim to mainstream biodiversity into other human actions and decision-making through conservation, restoration and sustainable use of biodiversity and nature’s contributions to people, with equity as the main enabler. The other targets are addressed by fewer response options as their specificity increases. For example, targets 7, 8 and 10 are addressed by response options focusing on reducing pollution, climate change and sustainable production, respectively. Some targets are addressed by only one or two response options each, but when bundled in complementarity ways can contribute to many more targets. For example, B10, Rights-based approaches (Section 5.1.3.10), supports targets 13 and 23; B09, Integrated landscape and seascape approaches (Section 5.1.3.9), B11, Multilateral environmental agreements (Section 5.1.3.11) and B13, Natural capital accounting (Section 5.1.3.13) together address targets 15, 18 and 19; and adding B14, Reconnecting people with nature (Section 5.1.3.14) also addresses targets 16 and 20. There is no single response option that supports all targets, although some have broad effects, particularly B01, Area-based conservation (Section 5.1.3.1), B09, Integrated landscape and seascape approaches (Section 5.1.3.9) and B11, Multilateral environmental agreements (section 5.1.3.11). In summary, the biodiversity response options span a range from conservation to sustainable use, local to global action, and on-the-ground action to legal instruments, highlighting the necessary of multiple approaches to ensure biodiversity and other nexus elements move closer to a more sustainable future. Real world implementation often combines several of these response options and relies on a broad diversity of local to global actors, including IPLC, with complementary roles for all sectors to meet nexus-wide objectives. Figure 5.1.19. Biodiversity response options supporting achievement of global goals. The figure shows whether an individual response option contributes to the achievement of the Sustainable Development Goals, the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework targets and the long-term goals for mitigation and adaptation of the Paris Agreement. Response options listed in order by alphanumeric code. Shaded cells indicate support for a goal or a target. Response option titles in bold font indicate that the response option supports the achievement of more than five Sustainable Development Goals and more than five Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework targets." ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.1.6" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Synthesis and conclusions" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.3.4> a ipbes:SubChapter ;
    ipbes:Chapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ch/NXS24ch5.3> ;
    ipbes:Reference <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/3NLQUAIH>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/7KJNCAFJ>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/CSZADU37>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/F6Z6X9D8>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/GAF2Z2TS>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/HVPIS7NJ>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/IZ55WBYH>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/KB6PBVCH>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/KUR8Z76V>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/L3UWCQH4>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/LGKQSKXU>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/LQIJQILQ>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/N2EU9YQ6>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/PNL4J6V3>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/S4QX8XEB>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/T4ZICBMZ>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/TWEYAVPN>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/UATRQ8UC>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/VSMWKCDT> ;
    ipbes:Report <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/NXS24> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Food system governance operates across and takes different forms at multiple scales. It involves local, national and international institutions and a diversity of actors, food system typologies, framings and configurations (Figures 5.3.17, 5.3.18 and 5.3.19). Examples include private governance (including voluntary guidelines) ; community self-governance ; participatory guarantee systems ; and rural-urban food coalitions directly linking producers and consumers (e.g., farmers’ markets, community supported agriculture) . Each governance form in turn contains a diversity of arrangements. Trade, particularly international trade, provides additional complexity. Trade systems can stimulate the development of new forms of governance (e.g., codes of conduct, certification schemes) which can have positive impacts on the nexus elements and livelihoods. However, poor governance can mean that trade exacerbates or induces undesirable outcomes for the nexus elements and people . Given this complexity and the different narratives and conceptions of food, a first step for food system governance is to agree on basic principles and values . This reflexive approach to governance can create structures that facilitate participation and involve diverse stakeholders and aligns with the “governance through goals” basis of the SDGs as well as with the concepts of nexus governance (Section 4.5). This approach includes: 1) systems-based (rather than single-issue) problem framing to deal with interlinked issues, drivers and feedback loops; 2) developing boundary-spanning and collaborative structures to cross siloed governance structures and include non-state actors; 3) ensuring adaptability to flexibly respond to inherent uncertainties and volatility; 4) promoting inclusiveness to facilitate support and legitimacy, particularly from civil society; and 5) developing transformative capacity to overcome path dependencies and create conditions that enable structural change . Other authors have suggested principles of learning, flexibility, adaptation, scale-matching, participation, diversity enhancement and precaution , all of which correlate with the concept of nexus governance. For example, the Global Strategy Framework for Food Security and Nutrition of the Committee on World Food Security, reviewed and approved annually by the committee Plenary, provides an overarching framework for food system strategies, policies and actions that can be used to develop integrated approaches. It offers guidelines to improve coordination and guide synchronized action by a wide range of stakeholders (policy- and decision-makers, development partners, cooperation and humanitarian agencies, international and regional organizations, financial institutions, research institutions, civil society organizations, the private sector, non-governmental organizations, and other relevant stakeholders). The Global Strategy Framework for Food Security and Nutrition is a living and flexible document that can be adapted to changing priorities and context, and is validated by the wide ownership, participation and consultation afforded by Committee on World Food Security. The framework emphasises the need for policy coherence across sectors which impact on the right to adequate food and nutrition and recognizes and puts emphasis on the central role of small-scale food producers. Polycentric approaches can address both different types and the multiple dimensions of food systems. They are characterized by multiple governing authorities and effective legal and regulatory instruments operating at scales from local to global, each with considerable independence to make norms and rules within a specific domain (Figures 5.3.17 and 5.3.18). Some response options require international arrangements to address global trade, consistent labelling for sustainability practices (Sections 5.3.3.4, 5.3.3.5 and 5.3.3.6) or sustainable healthy diets (Section 5.3.3.11). Taxes and subsidies have significant influence on food systems with some countries levying taxes on unhealthy foods, notably on sugar; agricultural subsidies in contrast strongly influence farmer behaviours with opportunities to support practices that produce public goods such as habitat and connectivity, improved water quality and climate change mitigation. Sub-national governance (e.g., at the level of cities or communities) can be important, for example food policy councils and cities networks focused on addressing food systems challenges (e.g., Sustainable Food Cities in the United Kingdom, National Network of Cities for Agroecology in Spain). Other response options will require local governance structures capable of dealing with the specific cultural, social, economic and ecological dimensions of food systems. Participants in polycentric systems have the advantage of using knowledge from across scales through their own expertise and learning from others . Rights-based approaches to food systems offer opportunities for good governance by providing consistency across policy arenas, accountability and implementation . The approval of the United Nations Declaration on the Rights of Peasants and Other People Working in Rural Areas has been identified as an opportunity to foster food systems transformation through better governance by centring the agency and authority of peasants and other rural communities in decision-making processes . The implementation of this declaration would foster response options supporting gender equality and land tenure rights (Sections 5.3.3.14, 5.3.3.15 and 5.3.3.16) as preconditions to achieve sustainable food systems as well as actions linked to restoring soil health (Section 5.3.3.2) and ecological intensification (Sections 5.3.3.4, 5.3.3.5 and 5.3.3.6). Creating appropriate spaces for participation is key for polycentric approaches to succeed (e.g., Section 4.5). Including producer communities in decision-making, within the frame of clear boundaries related to healthy and sustainable food, is essential to fostering just transitions and implementing some of the response options in this chapter. Inclusion of citizens through innovative structures, like the citizens assemblies promoted by some national, regional or local authorities, can foster transformative change and more ambitious policies than the ones promoted by governments . For instance, citizens’ assemblies in the autonomous community of Catalonia, Spain have supported actions linked to restoring soil health (Section 5.3.3.2), ecological intensification of croplands and rangelands (Sections 5.3.3.4 and 5.3.3.5), reducing pollution (Sections 5.3.3.7, 5.3.3.8 and 5.3.3.9), reducing food loss and waste (Section 5.3.3.10), sustainable healthy diets (Section 5.3.3.11) and securing access to nature resources and land (Section 5.3.3.16). Citizens’ assemblies in Spain have also proposed actions linked to ecological intensification of aquatic foods (Section 5.3.3.6), city region food systems (Section 5.3.3.12), reforming public spending (Section 5.3.3.13) and Indigenous food systems (Section 5.3.3.15) through use of traditional seeds. Other actions supported by these assemblies included training in sustainable production and consumption, promotion of bioregions, public infrastructures for farmers or public procurement. All these options are in all cases more ambitions than the policies adopted by both the Catalonian autonomous community and Spanish governments, respectively. Different food systems may require different governance, policy and institutional approaches5, but common principles based on resilience and social-ecological justice are needed to address the nexus from the food side TEMP. Coordinated actions are more likely to incorporate issues of fairness in process and to promote the interests and voice of vulnerable populations and more just outcomes. Most response options require combinations of policies or policy bundles that combine economic incentives to support positive behaviours, with regulatory policies that penalize harmful behaviours such as pollution. Policies that influence social norms are effective across both, and necessary both for changes in production and consumption behaviours across food system actors (Figure 5.3.19). Figure 5.3.17. Classification of each of the 16 food response options as an action or management (direct implementation on the ground), a policy instrument that guides action and/or an enabler that facilitates or improves an action, management or policy. See Chapter 4, Section 4.2.4 and Table 4.4 for descriptions of the policy categories. Figure 5.3.18. Actor involvement in the 16 food response options. Actor typologies are those discussed in Section 4.3.4. Influencer actors are people and institutions that influence decision-making process related to the nexus and therefore have an impact on those who implement decisions; this includes those that can act as lobbyists or as watchdogs to foster accountability through advocacy. Affected actors are people, including communities and marginalized groups, who are directly involved in (and dependent on) the implementation of nexus-related decisions and who have their own stakes and interests. Key players are people and institutions that can both influence and become affected by decisions. Actors not involved neither exercise influence nor are they affected (see Section 4.2.6). Figure 5.3.19. Enablers of and barriers to successful implementation of each of the 16 food response options. See Chapter 4, Section 4.2.5 and Table 4.6 for descriptions of the enablers and barriers." ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.3.4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Nexus governance to support food response options" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-5.5.2> a ipbes:SubChapter ;
    ipbes:Chapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ch/NXS24ch5.5> ;
    ipbes:Reference <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/298XLXV6>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/2Z7KIVMP>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/72N6ZQXJ>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/85HM6CVP>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/8GEWQA33>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/AY8W7T4U>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/AYKJQ7NW>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/C2FNQNUI>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/EWLFYXKV>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/GKKRXN2V>,
