        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/N38AIQIE>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/P3XLSSDC>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/P4CLBEVX>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/Q6QVRNU5>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/RQ9L4CYP>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/TCLFUBWW>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/W563S4UK>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/Z88F2P53> ;
    ipbes:Report <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/NXS24> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Several global assessments and reports call for the urgent need for coordinated action from governments, businesses and civil society to adapt to and mitigate climate change while preserving biodiversity and human well-being . Drawing from Chapter 1 (Section 1.3.3), the actors in the realm of climate action are broadly categorized into global and regional institutions, national governments, local governments and municipalities, knowledge and educational communities, civil society organizations, community-based organizations, private sector and business organizations, Indigenous Peoples and local communities (IPLC), science-policy interfaces, financing institutions, and media and the arts. Table 5.5.1 provides a detailed overview of the key actors and institutions involved in climate change adaptation and mitigation, categorized into these actor groups. It highlights the diverse and interconnected roles that the United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change (UNFCCC), national ministries, research institutes, grassroots organizations, and private enterprises play. The table emphasizes the collaborative nature of climate action, showcasing the wide array of stakeholders working across different scales and sectors to address the pressing challenges posed by climate change. Effective actor mobilization is essential for achieving climate change and nexus goals due to their diverse expertise, resources and ability to foster collaborative solutions, global coordination, capacity-building and funding for large-scale and local initiatives. Climate change governance is a complex, context-specific process involving the interplay of actors, institutions and policies. It varies widely from place to place, depending on factors such as institutional and technological capacity, socio-economic characteristics and geographic location. While individuals and households lead adaptation efforts, particularly in rural areas, their limited role in institutional responses, especially in the Global South, necessitates stronger government planning and civil society coordination . Analysis reveals that the dominant climate change mitigation strategies employed by various actors encompass diverse approaches, ranging from employee travel and office operations to offset programmes and green building standards with certification schemes. However, gaps in climate action may persist because actors are unaware of missed opportunities for deeper coordination . Table 5.5.1 Actors and institutions involved in climate action. See Chapter 1, Section 1.3.3 and Table 1.1 for actor category descriptions. Actor category Actor/institution involved in climate action Global and regional institutions United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change (UNFCCC) United Nations Environment Programme (UNEP) World Meteorological Organization (WMO) International Union for Conservation of Nature (IUCN) Global Environment Facility (GEF) The World Bank (WB) International Renewable Energy Agency (IRENA) Carbon Pricing Leadership Coalition (CPLC) Green Climate Fund The Energy Transition Commission (ETC) International Solar Alliance (ISA) Renewable Energy and Energy Efficiency Partnership (REEEP) Race To Zero Climate and Clean Air Coalition International Energy Agency (IEA) National governments National environmental and climate ministries National meteorological and climate services National energy agencies Climate action plans and policies National climate change commissions Ministries of agriculture and forestry with climate mandates Disaster management authorities Climate finance units within ministries National adaptation programmes of action (NAPAs) Local governments and municipalities City climate action networks (e.g. C40 Cities, ICLEI) Municipal environmental agencies Local climate resilience offices Urban sustainability departments City adaptation plans Regional climate councils Knowledge and educational communities Universities and research institutes focusing on climate science and policy Environmental and climate research networks (e.g. Climate Action Network, Earth System Governance Project) Climate change research centres (e.g. Grantham Research Institute on Climate Change and the Environment) Educational programs and curricula on climate change Professional societies (e.g. American Geophysical Union, Royal Meteorological Society) Climate modelling centres Institutes for sustainable development Civil society organizations International climate NGOs (e.g., Greenpeace, WWF, 350.org, Friends of the Earth) Local and regional climate advocacy groups Climate justice organizations (e.g. Climate Justice Alliance) Environmental advocacy coalitions Youth climate movements (e.g., Fridays for Future, Youth Climate Leaders) Faith-based environmental groups (e.g., Green-Faith) Human rights and climate justice organizations Grassroots environmental networks Community-based organizations Local climate action groups Environmental and sustainability cooperatives Community renewable energy projects Local watershed and conservation groups Community adaptation projects Rural development and climate resilience organizations Urban community gardens and green spaces Indigenous Peoples and local communities (IPLC) Indigenous Peoples’ climate alliances Local community groups focusing on traditional climate resilience practices Indigenous environmental networks Tribal climate change projects Customary land and water management committees Indigenous women’s environmental networks Traditional ecological knowledge sharing platforms Forest dwelling community organizations Private sector and business organizations Renewable energy companies Green technology firms Corporate sustainability initiatives (e.g., CDP, RE100, Science Based Targets initiative) Climate innovation hubs Environmental consulting firms Sustainable agriculture and fisheries businesses Clean transportation companies Industry associations focusing on sustainability (e.g., World Business Council for Sustainable Development) Real estate and construction firms with green building initiatives Carbon offset and trading companies Science-policy interfaces Intergovernmental science-policy platforms (e.g. IPCC, IPBES) National climate councils and advisory boards Climate change commissions Research-policy interface networks National ecosystem assessments Environmental economics and policy research institutes Climate resilience and adaptation planning bodies Financial institutions Green investment banks Climate finance institutions (e.g. Climate Investment Funds, Green Climate Fund) ESG-focused investment firms Development banks (e.g., World Bank, Asian Development Bank, African Development Bank) Insurance companies offering climate risk insurance Microfinance institutions Philanthropic foundations focusing on climate action (e.g., Rockefeller Foundation, Gates Foundation) Sustainable investment funds Carbon markets and finance mechanisms Media and the arts Environmental journalists and media organizations (e.g., The Guardian Environment, Climate Home News) Climate-focused art and cultural initiatives Documentary filmmakers (e.g., films like “An Inconvenient Truth”, “Before the Flood”) Artists and musicians advocating for climate action Environmental blogs and digital media platforms Climate change communication campaigns Educational broadcasting on climate issues (e.g., BBC Earth) Climate change exhibitions and museums Although diverse actors are essential for achieving climate governance goals, there are challenges in coordinating their efforts . For example, governments may have different priorities than businesses, and conflicting interests may arise when balancing economic and environmental objectives. Policy implementation gaps can emerge due to resource constraints, lack of political will or public support. Social and cultural factors, such as the exclusion of communities from decision-making processes, can also lead to resistance or non-acceptance of conservation, adaptation and mitigation measures . A key critique of current climate change strategies centres on their neglect of internal factors. These factors, encompassing distinct world views, values and beliefs, are posited to exert a significant influence on both the human behaviours driving climate change and the solutions that are developed in response . Coordinated action, understanding and consideration of specific context are therefore crucial for effective climate action (Figure 5.5.2). Figure 5.5.2. Actors, action and challenges in climate change mitigation and adaptation. The figure visualizes the interplay between the diverse actors involved in climate governance, who require orchestration to ensure coordination, equity of opportunity, implementation gaps and associated values for all stakeholders. The “top-down” and “bottom-up” refer to the authority levels of actors in achieving outcomes. Top-down involves central decision-making, often through command and control, while bottom-up entails actions from groups intended to implement policies . *Note: The level of action is not prescribed, as some actors operate at multiple levels and arenas. Figure based on Morrison et. al and Petzold et. al . Studies on climate governance actors indicate a division between national actors being central to climate change mitigation and local actors being prominent in adaptation . Non-governmental organizations (NGOs) are intermediaries, helping users acquire, understand and value climate information. To maximize the contribution of local actors, five areas of interaction and engagement are assessed: knowledge sharing, coordination, collaboration, co-production and learning. These areas require new actions from NGOs, governments (national, sub-national and local) and funders . Global targets alone are insufficient to motivate local actors, who may struggle to engage with abstract global issues . Non-state actors that can mobilize climate action among states and assess collective progress towards the Paris Agreement are essential . Varying patterns of power, politics and participation can both drive and hinder climate change adaptation and mitigation . Achieving effective climate action involves a complex interplay between governance arrangements, power dynamics and the intricate perspectives of the actors involved. Decentralized multilevel governance approaches, aligned with bottom-up or community-based decision-making, social innovation and institutional innovation, can enable transformative change to achieve long-term climate goals . It necessitates an evolving paradigm that acknowledges the multi-dimensionality of challenges and leverages each actor’s strengths in the nexus. This nexus governance approach, underpinned by evidence-based strategies and inclusive mechanisms, can move climate action to be transformative and equitable. Section 5.5.4 presents a more detailed discussion on governance for climate action." ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.5.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Key actors and institutions" .

