        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/BV9Z962U>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/CALRKWTH>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/D6N8LW3N>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/JZL93FTN>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/KE2HL87X>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/KMZRGLSG>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/KQCQTVL2>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/LFH666PG>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/LIE4YXPB>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/LPZK64RR>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/PU3YXQ9W>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/R34XNAMI>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/RCMSV5HL>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/UNG7FGCB>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/W48MFPFK>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/WAVV99A8>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/YLYK3LFL>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/Z2CUP7W2>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/4596166/items/ZJW8TQN3> ;
    ipbes:Report <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/NXS24> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Because many projects consider no more than one nexus element other than biodiversity from their onset, direct evidence of outcomes of biodiversity response options across multiple nexus elements is often limited in individual studies. Even for biodiversity, most evidence regards structure and not function. This could be improved by using and collecting data for priority indicators such as the Essential Biodiversity Variables and Essential Ecosystem Service Variables , and using international functional trait databases . Furthermore, impacts on security of access to food, health and water are rarely reported as outcomes of biodiversity-focused response options. The lack of comprehensive reporting across nexus elements creates a critical knowledge gap on trade-offs, with benefits being reported more frequently. There is also a lack of reporting of negative impacts to nexus elements and relevant actors. For example, trade-offs between benefits to climate change and biodiversity (e.g., in urban nature-based solutions) can be pieced together from disparate evidence sources but are not often addressed in individual studies or syntheses. Underreporting of non-significant or negative findings is chronic in the literature , compounded by the much slower publication time for non-significant or negative (rather than positive) results . For example, published case studies of nature-based solutions most often report co-benefits with little mention of any negative effects. However, there is a rich ecosystem services literature covering disbenefits or negative nature’s contributions to people . Cases of imperfect implementation or failures in the planning and implementation of nature-based solutions and other initiatives are also rarely reported in the literature or databases, although they provide critical information on how implementation processes influence environmental, social and economic outcomes and trade-offs. A synthesis on social, economic and environmental goals of specific response options is lacking. The analysis presented here only summarizes the limited information available across case studies, rather than these being reported systematically for individual cases of implementation. The assessment of economic and social costs and benefits and distributional equity is a major knowledge gap for biodiversity response options that hinders their implementation and scaling . Beyond a lack of availability and synthesis on financial costs, there is a chronic lack of information on total costs, including labour input by local communities and volunteers, and on markets. Despite interest in monitoring embedded in international standards, such as for nature-based solutions (e.g., see Box 5.1.1) , long-term monitoring and assessments of the effects of biodiversity response options on ecosystem functioning and services, socioeconomic benefits, and governance/decision-making processes (including the inclusion of stakeholders and IPLC) are still limited. Moreover, response options for biodiversity often require years to decades until environmental, social and economic benefits are fully delivered. Sociopolitical response options also frequently take many decades to undertake. There is a lack of consideration of complex systems dynamics and how they may reduce the effectiveness of area-based conservation or nature-based solutions initiatives (e.g., telecoupling, historical legacies ) . This also impedes broader and more nuanced assessment of their effectiveness, demanding new assessment methods along with longer-term monitoring efforts . Regular, consistent and locally sensitive monitoring and evaluation protocols and tools and leading examples for the effectiveness of adaptive co-management for response options have been proposed , but are not systematically applied. Further research is needed on inclusive, participatory and adaptive monitoring and evaluation mechanisms that cover all elements of the nexus, including how they can be incorporated into international monitoring and evaluation guidelines (see Section 7.3.8). The literature focuses heavily on some types of instruments over others: for example, urban nature-based solutions were reported as almost exclusively delivered via legal and regulatory options despite evidence to suggest that they are more successful when delivered with local communities through rights-based approaches or social and cultural norms . Novel governance structures have evolved for biodiversity response options, especially area-based conservation and rights-based approaches (see Section 5.1.4), but global assessments on the distribution and widespread use of these structures are lacking. In particular, community engagement, traditional institutional arrangements, bio-cultural and customary approaches, and the incorporation of traditional value systems of IPLC in the implementation of biodiversity response options are rarely reported. Likewise, there are very few reports of the role of IPLC cultural practices and innovation for response options. All these knowledge gaps hinder the scaling out, up, deep and down of biodiversity response options (see Section 4.4). While some barriers and levers for scaling-out biodiversity response options, and especially nature-based solutions, are well identified , evidence on successful examples is lacking, e.g., for ecosystem-based adaptation. Scaling out of response options is further limited by the lack of knowledge on enabling conditions and institutional arrangements for promoting networking response options. Finally, while on the ground cases usually combine several response options (e.g., “policy mixes”) , conceptual and methodological frameworks and evidence for describing their outcomes are needed." ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.1.5" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Knowledge gaps" .

