    ipbes:hasDescription "Health system governance refers to the rules and norms that shape roles and responsibilities, incentives and interactions in the health sector. A variety of conventions, cultural traditions, economic and environmental pressures influence governance arrangements and health system performance. These include new partnerships that recognize traditional health practices and new conceptual models of health such as Planetary Health and One Health. Good health and well-being are core values for society alongside sociocultural, economic and biophysical values . They cannot be achieved in isolation (Section 5.4.1) and hence nexus governance is required. 5.4.4.1. Response option implementation The 15 response options considered in this subchapter are directly implementable on the ground and embody action or management that can advance health while providing co-benefits for other nexus elements (Figure 5.4.13).6 Evidence documented in this subchapter shows that while response options are themselves enabled by various types of policy instruments, these also serve to advance and guide action to achieve multiple policy objectives beyond health. The choice of the most appropriate policy instrument will be context dependent. This is particularly the case for response options H04 to H11 and H14 where different types or combinations of policy instruments may be needed to action or enable the option on the ground depending on context. H03, Net-zero sustainable health care (Section 5.4.3.3) and H13, health impact assessments (Section 5.4.3.13) are associated with legal and regulatory instruments to advance action. H01, Universal health coverage (Section 5.4.3.1) and H02, Intercultural health services (Section 5.4.3.2) are rights-based approaches that drive policy action for harnessing co-benefits across nexus elements. Social and cultural policy instruments are associated with H15, Integrated health education (Section 5.4.3.15), while H12, Integrated watershed-health interventions (Section 5.4.3.12) are dependent on a bundle of policy instruments, some regulatory (e.g., pollution standards) and some sociocultural (e.g., education, training). Apart from advancing nexus co-benefits through integration of health with other nexus elements, some response options actively enable equity such as through H01, Universal health coverage (Section 5.4.3.1), H02, Intercultural health services (section 5.4.3.2) and H13, community-led health impact assessments (Section 5.4.3.13), and thus help contribute to the realization of nexus governance (Section 4.5). The health response options can be implemented successfully if effective policies that include a strong focus on equity and diversity are in place, as illustrated in Figure 5.4.14. All response options require equity and diversity-oriented enablers, and in the case of H02, Intercultural health services (Section 5.4.3.2), these are critical for successful implementation, in addition to institutional enablers. Strengthened institutional capacities are also important for enabling almost all other health response options, with this classified as being a strong enabler for two-thirds of them. Material endowments and the availability of economic and financial resources support the implementation of most health response options. These are of substantial relevance for achieving successful implementation of response options H04, Sustainable use of medicinal plants (Section 5.4.3.4), H06, Reduce overconsumption of meat (Section 5.4.3.6); H07, Pollution prevention (Section 5.4.3.7); H08, Urban green infrastructure (Section 5.4.3.8); H11, Biodiversity management for zoonoses (section 5.4.3.11); H12, Integrated watershed interventions (Section 5.4.3.12) and H13, Health impact assessments (Section 5.4.3.13). Specific examples of strong economic and financial enablers include supporting gentry farmers in the Netherlands (Kingdom of the) (Box 5.4.7), reducing the extremely high health costs of pollution (Figure 5.4.11) and financial incentives for urban greening in Singapore (Box 5.4.8). More evidence is required on financial and economic enablers for H05, Nature on prescription (Section 5.4.3.5); H08, Mangrove restoration and conservation for health (Section 5.4.3.8); H10, Forest conservation for health (Section 5.4.3.10) and H15, Integrated health education (Section 5.4.3.15). There is also a need for knowledge generation on the extent to which material endowments and financial and economic enablers matter for H02, Intercultural health services (Section 5.4.3.2); H03, Net-zero sustainable healthcare (Section 5.4.3.3) and H15, Integrated health education (5.4.3.15). While the extent to which material endowments are required for pursuing H01, Universal health coverage (Section 5.4.3.1) and H15, the One Health approach (Section 5.4.3.15) is unclear, the pursuit of these, as for several other response options, is likely to be substantially enhanced by economic and financial enablers. Investing in affordable and quality healthcare can be done in ways that lead to significantly positive outcomes for people and nature (Box 5.4.3). There is a lack of definitive evidence on how behavioural factors, lifestyle and values enable some of the response options. For achieving H01, Universal health coverage (Section 5.4.3.1); H02, Intercultural health services (Section 5.4.3.2) and H06, Reduce meat overconsumption (Section 5.4.3.6), available evidence does not provide a sufficient basis to unequivocally establish whether behavioural, lifestyle and values-oriented characteristics constitute enablers or pose barriers for advancing health. Behavioural factors are enablers, though not as strong as in the case of some other response options, for H04, Sustainable use of medicinal plants (Section 5.4.3.4); H11, Biodiversity management for zoonoses (Section 5.4.3.11) and H14, the One Health approach (Section 5.4.3.14). For instance, behavioural factors and values are integral to promoting Indigenous food systems that contribute to well-being (Box 5.4.1; Box 5.4.2; Box 5.4.4) and in ensuring health services within both western and Indigenous systems of practice (Figure 5.4.18A). Though the availability of technology is mostly an enabler, there are exceptions where there is no clear evidence on its potential as an enabler versus it becoming a barrier in specific contexts. Examples where technology is a strong enabler include disruptive technologies for cultured meat (related to H06, Reduce meat over consumption (Section 5.4.3.6)), cost-effective abatement technologies for air and water pollution (related to H07, Pollution prevention (Section 5.4.3.7)), educational and network technologies for advancing H14, the One Health approach (Section 5.4.14) as well as planetary health (Box 5.4.13) and locally adapted technologies for watershed interventions (Box 5.4.10) and community health impact assessments (Box 5.4.11). There is established evidence from across the world on the feasibility and availability of technology and know-how in enabling the H08, Mangrove conservation and restoration for health (Section 5.4.3.8); H10, Forest conservation for health (Section 5.4.3.10) and greening infrastructure in both rural and urban contexts (Section 5.4.3.9, H09, Urban green infrastructure (Box 5.4.5; Box 5.4.9). Overall, policy, institutional capacity and equity and diversity-oriented enablers emerge as three substantially relevant enablers for achieving successful implementation of the health response options. As per available evidence, eight response options (Section 5.4.3.1, H01 Universal health coverage; Section 5.4.3.4, H04, Sustainable use of medicinal plants; Section 5.4.3.5, H05, Nature on prescription; Section 5.4.3.7, H07, Pollution prevention; Section 5.4.3.9, H09, Net-zero sustainable healthcare; Section 5.4.3.10, H10, Forest conservation for health; Section 5.4.3.11, H11, Biodiversity management for zoonoses; Section 5.4.3.12, H12, Integrated watershed-health interventions) are strongly enabled by these three enablers, and six response options (Section 5.4.3.2, H02, Intercultural health services, Section 5.4.3.3, H03, Net-zero sustainable healthcare; Section 5.4.3.6, H06, Reduce meat overconsumption; Section 5.4.3.8, H08, Mangrove conservation and restoration for health; Section 5.4.3.13, H13, Health impact assessments; Section 5.4.3.14, H14, the One Health approach) are strongly enabled by two, and to a lesser extent by the remaining one out of these three enabler categories. 5.4.4.2. Actors involved in health governance Governance for health in the nexus requires health actors to engage in promoting health co-benefits across nexus elements. Such governance roles for health sector actors may range from providing basic services (e.g., ensuring school canteen options), supporting (e.g., addressing health disparities through cross-sectoral policy advocacy) or engaging in bilateral or trilateral partnerships (e.g., food safety legislation and enforcement) to leading delivery of a core mandate (e.g., adequate water and energy supplies to health facilities). Collaborative and distributed leadership, developing the capacity of the health sector to work with and learn from other sectors, and developing a shared problem perspective can constitute a way forward, including in low- to middle-income country contexts . Figure 5.4.15 categorizes actors in terms of their roles in the 15 response options presented in this subchapter, based on the assessed evidence for these response options. Global and regional institutions are influencer actors across response options, except for H02, Intercultural health services (Section 5.4.3.2) and H24, the One Health Approach (Section 5.4.3.14), where these actors are key players. As influencers, these actors play a major role in influencing decision-making. National governments are either influencers or key players, depending on the specific response option concerned. They are key players for H06, Reduce meat overconsumption (Section 5.4.3.6); H07, Pollution prevention (Section 5.4.3.7); H08, Mangrove conservation and restoration and conservation for health (Section 5.4.3.8); H09, Urban green infrastructure (Section 5.4.3.9) and H10, Forest conservation for health (Section 5.4.3.10). National Governments influence decision-making and implementation and are also affected by the decisions taken. For all the other response options, they are influencers, including, for instance, H04, Sustainable use of medicinal plants (Section 5.4.3.4) and H11, Biodiversity management for zoonoses (Section 5.4.3.11). In the case of both global and regional institutions and national Governments, their role in decision-making and influencing outcomes is pervasive across health response options. Local governments and municipalities are key players for response options, except in the case of H01, Universal health coverage (Section 5.4.3.1) and H05, Nature on prescription (Section 5.4.3.5) where they act primarily as influencers on decision-making, but largely are not seen to be impacted themselves by decisions because of their influencing roles. Barring a few, for most response options, knowledge and educational communities, civil society, community-based, private sector and business organizations are key players, who engage to a varying extent in decision-making, and in turn are affected by these decisions. IPLC are key players for most response options. They are affected actors, directly involved in implementation and at the same time having their own stakes and interests, as in the case of implementation of H01, Universal health coverage (Section 5.4.3.1). Actors in the science-policy interface and financing institutions are primarily influencers, except for a few response options. Actors in the media and arts domain are invariably influencer actors, wherever evidence of their engagement is available. In certain cases, as for the response options H15, Integrated health education (Section 5.4.3.15); H12, Integrated watershed-health interventions (Section 5.4.3.12) and H02, Intercultural health services (Section 5.4.3.2), there is lack of data and a need for knowledge creation on the role of certain types of actors. Similarly, there is a gap in understanding the engagement of IPLC in promoting H03, Net-zero sustainable healthcare (Section 5.4.3.3), although IPLC actors have an influencer role in H13, Health impact assessments (Section 5.4.3.13) and H14, the One Health Approach (Section 5.4.3.14). Figure 5.4.16 maps the actor categories shown in Table 5.4.1 against the 15 response options. A minimum of five actor categories are engaged in each response option, while a much higher number of actor types are involved in some, up to a maximum of 14 for H11, Biodiversity management for zoonoses (Section 5.4.3.11). Furthermore, all actor categories are engaged with at least three response options, while three actor categories are involved in the governance of ten or more response options. National, sub-national and local government bodies; national and sub-national NGOs; health professional and regulatory associations; and community and civil society-based organizations are among the most engaged actors. There is scope for improving engagement of IPLC, regional institutions, and financial and insurance institutions across several of the health response options. Key actors in health are also spread across the other nexus elements (Figure 5.4.16B). Actors from health and biodiversity are required for all the 15 response options. Actors from all five nexus elements are required for implementing seven of the response options: H03, Net-zero sustainable healthcare (Section 5.4.3.3); H06, Reduce meat overconsumption (Section 5.4.3.6); H10, Forest conservation for health (Section 5.4.3.10); H11, Biodiversity management for zoonoses (Section 5.4.3.11); H13, Health Impact Assessments (Section 5.4.3.13) and H14, the One Health approach (Section 5.4.3.14). Figure 5.4.13. Classification of each of the 15 health response options as an action or management (direct implementation on the ground), a policy instrument that guides action and/or an enabler that facilitates or improves an action, management or policy. See Section 4.2.4 and Table 4.4 for descriptions of the policy categories. Figure 5.4.14. Enablers of and barriers to successful implementation of each of the 15 health response options. See Section 4.2.5 and Table 4.6 for descriptions of the enablers and barriers. Figure 5.4.15. Actor involvement in the 15 health response options. Actor typologies are those discussed in Section 4.3.4. Influencer actors are people and institutions that influence decision-making processes related to the nexus and therefore have an impact on those who implement decisions; this includes those that can act as lobbyists or as watchdogs to foster accountability through advocacy. Affected actors are people, including communities and marginalized groups, who are directly involved in (and dependent on) the implementation of nexus-related decisions and who have their own stakes and interests. Key players are people and institutions that can both influence and become affected by decisions. Actors not involved neither exercise influence nor are they affected (see Section 4.2.6). Figure 5.4.16. Actors and institutions for good health. A) Number of actor types required to implement the 15 health response options described in Section 5.4.3 based on the 21 actor categories listed in Table 5.4.1 and listed on the y-axis of the chart in panel B. B) Number of response options served by each actor category. C) Actors and institutions from the nexus element sectors required to implement the 15 health response options.7 Substantial differences in health status, access to healthcare services and their utilization prevail across regions , and these influence the choice, operationalization, implementation and outcomes of nexus response options. Distributional inequities, across and within countries, and the growing inequality within countries is a global issue . Failure to provide health services and inequitable exposure to environmental health risks such as pollution, are long-standing challenges and linked to systemic issues associated with the existing global economic paradigm that perpetuates poverty and concentrates wealth and power in a minority (see Chapter 6). Thus, while the foundations of health-related response options may be a function of the health sector (i.e., provision of healthcare, addressing health risks, promoting health and well-being), key barriers to implementation are outside the health sector influence. To advance health in the nexus, different aspects of governance, including regulatory, institutional and procedural aspects, can build on some of the pre-existing arrangements. Collaborations between actors in health and the other nexus elements, and the international agreements and frameworks that have resulted from these, constitute important steps in developing shared aims, actions and processes that enable more integrative governance . Health has traditionally engaged with the other nexus elements in individual sector-specific ways, including on food safety and antimicrobial resistance (e.g., Codex Alimentarius, the FAO/WHO Food Standards Programme) and safe water (e.g., WASH, with UN-Water). The inclusion of health as a priority in the Nationally Determined Contributions submitted for the Paris Agreement, in National Adaptation Plans, in the IPCC Sixth Assessment Report, in the development of country specific Health National Adaptation Plans, and in the recognition of the need to reduce greenhouse gas emissions from healthcare demonstrates major progress on climate change and health. The WHO has collaborated with the Convention on Biological Diversity (e.g., to jointly identify public health and biodiversity conservation evidence and priorities. The adoption in 2021 by the Conference of the Parties to the Convention on Biological Diversity of a Global Action Plan for Biodiversity and Health and the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework are key subsequent developments. The Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework targets 9 to 13 address people’s needs through sustainable use and benefit-sharing of biodiversity-related determinants of health. However, it is likely these will predominantly focus on the environment or conservation agenda if they do not directly consider the health priorities of addressing disease burden or health coverage, or harmonization with health concepts and data . This subchapter, and the Nexus Assessment, contribute as a whole to addressing these challenges and progressing on health and biodiversity. The health sector has promoted healthy environments for people, particularly within neighbourhoods and cities and through whole-of-government health approaches for many decades . For example, cities, villages and islands, as well as institutions, have been systematically addressed as ‘health settings’ by holistically engaging government and community actors. These include the long established European Healthy City network and ‘Healthy Islands Pacific,’ which has provided a unifying vision of health, culture and environment across multiple nations (despite reporting only against its health metrics) . These well-established models for enhancing health within a physical landscape have rarely audited their impact on the environment or biodiversity explicitly (or on the health of other species). Health considerations in policy concerning food, water, biodiversity and climate change are made possible through ‘Health in All Policies.’ This is a whole-of-government approach to protecting health across multiple sectors that has been embraced by governments in several countries, including Australia, Denmark, Finland, the Netherlands (Kingdom of the) and the United States of America, who now have considerable experience in multi-sector health policy and decision-making . These health-promoting forms of governance have complemented regulation of environmental health risks (Section 5.4.1) and are adaptive to changing and expanding evidence across the determinants of health, including ecological and cultural determinants. Health promotion also embraces equity, Indigenous voices and knowledges and planetary health . Indigenous health governance is embedded in self-determination, partnerships and relationships, and respect for Indigenous knowledge on health and well-being. Sustainable practices that preserve traditional lands, waterways and natural resources are essential for maintaining the physical, spiritual and cultural identity of these communities. Effective Indigenous health governance prioritizes community engagement, traditional knowledge systems, and the integration of Indigenous values and practices into healthcare delivery. Collaborative decision-making processes that involve elders, healers and community leaders can ensure that health policies and programs are culturally safe , relevant and responsive to the specific needs of each Indigenous group. Institutional recognition through creation of Indigenous controlled health organizations through to international organizations, such as the WHO Global Centre for Traditional Medicine and the United Nations Permanent Forum on Indigenous Issues, has catalysed greater representation for IPLC at all levels of governance and health service planning . Progressing nexus approaches can be considered in terms of scaling up, out, down and deep (Section 4.4). Scaling up refers to impacting laws and policy such has occurred for the Quadripartite that supports H14, the One Health approach (Section 5.4.3.14) that now influences national and regional governance. Scaling out refers to reaching greater numbers of people. The transferability of health nexus response options to other locations and populations requires attention to the unique nature of each group and place, and further data gathering. Even a globally relevant response option such as F11/C15, Sustainable healthy diets (Sections 5.3.3.11 and 5.5.3.15) with H06, Reduced meat overconsumption (Section 5.4.3.6) requires adjusting for nutritional status of populations, women of reproductive age or pastoral communities with traditional high meat diets. Scaling down is also important; for example, scaling down the environmental footprint of the health sector (i.e., Section 5.4.3.3, H03, Net-zero sustainable healthcare) while not reducing its functions is important for future health. Scaling deep refers to change of cultural beliefs or norms to transform narratives and approaches such as reframing health through H15, Integrated health education for health professionals and actors (Section 5.4.3.15) and by engaging with the arts and other cultural world views to build empathy with nature (Figure 5.4.2; Box 5.4.1). 5.4.4.3 Supporting response option implementation through international frameworks Moving from multi-sectoral to integrated governance is facilitated by the SDGs, where SDG3 aims to “ensure healthy lives and promote health for all at all ages” actioned in synergy with other goals, or with them conceptualized as determinants of health (Figure 5.4.17) . However, direct evidence on the benefits of advancing human health through integrative approaches, particularly from low- and middle-income countries, is limited. These countries are falling short of reaching targets on most SDGs including health and meeting each goal and target separately dominates global efforts . Other countries have taken integrative approaches; for example, the Welsh Government’s Well-being of Future Generations (Wales) Act (2015) has, through legislation, required all new policy and development to address the SDGs and to ensure that the needs of the present are met without compromising future generations – thus supporting intergenerational health equity . Measuring health and well-being across multiple domains has replaced gross domestic product as the metric of success in some locations. For example, New Zealand produces a ‘Well-being Budget’ . Such developments create regulatory and legislative frameworks that can transform decision-making processes and accountability. Figure 5.4.17. Health-related linkages are recognized across the Sustainable Development Goals. Source: WHO South-East Asia Regional Office under licence CC BY-NC-SA 4.0. Nevertheless, the governance arrangements for integration across multiple sectors, including health, have few good examples, with the exception of H14, the One Health approach (Section 5.4.3.14). This offers a new governance structure to enable better integration through the Quadripartite Alliance of One Health: WHO, FAO, WOAH and UNEP (Boxes 1.4, 5.4.12 and 5.4.14). The One Health approach has gained significant momentum for its potential to operationalize “living in harmony with nature” including the reduction of disease risks. The Global Action Plan for Biodiversity and Health and the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework promote One Health. In addition, several countries have declarations and commitments towards adoption of a One Health approach. The Quadripartite Alliance for One Health 2022-2026 Joint Plan of Action facilitates the establishment of structures and mechanisms for enabling integrated governance and implementation of One Health. Recent developments on supportive legislation and regulatory mechanisms include ongoing discussions for incorporating a One Health article under an International Pandemic Agreement/Treaty, and the One Health principle being enshrined within the founding regulation of the European Centre for Disease Control and Prevention. The latter mandates the agency to use a One Health approach. Furthermore, the European Parliament in its final report about the COVID-19 pandemic included 13 One Health recommendations, among which is a call for the establishment of a European cross-agency task force dedicated to the One Health approach to advance transdisciplinary research and cross-sectoral scientific advice . Collaborative models under One Health promote a multi-disciplinary approach and address issues of knowledge, skills and competencies including soft skills for successful collaborations . Nexus governance domains for the health sector can be characterised as a fabric where horizontal (informal trust-based relationships) and vertical (formal and official relationships) threads intersect . In planning for health emergencies, for example, it is recognised that climate change, biodiversity loss and land-use change are important drivers, and accordingly effective and enforceable legislation, investment, capacity-building and integration of other sectors and professionals beyond health is required . Processes of governance for health in the nexus include putting in place appropriate mechanisms that allow for risk management and provide for checks and balances, including oversight, availability of finance and other resources, and capacities. Leadership development capacities in One Health approaches, often identified as a key need, covering systems thinking and promoting values and ethics require prioritization through in-service programmes for One Health professionals and university curricula . For example, early analysis suggests that governance mechanisms for the World Bank’s pandemic fund need to enable funding for response financing for outbreaks and link this with health security frameworks and instruments . Box 5.4.14. Committee for Monitoring and Anticipating Health Risks in France In France, at the end of the state of health emergency related to COVID-19 in July 2022, the government established the Committee for Monitoring and Anticipating Health Risks (COVARS) under the auspices of both the health ministry and the higher education and research ministry . The main objectives are (1) to maintain an independent and transparent multidisciplinary scientific advisory committee in charge of providing the government with evidence-based recommendations on health risks; (2) to have an integrated approach to health and therefore include risks related to the environment, the biodiversity crisis and climate change beyond infectious diseases alone; and (3) to anticipate potential health crises in order to promote prevention and preparedness in a context where trade-offs with non-health related issues must be taken into account. COVARS’ engagement letter clearly states that its work must be placed within the One Health conceptual framework. The One Health approach was reflected in its multidisciplinary composition that includes 16 experts with field experience in human, animal and environmental health and three representatives of the civil society (patients’ and citizens’ associations). COVARS operates in close interaction with other ministries (agriculture, ecological transition, foreign affairs and overseas territories) under the Prime Minister Cabinet umbrella, and also with other agencies in the health and the environment fields (such as the French Office for Biodiversity and non-governmental organizations). COVARS recommendations are actively disseminated to the public through press conferences and websites. In alignment with global targets, key governance actions include investments in public sector institutions and capacities of public servants within the health sector to identify trade-offs and synergies, leverage private sector actors and increase funding in order to enable nexus decision-making. Similarly, designing national enabling frameworks that recognise the role of sub-national governments through empowering them and providing them with human and financial resources would strengthen the implementation of the response options. Fiscal decentralization is important, and along with better quality governance in terms of control of corruption, government effectiveness, regulatory quality and accountability, can improve health sector outcomes . Strengthening learning in health systems has been recognized as a key means of advancing health through multisectoral governance and for scaling and addressing broader health goals ." ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.4.4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Nexus governance to support health response options" .

