    ipbes:hasDescription "In many ways, because all people must eat to survive and they make choices on what to eat when, all people everywhere are key actors. Too many people however are challenged in making food choices based on impacts across the nexus with over half of the world’s population struggling in one form or another to access healthy diets. Food producers are particularly important food system actors, though the number of individuals engaged in food production typically becomes smaller as countries develop economically. National actors are particularly important, developing policies, incentives and rules that determine what foods are produced, how, and whom amongst producer groups are rewarded. There is a significant private sector role in food, which spans food production, including inputs and technologies for food production, harvesting, and processing, through to retail and restaurants, including small scale food vendors. A diverse portfolio of food system actors is presented in Table 5.3.1 and are further described in this section. Table 5.3.1. Examples of food system actors involved in the implementation of food response options. See Section 1.3.3 and Table 1.1 for actor category descriptions. Actor category Actors Example Actors Global institutions Multilateral and bilateral agencies and intergovernmental organizations Food an Agricultural Organization of the United Nations (FAO), International Fund for Agricultural Development, World Bank, United Nations Environment Programme, World Food Programme, World Health Organization, United Nations Children’s Fund Regional institutions Regional agencies and organizations Regional organizations such as the European Union, African Union and Association of Southeast Asian Nations National governments, regional and local governments National, sub-national and local government bodies Ministries, departments and agencies with responsibility for processes and actors across the food system; e.g., terrestrial and aquatic food production, food standards and safety Knowledge and educational communities International knowledge generation actors IPBES, Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change, Committee on World Food Security; High Level Panel of Experts on Food Security and Nutrition, United Nations Food Systems Summit, the Convention on Biological Diversity, The Indigenous Partnership on Agrobiodiversity and Food Sovereignty International research and development institutions International Fund for Agricultural Development, CGIAR Research Centres, Association of International Research and Development Centres for Agriculture members, university research centres Capacity building and knowledge sharing Farmer field schools, food dietary guidelines, EAT, Global Forum on Agricultural Research and Innovation, Young Professionals for Agricultural Development, Global Seafood Alliance, Transformative Partnership Platform on Agroecology Educational institutions Continuing education, higher education, schools Civil society organizations Community and consumer-based organizations International, regional and national consumer organizations, peasant movements (e.g., La Via Campesina), small-scale fisheries, mobile people and small-scale farmers movements (e.g., World Forum of Fishers People), World Alliance of Mobile Indigenous People, health-focused organizations; The Indigenous Partnership on Agrobiodiversity and Food Sovereignty Non-governmental organizations National and international non-governmental organizations (e.g., Friends of the Earth International, FoodFirst Information and Action Network, International Collective in Support of Fishworkers) Community-based organizations Social and cultural organizations Women food entrepreneurs and cooperatives, grassroots movements (e.g., Urban-Rural networks: Generating New forms of exchanges between Citizens), consumer organizations Indigenous Peoples and local communities (IPLC) Community support Elders, Indigenous farming, fishery and forestry communities Institutions and systems Local and traditional food systems, Indigenous food systems, state authorities; FAO Globally Important Agricultural Heritage Systems Private sector and business organizations Food production Peasants, farmers, fishers and fish farmers, ranchers and pastoralists, farm workers, gatherers, Indigenous Peoples, and other groups of individuals and communities engaged in food production, private landowners and land-owning companies Companies and corporations Actors along the food value chain, including small, medium and large corporations as suppliers of agricultural technologies (e.g., seed, fertilizers, plant protection, mechanical tools, fuel and technologies), food processors, beverages industry and retailers Post-production Actors involved in storing, distributing, processing, packaging and preparing raw materials and/or food products Commodity traders and marketing Market managers (including short food supply chains), commodity traders, suppliers of marketing innovation and technologies Industry bodies Industry bodies or lobbying groups such as the National Dairy Association (United States) and the National Farmers’ Union (United Kingdom) Non-governmental organizations National and international non-governmental organizations Science-policy interfaces Science-policy dialogue actors IPBES, Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change, Committee on World Food Security, Agroecology Coalition, International Panel of Experts on Sustainable Food Systems Financing institutions Finance and insurance institutions UNCDF, International Fund for Agricultural Development, World Bank and regional development banks (e.g., Asian Development Bank, African Development Bank), agricultural finance and insurance companies Funding entities International and national (e.g., German Agency for International Cooperation), philanthropy and private funding (e.g., Bill and Melinda Gates Foundation, Rockefeller Foundation, McKnight Foundation, Global Alliance for the Future of Food, Agroecology Fund), non-governmental organizations (e.g., One Acre Fund) Media and the arts Public engagement institutions Mass media, museums, restaurants and food establishments, publishing, advertising Government actors and policymakers: This includes officials operating at scales from communities to countries to globally. Beyond agricultural or food safety policies, public health, environmental and financial decision-making play critical roles in the availability, affordability, quality and environmental impact of food. However, food policy is rarely coordinated across ministries or departments, which can result in policies being fragmented or siloed, and can drive antagonisms and incoherencies between policies . Companies and corporations: This includes a diversity of actors along the food value chain, including medium and large corporations as suppliers of agricultural technologies (e.g., seed, fertilizers, plant protection, mechanical tools, fuel and technologies), food processors, beverages industry and retailers. There is a high level of concentration among corporate actors, notably in globalized and industrial food systems where they exert power over other actors , including producers, consumers and even policymakers through lobbying activities. Financial institutions and insurance: National and transnational insurance companies, international financial institutions and national banks, private banks and equity funds, and corporations and civil society organizations providing micro-credits, such as One Acre Fund, play important roles. States also play an important role in connecting private capital to agriculture . The increased financialization of agriculture is related to the increased globalization of agriculture and value chains, and within globalized value chains, the need for capitalization and risk management . Insurance companies can also be considered as food systems actors by giving clear indications of risky practices and technologies . Food producers: This category includes peasants, farmers, fishers and fish farmers, ranchers and pastoralists, farm workers, gatherers, Indigenous Peoples, and other groups of individuals and communities engaged in food production for their own consumption, sharing, exchange, salary or sale. This category embraces a diversity of types of producers, linked to different production systems (e.g., large, medium or small-scale; industrial, organic or agroecological; peasant farming; industrial or artisanal fisheries; Indigenous food systems). Producers can participate in local, regional or global food chains. Their management practices may result in different enablers and barriers to the maintenance or regeneration of environmental functions. In particular, small-scale farms provide environmental, social and economic functions and services and are relevant contributors to global food security with higher levels of biodiversity than larger farms . While the largest 1 per cent farms (>50ha) operate more than 70 per cent of land, very small farms (<2ha) produce 35 per cent of the world’s food from 12 per cent of agricultural land . However, there are regional differences: large farms dominate production in North and South America, and Australia and New Zealand (contributing 75-100 per cent of cereal, livestock, and fruit production). Small farms (?20 ha) produce >75 per cent of most foods in sub-Saharan Africa, southeast Asia, south Asia and China. Very small farms are particularly important in sub-Saharan Africa, southeast Asia and south Asia, where they contribute approximately 30 per cent of most foods . Post-production: This category includes diverse and complex set of actors involved in storing, distributing, processing, packaging and preparing raw materials and/or food products from the producers. Some large companies operate in this activity as part of large corporations, and hence could also be incorporated in the companies and corporations actor category. A large percentage of post-production also takes place in the informal sphere, particularly, but not only, in lower middle-income countries but also in high income countries. Women play a particularly important role and are overrepresented in the informal sector . Consumers: Everyone on the planet - specifically when purchasing or choosing food - is an consumer whose daily choices can have a major collective impact on all nexus elements and their sustainability. This is obviously an incredibly diverse group of actors, with a vast range of interests, capacities and flexibility in their consumption habits, all influenced by a complex interaction of intrinsic and extrinsic factors. One of the unique features of healthy and sustainable food consumption is that consumer choice and individual agency have impact so long as food environments within which actors engage include affordable and available options. Indigenous Peoples and local communities: Indigenous Peoples and local communities (IPLC) contribute to the endurance, provision, maintenance, protection and surveillance of local foods and biodiversity, and collectively contribute to positive regional and global impacts on sustainability . Greater inclusion of ILK-derived perspectives of respect for intrinsic values of nature, socionatural equilibrium and ILK for present and future generations have important potential in shifting values towards a nexus framing of food and fostering food system transformation . This group is highly impacted by current global change, and particularly for land conversion for industrial use, including commercial agriculture . Non-governmental organizations, civil society and grassroot actors: This group of actors operate at a multitude of scales, have diverse goals and aim to shape food systems through campaigning, collaborating and educating. Significant overlap may exist between this and other groups; for example, organized consumer groups or farmer collectives advocating for ethical food production. Researchers, educators, extension services and knowledge brokers: These actors help shape companies, producers’ and consumers’ decisions through information and persuasion. This is a diverse group of actors, both publicly and privately funded, with some operating mainly at local scales, while others collaborate more internationally. There is an increasing emphasis within science but also among other actors and institutions on the need for inter- and trans-disciplinarity. The latter involves the co-creation of knowledge and the development and dissemination of technology with a range of stakeholders – sometimes including IPLC . The global food system is complex and dynamic, and involves a diverse range of actors, with different narratives, values and interests in food, as well as different motives, power and action capacity . Their interactions create and shape a diversity of local food systems . Acknowledging these interactions across the diversity of actors, objectives and interests is critical for understanding the response options available and who benefits from them, and for developing effective policymaking for the food system . Different narratives on food shape critical perspectives and assumptions of actors, although the boundaries between narratives and the different categories of actors that adopt them are blurred (Table 5.3.1). Food as a commodity highlights food as a tradable good with a production focus. This narrative is linked to the industrial food system and considered to be the dominant discourse regarding the valuation of food TEMP and the current configuration of agri-food networks. Food as a human right focuses on the social dimensions of food aimed at providing access to healthy, nutritious food but also to the positive cultural values associated with food, such as identity, taste and pleasure. This framing places emphasis on access to means of production, sustainability and no human exploitation, with the state as the main guarantor of the right . Food as a common good emphasises sustainability. Food is framed as having multiple dimensions, both social and environmental, with all being equally and properly valued and requiring different governance structures and institutions that promote food democracy and strong participation of citizen-consumers through social organizations, social movements and IPLC . Food as identity and culture puts the focus on the territorial diversity in food cultures, farming styles, ecosystems, climates and soils associated with the production and consumption of food . Regionalization is promoted as a reaction to globalisation of food, with production re-embedded into local contexts and associated with rurality and socially-constructed, value-laden concepts of tradition, authenticity and health . Finally, food as human’s closest link to nature highlights the complex relationship between humans and nature and considers that the act of eating provides an ideal object to reflect and reconsider the human-nature relationship and to establish more sustainable ways of living . Table 5.3.2. Framings of food and the main associated actors, typologies of food systems, agri-food networks configurations and governance approaches. The table includes examples of initiatives and norms supporting the governance approaches. For analytical purposes the table shows the main actors and configurations associated, but not all potential configurations. *Agri-food network configuration is based on T. G. Williams . **Self-consumption is not a category included in T. G. Williams , but has been incorporated based on the expert assessment of the authors of this chapter. Abbreviations: UNDRIP – United Nations Declaration on the Rights of Indigenous Peoples; UNDROP – United Nations Declaration of the Right of Peasants and Other People Working in Rural Areas; FAO GIAHS – Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations Globally Important Agricultural Heritage Systems. Framing of food as: Actors involved Typology of food system associated / Scale Agri-food networks configuration* Governance approach and initiatives and norms supporting Commodity * Global and regional institutions (World Trade Organization, European Union) * Governments (national) * Corporations (input suppliers, retailers, food processors, beverages), medium & large-scale farmers * Large cooperatives, farm workers * Universities and research centres * Financing institutions (World Bank) * Industrial/conventional food systems * Global, regional & national scales * Farmer dependence, conventional value chain control * Innovative conventional value chain * High power concentration in few actors * Siloed approach * Private food governance * European Union Common Agricultural Policy, United States Farm Bill Human right * Global and regional institutions (Committee on World Food Security) * Governments (national, regional, local) * Peasants, small-scale farmers & fisheries, farm workers (no exploitation) * Indigenous Peoples and local communities, civil society organizations, non-governmental organization (development, environmental) * Universities & research centres * Local food systems * National & local scale * State-supported alternative value chains * Civil society mobilisation * State as main guarantor (accountability, participation, implementation) * Voluntary guidelines for the Right to Adequate Food ; * Food sovereignty Common good * Global and regional institutions * Governments (national, regional, local), * Peasants, small-scale farmers & fisheries, Indigenous Peoples and local communities, civil society organizations * Universities & research centres * Local food systems * Local scale * Consumer-centric alternative value chain * Civil society mobilisation, * Farmer-consumer relations * Decentralised & polycentric governance * Community self-governance * Community seed banks; food sovereignty * Community supported agriculture * Participatory guarantee systems Identity & culture * Global and regional institutions * Governments (national, regional, local) * Peasants & peasant’s movements, medium and small-scale farmers, small-scale fisheries, Indigenous Peoples and local communities, civil society organizations * Indigenous food systems, local & traditional food systems * National & local scale * Consumer-centric alternative value chain * Farmer-consumer relations * Polycentric governance * FAO GIAHS; UNESCO Intangible Cultural Heritage; Protected Geographical Indication Humanity’s closest link to nature * Indigenous Peoples and local communities, civil society organizations * Indigenous food systems * Local & traditional food systems * Local scale * Self-consumption** * Community self-governance * Autonomous governance ; * Food provenance & links to nature (e.g., animism)" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.3.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Key actors and institutions" .

