    ipbes:hasDescription "The health sector has an important role in the nexus of biodiversity, food, water and climate change. It has a potentially significant impact because of its economic size and influence: globally, national health sector expenditure as a proportion of gross domestic product was 2-19 per cent (mean 11 per cent) in 2020 . Gains in human health, population size and longevity are largely built on materially improved lives and gains in food production that have displaced and degraded nature and contributed to climate change . In parallel, biomedical advancements have overcome many devastating diseases and extended lives, but resource-intense healthcare has a significant environmental impact that comes at a cost to equitable, future health . For example, the health sector contributes 5.2 per cent of greenhouse gas emissions globally . Chapter 2 (sections 2.5.3 and 2.6) reviewed past trends and the current status of interactions between health and the other nexus elements of biodiversity, water, food and climate change. Environmental risks, such as pollution, are responsible for at least 24 per cent of global deaths (and 28 per cent of deaths among children under five) . The heath impact of climate change is increasing, directly through heat and extreme weather events and through its impacts on food, water and biodiversity, including disease-causing organisms . The cumulative effect of environmental exposures across a lifetime (the ‘exposome’) are changing susceptibility to disease at a genetic, cellular and population level . Lack of effective environmental regulation and poor access to food, water and appropriate healthcare compounds poor health outcomes in vulnerable populations. For example, age-standardized Disability Adjusted Life Year rates for communicable, maternal, neonatal and nutritional disease are 2,147 per cent higher for the poorest billion compared to higher-income populations, as reported in 2017 Global Burden of Disease data . This subchapter follows the World Health Organization (WHO) definition (see Chapter 1, Box 1.1) of health referring to that of humans unless otherwise stated. Accordingly, health is an important indicator of quality of life and societal well-being; it facilitates and is a consequence of human development, empowerment, participation and self-efficacy . Neither disease, nor mental, physical and social well-being are distributed evenly across the global population or within populations . Redressing health inequity is dependent on broader social justice and governance challenges {4.2.5.2}. The right to enjoy the highest attainable standard of health acknowledges timely and appropriate healthcare and attention to the underlying determinants of health. These include an adequate supply of safe food, nutrition and housing; access to safe and potable water and adequate sanitation; healthy occupational and environmental conditions; and access to health-related education and information, including on sexual and reproductive health . Furthermore, in July 2022, the right to a clean, healthy and sustainable environment has also been recognized as a human right by the UN General Assembly . In this context, all nexus elements are recognized as rights and are essential determinants of health. The COVID-19 pandemic has demonstrated that investments in public (and community) health and well-being, and commitment to health equity (see Section 4.2.3.2), result in collective resilience and better health more cost effectively across populations . It also raised public awareness of disease risks arising from pressure on biodiversity and food systems and of global interlinkages. As environmental and climate change challenges become health priorities in the 21st century there is a need for, and greater interest in, integrated approaches mindful of planetary boundaries, trade-offs and different value systems. Countries such as Costa Rica, Cuba and Sri Lanka and the Indian state of Kerala have attained high levels of health and longevity without the ecological footprint of high-income countries . Low- to middle-income country attainments come from investment in community development and a comprehensive approach to primary healthcare that is focused on prevention . This integrates healthcare within empowered communities, with investment in social determinants such as education, housing, social inclusion and environmental (including the nexus elements) determinants of health . However, universal health coverage and access to the determinants of health represented by the nexus are embedded in the policy targets of the SDGs and the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework, ideally to be integrated and achieved globally . Although modern medicine and healthcare dominate much of the global discourse, health is valued in different ways by different cultures supported by different belief systems (see Box 5.4.1). A culturally inclusive valuation of health recognizes that for many this means the health of oneself is embedded in the health of one’s community, culture and the natural and spiritual environment . For many Indigenous cultures around the world, healthcare practices are inconceivable as something separate from relationships with nature: the body itself is nature, and relationships between humans and biodiversity implicitly include some degree of reciprocity . Traditional and complementary medicines usage is reported by 88 per cent of WHO member states both for cultural preference and because of restricted access to modern healthcare. Integration of modern and traditional healthcare is of importance to most of the global population and has important synergies with the other nexus elements. Good nutrition is fundamental to health (see Sections 2.5.2, 2.5.3 and 5.3.1). Furthermore, for many IPLC the distinction between nutrition and medicine is less apparent, including through the social, cultural and spiritual connection to Indigenous food systems that contribute to well-being (Box 5.4.2). Indigenous diets include functional food items which promote or protect health in addition to nutrition, and these may be included routinely in meals or in response to specific need. These may have multiple uses. For example, dietary items of the pastoral communities of the Himalayas are associated with diverse ethno-medicinal, nutritional and cultural values ; the wild mahua flowers (Madhuca longifolia var. latifolia) are used in traditional food and beverages, medicine (for headaches and heart, skin and eye diseases) and as fuel by tribal groups in Jharkhand state, in India, . Diverse foods within Indigenous diets contribute important micronutrients and may be more nutritious than their non-traditional counterparts . Moreover, traditional diets are local, ecologically adapted and frequently support health and sustainability better than alternative, imported and ultra-processed food. The latter are increasingly responsible for high rates of diet-related chronic disease globally, including for Indigenous Peoples and local communities (IPLC) .Futher, traditional diets and their source plants and animals are vulnerable to climate change , biodiversity loss , intersectionality and loss of Indigenous and local knowledge (ILK) . ILK and biodiversity are thus issues of global health concern (see also Sections 5.4.3.2 and 5.4.3.4). There are also potential risks: disease transmission from wild animals, hunted or farmed for medicine or food, is a significant concern . The importance of different world views to guide empathy and curtail destructive relationships with nature, particularly embedded in dominant Western culture, is recognized in transformative education curricula and training for health professionals. One Health, Planetary Health, and other integrated approaches are increasingly converging to address the concern that the foundations of human health and that of other species are threatened . As a result, an exclusively anthropocentric focus on health is broadening. Achieving health requires addressing the impact of people on the health of the planet and ultimately sustainably balancing and optimizing the health of people, plants, animals and ecosystems (see also Chapter 1, Box 1.4). However, adopting new nexus approaches or extending existing approaches to achieve nexus-wide benefits challenges the status quo, requires a range of actors (Section 5.4.2), new forms of governance and faces a range of enablers and barriers (Section 5.4.4). Changing how health is conceptualized, valued and achieved in a way that is not only equitable but recognizes that the conditions for life are dependent on nature is a prerequisite of the transformative change required for moving toward sustainable and just futures. Education, and strengthening engagement with diverse cultural perspectives (Boxes 5.4.1 and 5.4.2) and through arts-science communication (Figure 5.4.2) are critical enablers and important catalysts of this transformation. So, too, is identifying and addressing knowledge gaps (Section 5.4.5). This subchapter reviews and assesses evidence for response options that can be actioned by the health sector and partners to achieve best outcomes for health and other nexus elements (Section 5.4.3). In doing this, the diversity of challenges faced by different populations, regions and scales are acknowledged. End-users need to determine what response options presented here would address health issues and nexus interactions that are relevant to their specific context. Box 5.4.1. ?rram?t - an Indigenous holistic concept of health and well-being The Tuareg people are pastoralists whose ancestral land is in the arid Sahara and Sahel region in Algeria, Burkina Faso, Libya, Mali and Niger. ?rram?t is a concept in Tamasheq, the Indigenous language spoken by the Tuareg people, that describes a state of well-being shared by the environment, animals and humans?. It is written in Tifinagh, the Tuareg alphabet, as ??????? and teaches that if the lands (water, fish, animals, plants) are not healthy, then the people are not healthy. The ?rram?t concept illustrates the holistic approach to health of the Tuaregs through several elements in their traditional livelihood. For example, for the Tuareg some ihenzuzagh (habitat, land, environment) have the ?rram?t or contain the ?rram?t, which means that it is free of any echar (wrong) or elaroret (something that disturbs). Also, it means that humans can live in harmony among themselves as well as with other beings and nature, as this land gives enough resources (water, grass, beautiful landscape) to feed people, their livestock and other beings, consequently maintaining their physical, mental and spiritual health and well-being . This conception of interdependence between the health of all is captured by this verse of a Tuareg song: “I am ill. There is no medicine. My medicine is the scent of a riding camel.” . Strengthening health and well-being through indigenous-led conservation and sustainable relationships with biodiversity is the basis of the ?rram?t project . Box 5.4.2. Indigenous food systems and health: Inuit case study2 Indigenous food systems food supports health and well-being in multiple ways as part of an active and social lifestyle that may include hunting, fishing, gathering or farming and animal husbandry, while maintaining traditional and spiritual practices. During the dialogue workshops on Indigenous and local knowledge (ILK) for the Nexus Assessment, many participants have illustrated these perspectives. One participant explained: “Lake fish are essential for community health, and bile in the fish clears the human body of toxins. Fermented fish is equivalent to a box of vitamins. If people are sick, they go to the forest to be healed. When children were at boarding schools, they were given strong medicines but were not given good food, and they became sick” Inuit people live in the Circumpolar Arctic of Canada, Greenland, United States of America and the Russian Federation. Their food system is central to their nutrition but also to maintain their culture and relationship with biodiversity and the environment. “For Inuit, achieving food and nutrition security is about more than ensuring people are free from hunger, it is about the right to harvest and pursue a traditional subsistence way of life. In other words, Inuit view food security as a right that encompasses the cultural and environmental aspects of their lives.” Harvested foods are an important determinant of Inuit physical and mental health and well-being . A study involving Inuit traditional food samples collected across the Canadian Arctic demonstrated that their food system contains a variety of sources of vitamin C. The sources included raw beluga and narwhal skin (mattak), cisco roe and mountain sorrel, boiled beluga mattak, aged narwhal mattak, ringed seal and caribou liver, blueberries and kelp (Fediuk, 2002). Furthermore, another study reveals that when Indigenous traditional foods (meats or fish) are eaten, percentage daily intakes of energy as protein and many micronutrients (e.g., vitamin D, vitamin E, riboflavin, vitamin B-6, iron, zinc, copper, magnesium, manganese, phosphorus, potassium, and selenium) are higher . In addition, health surveys of an Inuit population from the Baffin region demonstrated that dietary quality improves when traditional food is consumed. In contrast, where less traditional food is available, the daily intakes are covered by high energy, sugar and saturated fats, leading to overweight and increased risk of non-communicable diseases such as type 2 diabetes . For Indigenous Peoples, particularly those living in the Arctic, culture and traditional foods are under threat as the effects of climate change increase . Climate change mitigation thus can support the Inuit people and their allies in their food sovereignty which also includes the valorization of their food systems for cultural identity and continuity, health and well-being. The Centre for Indigenous Peoples’ Nutrition and Environment at the McGill University is one of the few centres co-led by Indigenous Peoples, which is testing in its labs the qualitative and quantitative values of Indigenous foods. Figure 5.4.1. Inuk hunter who has just caught an Arctic char (a staple across Inuit Nunavik, now impacted by pollution and climate change). Johnny Airo ?? ??? (c. 1984), Kangirsuk, Nunavik. Permission granted from Inuit Circumpolar Council Canada to use photo. Figure 5.4.2. Health in the nexus. “We are made of the same structures, made of the same patterns” (Artist: Lina Kusaite under licence CC BY-NC-SA 4.0)." ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.4.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Role of health within the nexus" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/NXS24-3.5> a ipbes:SubChapter ;
