    ipbes:Report <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/NXS24> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "A multi-tiered approach to identifying and categorizing nexus response options for water was undertaken. This involved reviewing and extracting evidence from a wide range of policy documents to create an initial list of 110 response options, matching them to water challenges (Table 5.2.2) and identifying the number of nexus elements considered and the types and roles of actors involved in each response option .3 Thirty-two of the 110 response options only addressed the water element of the nexus, another 39 addressed water and a second nexus element, and a final set of 39 addressed water and two or more nexus elements (it is highly likely that the water element was interconnected to the other nexus elements as well due to sociopolitical complexity, and because the water element is seldom an end in itself). These results show that the current policy landscape for water is somewhat integrated and considers interventions that take account of interactions across nexus elements. Furthermore, some of the response options in current policy documents exemplified a wide variety of ways in which water quality, quantity, culture, governance, equity and supply challenges in turn affect biodiversity, food, health and climate change. For example, challenges to water systems imposed by climate change and ecosystems are (to date) mostly addressed using technological response options, e.g., development of water infrastructure, which often consider water and a second nexus element. Table 5.2.2. Water challenges used to group the initial 110 water response options. Water challenge Examples Climate change Floods, droughts, salinization, sea level rise, cryosphere changes Ecosystems Reduced water for ecosystems, wetlands degradation, natural regime fragmentation, species migration, invasive species Marine management Management of the high seas and oceans Water quality Pollution, nutrients, arsenic Water quantity Groundwater decline, water stress Water supply Water supply, sanitation and health (including water as a human right, implications of migration, conflicts) Culture ILK, IPLC, social knowledge on water, communication, behaviours Equity Access, fairness, gender, vulnerability Governance River basin management, monitoring, capacities, conflict resolution, networks and partnerships, transboundary cooperation Cross-cutting Linked to multiple water challenges Technical response options are used most frequently to respond to challenges related to water quantity and quality, but they tend to either address only water quantity or quality and infrequently at most one other nexus element. In addition, water supply is mostly addressed by governance actions with varying levels of integration with other nexus elements. Nonetheless, there is a vast set of response options that address social, behavioural and cognitive water challenges that cut across multiple nexus elements. Finally, cross-cutting challenges are naturally more nexus-inspired. In summary, out of the 110 response options identified from policy documents, 40 per cent respond to technological/scientific water challenges, another 20 per cent to governance challenges, followed by smaller numbers of response options addressing knowledge and cognitive challenges as well as economic mechanisms. Response options for legal and institutional challenges and social and behavioural processes are the least numerous and least developed (Figure 5.2.2). Figure 5.2.2. Summary of the 110 response options identified from existing policy documents. Response options are presented in terms of their general category of water challenge (climate, ecosystems, marine, quality, quantity, supply, culture, equity, governance and cross-cutting), the nexus challenge they address (financial (dark blue), complexity, values, scaling and governance (lighter blue) (see Section 1.1.2) and their nexus order (first order are response options that address only water, second order are response options that address water and a second nexus element, and third order are response options that address water and another two or more nexus elements). The 110 response options were clustered into 15 response option groups (Table 5.2.3; Sections 5.2.3.1 to 5.2.3.15), based on the chapter author’s expert opinion on similarities among them.4 The clusters cover broadly defined response options related to water quality and quantity improvement (W03, Dam operation (Section 5.2.3.3); W04, Efficient water use in agriculture (Section 5.2.3.4)); wise use of groundwater and surface water, including reductions in consumption (W09, Groundwater governance (Section 5.2.3.9); W10, Finance for water infrastructure (Section 5.2.3.10)); provision of clean water in urban areas or better sanitation or reduction in pollution from agriculture and other sources (W04 Efficient water use in agriculture (Section 5.2.3.4); W12, Manage wastewater (Section 5.2.3.12); W13, Water-sensitive urban infrastructure (Section 5.2.3.13)); regulatory options through laws and policies (W03, Dam operation (Section 5.2.3.3); W07, Rights of nature (Section 5.2.3.7)); or financial instruments (W10, Finance for water infrastructure (Section 5.2.3.10)). Integrated water resource management was also captured in clusters related to ensuring water quality and quantity (W02, Integrated water infrastructure (Section 5.2.3.2)), and ensuring all voices are heard so that there is an equitable distribution of water (W14, Addressing gendered burdens of water collection (Section 5.2.3.14)). Other clusters include the conservation of biodiversity (W05, Sustainable inland fisheries (Section 5.2.3.5); W11, Manage alien species (section 5.2.3.11) and the use of scientific knowledge to inform other options, but also the need to engage communities and further educate stakeholders on these options (W01, Inclusive water education (Section 5.2.3.1); W08, Transboundary water cooperation (Section 5.2.3.8); W15, Community water management (Section 5.2.3.15). Table 5.2.3. Brief descriptions of the 15 water response options assessed. Water response option Brief description of response option Section W01 Inclusive water education Formal and informal environmental education efforts that incorporate multiple knowledge systems and values with the goals of increasing knowledge about and awareness of water resources and empowering people to protect and conserve those resources. 5.2.3.1 W02 Integrated water infrastructure Multi-actor, multisectoral, basin-level approaches to managing infrastructure (built and natural) for water storage that balance societal needs for water with conserving and sustaining ecosystems and water resources. 5.2.3.2 W03 Dam operation Managing water releases from dams to enable flow regimes that support biodiversity, connectivity of floodplains and ecological resilience; this response option includes dam removal. 5.2.3.3 W04 Efficient water use in agriculture Planting drought-resistant crops to enable efficient use of water in crop production systems and optimizing fertilizer use to improve water quality by reducing chemicals in agricultural run-off. 5.2.3.4 W05 Sustainable inland fisheries Assessing inland fisheries to enhance knowledge generation in support of sustainable fisheries management, which in turn benefits freshwater biodiversity, ecosystem functions and resilience, and human health and well-being. 5.2.3.5 W06 Inclusive water management An approach to water management that is inclusive of genders, cultures and world views and enables and particularly empowers women to engage in decision-making processes that impact access to and management of water resources. 5.2.3.6 W07 Rights of nature A legal framework recognizing ecosystems and species as rights bearers subject to legal protection; the framework reflects Indigenous Peoples’ cosmovisions, which view humans and nature as deeply interconnected, with harm to one causing harm to the other. 5.2.3.7 W08 Transboundary water cooperation Cooperative action to assist with the management of transboundary rivers, lakes and aquifer systems to ensure sustainable, equitable use of transboundary water resources and shared costs and benefits. 5.2.3.8 W09 Groundwater governance A decentralized governance process to address groundwater depletion, pollution and salinization and improve the effectiveness of groundwater management; the process includes knowledge generation, access to information, policy, planning and finance. 5.2.3.9 W10 Finance for water infrastructure A cluster of options that includes: (a) water accounting; (b) mobilizing financial resources; (c) finance systems with environmental and social safeguards; (d) enabling conditions for financial viability and creditworthiness of the water sector; and (e) climate action. 5.2.3.10 W11 Manage alien species Managing aquatic invasive alien species to conserve freshwater biodiversity, primarily through reducing invasion pathways. 5.2.3.11 W12 Manage wastewater Managing wastewater and implementing sustainable sanitation practices to reduce the volume of wastewater generated, prevent and reduce water contamination, and recover and reuse components of wastewater (e.g., nutrients). 5.2.3.12 W13 Water-sensitive urban infrastructure A range of options for designing urban infrastructure based on the principles of water-sensitive urban design to conserve water, protect biodiversity and mitigate water-related risks such as pollution, flooding and water scarcity. 5.2.3.13 W14 Address gendered burdens of water collection Technological tools, educational strategies and economic support for women responsible for collecting and carrying water to alleviate the time burdens, negative health impacts and safety concerns associated with this responsibility and improve equitable water access. 5.2.3.14 W15 Community water management Collective action at the local level to manage common-pool water resources, with social capital and social knowledge underpinning solutions to address local water challenges and enable sustainable, equitable and just water stewardship. 5.2.3.15 The 15 response option groups have different numbers of the initial 110 response options within them, with policy and governance having the greatest number of options (probably because these were retrieved primarily from policy documents), while others were covered to a lesser extent in the policy documents reviewed, e.g., groundwater. A response option focused on nature-based solutions was not included because many of the 15 response options could have been categorized as such. The most diverse clusters of response options correspond to policy/governance, education and awareness, and economic and financial mechanisms, while groundwater, water supply and nutrients, and sustainable water consumption show the least diversity and are specific to a certain problem (groundwater and sustainable consumption for quantity, water supply and nutrients for climate change). Furthermore, not all response options clusters equally embrace an explicit nexus approach, with agriculture, water supply and nutrients, and water supply and sanitation addressing either water alone or including only one other nexus element. ILK components complement the assessment by combining different cosmovisions (Box 5.2.1) and systems of values. Box 5.2.1. Water as food for Mapuche communities5 Several IPLC groups share a cultural cosmovision where water is considered as food and is a key instrument to produce more food. For example, the Mapuche people, who live in Patagonia, Argentina and Chile, reflect this perspective through different approaches . The negative impact of water uses on the river’s ecological flow, which has been linked to decreased biodiversity , is not only reflected in physical damage to the body of water, but also on the life generated around it. Water bodies are social and meeting spaces used by the people who live on the banks, a space where they go to fetch water to drink, bathe, wash clothes and water animals. In this sense, the river is a community space for families, friends and neighbours. These communities consider that health problems associated with water quality can only be recognized when their significance in cultural terms has been understood , so in these cases they can relate natural, cultural and health knowledge in a holistic way. These communities consider that “The fish they caught from the river is a source of food. It was like to live on what was taken from the river, not so much as to take out and sell, or what sold was the minimum to buy the salt or sugar, but it was more for their consumption”. Also, that “Many came to fish in the river, they brought their food, their salmon.” . The Mapuche people usually implement strategies to increase soil fertility (i.e., types of organic/inorganic fertilizers, methods of use) and improve water management for crops. They use surface water by using small constructions and rudimentary devices called ‘bocatomas’ to capture and divert water to improve water irrigation. These constructions are made with stones, sandbags, animal hides and logs, among other materials, and then drive the water through lateral channels which are helped by extraction pumps (i.e., supplied with fuel). About 92 per cent of local inhabitants mainly used superficial and subterranean sources of water consumption. They are aware of decreases in water quality but gave importance to the organoleptic characteristics such as texture, color, sound and temperature . The 15 response options correspond to the fluxes within the water cycle. Response options can operate at three levels. The first set acts more broadly than the water cycle (e.g., integrated water infrastructure) as these have a broader influence on water availability and fluxes. The second set operates at specific locations of the water cycle (e.g., freshwater, urban and agricultural areas, groundwater management) and the third set is associated within the human realm (e.g., equitable water education, equitable participatory processes, capacity-building for women, economics). Critical aspects of each response option are summarized in this section based on the synthesis of evidence and examples of implementation (Figures 5.2.3, 5.2.4, 5.2.5). Each response option has been analysed and scored6 based on assessed evidence against 10 criteria (see Chapter 5.0, Table 5.0.1) related to its potential to produce benefits across multiple elements of the nexus, its feasibility and breadth of applicability, its impact on equity and its potential to advance the goals of existing global policy frameworks. Individual response options are described in Sections 5.2.3.1 to 5.2.3.15. From the 15 response options assessed, five focal response options were selected for more detailed consideration: W11, Manage alien species (Section 5.2.3.11); W12, Manage wastewater (Section 5.2.3.12); W13, Water-sensitive urban infrastructure (Section 5.2.3.13); W14, Address gendered burdens of water collection (Section 5.2.3.14) and W15, Community water management (Section 5.2.3.15). These were selected to demonstrate different aspects of response option implementation. They are not intended to represent priorities or recommendations. Rather, they are chosen to illustrate the range of issues and actors involved in response options and provide real-world examples of response options in action. Figure 5.2.3. Impacts of water response options on nexus elements. Colours indicate positive impacts (blue; i.e., synergies) and negative impacts (red; i.e., trade-offs). Intensity of colour represents the magnitude of impact (light = small; medium = medium impact; dark = large;). Grey colouring indicates a score of 0. NA indicates not applicable, NE indicates no evidence, and INC indicates the evidence is inconclusive. Figure 5.2.4. Water response options within the context of social, economic and political dimensions, international frameworks and transformative change potential. Colours indicate positive impacts (blue; i.e., synergies) and negative impacts (red; i.e., trade-offs). Intensity of colour represents the magnitude of impact (light = small; medium = medium impact; dark = large;). Grey colouring indicates a score of 0. Where impacts differ depending on context, impact ranges are shown by multiple colours within a cell. NA indicates not applicable, NE indicates no evidence, and INC indicates the evidence is inconclusive. Figure 5.2.5. Temporal and spatial scales and technical feasibility of the water response options. Ranges shown temporal and spatial scales and feasibility indicated by Technology Readiness Level.7 5.2.3.1 Inclusive water education (W01) Formal and informal environmental education is a preventive policy and management instrument which contributes to promoting behaviours and recognizing cosmovision and values related to water protection using a holistic approach, including spiritual and traditional approaches and customary governance systems . This can also include consideration of biodiversity. Water education, with a gender perspective and including traditional knowledge of IPLC in schools (e.g., using water use and footprint calculations) (Box 5.2.3), is a significant first step towards building water consciousness and effecting behaviour change . In cases where this is not yet achieved, it is important to progress the generation of knowledge so that communities feel challenged and motivated to assume autonomy, responsibility and choice, which enables a transformation of water culture . It is difficult to measure the impact of environmental water education in connection with the nexus elements, except for health (Chapter 5.4). Some studies revealed that higher water knowledge was associated with higher education levels, confirming its importance and allowing identification of potential subgroups that may benefit from targeted support of water management initiatives . The recognition of the need for education programs is widespread in the different global policy frameworks, e.g., explicitly mentioned in Targets 16 and 21 of the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework (Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework), SDG6 (clean water and sanitation), SDG4 (ensure inclusive and equitable quality education and promote lifelong learning opportunities for all) and SDG5 (gender equality) to have a holistic education . Such awareness-raising activities are important parts of environmental education, for example, to increase knowledge and values promoting the protection and conservation of the environment, e.g., by protecting water sources from contamination and more generally to inform and empower people to help protect water . 5.2.3.2 Integrated water infrastructure (W02) Integrated water infrastructure includes integrated approaches to the management of water storage infrastructure for multiple uses, including storage in natural ecosystems, built infrastructure and combinations of these, and managing these infrastructures in ways that recognize co-dependencies towards the delivery of a targeted societal service (such as meeting water supply needs) and sustaining ecosystems . The response option envisages the management of water resources at a basin level, rather than political divisions (Figure 5.2.5) which is also the intent of the European Union Water Framework Directive and is applicable globally. Implementing this response option requires water actors to work together with actors in biodiversity (e.g., national park and wetland managers) and climate change (e.g., climate change adaptation planners) to enable (a) a more strategic focus on water storage; (b) measuring and modelling storage in an integrated way; (c) engaging stakeholders to define the storage needed and the trade-offs; and (d) using an integrated planning methodology to identify and prioritize investments in natural and built water storage . Integrated water infrastructure has well-established benefits for water for both humans and ecosystems (Figure 5.2.3). Providing water for food is also one of the core outcomes of this response option, for example, in enhancing water use efficiency for food security and reducing risks of water-related disasters . Conceptualizations such as green and blue water flows and the water footprint have deepened the water for food discourse by bringing in a holistic understanding of the impact of food on water resources. The ecosystem approach can enable better consideration of biodiversity (such as the role of native wetland vegetation in buffering water regimes in certain situations), building upon the co-production of knowledge and modelling which draws from multiple sources, including ILK (such as incorporating ecological and evolutionary understanding in ecosystem management) . Evidence of the influence of this response option on health is inconclusive but some benefits have been shown on water, sanitation and hygiene, water for ecosystems and other dimensions . Pathways connecting the response option to climate change mitigation are linked with the coupling of water and carbon cycles and the role of water management in sustaining carbon stores . While this response option can benefit food production and ecological function, it has high economic costs (Figure 5.2.4). The integrated approach supports a wide range of SDGs beyond water (SDG6), including those related to health (SDG3), agriculture (SDG2), poverty (SDG1), energy (SDG7), climate change (SDG13), biodiversity (SDGs 14 and 15), gender (SDG5) and peace (SDG16) (UNEP, 2021). An example of the use of integrated storage approaches is that of the City of Windhoek, Namibia, which increased storage (the three-dam system) by managing surface and groundwater jointly, investing in direct potable reuse, implementing water demand management and conservation measures, and also used the Windhoek Aquifer as a “water bank” . 5.2.3.3 Dam operation (W03) Allowing water releases from dams that mimic a natural flow regime is well established to maintain or increase biodiversity, connectivity of floodplains, and ecological resilience , but ecological responses are often context and scale-dependent. Dam removal, which is growing in social acceptability, to help restore natural flows is an increasingly popular management action to help maintain or restore native biodiversity. However, flows can also balance biodiversity needs with other uses . Dam water releases can affect a larger geographic scale when dams are operated together (Figure 5.2.5) and can balance multiple needs that link environmental flows with migration, trade, tourism and disease spread . Dam operations directly affect the delivery of water quantity and quality for both people and ecosystems. However, multiple demands on water resources, such as hydropower, irrigation, drinking water and biodiversity, are often conflicting (Figure 5.2.3). For example, implementing environmental flows from dams could reduce the potential for irrigated agriculture to contribute to global food production by reducing water withdrawals and irrigated areas . However, flow releases can improve fisheries and livelihoods and human well-being . Dam operations may also accommodate traditional fishing and cultivation practices without substantial trade-offs on food security and hydropower . Dam water releases can have negative impacts on human health (Figure 5.2.3) through increased access and proximity to water which can increase vector-borne and other diseases and pollute drinking water for downstream villages . However, modifying dam releases may help with malaria control with minimal trade-offs for other uses . Adjusting dam releases shows potential economic benefits from electricity and agricultural production, and flood damage control . Environmental flows have societal support, align with political priorities and are incorporated into water basin management plans or assessment strategies (Figure 5.2.4) . They can consider different social and cultural views, but financial values such as hydropower and fisheries harvests are given higher value and could lead to inequities in the implementation of flows. Moreover, dam operations such as the decision to hold or release water downstream can provide water for food production, healthy lives and sanitation, but holding water released downstream may also increase the burden of disease . Economic assessments of environmental flow policies show the potential effectiveness of water trading as the value of instream flows for environmental reasons was similar to or greater than agricultural uses, however, successful approaches require a basin-wide viewpoint and multi-stakeholder dialogues . 5.2.3.4 Efficient water use in agriculture (W04) Efficient water use in agriculture (both water quantity and quality) can be implemented by planting drought resistant crops, optimizing fertilizer use, and practices such as conservation agriculture and can be used to alleviate water scarcity and food insecurity and improve water quality, human health and biodiversity . There is well established evidence to show that optimizing fertilizer application reduces chemicals in water and thus increases water quality, and the respective economic costs and benefits have been extensively studied . The benefits of implementation of this response option vary locally , with the most pressing areas being water-scarce regions (Vishwanathan, 2021), where 25 per cent of the global population lives (WWAP, 2015). Efficient water use in crop production, which can be achieved through drought-resistant crops, is also one of the 10 science-based policy recommendations for agriculture (see Section 5.3.3.4). However, the temporal scale is short because crops are grown annually and fertilizer is often applied annually or more frequently (Figure 5.2.5). In contrast, long-term fertilizer application may have effects on human health that are not observed over shorter time periods . Implementing this response option benefits water for both humans and ecosystems (Figure 5.2.3) , as it reduces competition for freshwater supplies and improves water quality (Darch, 2014). Drought resistant crops can decrease farmers’ risks and increase crop yields and farm income , while also providing food security by using underutilized crop species . There are also health benefits from naturally drought resistant plants in their contribution to pharmaceutical compounds , and it is possible that the drought resistance as well as the quantity and quality of such compounds can be enhanced with plant growth-promoting rhizobacteria , or through fertilizer reduction that may reduce cancer and non-cancer disease risk by up to 21 per cent from changes in micronutrient (e.g., lead, zinc, copper) uptake . The use of drought resistant crops can improve land management (e.g., when aligned with conservation agriculture and land tenure rights . Drought resistant crops can also reduce emissions of methane, and implementing optimal fertilizer application rates can reduce greenhouse gas emissions by up to 20 per cent by 2050 . The use of inorganic nitrogen fertilizer can disproportionally affect uneducated and poor women and children through increased morbidity and mortality by absorbing toxins from rice paddies where the fertilizer is used , and lack of a free market means some countries may impose tariffs that create economic inequities . Reducing the impacts of agriculture on water quality and quantity can free up water to be used for other uses and is also more cost-effective as fertilizer application rates are reduced to minimize runoff of fertilizers into water sources. 5.2.3.5 Sustainable inland fisheries (W05) Increasing knowledge to improve inland fisheries assessments and management is a response option that can be applied globally on all managed inland waters (Figure 5.2.5) . It also furthers equity initiatives and aligns with international framework targets through increased catch that can reduce poverty, while also increase economic stability and improved status in the community . However, results of the knowledge generated from this option, how that knowledge can be used and how practitioners choose to implement it will affect its impact . Therefore, there is a high-level of context-dependence inherent in this response option. Well-managed fisheries are dependent on suitable water quality, as this directly affects fish populations and communities and fisheries' health. However, there is high uncertainty on the valuation of ecosystem services such as water quality . Therefore, the lack of appropriate data and the lack of understanding of the effects of water quality changes on fish and fisheries remains unresolved . Evidence on how the physiology of a fish responds to changes in temperature, water quality and other stressors would help gain a better understanding on how to manage these fisheries in the future . Therefore, increased knowledge around fisheries management is likely to benefit water for both humans and ecosystems (Figure 5.2.3) . Inland fisheries have an important role in sustainably feeding growing human populations, as they deliver quality nutrition to some of the world’s most vulnerable populations in a manner that is both accessible and affordable . They help meet SDGs 2, 6 and 15 and can play a role in cost-efficient water quality management . Inland fisheries catch represents ~12 per cent of global fishery production, however, values may be considerably higher given data and reporting limitations. Thus, further assessments would improve understanding of inland fisheries as a source of food to support nutritional security and overall well-being . For other aspects of health, there is a need for additional information on well-being (Figure 5.2.3). Implementing fisheries management plans can positively impact freshwater biodiversity, which can serve as an indicator of ecosystem health . In general, the level of knowledge on freshwater biodiversity (i.e., species richness, endemism, production, level of endangerment and value) is poor or out of date . Increased knowledge would allow countries to assess the status of their ecosystems to predict future impacts and quantify the effects of management on fisheries for their future needs in food security, livelihoods, recreation and investment . There is limited evidence on the transformative change potential for this response option alone (Figure 5.2.4), though when combined with others, it may have high transformative potential and may be considered a cross-cutting option for addressing interactions across the nexus. 5.2.3.6 Inclusive water management (W06) Inclusive water management recognizes that there needs to be an inclusive process for participation in water management that includes other cultures, multiple genders and diverse world views . This need is also recognized by different international instruments such as the SDGs, Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework and Paris Agreement. Others focus on achieving a fairer approach, such as the 1992 Dublin Declaration on Water and Sustainable Development, which recognizes expressly in its principle No. 3 that “Women play a fundamental role in the supply, management and protection of water .” More recently SDG6 established that society must strive to “guarantee the availability of water and its sustainable management and sanitation for all”, with its goals making special mention of the case of women and girls in access to this basic right. The scale of this need ranges from local to national but has applicability wherever inequitable processes occur (Figure 5.2.5). Because of gendered roles, responsibilities and asymmetrical power relations in the household and community, interventions that alter access to water can differentially affect women (Bartlett, 1990). The technical feasibility of this response option is high and existing policies are in place to implement it (Figure 5.2.5). However, governance involves working within existing institutional structures to create more civic space for women to engage in decision-making that affects their interests (MacArthur, 2023). This can lead to the creation of gender-transformative governance programs with an enduring effect. To better engage different genders in water management, policies need to be in place specifically to recognize gender and contribute to women’s participation in all levels of decision-making (Box 5.2.2). Incorporating a gender (and broadly intersectional; see Section 4.5) approach into integrated water management can lead to more equitable decisions and provide better standards of well-being, psychosocial and physical health and human-environment interactions . The response option thus has a strong potential to create transformative change. Embedding gender relations and interests within institutional structures can lead to gender-transformative governance programs to better ensure that change and the potential for transformation will be enduring . Box 5.2.2. Role of professional women in water management A case study in Morocco describing the role of professional women in water management highlights the barriers they face, particularly in terms of having less opportunities than men, and the urgent need to develop new public policies to address these issues. The study also shows how women face similar barriers in connection with the incorporation and acceptance of applying an interdisciplinary approach to water management activities, and how this directly relates to the development and transfer of technology to women to help them with water management . In order to learn what role women professionals working in the water sector play in the planning and management of water resources, the study undertook interviews of senior level staff members of the major water-related institutions in Morocco (public and private sector, universities and non-governmental organizations) in the cities of Rabat, Marrakech and Casablanca . The study author states that women professionals who were interviewed informed that “while in the universities nearly 50 percent of the students are women, strikingly, nearly 80 percent of them disappear from the labour market later on for one reason or another. While this may be the result of cultural baggage from the past ... even though family constraints were mentioned, mainly by some men as a reason for women not to develop professionally, women repeatedly pointed out that they, and any women, can work hard and become professionally successful.” The study concludes that there is a need for acceptance of professional women in the workforce and the implementation of an equitable approach to gender. This implies the application of positive policies to address women's specific needs and to equip and empower women to participate at all levels in water resource programmes, especially decision-making and implementation. 5.2.3.7 Rights of nature (W07) The “rights of nature” framework is grounded in the recognition that humanity and nature share a fundamental non-anthropocentric relationship based on valuing biodiversity. Ecocentrism affirms the intrinsic value of the ecosystem, and of each one of its constituents, and biocentrism claims the primordial value of life as a value in and of itself . The rights of nature approach is also an expression of cosmovisions for some IPLC and involves guaranteeing the right of access to water and the recognition of ecosystems as subjects of law (see also Section 5.1.3.10 (B10, Rights-based approaches)). This contrasts with the United Nations General Assembly Resolution 64/292 , which recognizes the human right to access to drinking water and requires states to adopt legislative, administrative, budgetary and judicial measures . There are several examples of legalization of rights of nature from Bangladesh, Bolivia, Bhutan, Colombia, Ecuador, India, Mexico, New Zealand, Uganda and the United States of America . The first case that was recognized was the Whanganui River, New Zealand . This river was granted rights of personhood and later these rights were extended to species and land (Annex 7.2). The T?hoe people collectively and the New Zealand government agreed to serve as guardians for the protection of Te Urewera (a majorly forested hill country in the country’s North Island). More recently, rights of nature frameworks have been applied to specific ecosystems, such as wetlands and rivers . However, application challenges remain, for example, addressing concerns related to assigning personhood to nature and challenges related to accountability and jurisdiction . Implementing this response option has strong benefits for water and potentially positive impacts on biodiversity and human needs by ensuring ecological flows in rivers (Figure 5.2.3, Box 5.2.3). However, some studies have noted that recognizing rivers as legal entities may also lead to increased competition where rights of rivers conflict with human water use . The review of evidence also suggests that it aligns well with international frameworks, e.g., incorporation of different value systems such as Mother Earth , and has the potential for transformative change (Figure 5.2.4, Box 5.2.3). Box 5.2.3. The importance of Indigenous and local knowledge and cosmovisions in the enactment of regulation and jurisprudence on rights of nature around the world Rivers, lakes and streams are considered as subjects by law and jurisprudence in the water response option on rights of nature . IPLC, governments and judges are the main actors included in this response option because governments enact laws applicable to IPLC and issue judgements. Indigenous cosmovision and knowledges have been considered by some states during legal proceedings; for example, Ecuador recognized rights of nature in its National Constitution of 2008. Non-governmental organizations and civil society have also played a key role in the legal process associated with this response option through different social strategies such as protests and legal and media complaints. One area of interest that has emerged is that judges have started to consider ecosystems (a river, a lake or a stream) as a unit to which they assign “environmental guardians”, such as IPLC, to protect in accordance with the jurisprudential cases enacted by the courts. This occurred in Colombia with the recognition of the Amazonas as a subject of law. Social participation and community engagement is particularly important in such cases because people are usually consulted by tribunals and/or public agencies about environmental protection of forests, rivers and other ecosystems. In Colombia, a case was filed in 2016 in the Constitutional Court of Colombia for guardianship on behalf of IPLC that inhabit the Atrato River basin against the Presidency of the Republic and others . They argued that they were subject to damage to the health of the Atrato river because of illegal mining activities and requested that the fundamental rights to life, health, water, food security, a healthy environment, culture and territory of ethnic communities be protected. The plaintiffs were granted protection and there was recognition of the existence of a serious violation of fundamental rights that were attributable to the entities of the Colombian state. 5.2.3.8 Transboundary water cooperation (W08) Working across political boundaries to balance water needs across countries and sectors can have global significance (Figure 5.2.5) with 153 countries having territory within at least one of the 286 transboundary river and lake basins and 592 transboundary aquifer systems . Transboundary cooperation is mainly supported by cooperative water agreements, which cut across several sectors, including water resources, irrigation, hydropower, environment, biodiversity, agriculture, fisheries and others. For example, the Convention on the Protection and Use of Transboundary Watercourses and International Lakes (Water Convention), adopted in Helsinki in 1992, is an international environmental agreement wherein the parties take actions to prevent, control and reduce transboundary impacts on biodiversity and other nexus elements, use transboundary waters reasonably and equitably and ensure their sustainable management . Successful transboundary cooperation can be enabled by a functional water cooperation mechanism that regulates transboundary water management and diversifies transboundary water governance responsibilities, sharing benefits within and outside transboundary river basins and making systemic efforts to pursue transboundary water management in a sustainable, equitable and integrative manner . Cooperation on transboundary waters cuts across several nexus elements (Figure 5.2.3). Examples include cooperation between several Balkan countries which share the Dinaric Karst Aquifer System, which has enabled national and regional management approaches for ecosystem protection . The Permanent Okavango Basin River Commission’s livelihood and socioeconomic development thematic programme has enabled cooperation between Angola, Botswana and Namibia, and demonstrates ways in which basin-wide strategic development programmes can address underlying drivers of poverty (Permanent Okavango Basin River Water Commission, 2019). The 2012 Great Lakes Water Quality Agreement has provided a framework for common action by Canada and the United States of America to reduce “chemicals of mutual concern” that threaten human health and the environment (Government of Canada, 2012). Countries of the lower Mekong River basin have adopted the Mekong Climate Change Adaptation Strategy and Action Plan (Mekong River Commission, 2018) to coordinate climate action. Transboundary cooperation on adaptation and mitigation of climate change can also bring additional benefits, such as conflict prevention, socioeconomic development and human well-being . Cost- and benefit-sharing between riparian states can solve water pollution issues and support sharing of benefits from collaboration related to hydropower, flood control, monitoring and facility use . Transboundary management can facilitate “win-win” opportunities enabling financial and water transfers, cross-border water storage, river basin restoration, pollution control and wastewater management . Data from 101 of 153 countries indicated that Europe and North America have the fullest coverage of operational arrangements (covering 90 per cent or more of their transboundary river and lake basin area), followed by sub-Saharan Africa (18 of 42 countries) . The SDG target 6.5 is closely aligned with this response option, with benefits contributing to several SDGs . 5.2.3.9 Groundwater governance (W09) Groundwater, which accounts for approximately 99 per cent of all liquid freshwater on Earth , is threatened by high rates of groundwater storage depletion in several parts of the globe , as well as pollution and salinization in coastal areas . However, 2.6 billion people live in river basins where groundwater is needed due to the lack of surface water . Effective groundwater management increases welfare; however, several factors, e.g., economic, hydrologic and agronomic, may lead to situations where the benefits are negligible . New forms of decentralized governance specific to groundwater are emerging in response to severe groundwater deficit in various parts of the world, aided by the advent of deep tubewell groundwater lifting technology, state-led incentives for its adoption and de facto groundwater regulatory regimes . It is well established that groundwater governance is best conceptualized as a process and not as a set of descriptive managerial policies. This encompasses the biophysical conditions of the resource and interactions between groundwater users and the resource, and between those between users, communities and the state . Integral elements of groundwater governance include: updating knowledge on groundwater resources (including cases where these resources are present as ‘fossil-water’) ; policy and planning at aquifer level while also setting national groundwater visions; management to regulate removing groundwater from aquifers as well as to address the effects on ecosystems, surface waters, land subsidence and other effects; and ensuring availability of tailored finances . Recognition of the significance of institutions, improved availability and access to groundwater information and science, robustness of civil society, and supportive economic and regulatory frameworks are key enablers of effective groundwater governance . For example, in India, participatory forms of groundwater management based on ‘aquifer-based, common pool resource’ approaches are being used to address rapid declines in groundwater resources in several parts of the country . Groundwater management can also deliver potential climate change mitigation benefits without detrimental effects to biodiversity and food (Figure 5.2.3), however, the role of governance in such projects is yet to be systematically investigated. The potential of saline aquifer formations, onshore and offshore, to store carbon dioxide has been explored , however, the energy footprints of such measures have not been considered in such estimates. Projects in the North Sea and Canada have demonstrated the potential of large geological storage of carbon dioxide in saline aquifers (i.e., 1 Mt of CO2 per year) . Adaptation to climate change-induced water stress is increasingly employing groundwater-based adaptations, such as managed aquifer recharge . Groundwater is linked to at least 53 targets in SDGs 6, 12, 13 and 15 of the 2030 Agenda for Sustainable Development (Figure 5.2.4) While in most of the cases there exists reinforcing linkages between achieving the target and trends or aspirations regarding groundwater, conflicts also exist for a few such as SDG Target 7.1 (access to reliable and affordable energy), which may negatively affect groundwater quality . 5.2.3.10 Finance for water infrastructure (W10) This response options cluster includes (i) improving water accounting; (ii) mobilizing financial resources for investment in infrastructure, human capital and institutional capacities for integrated water management (including mobilization of private sector expertise in these areas); (iii) stimulating innovative financing systems with environmental and social safeguards and regulation, (iv) establishing enabling conditions for financial viability and creditworthiness of water sector, and (v) advancing climate actions through mitigation and adaptation actions in the water sector . Financing water infrastructure for integrated water management occurs from local to national scales (Figure 5.2.5), with examples from Europe , North America and the Global South . However, evidence on their benefit to biodiversity is unresolved . Market-based water quality trading programs are also a mechanism for using price signals in river-basin management . Water quality and pollution trading in some markets might create incentives for more sustainable food production as farmers reduce water pollution , but this has not been the case in much of the United States of America . A review of water quality trading practices found that the implementation of these programs is complex with institutional, regulatory, environmental, economic and social factors contributing to the success or failure of these programs . Water banking, or use of an intermediary acting between buyers and sellers of water rights to facilitate market transfers, is another promising response to drought and other water crises . The benefits of water-related economic instruments for achieving nexus-wide benefits are generally unresolved (Figure 5.2.3). However, economic instruments have been shown to help in increasing irrigation water efficiency by reducing water demand. For example, increases in irrigation water price led farms to choose rainfed crops in northern Spain , and a 1-10 per cent increase in water pricing in Europe and the United States of America reduced water consumption by 10-48 per cent . Economic incentives also have been used to determine crop rotations that reduce water consumption . Blended finance can also play a critical role in mobilizing commercial finance to achieve SDG6 (water and sanitation for all) and hence have co-benefits for health (SDG3) (see Section 6.2). At the same time, implementation of economic instruments is challenging due to the common pool nature of water use and their lack of fit with abatement pollution measures (i.e., for non-point source pollution where water pricing may not be tied to the polluter) and overall effectiveness in ensuring efficiency and equity . Recognizing multiple values of water , including non-market water values, in water accounts and financing are key components of transformative pathways towards more sustainable water futures. For example, the impacts of water resource development on Australian Indigenous customary economies have been assessed using replacement cost methods . Economics, pricing and financing can be transformational in global water allocation in terms of by whom, what, where and when water is used (Figure 5.2.5) . 5.2.3.11 Manage alien species (W11) The management of aquatic invasive alien species (invasive alien species) to conserve freshwater biodiversity requires an understanding of the ecology of invasive alien species populations and their interactions within and across trophic levels of fishes . Invasive alien species cover a wide range of taxonomic groups (macrophytes, phytoplankton, zooplankton, benthic invertebrates and fish) across different trophic guilds (primary producers, filter collectors, omnivores and predators) and habitats (rivers, lakes, and estuaries), with strong and pervasive negative impacts on native communities and aquatic habitat functioning . Economic wealth affects the risk of invasive alien species because increases in economic growth may encourage sectors to benefit from alien species . For example, invasive Blue Catfish in the Chesapeake Bay in the eastern United States of America create recreational fisheries and economic benefits but also are detrimental to native fish populations . The main invasion vectors differ between high-income countries (imports of pets and plants) and low-income countries (shipping of goods and people), with the latter tending to have more reactive national policies. Countries with high incidence risk for invasive alien species tend to have proactive capacities, yet there are only a few examples of strong policies even in those countries with high incidence (Box 5.2.4). Furthermore, species interactions with native species might hinder the ‘ideal’ timing and success for this response option . The literature review focused on reducing pathways for aquatic invasive alien species as these are one of the top five priorities for conserving freshwater biodiversity and addressing biodiversity loss . The geographic scope of aquatic invasive alien species is well established as global , but with interventions at local levels (Figure 5.2.4 along the invasion pathways from source to establishment and persistence . These interventions are well established with mostly positive outcomes that can help ‘bend the curve’ on freshwater biodiversity loss . The temporal scale is variable (Figure 5.2.5) and depends on the specific aquatic invasive alien species (e.g., ). Most studies suggest that invasive alien species have variable impacts on water, such as changing turbidity, which affects water quality, or increasing water filtration and purity . Managing aquatic invasive alien species could result in better water saving outcomes, but this has not been quantified. Managing invasive alien species often focuses on removals to control the species/taxa but can also include prevention, but both measures have mixed success . Prevention of invasive species is more cost effective than actual removals or control once a species is present . However, prevention is complex as some aquatic invasive alien species contribute to food security by providing foods to local communities and economic benefit (Figure 5.2.3) . Some invasive alien species also outcompete native species which could also provide food security . Aquatic invasive alien species can have co-benefits for climate change adaptation and may be carbon sinks or sources . However, knowledge is still inconclusive because of the variety of aquatic invasive alien species that need to be assessed and the range of conditions where they occur. There are also health benefits, such as invasive alien species contribution to disease regulation and recreational activities , but also health disbenefits, such as invasive alien species acting as new vectors of disease . Management of aquatic invasive alien species is contested. Although they provide services and livelihoods (e.g., food, materials, tourism, etc.) that have economic benefits , those benefits typically do not mitigate their negative impacts, given that the total cost of alien species effects on humans is estimated to be over $423 billion . Acceptance of invasive alien species management is variable and depends on the local cost and benefits, which are often unresolved . In addition, many smallholders, fishers and communities already manage aquatic invasive alien species using traditional knowledge and experience . Overall, the management of aquatic invasive alien species is well established in many political priorities (Figure 5.2.4) and can be technically feasible (Figure 5.2.5), but feasibility depends on context . In connected systems such as rivers, eradication can be ineffective . However, managing aquatic alien species aligns with global goals for climate smart food production, biodiversity and food and water security . The implementation of management interventions for aquatic invasive alien species is linked to many global frameworks (e.g., target 7 of the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework; UN Food System Pathways 2; SDG15) and has medium transformative change potential with the right economic investment , but evidence is incomplete. Box 5.2.4. Management of invasive alien species in the Sacramento-San Joaquin River delta in California, United States of America The Sacramento-San Joaquin River delta is an inland delta, historically controlled for land reclamation for agriculture and leveed to control water flows, reduce freshwater salinity and provide freshwater to southern California. The system is managed with the co-equal goals of protection of biodiversity and water . This highly modified system has been home to many invasive alien species from plants across different water levels to fish and mussels, all of which interact and have repercussions for water quality. The management of invasive alien species plants such as Brazilian waterweed began when it started to invade the delta’s waterways. Its current extent varies annually between 12-15 per cent of the waterways. The large-scale management of this and other invasive alien species was required not only because of the large expanses that these species were covering but also because they started to change system states, potentially leading to a new stable state. This resulted from the large mats of submerged invasive alien species leading to changes in water velocity, which became slower, with higher rates of sedimentation and changes to the water column turbidity. These changes in water properties resulted in the alteration of the habitat required by a fish species listed under the United States of America Endangered Species Act, the delta smelt Hypomesus transpacificus, is critically endangered (IUCN 3.1). As such, active management was required to manage submerged plant growth to improve delta smelt habitat. The management actions to help delta smelt included control of aquatic vegetation through multi-faceted control measures that include chemical, biological and physical control methods . However, the costs of herbicides are prohibitive ($12.5 million in 2015 alone) and biological control by introducing herbivores that will consume invasive plants and creating physical barriers to plan expansion have varying degrees of success . Furthermore, invasive alien species fish species introduced for recreational fisheries are potential predators of delta smelt that can thrive in the clearer and slow waters of this invasive alien species-modified system. Climate and land-use changes threaten the persistence of this system, with modifications of management being proposed to restore ecological flows, maintain the twice-daily tidal exchange, increase mixing of water in the system and increase the penetration of saline waters inland. All these are predicted to lead to a decrease in invasive alien species plant extent, with potential cascading effects on trophic elements in this system. Water is now a fundamental resource in this system, given the climate change-induced increases in air and water temperature and the increasing fire frequency in the state. The management of the delta is therefore a wicked problem that is fundamental and central to the state’s ecosystem and people’s well-being as well as an example of where a nexus approach is required. 5.2.3.12 Manage wastewater (W12) Wastewater pollution, including excessive fertilizer use, can be reduced by repositioning wastewater management from an end-of-pipe problem to a circular economy opportunity which treats wastewater as a renewable resource to be conserved and sustainably managed . Sustainable sanitation practices play a crucial role in addressing the interconnected challenges of water availability, biodiversity conservation and climate actions . Safe and appropriate wastewater management has potential co-benefits such as improved ecosystem health, human health and well-being, reduced dependence on synthetic fertilizers and economic opportunities (Figure 5.2.3) (UNEP, 2023). Actions include: (a) reducing the volume of wastewater produced; (b) preventing and reducing water contamination; and (c) sustainably managing wastewater for resource recovery and reuse. Several solutions exist for the collection and treatment of wastewater to recover resources (such as nutrients) of appropriate quality standards depending on intended end use . Access to adequate sanitation services and domestic wastewater treatment is a critical issue in several parts of Latin American, Asian and African countries. Hence, sanitation projects implemented in these regions are applying ecological sanitation approaches for the recovery of resources like biogas, struvite, carbon dioxide and clean water from domestic wastewater to ‘close the loop’ . Implementing such approaches has many benefits for the nexus elements. Wetlands, both natural and constructed, are a nature-based solution that can remove nutrients, reduce maintenance costs and energy consumption, and provide wastewater reuse and application of biosolids in agriculture . For example, plants from constructed wetlands can be used for biofuel and can reduce cooking fuel by up to 55 per cent . Preventing contamination of drinking water sources, such as aquifers and surface waters, from wastewater is key to ensuring the quality of water resources, providing benefits to biodiversity and improving human health by reducing diseases such as diarrhoeal illness . Improved sanitation also saves lives and facilitates economic growth . Wastewater management is also pitched as an important component of One Health, especially for challenges such as antibiotic resistance , and addressing marine pollution . Enhancing the sustainability of sanitation projects entails co-designing and co-managing facilities with communities to invoke a sense of ownership, educating users with proper hygiene maintenance and building capacities to regulate the flow and treatment of wastewater (Brands, 2014) as well as developing effective business models . To address the existing institutional and financial fragmentation in the provision of sanitation, comprehensive and integrated policies supported by cooperative and participatory methods are needed . Closing the funding gap in sanitation calls for specialized cross-sectoral nodal entities or the convergence of efforts across ministries to produce significant sanitation results. For example, the African Water Facility supports governments in the implementation of several water supply and sanitation projects in sub-Saharan Africa (GBAD, 2020). The circular economy appears to be an effective model for sanitation because it focuses on the entire sanitation chain, which includes the provision of toilets, waste collection, treatment and transformation into by-products . Reusing water in agriculture can help with irrigation, increase agricultural yield and even extend the growing season, while also lowering the danger of drought for crops. Wastewater and agricultural inputs can be used for any kind of crop, depending on the treatment standard and the procedures followed . Farmers in low-income countries have cited the year-round availability of wastewater as a significant reason in favour of its reuse (Drechsel, 2010). Systems that assure wastewater treatment prior to discharge mitigate risks to ecosystems and nature’s contributions to people. These include enhancing freshwater quality and safety, which makes it more usable, and lowering pollution and eutrophication in food-producing ecosystems . Significant amounts of nutrients can be recovered from wastewater and excreta; estimates suggest that all current fertilizer use in smallholder-dominated countries could theoretically be replaced with nutrients recovered from human excreta (Box 5.2.5). The 330 km3 of municipal wastewater generated every year worldwide could theoretically irrigate around 15 per cent of all currently irrigated crops . The primary organic matter found in excreta and wastewater is composed of lipids, carbs and proteins, which can be used as a soil conditioner which would address declining soil quality. This is especially true if it is combined with food waste and agricultural leftovers . Additionally, improving soil organic matter content reduces the impacts of run-off and eutrophication issues by enhancing nutrient and water retention . Efficiently producing renewable energy from wastewater and excreta can also be a successful strategy for mitigating the effects of climate change . Enhancing sanitation and managing wastewater can significantly aid in mitigating climate change by decreasing emissions of carbon dioxide, methane and nitrous oxide (Figure 5.2.3), as the waste sector contributes significantly to global emissions. Producing biogas is an effective approach to extract the energy from these waste streams and the most compatible with resource recovery . It can also be an inexpensive and healthier substitute for wood and other biomass fuels used by low-income households . The estimated 330 km3 of municipal wastewater output could theoretically supply electricity for almost 130 million people based on an average yearly electricity usage of 3,500 kWh/household . The controlled burning or incineration of waste streams is another method of extracting energy, which is prevalent in Sweden and Denmark; China is also making significant investments in the incineration of solid waste . For example, changing the treatment design in a more traditional wastewater management system can cut carbon dioxide emissions by 35 per cent . Therefore, recovering energy from excreta, wastewater and other organic waste and making treatment systems efficient can aid in increasing access to contemporary energy while also reducing greenhouse gas emissions. Box 5.2.5. The success of urine diversion dehydration toilets in African schools Urine diversion dehydration toilets are sustainable sanitation systems that undertake separate collection of feces and urine without any flush water, resulting in odor-free operation and pathogen reduction by drying. This preserves water quantity and quality for other needs such as biodiversity and human health. These toilets have been used widely in schools in countries such as Ethiopia, Kenya, Rwanda, Tanzania and Uganda. Generally, urine diversion dehydration toilet projects are favourably received by user groups (school children and teachers), especially due to the lack of odour and pests such as flies. Maintenance and operation are also generally easy and smooth, particularly if the projects are developed with participation from the local communities. Moreover, the toilets are not only low-cost interventions, but they also generate additional income for the schools through utilization of the byproducts, such as waste for small-scale agricultural projects. However, the effectiveness of the urine diversion dehydration toilets often depends on the extent to which user groups have a sense of ownership towards the facilities and whether gendered needs of sanitation facilities are integrated into design considerations. A particularly famous case is that of the Sacred Heart Sisters boarding school for girls, situated in Kalunga, Uganda. Since the project’s implementation, which comprises of single vault urine diversion dehydration toilets and constructed wetlands for the treatment of waste, the school has gained recognition for its creative approach to sanitation both in Uganda and globally. The waste generated is used as fertilizer for agricultural purposes, which have been reported to be of high value, and is being used for the school gardens with the potential to enhance their biodiversity. Source: 5.2.3.13 Water-sensitive urban infrastructure (W13) This response option focuses on applying principles of Water Sensitive Urban Design in the design of urban infrastructure to conserve water while simultaneously protecting the health and biodiversity of waterways and mitigating the risk of damage to life and property from flooding . Water Sensitive Urban Design systems are applicable globally in all urban areas, being of greatest value in those that are prone to water-related risks (Box 5.2.6). They are implemented at a range of scales from the household to city levels (Figure 5.2.5). Water Sensitive Urban Design systems can encapsulate a range of interventions from building infrastructure for the drainage and harvesting of stormwater to the installation of treatment facilities for the removal of water-borne pollutants . They can also be implemented to improve urban landscapes by incorporating built reservoirs/wetlands into the planning process . Water Sensitive Urban Design systems that implement infrastructure for the collection and storage of stormwater improve the availability of water for human use (Figure 5.2.3). Water Sensitive Urban Design systems can also enhance biodiversity and the ecological functioning of urban environments and natural lands , and can make urban-adjacent environments more ecologically resilient . As one of the primary objectives of Water Sensitive Urban Design systems is to mitigate urban flooding risk, they can benefit the health and well-being of urban residents . Improving the ecological health of waterways by reducing the occurrence of water-borne pollutants can also benefit the health of the population using the drainage catchment as a water source . Water Sensitive Urban Design systems implemented with landscape architects and city planners that prioritize urban water bodies and green spaces can contribute to mitigating urban heat island effects . Furthermore, Water Sensitive Urban Design systems can make cities more resilient to climate change-induced water scarcity . Water Sensitive Urban Design systems provide substantial non-financial benefits and also have positive returns on investments . Hence, local communities are in broad alignment with the objectives of Water Sensitive Urban Design practices in urban infrastructure . The key impediment to the uptake of Water Sensitive Urban Design systems is the lack of regulatory guidelines (due in part to their complexity) mandating their implementation within urban development projects . Suitable amendments to policy frameworks are necessary to ensure their widespread adoption, along with increased community awareness of the costs/benefits of Water Sensitive Urban Design systems . The implementation of Water Sensitive Urban Design systems without the consultation of local residents, urban developers, civil engineers, landscape architects and ecologists will lead to increased costs and unrealized benefits . Lack of engagement of community stakeholders and the costs of capacity building and maintenance and upkeep of Water Sensitive Urban Design systems can also present major challenges to the adoption of Water Sensitive Urban Design systems and the realization of their benefits . The adoption of Water Sensitive Urban Design practices in urban infrastructure projects contributes towards Targets 1, 2, 3, 4, 7, 11, 12 and 14 of the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework and SDGs 3, 6, 11, 13, and 15. Box 5.2.6. Water Sensitive Urban Design in South Australia: Prospects, benefits and implementation challenges This example of Water Sensitive Urban Design is from Australia where the urban water reform agenda of the National Water Initiative, an Australian Intergovernmental Agreement has been implemented . The Water Sensitive Urban Design Technical Manual was created in South Australia by the old Department of Planning and Local Government, and it offered instructions for using 12 Water Sensitive Urban Design tools throughout the greater Adelaide area . According to South Australia’s inventory of Water Sensitive Urban Design systems, local governments have traditionally used these systems primarily for stormwater control. Managed aquifers, bioretention, wetlands and infiltration (swales) are the most often used Water Sensitive Urban Design techniques in Adelaide. The great majority of Water Sensitive Urban Design systems are retrofit projects that were primarily overseen by local government and constructed on already-existing public land. Water Sensitive Urban Design offers multiple benefits, thereby encouraging widespread uptake of the systems by local governments. The key motivators for Water Sensitive Urban Design installation are to lower stormwater flow, improve run-off water quality and encourage water conservation. Other motivators include the desire to preserve vegetation or enhance landscape amenities, as well as a decrease in maintenance expenses. At the community level, Water Sensitive Urban Design systems are well embraced for their water conservation and flood mitigation roles, as well as the landscape amenity benefits, which help foster the sense and aesthetics of the community. However, some key barriers still remain which hinder the scaling-up of Water Sensitive Urban Design. For example, councils are unable to create a compelling economic case for Water Sensitive Urban Design initiatives due to the challenges involved in evaluating the long-term costs and benefits. It may also be challenging for local governments to secure an operation and management budget for these features. Moreover, a lack of information regarding Water Sensitive Urban Design maintenance requirements and the dearth of Water Sensitive Urban Design post-implementation monitoring and performance assessment are some of the biggest barriers to Water Sensitive Urban Design uptake. More importantly, because community members are the final recipients and users of the products of Water Sensitive Urban Designs, community participation should be emphasized. In South Australia, despite the positive views of community members towards Water Sensitive Urban Design, they lack communication channels between the local government, developers and community members, and are thus unable to offer input for better design, operation and maintenance of Water Sensitive Urban Design systems based on their experiences. This is potentially why the ability of Water Sensitive Urban Design to improve water quality remains under-appreciated by the community members. Therefore, in order to raise the standard and rate of Water Sensitive Urban Design system implementation, the ways forward should focus on enhancing Water Sensitive Urban Design capabilities and increasing community participation. 5.2.3.14 Address gendered burdens of water collection (W14) Women often experience increased exposure to impacts of environmental changes . For example, women can be more vulnerable to the impacts of climate change since they are generally responsible for ensuring the survival of families by providing water and food. Natural disasters such as floods can destroy water systems established specifically for communities that depend on untreated water and health resources, which leads to water pollution ). Given that women use different ecosystem services than men, these differences are important to understand in order to implement more sustainable and equitable policies and interventions . Water collection is challenging due to distance, scarcity and lack of protection. Women face increased risks of disease due to direct contact with poor quality water and attacks during their journey to natural resource sources. For example, water tankers in western Rajasthan benefit upper-caste households, but poor and low-caste households remain dependent on drying water sources, particularly women from low-caste households who walk long distances to access water . These long distances result in detrimental effect on the health of women of all ages, where water-carrying methods impose physical loads with the potential to cause musculoskeletal disorders and related disability . This response option focuses on technological tools, educational strategies and economic support that can reduce the burden of water collection on women and girls and, as a result, provide more time to study, work and/or for leisure (Box 5.2.7). It benefits water for humans and provides increased access to healthy foods, which improves human well-being (Figure 5.2.3). There is substantial evidence showing connections between water availability and accessibility and well-being including psychosocial health, physical health, personal beliefs, social relationships and human-environment interactions . Moreover, diseases increase especially in women and girls due to water access inequalities . Improvements in rural water supply allows women to transform the time saved from water collection into money . No evidence was found to show an effect of this response option on biodiversity (Figure 5.2.3). Technologies for facilitating improved access to water for women and girls to alleviate time burdens and the detrimental impacts on their health and safety from carrying water can change rapidly and hence evidence on their feasibility is inconclusive. There are international instruments that regulate or recognize gender and water access rights, but some of these include the need for more infrastructure technology, such as connecting water to homes and solar-powered pumps to reduce labour for collection . Box 5.2.7. Gender and the water, energy, land and food security nexus in sub-Saharan Africa Water, energy, land and food are critical aspects of the livelihoods of women, and all can be linked to biodiversity. However, gender discrimination across sectors and a lack of focus on gender issues in water-energy-land-food nexus frameworks have limited the consideration of gender aspects in Sub-Saharan Africa. The main challenges that perpetuate gender gaps are poverty, unemployment, inequality and climate change. These are further exacerbated by issues related to political will, budgetary constraints and the persistence of patriarchal mindsets. This ultimately compromises gender equality and the sustainability of development initiatives . Climate change-induced ecological and hydrological changes have further compounded gender disparities in income, health and education in sub-Saharan Africa by exacerbating existing development challenges. The productive and reproductive roles of women within the household make them increasingly vulnerable to climate change impacts, especially in rural areas as they are responsible for providing food for their households and procuring fuelwood for cooking and drinking water. The gendered social roles and gender-based inequalities in access to assets have also led to differences in adaptive capacities between men and women . Attention to gender and inclusion of women into policies and practice is crucial for achieving gender equality and operational effectiveness as the region advances climate-resilient strategies related to water, energy, land and food. This can be enabled by gender-responsive planning for the water-energy-land-food nexus, while also recognizing the power differentials among men and women within the sectors, and promoting gender sensitive roles for management of water, energy, land and food nexus interactions . Monitoring and reporting mechanisms (public and private) are key issues to achieve and maintain progress on the effective implementation of policies that contribute to gender equity . Furthermore, it is crucial for women to have the same access to resources as men, and for women to be empowered as decision-makers, which can result in positive outcomes for all areas of society . 5.2.3.15 Community water management (W15) Community water management is based on the premise that water challenges at the local level can be overcome if communities collectively develop their own behavioural choices and build social capital regarding the beneficial effects of collective action . Placing communities at the heart of water management solutions, community water management recognizes the potential for local solutions to emerge from human networks, social interactions and local knowledge , which also result in benefits for biodiversity (Figure 5.2.3). Collective action to manage water commons may also include setting contextualized incentive-based rules, thus providing flexibility in contrast with a common and control-based approach setting specific parameters such as irrigation technology mandates . These actions, especially in rural settings, are relevant globally but implemented locally . Nexus approaches can support community water management in various ways (Boxes 5.2.8 and 5.2.9). For example, modern small-scale decentralized hydro-power based energy solutions in select low income countries have the potential for co-benefits in food security, income, health, the empowerment of women and resource conservation when synergies between these solutions and elements of the nexus are considered in programme development and implementation . Nexus approaches have also been used to provide insights into the linkages between sustainable energy projects and the food and water sectors to avoid trade-offs and enhance development outcomes and impacts of energy projects . Since the 1970s, water user associations have emerged to improve coverage and efficiency in low-performing state-managed irrigation systems as well as to improve small-scale community-based and farmer-led irrigation systems , and rural drinking water with varying success. Through visioning and prioritization, knowledge-sharing and story-telling, the capacity of communities to act as valued environmental stewards increased . Similarly, collective action can be targeted at adaptation to water scarcity, including those triggered by climate change , yet demonstration of the effectiveness is limited. Community-based water stewardship around locally-led adaptation, when supported with inclusion, diverse and democratized knowledge and equity and justice could provide an important opportunity for greater climate-resilient development . Community-led initiatives are considered technically feasible and socially acceptable (Figures 5.2.5 and 5.2.6). They already contribute at a local scale to several SDGs, and even to synergies between different goals . In several cases, these initiatives have pre-empted many aspirations of the 2030 Agenda to integrate diverse environmental and social goals . Box 5.2.8. Incorporating Indigenous and local knowledge in community water management Community water management as a nexus response option can be greatly facilitated by consideration of ILK and IPLC perspectives . This box describes two case studies where community water management has been successfully implemented by Indigenous communities. The first case study concerns restoration in Kahnawa-ke, an Indigenous community located south of the city of Montreal in Canada . The community purchased a boat designed to harvest aquatic plants so that it could remove weeds that had infested the bay, helping to increase water flow and enjoyment of the water as a beach was created on the island's eastern area. The restoration project contributed to biodiversity conservation and the production of different medicinal plants which are used by the community as part of their traditions and culture. Although they faced challenges, including limited public funds and expropriation of part of the bay’s territory for infrastructure development (the St. Lawrence seaway), the community was able to collectively design and implement the project. The second case study concerns water defence in the Central Valleys of Oaxaca in Mexico . Indigenous communities negotiated with the Mexican state about repealing a nationally imposed ban on local water use that had been used to favour extractive industries (e.g., mining) and also sought legal recognition of the local knowledge which was used for water management implementation in the area. This traditional knowledge was called ‘sowing water’, a rainwater catchment method used in situations of ecological crisis due to droughts. The water ban was removed in 2021 which allowed the Indigenous communities to manage water based on their beliefs and knowledge. Implementing these changes through community water management and inclusive water management response options allows for local communities to have leverage to better balance water needs for other uses such as food production. Box 5.2.9. Participatory irrigation management in Barind, Bangladesh The Barind region, once one of the driest parts of Rajshahi Division of Bangladesh, has historically struggled to make water available for agriculture. Despite plentiful monsoons, the soil and ephemeral rivers dry up soon afterwards, thus making agriculture difficult. In 1985, the Bangladesh government established the Barind Multipurpose Development Authority with a mandate to promote the all-round development of the region. Core to the mandate was the implementation of community managed tubewell irrigation based on participatory irrigation management. The Barind Multipurpose Development Authority, rather than implementing a top-down approach for constructing tubewells, followed a demand-driven approach wherein to access tubewells farmers were supported to form water user associations with a minimum irrigated area of 30 acres and paying a membership fee. The tubewells were constructed based on resource availability and with technology appropriate to the region. The irrigation water was priced to cover the full cost of energy, operations and management, as well as establishment costs. The revenue is collected by mobile vendor units, mainly operated by rural women, who also receive a small proportion of the revenue as an incentive. Backstopping is done by Barind Multipurpose Development Authority technicians. The Barind project has created positive impacts at several levels. In 2015-2016, the Barind Multipurpose Development Authority’s 15,813 deep tubewells provided irrigation water to 496,200 ha, 66 per cent of Barind’s irrigated area was cultivated by 250,000 farmers (56 per cent of Barind’s irrigators) who use smart cards to pay for water via the mobile vendor units, and cropping intensity increased from 117 to 200 per cent, while Bangladesh’s national average was 175 per cent. Supplementary jobs have been created for women farmers who also work as tubewell operators or as mobile vending unit operators. The case study showcases the use of a nexus approach linking water, food, energy and livelihoods systems to promote development in a water-stressed geography. Consideration of co-benefits for climate change can further the adaptation potential of the response option through technologies such as solar pumps for irrigation. Hossain , Rahman and Shah" ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.2.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Nexus response options for water?" .

