    ipbes:Report <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/NXS24> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "An initial set of response options that address health issues and at least two additional nexus elements were identified by chapter authors based on expert opinion informed by searching the literature and major reports (e.g., WHO Environment, Climate Change and Health reports, IPBES assessments, the Workshop Report on Biodiversity and Pandemics , National Biodiversity Strategies and Action Plans, World Organisation for Animal Health terrestrial and aquatic animal health codes, Health-Climate National Adaptation Plans (H-NAPS) and Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change (IPCC) reports, including the contribution of Working Group II to the Sixth Assessment Report of IPCC and the report from the first ILK dialogue workshop for the Nexus Assessment . This produced 51 response options after removal of regional or thematic duplication (see Annex 5.4.1). Fifteen response options with strong nexus interlinkages were selected for assessment using a multi-criteria decision analysis .3 The initial set of response options were categorized as equitable, nexus-supportive healthcare; addressing environmental risks to health; promoting nature’s benefits to health; and nexus-integrated healthcare (Figure 5.4.3) and ranked in sets to eliminate ranking thematically different options against each other. The categories approximate existing health and health-biodiversity frameworks (e.g., , and with special attention to IPLC inclusion. The selected response options were chosen as examples for which sufficient evidence for analysis currently exists. See Table 5.4.2 for brief descriptions of the 15 response options assessed. Figure 5.4.3. Framework used for selecting the response options presented in this chapter. Table 5.4.2. Brief descriptions of the 15 health response options assessed. Health response option Brief description of response option Section H01 Universal health coverage Increasing access to comprehensive primary healthcare, including essential health, reproductive health and family planning services. 5.4.3.1 H02 Intercultural health services Formal arrangements between health departments and traditional health practitioners that enable inclusive, equitable and culturally relevant healthcare for Indigenous Peoples and local communities and are informed by diverse knowledge systems. 5.4.3.2 H03 Net-zero sustainable healthcare Reducing negative impacts of resource-intensive healthcare sectors; this includes reducing greenhouse gas emissions, sustainable procurement practices, reducing pollution and waste, and a focus on preventive and community healthcare. 5.4.3.3 H04 Sustainable use of medicinal plants Using medicinal plants in a manner that supports biodiversity, ecosystem functions and human health and well-being in the present and future; this includes plant conservation, cultivation, sourcing, harvesting and trade, and compliance with the Nagoya Protocol. 5.4.3.4 H05 Nature on prescription A complementary healthcare approach in which healthcare professionals prescribe activities in nature to individuals or groups to treat a range of health conditions. 5.4.3.5 H06 Reduce meat overconsumption Reducing overconsumption of red and processed meat in support of adopting sustainable healthy diets that rely on sustainable food systems and contribute to human health and well-being. 5.4.3.6 H07 Pollution prevention A broad response option that includes strategies for reducing air, water and soil pollution in the context of protecting human health and reducing disease burdens. 5.4.3.7 H08 Mangrove conservation and restoration for health Conserving and restoring mangroves to mitigate disaster risks from coastal hazards, including the risk of lives lost to coastal disasters, protect coastal and marine biodiversity and habitat, and support sustainable coastal and marine fisheries and food systems. 5.4.3.8 H09 Urban green infrastructure Natural, semi-natural and artificial green spaces in urban environments that contribute to human health, well-being and quality of life, increase biodiversity and ecosystem resilience and support nature’s contributions to people. 5.4.3.9 H10 Forest conservation for health Conserving forests or reducing deforestation to support human health and well-being while also maintaining biodiversity and sustaining nature’s contributions to people; this includes forested Indigenous community conserved areas and territories. 5.4.3.10 H11 Biodiversity management for zoonoses Conserving biodiversity to prevent the transmission of pathogens from animals to humans (i.e., reduce the risk of spillover), reduce the spread of emerging zoonotic diseases and reduce the risk of pandemics. 5.4.3.11 H12 Integrated watershed-health interventions Place-based responses that involve participatory planning and integrate health promotion and interventions with landscape, seascape and watershed management approaches to support human health and well-being and enhance nature’s contributions to people. 5.4.3.12 H13 Health impact assessments A decision-making tool to assess how a policy, programme or project may potentially affect population health; the tool is underpinned by stakeholder engagement, equity, sustainable development and the ethical use of evidence and can be used by many different sectors. 5.4.3.13 H14 The One Health approach Application of the One Health approach to address interlinkages among humans, animals and ecosystems in the context of health challenges, such as emerging infectious diseases, antimicrobial resistance and food safety, to safeguard human, animal and ecosystem health. 5.4.3.14 H15 Integrated health education Education informed by conceptualizations of health (e.g., planetary health, One Health, eco-health) that describe health in the context of Earth system functions, biodiversity, ecosystems and diverse world views, including Indigenous conceptualizations of health. 5.4.3.15 Each response option was analysed and scored4 based on assessed evidence against 10 criteria (see Chapter 5.0.3, Table 5.0.1 and Annex 5.0.1) related to its potential to produce benefits across multiple elements of the nexus, its feasibility and breadth of applicability, its impact on equity and its potential to advance the goals of existing global policy frameworks. In addition, specific case studies are provided to illustrate response options. The geographical distribution of case studies is shown in Figure 5.4.4. A synthesis of the scoring is provided in Figure 5.4.5 (impacts of the response options on nexus elements), Figure 5.4.6 (health response options within the context of social, economic and political dimensions, and international frameworks) and Figure 5.4.7 (temporal and spatial scales and technical feasibility of the health response options). Individual response options are described in Sections 5.3.4.1 to 5.3.4.15. From the 15 response options assessed, five focal response options are analyzed in greater detail2: H02, Intercultural health services (Section 5.4.3.2); H06, Reduce meat overconsumption (Section 5.4.3.6); H09, Urban green infrastructure (Section 5.4.3.9); H10, Forest conservation for health (Section 5.4.3.10) and H13, Health impact assessment (Section 5.4.3.13). These focal response options are not intended to represent priorities or recommendations. Rather, they are chosen to illustrate the range of issues and actors involved in response options and provide real-world examples of response options in action. Figure 5.4.4. Distribution of response option case studies by location. See Boxes 5.4.1-5, 5.4.7-11 and 5.4.13-14. Note two case studies are not linked to a specific location and two refer to multiple locations. Figure 5.4.5. Impacts of health response options on nexus elements. Colours indicate positive impacts (bluel; i.e., synergies) and negative impacts (red; i.e., trade-offs). Intensity of colour represents the magnitude of impact (light = small; medium = medium impact; dark = large;). Grey colouring indicates a score of 0. White colouring indicates no score or a non-numerical value. Where impacts differ depending on context, impact ranges are shown by multiple colours within a cell. NA indicates not applicable, NE indicates no evidence, and INC indicates the evidence is inconclusive. Figure 5.4.6. Health response options within the context of social, economic and political dimensions, international frameworks and transformative change potential. Colours indicate a positive impact (blue; i.e., synergies) and a negative impact (red; i.e., trade-offs). Intensity of colour represents the magnitude of impact (light = small; medium = medium impact; dark = large;). Grey colouring indicates a score of 0. White colouring indicates no score or a non-numerical value. Where impacts differ depending on context, impact ranges are shown by multiple colours within a cell. NA indicates not applicable, NE indicates no evidence, and INC indicates the evidence is inconclusive Figure 5.4.7. Temporal and spatial scales and technical feasibility of the health response options. Ranges shown temporal and spatial scales and feasibility indicated by Technology Readiness Level.5 5.4.3.1 Universal health coverage (H01) Universal health coverage using a comprehensive primary healthcare approach includes essential health services and reproductive health and family planning (SDG targets 3.7, 5.6), and reorientates spending from expensive treatment-focused care (which is increasingly unsustainable, see H03) to prevention and healthy living conditions . Approximately half the global population do not have access to primary healthcare and almost a billion people are vulnerable to out-of-pocket health spending for healthcare that puts them at risk for poverty . In practice, access to essential health services are prioritized and impacts on nexus elements are related to proximal human needs (e.g., water for human needs, not the environment) or not reported (Figure 5.4.5). This has scope to change with wider recognition of the health impacts of climate change and global emergencies and the need for climate-resilient health services as a first line of defence . Biodiversity or nature is rarely explicitly addressed in discussion of universal health coverage or primary healthcare (see also Section 5.4.3.4, H04, Sustainable use of medicinal plants and Section 5.4.3.5, H05, Nature on prescription) but this may change as access to clean, healthy and sustainable environment becomes critical . A stronger case for protecting the environment and restoring biodiversity could be made through better identifying, treating and collecting data on environment-linked diseases. However, providing access to healthcare in biodiverse, remote areas can enhance biodiversity (Box 5.4.3). Universal access to sexual and reproductive health and reproductive rights has potential to address approximately 121 million unintended births p.a. . This and achieving optimal child spacing would improve the lives of millions of women and children and address gender-related barriers to healthcare. This also has potential to significantly reduce population growth and pressures on the nexus elements, although the latter is subject to geographical differences in location and the pressure of per-capita consumption (the higher the per capita consumption level, the lower the ecologically sustainable population . Although estimates of cost-effectiveness of healthcare coverage vary widely due to various methodological, empirical and value driven factors , studies indicate that expanding population level and primary healthcare services among populations with high deficits in healthcare coverage are likely to be cost effective in terms of averted deaths, increased life expectancy and an improved quality of life . Universal health coverage is a tool for achieving health equity and the right to health and has transformative potential for those without access. This response option is inclusive of evidence-based traditional healthcare (Figure 5.4.6). Primary health services are ideally designed in partnership with communities to ensure distributive, inclusive and procedural equity. In the absence of health coverage and essential healthcare, other health response options with greater impacts on nexus elements (Figure 5.4.5) may have low social acceptability or may be less effective (see Section 5.4.3.11, H11, Biodiversity management of zoonoses). Box 5.4.3. Conservation, primary healthcare and rights-based approaches to reproductive health and family planning Provision of health and other services has been a feature of conservation projects in many remote, biodiverse areas. For example, many projects have included population, health and environment programs ) and since 2022, the International Union for Conservation of Nature has had a Biodiversity & Family Planning Task Force, focused on removing barriers to rights-based voluntary family planning. These health services recognize local priorities and unmet needs in biodiversity hotspots that are often remote and restricted in access to external services. However, some short-duration services, real or perceived restriction of services based on support for conservation, or fertility reduction approaches have resulted in harm to people and in some instances resulted in negative (retaliatory) outcomes for conservation . Health in Harmony is an example of an effective, low-cost network of health centres integrated with local government services near a district in Gunung Palung National Park, Kalimantan, Indonesia where illegal logging occurs . It set out to achieve (and document) positive impacts for people and the environment through access to affordable, quality healthcare. It does this in parallel to comprehensive conservation interventions, including a memorandum of understanding to abstain from illegal logging, payment through pro-reforestation barter activities, education and support for alternative livelihoods. However, healthcare access is available to all, including illegal loggers, and over a ten-year period, 28,462 individuals have used the health services (42 per cent of whom came from areas without a memorandum of understanding). Significant declines in diagnosed cases of malaria, tuberculosis, childhood-cluster diseases, infant mortality, neglected tropical diseases, chronic obstructive pulmonary disease and diabetes were documented, but lower and upper respiratory infections increased, possibly because of extensive forest fires in 2015. The birthrate also decreased. Over this time the livelihoods of residents of memorandum of understanding districts shifted from illegal logging and income was stable. This was associated with a 70 per cent reduction in deforestation (equivalent to 27.4 km2 of forest loss and 0.59 Tg of above ground carbon loss averted (90 per cent CI, 0.27–1.13 Tg, parameterised for Borneo, equivalent to $65.3 million in 2019). Participants were surveyed regarding the relative effectiveness of the interventions to stop illegal logging and 50 per cent reported healthcare discounts and 25 per cent reported alternative livelihoods as influential. Community leadership in the design and implementation of interventions was critical. Health in Harmony has now been expanded to Madagascar and Brazil. 5.4.3.2 Intercultural health services (H02) Intercultural healthcare is an approach that aims to bridge the gap between Indigenous and Western (or biomedical) health systems, emphasizing mutual recognition and respect of different knowledges, the scope of these medical practices and the conditions that they treat . They are formal arrangements between health departments and traditional health practitioners to provide dual health services. Notable examples of biomedical health systems integrated with traditional medicine are found in India (with Ayurvedic practices) and in China (with traditional Chinese medicine) and are also found in New Zealand, Australia, Canada, Brazil, Suriname, Chile and Colombia, among other countries . Access to intercultural healthcare programs and services could benefit around 476.6 million Indigenous Peoples representing ?6.2 per cent of the world’s population . It is estimated that approximately 80 per cent of the world's population, both Indigenous Peoples and other traditional and local communities, use traditional healthcare and medicinal plants (see Section 5.4.3.4, H04, Sustainable use of medicinal plants). However, the best treatment for many diseases includes modern medical and surgical knowledge delivered with respect to cultural and traditional practice . Recognition of cultural determinants of health are essential in reducing differences in health outcomes in IPLC in Australia and have been shown to improve community well-being, in Chile, Suriname and Colombia . As demonstrated in Brazil , recently contacted Indigenous populations are vulnerable to adverse effects of biomedical care. Intercultural health services have a key role to play in the management of emerging risks, such as food insecurity, water insecurity and vector-borne disease (Hofmeijer, 2013). They significantly advance the goal of providing nutritious and culturally appropriate food, a critical component of overall well-being . Noteworthy initiatives have been undertaken in Thailand, Canada and Colombia (Figures 5.4.5 and 5.4.6). Holistic approaches to health that incorporate respect and care for nature (for example, Box 5.4.4) are features of many Indigenous health systems. These are undermined by loss of access by IPLC to their lands and resources and from the impacts of climate change and biodiversity loss. The therapeutic properties of treatments are also affected by these factors. As the productivity of agro-ecosystems is reduced, traditional medicines compete poorly with biomedical healthcare . A potential trade-off of intercultural health services involves the use of wild animal products as traditional medicine or as a health food. Species may be threatened so that biodiversity becomes a trade-off with health. In China, traditional and allopathic medical care systems are closely integrated, and large hospitals commonly use both wild plant and animal products for healthcare. Some of these practices, e.g., the butchering process or drinking raw blood, are potential pathways for zoonotic infection and therefore a trade-off with health itself . Intercultural health services are demonstrating effectiveness in providing cost-effective solutions (Figure 5.4.6). Traditional health services, often the first choice for various conditions, result in significant savings for biomedical health services . However, traditional healers often face a lack of professional recognition and receive no monetary compensation for their services, for example, traditional midwives in Bolivia and Guetemala collaborating with hospitals have no expenses covered . However, in some places such as remote Australia, Canada and New Zealand healthcare systems support traditional healers Intercultural health services are also key in promoting equity (Figure 5.4.6), particularly in access to healthcare (see Section 5.4.3.1, H01, Universal health coverage) as community driven responses including Indigenous knowledge, go beyond conventional healthcare systems, with a strong emphasis on cultural relevance and Indigenous self-governance . Reorienting health services and governance systems based on the capabilities of Indigenous Peoples , strengthens control over resources ad land . IPLC’ traditional medical practices link cultural and biological diversity and recognizing these through intercultural health services has potential to be transformative (Figure 5.4.6). However, cultural values are undermined when these practices are decontextualized, commoditized or appropriated. Unsustainable demand for traditional medicines can lead to a negative effect on the nexus elements and on the IPLC who are legitimate holders of this knowledge . Traditional medicines may be commercialized and distributed back to communities for profit, devoid of any communal benefits (Rose, 2012). Additionally, some rituals and practices, such as the consumption of specific plants or fungi, have gained popularity among outsiders for recreational purposes, inadvertently causing displeasure among spiritual entities and inducing scarcity for IPLC. Biopiracy threatens equitable distribution of benefits derived from ILK and biodiversity used to address Indigenous health issues. Brazil has addressed this by introducing new regulations focusing on access, benefit sharing, utilization or third-party transfer . Box 5.4.4. Intercultural health programs and services in Latin America Different experiences in Latin America provide evidence of the diversity of intercultural health programs and services. In Chile, the Mapuche political organization within the intercultural health framework has generated improved access to both Western and traditional medicine. Inclusion of Mapuche medical knowledge with biomedical knowledge in the hospital has been shaped by interactions between community participation and the relatively distanced, yet supportive, state. There are initiatives where Indigenous healers actively participate by supporting home visit activities and complementary health actions aimed at activating the relationship between Indigenous and biomedical healthcare. For example, an intercultural and complementary health approach to diabetes and mental healthcare includes being treated jointly by Indigenous medicine agents and psychologists and knowledge exchanges between Indigenous doctors and health teams. With the incorporation of Indigenous health services in the healthcare network, spaces have been set up for care, delivery of Indigenous medicine supplies based on herbal therapeutic resources from the territories and referral of care in ancestral community spaces that are now included in the statistical records of activities. There has also been promotion of medicinal gardens . Knowledge exchange is facilitated by regular meetings. For example, Machi Herminda Nahuelpi, Mapuche Ancestral Medicine Agent, was visited by the Rayen Leufú Association and intercultural facilitators of the Concepción Health Service for health teams to gain insight into the experience developed since 2006 in addressing the needs of users seeking healthcare with greater intercultural relevance to the Mapuche community and their neighbours (Figure 5.4.8). In Peru, Comando Matico COVID-19 was developed by a group of Shipibo-Conibo youth from the Ucayali region in the Peruvian Amazon and has demonstrated the capacity of Indigenous Peoples to implement intercultural initiatives autonomous from the central State. The Comando Matico COVID-19 emerged to confront the spread of the pandemic by using Indigenous knowledge and practices, with the objective of using Indigenous medicine to alleviate the symptoms of Indigenous community members who did not have economic resources or access to the other medical services . In Cauca, Colombia, the involvement of Indigenous organizations has facilitated Indigenous governance and management of healthcare, with intercultural health playing a pivotal role in garnering community support for backyard edible gardens. By promoting these gardens, the diversity of green vegetables incorporated into diets has increased. The cultivation of these gardens not only brought local produce closer to households but also contributed to the stability of their availability, ultimately enriching the sustainable supply of healthy food sources . Figure 5.4.8. Concepción Health Service in Chile. Photo credit: Rayen Leufú, Association under licence CC BY-NC4.0. 5.4.3.3 Net-zero sustainable healthcare (H03) Healthcare providers and the health sector broadly are addressing the need for urgent action to safeguard health by reducing their carbon and environmental footprints. This is being done by changing practices (including increased prevention and community care to reduce the need for hospitalisation), and materials, energy sources and procurement across supply chains . Over sixty countries pledged action to achieve net-zero sustainable healthcare following the United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change COP26 Health Programme and more than 68,000 hospitals and health centres in 81 countries are members of Healthcare Without Harm’s Global Green and Healthy Hospitals network . The impact of transforming healthcare can be estimated as a reduction of 2.26 × 10?4 excess deaths from heat, per ton of greenhouse gas emissions avoided per year . Healthcare carbon footprints range from 1.51 t/greenhouse gas/per capita in resource-intensive healthcare in the United States of America to 0.06 t/greenhouse gas/per capita in India . Reducing all impacts across the nexus would have even greater impact (Figure 5.4.5). Health harms from air and water pollution and plastic and biomedical waste are also targeted. Water conservation and protection from contamination with pharmaceuticals (in concert with efforts to reduce waste and pollution from pharmaceutical production and prescribing is being addressed in the United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern Ireland , United States of America , Australia and Europe . Food and catering are a major area of healthcare resource use and shifts to providing menus based on locally produced, plant based sustainable healthy diets ( see Section 5.4.3.6, H06, Reduce meat overconsumption; Box 5.4.7) with reduced food (and all other) waste are increasingly being adopted by hospitals . All of these changes benefit biodiversity indirectly but ‘greening’ of hospitals and land owned and operated by health sector or organizations is also occurring in recognition of the benefits of nature-based therapies and prescribing (Section 5.4.3.5, H05, Nature on prescription) and the opportunity to increase and improve the area available for biodiversity (Box 5.4.5). These initiatives have broad social acceptability and are potentially transformative. They can be enabled by more regulatory approaches by national and subnational institutions (Figure 5.4.5). For example, hospitals in New York are required by law to offer plant-based meals so some have made it the default menu . This option strongly supports implementation of the Paris Agreement (Figure 5.4.6). Equitable, global progress to net zero and sustainable healthcare require leadership from high-income countries for innovation and decarbonization. Economic risks to low-income countries from restructuring are possible, raising some concerns regarding distributional equity . In many low-lying island states and exposed vulnerable populations, the priority is in developing climate-resilient health services . Transforming the health sector may catalyse other sectors to do the same, and increase public awareness and support. Reported savings are predominantly from investment in disease prevention, energy and waste reduction . Box 5.4.5. Greening the United Kingdom National Health Service The United Kingdom National Health Service is a pioneer in mapping and addressing its greenhouse gas emissions, including scope 1 (energy use, e.g., hospital power, patient transport) and scope 3 (embedded within supply chains, e.g., food, pharmaceuticals, equipment) emissions, and has reduced greenhouse gas emissions by 26 per cent since 1990 . It is also the first health system to embed net-zero targets for healthcare into national legislation . Each of the National Health Service’s trusts have developed ‘green plans’ as part of their Integrated Care Systems (e.g., ) to guide reduction and decarbonization of transport and delivery, increase investment in disease prevention, optimize prescribing, reduce unnecessary treatments and pathology, and use low greenhouse gas or carbon alternatives where possible (e.g., for anaesthetic gasses, food and catering). The next phase of ‘green plans’ will prioritize space for nature and adaptation to climate change , although there are already projects addressing these issues. In addition, the United Kingdom Environment Act 2021 now requires all new land developments to generate 10 per cent biodiversity net gain. The National Health Service owns 6,500 hectares of land in the United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland and has strategies to use the land for improving health and well-being, including by creating greenspaces for physical activity, play, social activity and food growing (see Section 5.4.3.5, H05, Nature on prescription; Section 5.4.3.9, H09, Urban green Infrastructure; Box 5.4.7). The National Health Service has also targeted increasing the quality of greenspace for wildlife as well as people. Local health districts have redeveloped healthcare greenspace as restorative sites for nature and social contact and to provide sensory stimulation and nutrition from kitchen gardens . Social housing, renewable energy (see Section 5.5.4.6, C06, Solar photovoltaics on land) and greenspace that can act as flood plains (see Section 5.2.3.13, W13, Water-sensitive urban infrastructure) are other uses for this land . Furthermore, the United Kingdom Centre for Sustainable Healthcare program NHS Forest has planted 100,000 trees on or near 330 National Health Service sites since 2009. These woodlands (and wildflower meadows and food gardens) have been made creatively accessible to residents and patients and their health benefits and their contribution to ecosystem services (reducing temperature, flooding, noise, pollution and carbon sequestration) have been valued up to £16,000 p.a. for woodland covering <1-5ha of National Health Service land adjacent to hospitals . 5.4.3.4 Sustainable use of medicinal plants (H04) Traditional and complementary medicines usage is reported by 88 per cent of WHO member states and an estimated 4 billion people across all countries critically rely on wild flora and fauna sourced medicines (Figure 5.4.7). Between 50,000-70,000 medicinal plants are used globally : 35,407 species have well-documented use , 3,000 species are globally traded and 60-90 per cent are sourced from the wild with high risk of overextraction . While IPLC hold extensive associated knowledge of health and nutrition inherently linked to their diverse socioecological systems, there is also historically codified traditional medicinal plant knowledge that is well integrated and regulated within national health systems (e.g., Ayurveda and Traditional Chinese Medicine ) that is driving significant global demand for wild resources . This highlights the need for comprehensive assessments on medicinal plant sourcing, conservation, trade, cultivation status and usage patterns within the health sector . Promotion of sustainable harvest and use and conservation education among stakeholders is in progress. Strengthening the value chain and developing economic opportunities for dependent communities to facilitate integrated biodiversity planning can strengthen ecological stewardship among communities . Technological innovations for improving yield and quality of medicinal plants, identification of possible substitutes for endangered species and large-scale, in-situ and ex-situ conservation programs are also gaining recognition and application . Use of traditional and complementary medicines contributes to accessible healthcare, especially in regions where it is less developed. Some phytochemicals and traditional therapies have a potential role in treating neglected tropical diseases such as leishmaniasis , trypanosomiasis , filariasis , chikungunya , helminthiasis ; malaria , non-communicable diseases and mental well-being (Fajemiroye, 2016; Pferschy-Wenzig, 2022; Saki, 2014) . Medicinal plants continue to be a source of bioactive leads and effective drug development and hold potential with novel methods . Integrated research on potential health benefits, quality, safety and sustainable use need to be expanded and the recently established WHO Global Traditional Medicine Centre has a strategic plan to support evidence synthesis and research on these traditional medicinal practices globally. Implementation of free, prior and informed consent and appropriate mechanisms to assure equity for traditional knowledge holders in compliance with the Nagoya protocol requires ongoing attention (Figure 5.4.6). Climate change and biodiversity loss have created significant impact on the present and future availability and quality of medicinal plants . Climate migration can also reduce access to these resources . Medicinal plants can contribute to healthy food due to their rich sources of health-promoting ingredients . Agro-biodiversity systems such as homestead gardens can improve access to these plants . A significant portion of medicinal plants are aquatic or semi-aquatic ; water stress can impact on availability of secondary metabolites , while contaminated water can lead to uptake of toxic substances . This response option can enhance nature’s contributions to people and conservation targets , but may also result in unsustainable use (Figure 5.4.5). Medicinal plant and biofuel cultivation may have negative impacts on food production . Conservation and sustainable use management ), increased cultivation and landscape restoration programs show indications of positive impact on the status of medicinal plants as well as nexus elements. For example, the National Medicinal Plants Board of the Government of India supports in-situ conservation, research and education across the country with 110 medicinal plants conservation areas with ex-situ conservation within medicinal plant conservation parks . Seventy-two medicinal plant conservation development areas have more recently focused on community engagement and opportunities for alternative livelihoods, such as through enrichment of existing farming systems and integration of medicinal plants with joint forest management (. As 90 per cent of medicinal plant species in India occur in forests, appropriate governance and multistakeholder partnerships between the Department of Forestry, research organizations and communities are key to successful implementation . 5.4.3.5 Nature on prescription (H05) Health promotion through contact with nature is supported by nature on prescription and nature-based health interventions when healthcare professionals refer individual patients or groups with a range of health conditions to activities in nature . There is increasing evidence that the natural environment promotes health through supporting physical activity, reducing stress and/or depression, prevention or treatment of obesity and diabetes, cooling during heat waves, and improved quality of life, well-being and social support (Figure 5.4.5). This includes blue (water) and green spaces (see also Section 5.1.3.2) as healing environments. Natural environments also enable beneficial contact with microbial biodiversity and associated health benefits (Box 5.4.6). Research on health benefits of nature contact is not distributed evenly geographically. Mental health benefits are mainly reported in Europe and Asia, suggesting a bias . Risk of tick-borne diseases in some regions also highlights the existence of negative consequences . Nature-based health interventions can support biodiversity conservation when combined with increased nature connectedness and pro-environmental behaviour but can also put pressure on biodiversity. This became more apparent in the lockdowns during the COVID pandemic . Nature on prescription can promote use of greenspace during heat waves, e.g., among vulnerable groups like the elderly . Health benefits of community gardening and care (or social) farms are relevant to nature on prescription (Figure 5.4.9). Figure 5.4.9. Care Garden, Residential and Care Centre Alfons Smet Residences, Dessel, Belgium. Photo credit Marc Mangelschots under licence CC BY NC ND 4.0. Nature on prescription is emerging as a complementary healthcare approach , and can be applied globally (Figure 5.4.7). For example, nature on prescription is gaining interest from the healthcare sector (e.g., in the United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern Ireland and Canada ) but is far from common practice and needs implementation and evaluation research . Access inequalities through gentrification and unequal accessibility to natural areas can impact equity of nature on prescription (Figure 5.4.6). In terms of avoided healthcare costs, it has the potential to contribute significantly at a population level to address non-communicable diseases , as well as in decreasing the environmental footprint of the healthcare sector (Section 5.4.3.3, H03, Net-zero sustainable healthcare) . However, nature on prescription cost-effective studies are currently lacking . Nature on prescription is potentially consistent with ILK, inclusive and participatory. There is limited evidence of transformative change potential for this response option alone, though when combined with other initiatives for example through nature connectedness, nature conservation, community garden activities and social prescription it has significant transformative potential (Figure 5.4.6). Box 5.4.6. Beneficial contact with microbial biodiversity for health Recent research highlights the pivotal role of beneficial contact with diverse microbial communities for human health. The environmental microbiome of heavily urbanized regions differs significantly from the microbiome of areas with higher naturalness , which can have negative implications for urban human health. The biodiversity hypothesis of health proposes that humans require contact with natural environments, as they can enrich the human microbiome and protect against diseases by promoting immune balance (Figure 5.4.10). Several studies implementing next-generation microbiome sequencing suggest that microorganisms present in the air, on plants and in other compartments of the environment can transfer to the human microbiome. For example, contact with urban green spaces can increase diversity and shape the microbial community composition of the human skin and airways . Figure 5.4.10. Staphylococci sp. attached to human skin cells. Commensal bacteria such as Staphylococci sp. (green dots) can colonize the human skin and train our immune system. Photo credit Irina Spacova under licence CC BY-NC-ND 4.0. Recent microbiome insights pave the way for leveraging urban green spaces and other nature-based solutions not only to mitigate climate change and improve biodiversity, but also to provide co-benefits of improved human health outcomes via microbiome-mediated mechanisms. The Microbiome Rewilding Hypothesis proposes that restoring biodiverse habitats in urban green spaces can rewild the environmental microbiome to a state that enhances primary prevention of human disease . While controlled double-blind nature-based intervention studies are highly challenging to organize, the initial insights regarding the protective effects of microbially biodiverse environments are promising. For example, implementation of microbially-enriched biodiverse sand in daycare centres in Finland was shown to beneficially modulate children’s skin microbiome, which was also linked with enhancement of regulatory immune pathways . Human exposure to ocean water was likewise shown to increase the skin microbiome diversity . However, some of the ocean bacteria that were deposited on human skin can cause infection, highlighting the importance of microbial water quality. Further investigation of the microbially-mediated mechanisms of action is necessary for effective implementation of nature-based health interventions by health professionals, urban planners and other stakeholders. Increasing urbanization, globalization and climate change can disrupt the human microbiome balance. Beneficial bacteria naturally present in the human microbiome or in fermented foods are protective against infectious diseases via multifactorial modes of action, such as antipathogenic activity, immune system stimulation and microbiome modulation. By implementing rigorous selection pipelines based on bacterial safety, applicability and functionality it is possible to select and formulate novel live biotherapeutics, for example against viral diseases . Recent studies have already implemented beneficial bacteria against COVID-19 in the form of a throat spray and in food supplements , with promising trends observed regarding reduction of viral loads and reported symptoms. This can translate into significant health and economic benefits. A modelling study predicted that intake of beneficial bacteria by the population of the United States of America could lead to a yearly $1.4 billion total savings for society . Consequently, microbiome-based solutions represent a cost-effective strategy to improve health, while helping address the rising burden of antibiotic prescriptions and preserving the human microbiome balance. 5.4.3.6 Reduce meat overconsumption (H06) Reducing overconsumption of red and processed meat is a globally applicable response option wherever overconsumption takes place (Figure 5.4.7). Currently, health recommendations vary between 98-500g (cooked) lean red meat/week for adults and little or no processed meat . The associated burden of disease and recommended intake continue to be revised with variation between studies related to specific health risks and reference populations . Moderating consumption of red meat has synergies with other nexus elements, particularly climate change and biodiversity, with few trade-offs (Figure 5.4.5). The response option recognises that undernutrition associated with underconsumption (including for meat-associated iron, B12 and zinc, and protein) persists in many populations . The highest overconsumption of red meat occurs in high-income Australasia, North America, western Europe and southern and tropical Latin America, with 2.1 to 2.8 times optimal intake for reduced all-cause mortality .et al., 2019) (see also Sections 2.3.1.2 and 2.3.2.1). The highest overconsumption of processed meat occurs in high-income North America, Asia Pacific, western and eastern Europe and southern Latin America, with 4 to 13 times optimal intake . This response option has the potential to reduce approximately 4.9 million disability-adjusted life years (DALYs) and 155,000 deaths from diets high in red and processed meat globally per year. When combined with increased intake of plant-based foods (especially whole grains, fruits, nuts and seeds), there is a further global potential to reduce approximately 197 million DALYs and 7 million deaths per year from diets low in these food items . This aspect of healthy diets is linked to response options discussed in other subchapters (F11/C15, Sustainable healthy diets, Section 5.3.3.11 and Section 5.5.3.15). Reducing meat overconsumption improves the environmental performance of food production and reduces greenhouse gas emissions . The mean land use needed to produce 100 g of protein is highest for lamb/mutton (185 m2year) and beef from beef herds (164 m2year) . Similarly, the mean greenhouse gas emissions to produce 100 g of protein also are highest for beef from beef herds (50 kgCO2eq) and for lamb/mutton (20 kgCO2eq). In comparison, a diet without these two red meat types, and accounting for increased consumption of plant-based foods, would reduce global land use by 46 per cent (from 4,130 to 2,210 million ha). In addition, it would reduce terrestrial acidification by 6 per cent (from 89.0 to 83.4 MtSO2eq) and eutrophication by 9 per cent (from 64.7 to 58.8 Mt phosphate eq) . In principle, freed-up land could be managed in part to improve its condition and level of native biodiversity (see Section 5.1.3.7) and in part for sustainable intensification of plant-based food production (see Sections 5.1.3.3 and 5.3.3.3). This response option would reduce greenhouse gas emissions by 20 per cent (from 13.65 to 10.98 GtCO2eq) . There are several countries in which the response option is included in national priorities and it is endorsed by 21 cities worldwide . While there is broad international support for this response option , its social acceptability is variable, depending on several factors, including frequency of meat consumption, attitude toward environmental protection and concern about animal welfare (Figure 5.4.6). Compared with the cost of current diets in upper-middle-income to high-income countries, nutritionally balanced and predominantly plant-based dietary patterns are up to 22-34 per cent lower in cost on average . Such a diet change has high transformative change potential, given its high potential to facilitate innovations (e.g., disruptive technology like cultured meat), transform structures across the food chain, and lead to new views that promote a sustainable future. There is growing interest in animal products produced through systems that enhance carbon sequestration (see Chapter 5.3, Section 5.4.3.5, F05, Ecological intensification - rangelands) and produced locally with greater connection between consumers and producers (Box 5.4.7; see also Section 5.3.3.12, F12, City region food systems). Sustainable management of fish and game, particularly for IPLC are also an important resource for sustainable healthy diets (Box 5.4.2). Box 5.4.7. Farmer-consumer partnerships, Herenboeren (“Gentry Farmers”), in the Netherlands (Kingdom of the) Transforming the relationship between consumers, food producers and the production and consumption of healthy and sustainably produced food, including meat, is illustrated by Herenboeren (“Gentry Farmers”) in the Netherlands (Kingdom of the) that support citizens to develop nature-driven cooperative farming communities. These are mixed farms of around 20 hectares, each supplying food to about 200-270 households (about 500 people). Together, these households are co-owners of their Gentry Farm, and employ several farmers to run the farm as agricultural managers. Every week, co-owners collect their share of the harvest, which can include vegetables, fruit, eggs and (if requested) occasional meat. Herbs, grain or potatoes are also possible. Each household puts in a one-off payment of €2,000 to €2,500 to start a Gentry Farm. Single-person households often pay half this amount. For running costs, there is a weekly contribution of about €10 to €15 per person. As a national organization, Foundation Gentry Farmers Netherlands coordinates the setting up and successful operation of all Gentry Farms. Each new initiative group concludes a cooperation agreement with the foundation based on the three pillars and seven principles of the Gentry Farmers concept. The three pillars are: nature-driven, socially connected and economically supported. The seven principles of a Gentry Farm require that they are cooperative; they minimize hired labour as guaranteed in the budget; production geared to the nutritional needs of the members; products are not sold; financing is only through deposits and contributions; production is nature-driven; and that they are an active part of a learning and experimenting network. Nature-driven food production also means that the natural behaviour and habitat of animals are paramount. Animals (as in nature) have a function in an ecosystem. They ensure healthy soil through the provision of manure, and their rooting and treading also improve the soil. In time, nature-driven food production systems develop into ecosystems that autonomously establish how many (consumable) animals they can support. In this way small numbers of animals are available for meat consumption from a production system with optimal welfare considerations. Since 2013, 18 of these Gentry Farms have been launched across the country. The approach of Gentry Farmers encourages responsible use of agricultural land and brings farmers and citizens closer together. This example illustrates the potential for local farmers to assist consumers to view food, including meat, differently to transition to a sustainable healthy diet. Furthermore, increasing interest from parties to establish a Gentry Farm in other countries has led the foundation to seek ways to support the international expansion of the concept . 5.4.3.7 Pollution prevention (H07) Health risks from pollution (air, water and soil) are influenced by anthropogenic actions including human induced pollution, dietary and consumption behaviour, unregulated economic growth, industrialization and demographics . Estimates indicate that 9 million premature deaths occur per year from pollution, of which 90 per cent are in lower-middle-income countries and among vulnerable populations, with variation in attributable sources of pollution and in disease burdens within regions and countries . There are multiple benefits to other nexus elements by linking pollution prevention and health protection (Figure 5.4.5). Reductions in indoor and ambient air pollution, biomass consumption, and air-water-chemical pollution-induced habitat and biodiversity loss can enhance access to safe water and clean air, enhance sustainable food production and food and nutritional security and avoid the emergence of diseases . Several interconnections exist between climate change, biodiversity and pollution management for advancing human well-being . Examples include the sustainable use of medicinal plants and reducing pollution within the health and care sectors through reduced emissions and residues, promotion of clean energy, green infrastructure and aligned procurement policies . Reducing pollution was also assessed by other subchapters of Chapter 5 (see Section 5.2.3.12, W12, Reduce water pollution; Sections 5.3.3.7, 5.3.3.8 and 5.3.3.9 (F07, F08 and F09, reducing nutrient, pesticide and plastic pollution, respectively; Section 5.5.3.12, C12, Reduce short-lived climate pollutants). Actions to benefit health and other nexus elements includes labelling of both nutritional and environmental outcomes for promoting healthy diets ; command and control instruments such as bans (e.g., for plastic pollution); impact evaluations to trigger response options such as water pollution treatment for reducing morbidity and air pollution reduction for increasing life expectancy in lower-middle-income countries ; reducing harm from noise and air pollution by increased exposure to biodiversity ; and lowering risks of mortality and morbidity from air pollution reduction through green areas . Solutions for pollution may come with economic costs, including those borne by individuals (e.g., through temporary job losses in response to changing technologies), public or private entities (e.g., losses from closure of thermal power plants), and governments (e.g., costs of funding transitions to net zero or changing land use in favour of conservation) (Figure 5.4.6). This deters action even where cost-effective technologies are available (e.g., improved cookstoves, clean cooking fuels, greening infrastructural interventions, tackling short-lived climate pollutants and waste treatment in healthcare and pharmaceutical sectors) . The high social costs of pollution (e.g., lowered labour productivity, morbidity, mortality, opportunity costs from diversion of public resources, including finance and human resources to deal with the consequences for healthcare) and the fact that markets fail to convey these costs to society, calls for publicly supported actions for pollution prevention. The social costs of inaction are usually much higher than the costs of prevention . While restoration is costlier than conservation for both ecosystems and human health , there is evidence that the health co-benefits to humans and nature from climate change mitigation, inclusive of air pollution reduction, are greater than the costs of such policies . Reducing pollution and improving health, supported by a judicious mix of regulatory, financial and social mechanisms, and supported by value systems, can be used to realize the social gains, internalize externalities and manage costs. For example, efforts to minimize land and water pollution have been initiated as actions to enhance relational (nature for culture) values in parts of the Hindu Kush Himalaya . Health actors can engage in evidence generation, management and co-creation of integrated public policies influencing funding, social behaviour and legal instruments to prevent pollution. Pollution reduction is key to achieving global goals and targets on health as enshrined in the SDGs, the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework and Paris Agreement (Figure 5.4.6). Reducing air pollution has transformative potential reaching across nexus elements, with the most immediate and significant benefits and co-benefits accruing to human health (Figure 5.4.11). Figure 5.4.11. Anthropogenic sources of air pollution impact human health adversely. 5.4.3.8 Mangrove conservation and restoration for health (H08) Disaster risks from coastal hazards that threaten lives and livelihoods such as flooding, storms, tsunamis and sea level rise can be mitigated by nature-based solutions provided by mangroves. Mangrove conservation and restoration can be applied globally within the natural range of mangroves which are also key ecological niches at the interface between land and water in the tropics and subtropics (Figure 5.4.7) (see also Section 5.1.3.1, B01, Area-based conservation; Section 5.1.3.6, B06, Restoration of coastal and marine systems; Section 5.3.3.6, F06, Ecological intensification - aquatic foods; Section 5.5.3.13, C13, Restoration of coastal and marine ecosystems for carbon sequestration). The ongoing destruction of mangrove is a threat to human, animal and ecosystem health (see also Sections 2.3.1.1, 2.4.1 and 2.4.4). Mangrove conservation and restoration has synergies across health and other elements of the nexus (Figure 5.4.5). Its health benefit is based on the combination of significant reduction of lives lost from extreme events and the provision of medicinal plants and animals , although associated with mosquitoes . More than 1 billion people live within 50 km of mangrove forests potentially benefit from them . This response option improves water quality by filtration and absorption of effluents from aquaculture operations and land-based sources of pollution . Mangroves provide shrimp and fish for healthy and culturally appropriate food and are an important support for fisheries due to their function as fish nurseries . Maintaining and restoring mangroves helps to promote biodiversity by increasing the area of mangroves in good condition and providing critical habitat for an estimated 341 threatened species of reptiles, amphibians, mammals, fishes and birds . Restoring and conserving mangroves aligns with multiple international frameworks and is explicitly included in and actioned through the national priorities of many countries (Figure 5.4.6). Recognition of broad health benefits of mangroves is uneven but disaster risk reduction, including for climate resilient health systems, is one driver of action . In the absence of health coverage, mangroves are protected locally as an important source of traditional medicines (see also Section 5.4.4, H04, Sustainable use of medicinal plants). Summing up all measured goods and services, the total economic value of managing intact mangroves is substantially higher than conversion to aquaculture . The response option is technically feasible (Figure 5.4.7), provides fair distribution of costs and benefits to communities involved, and allows for participation of local stakeholders in decision-making and implementation. It is equitable in that it can protect the local use of mangroves by local, vulnerable groups from competition with outsiders (Figure 5.4.6). It also facilitates a shift in views and values of mangrove forest from short-term extraction to long-term stewardship and sustainable use. 5.4.3.9 Urban green infrastructure (H09) Urban green infrastructure broadly defined as a network of natural, semi-natural and artificial green spaces that deliver multiple ecosystem services , is a strategy to transform urban environments to be more healthy, liveable and biodiverse . Management of urban green and blue spaces affects the health and well-being of over 50 per cent of the global population (Figure 5.4.7). However, only 27 per cent global urban centres had adequate greenspace in 2021 . This response option complements others (see B02/C14, Urban nature-based solutions in Section 5.1.3.2 and Section 5.5.3.14) with a focus on urban and peri-urban forests and trees, which can be a cost-effective way to improve health outcomes and address multiple socioecological needs. Urban green infrastructure provides multiple health benefits (Figure 5.4.5). Green space metrics are associated with reduced cardiovascular, respiratory and heat-related mortality and increased life expectancy . Urban green infrastructure is consistently associated with better mental health, elevated mood levels and lower anxiety across life stages . Increasing tree canopy could potentially reduce hundreds of premature deaths within a single urban area, valued at billions of dollars . Urban trees can increase biodiversity and ecosystem resilience . High tree diversity areas support more animal diversity and are more resilient to climate change, pests and diseases than tree monocultures . Publicly accessible fruit and nut trees also improve food access and reduce food insecurity . Urban green infrastructure also filters stormwater pollutants and helps maintain local water supplies , further benefitting health. Moreover, it can improve ecological connectivity , but knowledge gaps remain around which configurations maximize connectivity regionally and how to minimize vector-borne disease risks . Estimates of climate change mitigation potential and urban green infrastructure outcomes generally (Chapter 5.1, Figure 5.1.5) are limited to specific cities and geographical regions (e.g., Quaranta, 2021). One estimate of urban greening mitigation potential globally is 1.7-3.6 GtCO2/yr (Lal, 2018), which likely represents the upper range. Other benefits may be more important than carbon sequestration. Moderate increases in tree cover improve climate change adaptation potential through improved air quality , flood risk mitigation and reduced heat island impacts . Urban green infrastructure is largely viewed as a low-cost intervention to address multiple environmental and human health benefits (Figure 5.4.6; Box 5.4.6; Box 5.4.8). Increasing tree cover is a goal frequently incorporated into municipal plans (e.g., Million Tree initiatives) and national and multi-national strategies. The return on investment for urban green infrastructure varies but economic benefits typically outweigh costs (e.g., increased property values and reduced costs associated with storm water infrastructure, heat reduction, building energy use and healthcare . Equitable access to high quality green infrastructure is critical to reduce disparities and maximize health benefits and is a specific SDG target (11.7) (Figure 5.4.6). The WHO recommends all people reside within 300m of green space at least 1 hectare in size ; the 3-30-300 rule recommends three visible trees from each home and at least 30 per cent tree cover in every neighbourhood . Yet, the more vulnerable often reside in areas with less and lower quality green space and benefit less from associated ecosystem services . Those with lower socioeconomic status achieve proportionally greater health benefits from high-quality nearby natural areas . Therefore, addressing distributional equity would improve health and social outcomes and increase population-level benefits . Co-production and co-design processes are increasingly used in urban green infrastructure planning but are not applied universally. Urban green infrastructure has benefits across nexus elements and demonstrated implementation feasibility. Greenspace must be carefully managed to support local biodiversity while limiting disease transmission risks (see Section 5.4.3.11, H11, Biodiversity management for zoonoses), allergens, storm damage and fire. Thoughtful planning is also critical to achieve more equitable health outcomes . Nevertheless, urban green infrastructure can support structural and institutional transformation by enabling co-production and co-design of urban spaces and shifting views and values toward a more sustainable urban future (Figure 5.4.6). Box 5.4.8 Singapore: A biophilic city A growing number of different initiatives seek to develop high quality urban green infrastructure that benefit nature and human health (e.g., National Park Cities, BioCities, Biophilic Cities). Particularly relevant to health is biophilic design, which aims to increase human-nature connections to benefit human health, well-being and quality of life. Key strategies of biophilic design cover several other nexus elements besides health . Interest in biophilic design has led to an increasing number of cities implementing various biophilic programs, policies and initiatives . This process has been facilitated by the Biophilic Cities Network (www.biophiliccities.org/partner-cities), a global network of partner cities dedicated to improving the connection between people and nature in urban settings. Singapore is one of the best examples of a biophilic city with its vision of becoming a “City in a Garden” , where natural elements are an intentional and mainstream component of urban design. Although the population of Singapore rose by nearly two million people between 1986-2007, the country managed to increase green cover from 36 to 47 per cent during that time . Open spaces have been transformed to parks or gardens, and greenways or park connectors allow people to use green space to transit between different areas of the city. Vegetation is also strategically used on roofs and building facades to reduce urban heat island effects and purify air. One example of successful implementation of biophilic design is the award-winning Koo Teck Puat Hospital. The hospital offers park-like ambiance for patients and caregivers, food producing roof gardens, and bird and butterfly habitat. The effective use of vegetation and water features also serve to cool the building. Singapore has also purposefully designed therapeutic gardens to address needs of local communities , guided by theories of attention restoration, stress reduction and biophilia. The city currently has 10 such gardens with plans to have 30 by 2030. For example, the therapeutic garden in Bishan-Ang Mo Kio Park incorporates biodiversity, fragrance, edibles, medicinals, and colours and texture zones targeted to elderly residents and those with dementia, accompanied by therapeutic horticulture engagement activities (Figure 5.4.12). Emerging biophilic cities such as Singapore use a variety of reinforcing policies, laws and programs to promote nature. Singapore combines a landscape replacement policy with other efforts that subsidize urban greening while simultaneously investing in education, outreach and research. A specialized management department called the Skyrise Greenery Section encourages rooftop and vertical greenery by publishing design guidelines and providing incentives for installation via a Skyrise Greenery Incentive Scheme. In partnership with the Workforce Development Agency, Singapore’s NParks offers training opportunities for the landscape industry through the Centre for Urban Greenery and Ecology. Community engagement happens in various ways, including through Community in Bloom and Plant-A-Tree programs. Singapore has also made deliberate efforts to engage the private sector. Creativity and innovation are encouraged among developers through awards programs and financial subsidies from NParks, which also serve to improve designs and increase public recognition for projects . Incorporating biophilic concepts into architecture and city planning that improve health outcomes requires collaboration among multiple actors, including government, developers, health professionals, design professionals, property managers and community members. As demonstrated in Singapore, government initiatives can successfully facilitate urban greening. However, participation by civil society organizations such as non-profits and non-governmental organizations are also critical, particularly as the importance of co-design processes are increasingly recognized. Long-term success of greening efforts such as the Biophilic Cities initiative requires robust and extensive social networks to help ensure projects are designed and implemented in ways that meet local community and environmental needs . Additional efforts are also needed to ensure implementation of biophilic approaches are holistic and centre human health and well-being . Design processes that ensure sociocultural nuances of a place are understood and addressed in addition to environmental and health-related needs are most likely to be accepted by the community and become part of the place identity . Figure 5.4.12. Bishan-Ang Mo Kio Park, Singapore is one of the city’s largest urban parks. It features the re-naturalised Kellang River, many recreational opportunities, a resident specific therapeutic garden and is part of an island-wide network of linear green corridors and walkways linking major parks and nature areas. Photo credit: cattan2011 via Flickr under licence CC-BY 2.0. 5.4.3.10 Forest conservation for health (H10) Forests are critical for human, animal and ecosystem health for their role in removing carbon dioxide from the atmosphere and storing it in exchange for oxygen (see also Ssection 5.5.3.12, C12, Forest-based practices to address climate change). Their loss through removal and fire is a source of direct harm to human health, including through extensive air pollution and respiratory disease . However, deforestation continues to take place at alarming rates (see also Sections 2.3.1, 2.4.1, 2.4.2, 2.4.4, 2.5.2.1 and 2.5.2.2). Promoting forest conservation is a response option that can be applied globally wherever forests exist (Figure 5.4.7). Benefits and impacts are from local to global scales, with 60 million people, many IPLC, living within and wholly dependent on forests . Exposure to forests is also associated with positive health benefits in pregnancy and childhood (foetal growth, brain development, mental health in childhood, and prevention and reduction of childhood infectious diseases and malnutrition), adult life (mental and spiritual well-being and quality of life, and cardiometabolic health), and later stages of life (strong positive effects on cognitive decline and dementia, mental health and well-being, and longevity and mortality) . Forest conservation efforts enable these exposures and can also reduce malaria prevalence (Figure 5.4.5). Water quality for both humans and ecosystems is improved by forest conservation, since an estimated 75 per cent of the world’s accessible freshwater comes from forested watersheds . Forest conservation provides forest-based foods and products to an estimated one billion people . It is well established that the benefits of avoiding and reducing deforestation and promoting restoration can be significant for biodiversity . It also contributes to climate change mitigation, with nine countries having reported 8.82 Gt of greenhouse gas emission reductions due to reduced rates of deforestation and forest degradation under the United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change framework for Reducing Emissions from Deforestation and Forest Degradation and the role of conservation, sustainable management of forests and enhancement of forest carbon stocks in developing countries (REDD+) as of January 2020 . There is broad public support for forest preservation, and it aligns with international priorities that promote forest conservation as well as being included in many National Biodiversity Strategies and Action Plans (Figure 5.4.6). As the world has globalized, so has the space of debate on public affairs, including forest conservation, also shifted from the national to the global, prompting the emergence of a global civil society and global governance . Global public support for forest preservation is exemplified by concern for the Amazon rainforest. International pressure of public opinion led the Brazilian agribusiness sector to adopt the Soy Moratorium, a voluntary agreement that calls for large companies to refrain from buying soy originating from areas in the Amazon deforested after 2008 . Another example is the New York Declaration on Forests, which strives to halve deforestation by 2020 (not achieved) and end it by 2030 through global action . Actioning forest conservation to manage fire risk has contributed to management decisions that promote greater diversity and resilience of forests . Equitable access to the health benefits of forests and other conserved natural areas has been promoted specifically for health and well-being, particularly in high income countries (Box 5.4.9). Forest conservation can be equitable when IPLC living in and around forests participate in decision-making (Figure 5.4.6). The protection of land by IPLC has positive outcomes for environmental, social and economic sustainability. Forests provide many economic benefits to people in terms of forest products (see Section 5.4.3.4, H04, Sustainable use of medicinal plants) in addition to non-consumptive uses. Notably, in Africa, Asia and Latin America, the areas with lower deforestation rates are those in which Indigenous Peoples have secured rights over forest resources through community-based tenure . Cases for Indigenous community conserved areas and territories in forests (and in other biomes) can be seen as conservation success stories, demonstrating how conservation practices that protect or restore local values and livelihoods are much more likely to be legitimized locally and actively supported over the long-term (see also Section 5.1.3.10, B10, Rights-based approaches). Support for traditional IPLC management of forest fire risk has further potential to improve IPLC rights and equity as well as broader population health benefits. Conserving forests is technically highly feasible (Figure 5.4.7) and can be highly transformative by enabling a shift to a broader and more inclusive world view of forests (Figure 5.4.6). Box 5.4.9. Healthy Parks Healthy People Healthy Parks Healthy People was established by Parks Victoria, Australia in 2000 and has now become a global movement including in the United States of America, Canada, South Africa and Finland and promoted at the 2014 IUCN World Parks Congress. The key principles of Healthy Parks Healthy People are that parks and protected areas nurture healthy ecosystems and are fundamental to vibrant and healthy communities and economic growth; the well-being of all societies depends on healthy ecosystems; and that contact with nature is essential for improving emotional, physical and spiritual health and well-being. Healthy Parks Healthy People was developed closely with health researchers to provide robust evidence for activities to promote mental and physical well-being, social inclusion (including for migrants), cultural identity (including opportunity to reconnect with nature for Indigenous Australians) and healthy ageing and child development . Its success is not only demonstrated by its extension across Australia and overseas, but the Healthy Parks Healthy People framework is supported by the Victorian Memorandum of Health and Nature, co-signed in 2017 by the ministers for Health and Energy and Environment and Climate Change and is now part of Victoria’s Public Health and Well-being Plan 2019-2023. Ongoing promotion and assessment of physical and mental health outcomes are embedded in these programs and are an example of strong cross-sectoral policy links. In the United States of America, Healthy Parks Healthy People has been associated with human well-being, particularly social justice . Programs of engagement and trails across many United States of America national parks have been developed, and some specifically studied to assess health outcomes with ethnic minority groups . There is a potential trade-off in the ecological value of protected areas due to overuse as the increasing focus on human well-being is recognized . While Healthy Parks Healthy People provides a model of formalizing the linkage between conserved and protected natural areas and health, the evidence is more diverse for IPLC, particularly where protected areas present restrictions to traditional livelihoods, injury, crop and property damage from wildlife, frustration and resentment . However, for some groups, protected area status increases access, agency and indirectly improves health. For example, ‘Caring for country’ work in northern Australian Indigenous (controlled) protected areas has been demonstrated to be a cost-effective primary prevention for chronic disease . In a review of health data from children and households within 10km of protected areas across 34 low to middle income countries, positive impacts (and no negative impacts) on health were reported . 5.4.3.11 Biodiversity management for zoonoses (H11) Zoonotic diseases (see Sections 2.3.2.1 and 2.4.3 for a description and current trends) include those of pandemic potential such as COVID-19, with estimates from the WHO of approximately 0.8 billion cases worldwide by late-June 2023. Zoonotic diseases also include many of the diseases referred to as neglected tropical diseases that impact almost 1 billion of the poorest people globally . However, global funding and scientific research is lower for zoonotic neglected tropical diseases despite their impacts on disability-adjusted life years . Stopping biodiversity loss has been presented as the best prevention strategy to limit the risk of spillover at source and spread of emerging zoonotic diseases , while reducing the costs of response to pandemics . However, the relationship with biodiversity is multidirectional and often highly disease-specific such that increased conservation by itself is not assured to prevent zoonotic diseases emergence . It is also dependent on context – countries with effective surveillance, coordinated health systems (including H01, Universal health coverage and primary healthcare (Section 5.4.3.1)), and trust in government have experienced better responses to outbreaks . Interactions across nexus elements have critical roles in this response option but have not been fully assessed (Figure 5.4.5). Climate change is an important driver for zoonotic diseases spread , and more than half of zoonotic infectious diseases can be aggravated by climate change . Climate change is also increasing direct threats on biodiversity including the wildlife hosts and vectors of diseases , stressing the interlinkages between health, biodiversity and climate . Globally, water resources are under stress, increasing water insecurity and threatening freshwater biodiversity . The increasing number of dams favours emerging water- and vector- borne diseases . The protection of wetlands, which were historically linked with unsanitary living conditions, is seen as a key approach to reduce the spread of emerging infectious diseases such as avian flu . Agricultural expansion has major negative impacts on biodiversity, climate change, pollution and invasive species . Significant relationships between zoonotic disease outbreaks with expanding human demography, livestock, poultry, tree plantation and artificial land expansion suggest that changes to the global food system could reduce the risk of emerging infectious diseases and contribute to many of the SDGs (Figure 5.4.6). The consumption of wildlife and wildlife farming are further zoonotic risks . The definition of the prevention of spillover at source identifies factors to be addressed as using a nexus approach and taking into account the socioeconomic context and wildlife supply chain . This has the potential to address equity issues, e.g., for those involved in wildlife procurement or sale, and reduce economic costs through a more complex approach to prevention. This approach stresses that the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework (i.e., target 5) should explicitly implement the objective of spillover risk reduction 5.4.3.12 Integrated watershed-health interventions (H12) Health interventions have been developed explicitly within integrated landscape and watershed management , integrated landscape-seascape approaches (Section 5.1.3.9, B09, Integrated landscape and seascape approaches) and ecosystem health (‘Ecohealth’) approaches . These vary in focus, scale and the degree of integration with health departments or within broader health promotion (see also Section 5.4.5). They are specific, place-based responses, as illustrated in Box 5.4.10 featuring a successful and well documented example from Watershed Interventions for Systems Health in Fiji (WISH Fiji) . Protected catchments provide clean water for people and the environment, including coastal fisheries (Figure 5.4.5) (see also Section 5.1.3.6, B06, Restoration of coastal and marine systems). Such catchments reduce the distribution and transmission of waterborne diseases, such as leptospirosis, typhoid and dengue which have increased incidence associated with flooding and extreme weather events . Under-5 mortality involving diarrhoea particularly occurs in rural areas where most drinking water is sourced direct from (microbially polluted) local catchments . Restoration of vegetation to protect catchments has further benefits: enhanced carbon sequestration and the reduction of landslides, flooding and the impact of extreme weather events . In addition, reducing nutrient and sediment pollution of coastal water protects marine health. Such pollution is a top-ranked pressure on coral reefs worldwide . Integration of human health into landscape and watershed management requires participatory planning; in cultural landscapes participatory planning has enabled IPLC cultural well-being to be addressed . Technical feasibility is established but requires adaptation to local contexts and issues (Figure 5.4.7). Assessing the economic cost and benefits of such an integrated approach depends on the timescale and sectors or externalities included (Figure 5.4.7). Integrating health within similar interventions such as ridge-to-reef and land-sea interactions could be transformative as approximately 1.3 billion people live within 100 km of coastlines in the tropics (Figure 5.4.6). Box 5.4.10. Watershed Interventions for Systems Health in Fiji (WISH Fiji) Through community planning, infrastructure, catchment management and monitoring, Watershed Interventions for Systems Health in Fiji (WISH Fiji) has provided clean water to 5000 residents in 29 villages in four catchments; developed water and sanitation safety plans for each community; and improved the quality of water that flows into the ocean . The initial project identified catchments with high prior incidence of water-related diseases with risks for downstream environmental impact. Participatory knowledge co-production through community meetings identified risks to public and ecosystem health and prioritized risk reduction actions that addressed animal management, drainage, health systems surveillance, hygiene, integrated planning, land use management, sanitation systems, waste management and water systems. Actions were supported by prior research on health risks associated with poor catchment water and ecosystem quality ; enhancing food and water security and safety through livestock management and improved health of coral reefs and near shore fishing resources ; and increasing catchment forest cover to enhance water quality and improve freshwater fish abundance and richness . The increased areas under community forest management will contribute to addressing climate change, with figures for GtCO2eq yr-1 mitigated currently being calculated, as well as reducing landslides and flooding events . Participatory processes recognizing customary land tenure and socioculturally acceptable solutions and building local capacity have been central to this program . This enhances distributional and recognition equity. Strong engagement with communities, community water and resource management committees and government leaders, including the Fiji Department of Health have been key to social and political support. However, projects are supported by NGOs and external funding and scaling out requires sustained investment . The project also demonstrates challenges to multi-sector evidence analysis as pre-existing public health data collection units do not map to the catchment and some environmental health responses accrue over long time scales. WISH+ is now expanding in Fiji, Solomon Islands and Papua New Guinea . 5.3.4.13 Health impact assessments (H13) Health impact assessment is an approach to assess the potential health effects of a policy, programme or project on a population (particularly on vulnerable or disadvantaged groups) that may follow international or other guidelines (e.g., ). Stakeholder and community engagement, equity, ethical use of evidence, sustainable development and use across sectors are core features . Health impact assessments help decision-makers make choices about alternatives and improvements to prevent disease or injury and to actively promote health. The approach recognizes the small extent to which health is considered within environmental impact assessments and emphasizes the importance of the learning process to envision a holistic approach to health and well-being . The community health impact assessments are an extension of the health impact assessment; they empower communities to conduct health impact assessments, to use the information and results gained, and to negotiate during public policymaking processes . The community health impact assessment is an example of co-production of knowledge between a local community, scientists and policymakers, and a learning process that can lead to changes in unsustainable practices and foster increased understanding of health issues resulting from development projects and policies through collective attempts to find solutions. The community health impact assessment is an approach communities can use to define what means for a community to be healthy . Community health impact assessment can benefit multiple nexus elements (Figure 5.4.5). The approach shows positive impacts on development projects on community health, well-being and livelihoods, including consideration of the health of their ecological systems. Community health impact assessment improves the consideration of health outcomes in development projects and policies on agriculture, water management, forest conservation, mining or tourism . The methodology of health impact assessment, as used in Thailand (Box 5.4.11), is traditionally based on social norms and beliefs and consideration of natural resources. Implementations of health impact assessment have provided measurable pathways and outcomes for reducing the level of communicable and non-communicable diseases associated with development policies . Health impact assessment is a powerful process for assessing health impacts in the face of climate change , such as the impacts of climate change on the health of the population of Vanuatu, which focused on potential adaptation strategies, actions and decision-making . A health impact assessment conducted in the semi-arid Kolar district in India revealed positive health impacts on nutrition, sanitation and water quality, but identified negative health impacts on vector-borne and zoonotic diseases within watersheds identified for development projects . Health impact assessments can be used to identify risks and challenges to health relating to food, for example by identifying risks to food production and choosing the most promising policy intervention to improve nutrition, as has happened in Fiji and Tonga . Results of community health impact assessments (including processes, types of data collected, recommendations and outcomes) benefit from dissemination and critical assessment. Community health impact assessments should be associated and/or complemented by a strategic environmental assessment and environmental impact assessment in order to better influence decision-making on the integration of health while addressing biodiversity and ecosystem conservation . Health impact assessment, and by extension community health impact assessment, is a well-established, response option that can connects health impacts with all elements of the nexus and gives importance to the community's core values. Community health impact assessment applies various adaptive and inclusive tools, making the process highly policy relevant and high in distributional, inclusion and procedural equity (Figure 5.4.6). Box 5.4.11. Community Health Impact Assessment in Thailand The community health impact assessment has been established in the constitution of Thailand, with a dedicated focus on community rights. It binds all institutional organizations to undertake health impact assessments, with a process that can be launched by “any people, community, local administrative organization, non-government organization or academic institute” . The community health impact assessment underlines the importance of the learning process as the local community learned how to comprehend a holistic approach to health and take an active part in the development of public policies that might affect their health, well-being and livelihoods. The community health impact assessment involves four steps. The first is the development of tools, which uses the People Assessing Their Health (PATH) methodology and was designed to help people to control and improve their health . PATH builds the capacity of people to become active participants in the decisions that affect the well-being of their community and to develop the tools needed to achieve their vision . The second step is the assessment of the health of a community that faces an existing or forthcoming development project. This includes assessing whether the project will cause a positive or negative change in the health determinants of the community. The third step relates to the decision-making process where information compiled from the community health impact assessment is used by policymakers at different levels and in related sectors, with the ultimate goal to empower the community to choose a development project that best suits their community, taking into account their livelihoods and well-being. The fourth step involves the implementation of the project and its monitoring and evaluation. In Thailand, community health impact assessment has been enforced in the National Health Act and the Constitution by the national government, and a health impact assessment commission, health impact assessment consortium and regional community health impact assessment networks have been put in place. In addition, there is an international health impact assessment network under the Association of Southeast Asian Nations (ASEAN) to share and learn about health impact assessment and support trans-border impact assessment . Examples of implementation of community health impact assessment include a community in Northern Thailand who explored local people’s health problems in relation to orange tree plantations in the area to contribute to the definition of a Healthy Public Policy in the Agricultural Sector. A further example is that of the Mae Thod Village in Lampang Province that conducted a community health impact assessment in opposition to a concession for iron ore mining. They found that the area was in the watershed of the Mae Thod stream where rare varieties of herbs grow, as well as a waterfall and caves. The community chose to focus their village on herbs and ecotourism. Other examples of community health impact assessment have concerned mining, fracking, economic zones and economic corridors development projects . 5.4.3.14 The One Health approach (H14) As a holistic approach, One Health has the potential for mobilizing a transformation of governance, socioeconomic systems, habits and mindsets towards nexus approaches . The IPBES Workshop Report on Biodiversity and Pandemics , for example, noted the importance of strengthening One Health approaches to pandemic prevention by addressing issues such as sustainable land use, biodiversity conservation, wildlife trade, deforestation and integrated surveillance systems, in addition to public health measures. Other diseases associated with food production (such as avian influenza, foot and mouth disease, antimicrobial resistance) and ecosystems (such as many neglected tropical diseases, plant diseases and insect pest species in forests and cropland) are also successfully addressed with One Health approaches (see also Section 5.4.3.5, H05, Nature on Prescription; Section 5.4.3.11, H11, Biodiversity management of zoonoses; Section 5.4.3.15, H15, Integrated health education). Antimicrobial resistance is one of the areas where the One Health approach has perhaps made the most significant progress in terms of operationalization. The spread of resistant microorganisms and resistance determinants across human, animal and environmental compartments, combined with the shortage of new antimicrobial drugs has raised the alarm for the need of integrated and interdisciplinary approaches at both national and international levels. The WHO online catalogue of antimicrobial resistance national action plans is a good testimony of the broad utilization of One Health in fighting this often-called silent pandemic . However, assessing the impact and success of One Health initiatives across food, biodiversity, water and climate change domains often presents a challenge given the complexity of policy coordination across different sectors and the frequent anthropocentric focus of many of these policies, the power imbalances among stakeholders, and prevailing lack of funding for preventive approaches . Studies have shown that prevention costs guided by a One Health approach would range between $10.3 billion to $11.5 billion annually, compared to the cost of managing pandemics which, according to the recent estimate by the G20 Joint Finance and Health Taskforce, amounts to about $30.1 billion per year (Figure 5.4.6). One Health can strengthen the protection and promotion of healthy ecosystems and the technical feasibility of such initiatives has been established but they require adaptation to local contexts and issues (Figure 5.4.7). Implementation and evaluation challenges have been presented as barriers to operationalizing One Health, and the development of inter-/trans-disciplinary One Health evaluation frameworks and methodologies needs to continue. Full integration of disciplines requires strong support, political will and funding to transcend intent . With the international support of the Quadripartite Alliance for One Health there is a high-level framework to promote cross-sectoral and interdisciplinary approaches to health problems in humans, animals and ecosystems. Box 5.4.12 highlights some examples at the nexus of biodiversity, food, water and health security (Figure 5.4.5), showing that future global health frameworks can be built in solid adherence to key One Health principles across the human-animal and environmental health continuum . Box 5.4.12. One Health in action Brazil created the Unified Health System in the 1990s, with the opportunity for diverse professionals, including veterinary doctors, to become part of a multidisciplinary team . In 2008 this system was enhanced with the creation of the Family Health Support Unit, in which doctors and veterinarians worked together to enhance the practical capacity to prevent zoonoses while contributing to sanitary, environmental and epidemiological interventions, analysis of environmental changes caused by man and natural disasters and defining prevention and control strategies . In Rwanda, the establishment of a national One Health plan in 2015 enabled the quick implementation of a One Health-informed response to the COVID-19 pandemic . This included the suspension of all gorilla tourism, and the development of guidelines for tourism reopening. This contributed to the protection of a highly endangered species and the protection of local biodiversity , with a positive overall impact on tourism, a key economic contributor to Rwanda’s national economy. The importance of water security and quality for global health is widely recognized . The increasing need of water for food production and a growing world population accompanied by, e.g., environmental degradation and biodiversity loss has highlighted the importance of the One Health approach . Water resources management and water, sanitation, health and hygiene have typically been addressed separately, but One Health seeks to connect them and tackle the water crisis as a unified whole. A project in Kenya and Tanzania, Water for One Health, demonstrates the potential of such an approach . 5.4.3.15 Integrated approaches in health education (H15) Planetary Health, One Health, EcoHealth and related approaches seek to transform how health is conceptualized within Earth system functions, across species and cultural world views. Curricula for teaching these approaches exist and, while not identical, are complementary and potentially converging . Currently, Planetary Health, which situates human health within Earth system function , and One Health, which aims to achieve optimal health for humans, animals and ecosystems (OHLEPP, 2022), are most commonly taught. Even more traditional fields of Global Health reference these approaches so there may be future convergence of these curricula . The curricula primarily address human health priorities within the nexus (for water, food, climate change mitigation and adaptation) and for biodiversity the focus is on risks (e.g., zoonotic disease emergence – see Section 5.4.3.13, H13, Biodiversity management for zoonoses) and protective pathways (e.g., microbiomes, see Box 5.4.5) (Figure 5.4.5). Key features of Planetary Health and One Health published curricula are shown in Box 5.4.13. Approaches to education that frame health across scale, species and cultures are potentially highly transformative by building capacity for new ways of thinking and addressing problems (Figure 5.4.6). Technical feasibility is high as these curricula are already being taught in multiple countries (Figure 5.4.7; Box 5.4.13). Costs are equivalent in comparison to similar professional education (Figure 5.4.6). Education for sustainability and climate change issues specifically addresses SDG target 4.7. Distributional, inclusion and procedural equity should continue to be carefully reviewed for these curricula as their early development was predominantly in the Global North (where societies are least sustainable). Pre-tertiary integrated health education could be highly beneficial in changing world views and responsibility more broadly. Box 5.4.13. Planetary Health and One Health education Planetary Health education emphasizes equity and social justice in line with the full sentiment of SDG target 4.7, systems thinking, complexity, movement building and systems change, and places explicit importance on Indigenous concepts of health that include a central relationship and responsibility for nature . It is focused on the breadth of human health issues affected by transgressing planetary boundaries (e.g., climate change risk amplification) with some education foci on food (more specifically healthy and sustainable diets), water, climate change adaptation and mitigation, and ecosystems . Planetary Health also emphasizes transformation of the health sector (see Section 5.4.3.3, H03, Net-zero sustainable healthcare). The Planetary Health Alliance has 350 organizational members from over 60 countries, primarily located in medical schools in the Global North. This underrepresents the extent to which planetary health has appeared in curricula and discourse as a result of the dedicated Lancet journal. However, a global survey reported only 15 per cent of medical schools addressed climate change and health . One Health education addresses the complex health challenges that link human, ecosystem and animal health and promotes inter-professional collaboration and practice (see Section 5.4.3.14, H14, The One Health approach). It is taught in extensive university networks and curricula (of 90+ institutions), including the Southeast Asia One Health University Network, Africa One Health University Network and networks in the United States of America . Education frameworks also exist for professional settings (e.g., ) and as open-access online courses and other resources (e.g., One Health Commission, WHO Academy). One Health education is historically hosted by veterinary faculties with an emphasis on zoonoses, food safety, food production, anti-microbial resistance, and some wildlife and plant diseases but this is expanding and diversifying . While most of the academic advanced courses on One Health take place in the northern hemisphere, a vast range of educational possibilities (undergraduate, graduate and post-graduate) initiatives are gradually taking place across low-to middle-income countries with good representation of the Global South. One Health has not traditionally encompassed different world views of health . However, this is changing with a transition from anthropocentric to biocentric health considerations, equity and engagement with plural world views ." ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.4.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Nexus response options for health" .

