    ipbes:hasDescription "The selection of response options for assessment in this subchapter was adapted from the general approach (see Chapter 5.0, Table 5.0.1 and the associated data management report)2 through a review of the relevant literature, including major international assessments , and is based on their (i) potential for addressing multiple nexus elements; (ii) the involvement of multiple actors and stakeholders; (iii) relevance to international policy targets, especially those of the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework (and its predecessor the Aichi Targets) and the SDG; (iv) global applicability; and (v) availability of evidence on implementation. They comprise approaches around the protection, sustainable use and management, restoration and governance of biodiversity for expanding actions beyond protected areas (Table 5.1.4). Nine response options are more focused on actions, while five are sociopolitical response options that support implementation of the other nine, thus often constituting a response option of their own. The five sociopolitical response options comprise examples of the main categories of policy instruments considered in Chapter 4 (Section 4.3.5): rights-based and customary, legal and regulatory, economic and financial, and social and information-based instruments. Any specific intervention on the ground often combines several of these response options in bundles of complementary actions, although some have different aims and entry points (Table 5.1.5). Table 5.1.4. Brief descriptions of the 14 biodiversity response options assessed. Biodiversity response option Brief description of response option Section B01 Area-based conservation Conservation strategies, including but not limited to protected areas, and other effective conservation measures that use a nexus approach to conserve biodiversity across landscapes and seascapes. 5.1.3.1 B02 Urban nature-based solutions Implementing nature-based solutions in urban areas to support sustainable development objectives and reconnect people with nature; current urban nature-based solutions focus primarily on climate risk management and adaptation to climate change. 5.1.3.2 B03 Agroecology Designing and managing agricultural and food systems using ecological and social concepts and principles to support sustainable agricultural production, minimize negative environmental impacts of production and secure nature’s contributions to people. 5.1.3.3 B04 Ecosystem-based adaptation in rural landscapes Using biodiversity and nature’s contributions to people to reduce human vulnerability to climate change by facilitating adaptation and building resilience to the impacts of climate change. 5.1.3.4 B05 Forest landscape restoration Restoring forests at the landscape scale to increase forest cover and improve ecological functions on restored lands, including the restoration and enhancement of nature’s contributions to people from forest ecosystems. 5.1.3.5 B06 Restoration of coastal and marine systems Restoring coastal and marine systems (e.g., mangroves, salt marshes, seagrasses, seaweed, coral) to improve habitat integrity and connectivity, ecosystem functioning and ecosystem resilience and secure nature’s contributions to people from these ecosystems. 5.1.3.6 B07 Restoration of inland water systems Restoring inland water bodies (e.g., lakes, rivers (including riparian areas) and wetlands) to improve water flows, water quality and hydrologic connectivity, increase water security and support the sustainable use of aquatic biodiversity. 5.1.3.7 B08 Rewilding An approach to restoring ecosystems that focuses on recovering ecological processes, improving ecological connectivity, restoring wildlife populations and reconnecting people with nature; natural processes lead the recovery rather than a defined ecosystem end state. 5.1.3.8 B09 Integrated landscape and seascape approaches Multi-stakeholder, multisectoral, collaborative processes to adaptively co-manage landscapes and seascapes over the long term with an emphasis on connectivity between socioecological systems with synergistic outcomes for environmental, social and economic objectives. 5.1.3.9 B10 Rights-based approaches Applying human rights principles to conservation and other measures, and accounting for the rights of nature and the rights of non-human entities; this includes recognizing the rights of Indigenous Peoples and local communities and of women, such as to land and territories, water, food, health and a safe and clean environment. 5.1.3.10 B11 Multilateral environmental agreements International environmental agreements, particularly those focusing on flexible implementation processes that account for local knowledge and needs, foster intergovernmental cooperation and promote synergistic outcomes across agreements and development priorities. 5.1.3.11 B12 Land and sea planning Employing spatial planning to configure the use of terrestrial and marine territories over the medium to long term to balance trade-offs among multiple uses of the natural environment, support sustainable development objectives and meet sociocultural needs. 5.1.3.12 B13 Natural capital accounting Measuring and reporting on the stocks and flows of natural assets (renewable, non-renewable, biotic, abiotic) at the individual and ecosystem level to manage, sustain and enhance nature’s contributions to people. 5.1.3.13 B14 Reconnecting people with nature Restoring and fostering a deep connection between people and the natural world, including deepening understanding and appreciation of nature; the process draws on multiple knowledge systems and benefits human health and well-being and nature. 5.1.3.14 Table 5.1.5. Summary of response options for biodiversity according to a set of defining characteristics (columns) that highlight their similarities and differences or complementarities. In the column “aim of response option”, management is defined as the promotion of specific elements of biodiversity; conservation relates to the preservation of biodiversity (through statutory and non-statutory means); restoration is defined as an action that aims to improve the state and/or functioning of biodiversity but does not imply that the end goal of restoration is necessarily a natural (non-utilised) state. The column “nexus entry point” reflects whether, based on available evidence, some response options or more often specific cases of implementation have a focus on another entry point initially, although the aim is related to biodiversity and therefore they were assessed as biodiversity response options. The column “nature value being promoted” shows how response options align with the value perspectives of the IPBES Nature Futures Framework (Chapter 3, Box 3.2). The column “actions and enabling conditions” summarizes what actors do to implement the response option and the main enabling conditions, highlighting those actions of highest importance for implementing a nexus approach and having positive social outcomes. The column “co-benefits” summarized for the nexus elements, highlighting in bold those that are most salient in the literature. Response option Aim of response option Nexus entry point Nature value being promoted Actions and enabling conditions Bold text indicates actions of highest importance Co-benefits B01: Area-based conservation Conservation of biodiversity Biodiversity Nature for nature Nature for people Nature as culture * Inclusion of diverse knowledge systems * Devolution of decision-making to local actors * Promotion of participation/co-design from diverse actors * Recognition of local institutions and rights * Recognition of reciprocal relationships between social and ecological systems * Understanding of interactions between local to landscape scales * Promotion of connectivity to enhance biodiversity values of interest * Adaptation impact * More equitable decision-making * Maintains ecological condition * Enhances ecosystem functioning * Enhances diversity * Reduces land degradation B03: Agroecology Management of biodiversity Restoration of biodiversity Conservation of biodiversity Food Nature for people Nature as culture * Maintenance/restoration of soil health including carbon and biodiversity * Maintenance/restoration of pollinator diversity/pest or disease control * Maintenance/restoration of water availability * Hierarchical governance with top-down organization and policy * Inclusion of diverse knowledge systems * Recognition and inclusion of local institutions and rights * Enhances biodiversity function and state * Climate change adaptation * Environmental performance of food production * Low cost of implementation B04: Ecosystem-based adaptation in rural landscapes Conservation of biodiversity Management of biodiversity Restoration of biodiversity Climate change Nature for people * Management and restoration of habitats * Inclusion of diverse knowledge systems * Promotion of participation/co-design from diverse actors * Enhances biodiversity function and state * Climate change adaptation and mitigation * Disaster risk regulation * Improves water quality (but not quantity) * Resilient food production (but may reduce quantity) * Timber * Social and cultural benefits B05: Forest landscape restoration Management of biodiversity Biodiversity Nature for people * Systematic approach for designing, planning, steering and monitoring to meet diverse needs * Stakeholder engagement including vulnerable groups, in planning and decision-making * Tailored local context adapted to the local social, cultural, economic and ecological values, needs and landscape history * Adaptive management that is iterative and adaptive, with recurring consultations among stakeholders * Guiding principles such as focusing on landscapes, restoring ecological functionality, allowing for multiple benefits and avoiding the conversion of natural ecosystems * Restoration techniques informed by both primary and secondary sources, including tree planting, natural regeneration or agroforestry * Enhances biodiversity state * Improves ecological functioning * Human well-being/ improved livelihoods * Climate change mitigation B06: Restoration of coastal and marine systems Conservation of biodiversity Restoration of biodiversity Health Nature for people * Recognition of reciprocal relationships between social and ecological systems and economic value of biodiversity * Selection of species and their propagules to restore by considering globally changing environmental conditions, native biodiversity and social licence for project acceptability and long-term success * Promotion of participation/co-design from diverse actors * Systematic planning * Economic restoration * Livelihoods * Disaster risk reduction * Improves habitat condition, resilience and connectivity * Enhances biodiversity function * Food security * Health * Climate change mitigation * Strengthens stakeholder participation B07: Restoration of inland water systems Restoration of biodiversity Water Nature for nature Nature for people * Restoration actions: returning degraded lakes, rivers, wetlands and other waterbodies to healthier and more natural flows, cleaner states and the conservation and sustainable use of biodiversity * Threat mitigation: dam removal, reduced pollution and responsible fisheries * Integration of multiple dimensions of human - biodiversity dependence to manage trade-offs. * Community-based approach * Improves habitats * Human well-being * nature’s contributions to people restoration * Risk reduction * Reduces pollution B08: Rewilding Restoration of biodiversity Biodiversity Nature for nature * Dam removal * Habitat restoration * Reintroduction of large carnivores * Agricultural rewilding * Enhances biodiversity state and functioning * Climate change adaptation and mitigation * Recovery of ecological functioning * Social and cultural transformation * Habitat creation * Species recovery * Water quantity and quality * Food security and health * Timber B09: Integrated landscape and seascape approaches Management of biodiversity Restoration of biodiversity Food/ Health / Biodiversity Nature for people Nature for nature * Adaptive management * Collaborative governance * National and global policy * Promotion of participation/co-design from diverse actors * Recognition and inclusion of local institutions and rights * Spatial planning on landscape and seascape scales * Certification * Habitat restoration * Payments for ecosystem services * Sustainable agriculture * Participatory monitoring and evaluation * Enhance biodiversity state and function * Synergy of multiple goals * Improves biodiversity * Improves ecological functioning * Water for ecosystems * Water for humans * Improves water quality * Sustainable food production * Access to healthy food * Improves societal resilience (health) * Mitigates climate change * Recognition of nature’s contributions to people * Fosters collaboration * Manages competing interests B10: Rights-based approaches Conservation of biodiversity Biodiversity Nature for people Nature as culture Nature for nature * Recognition and inclusion of local institutions and rights * Hierarchical governance with top-down organization and policy * Devolution of decision-making to local actors * Empowerment of women * Promotion of participation/co-design from diverse actors * Consideration of justice issues * Enhances biodiversity state * Improves human well-being * Strengthens territorial rights * Sustainable use * Gender equality and empowerment * Resolves conflicts * Biodiversity conservation * Social justice and human rights B11: Multilateral environmental agreements (MEAs) Conservation of biodiversity Restoration of biodiversity Management of biodiversity Biodiversity Nature for nature Nature for people Nature as culture * Intergovernmental cooperation * Promotion of participation/co-design from diverse actors * Hierarchical governance with top-down organization and policy * Transboundary cooperation * Recognition and inclusion of local institutions and rights * Promotion of synergies between multiple goals * Enhances biodiversity functioning and state * Focus on conservation priorities * Reduces land degradation * Mitigates climate change * Agricultural sustainability B12: Land and sea planning Management of biodiversity Biodiversity Water Nature for nature Nature for people Nature as culture * Production of local and national strategic plans * Promotion of participation/co-design from diverse actors * Coordination between local and national governments * Biodiversity protection * Water protection * Climate change mitigation * Food availability * Protection of rights * Sustainable development B13: Natural capital accounting Restoration of biodiversity Biodiversity Nature for nature Nature for people * Intergovernmental cooperation * Promotion of participation/co-design from diverse actors * Hierarchical governance with top-down organization and policy * Promotion of synergies between multiple goals * Economic accounting * Recognition of reciprocal relationships between social and ecological systems and economic value of biodiversity * Financial acknowledgement of biodiversity gain/loss * Attribution of economic loss or gain to actor involved B14: Reconnecting people with nature Management of biodiversity Health Nature for people Nature as culture * Promotion of activities in nature * Information and infrastructure for access to nature * Outdoor education programmes * Creation and management of green spaces * Nature-based arts and crafts * Encourage nature-based spiritual practices * Recognition of ILK * Health * Conservation and restoration of biodiversity * Habitat restoration * Improves water resources * Climate change adaptation and mitigation * Food production * Access to healthy food * Economic benefits Critical aspects of each response option are summarized in this section based on the synthesis of evidence and examples of implementation (Figures 5.1.2, 5.1.3, 5.1.4). From the 14 response options assessed, five response options are analysed in detail. These focal response options are not intended to represent priorities or recommendations. Rather, they are chosen to illustrate the range of issues and actors involved in response options and provide real-world examples of response options in action. Based on the general method for selecting five response options to analyse in detail (see Section 5.0.3), the following criteria addressing bottlenecks in conservation and achieving global targets (SDGs, Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework) were used: * Maximising the number of nexus elements addressed, mainly mainstreaming synergies. * Promoting equity and power sharing, with particular attention to IPLC. * Implementation in multiple case studies and at large scale, or scalable. Based on these criteria, five focal response options that cut across the terrestrial, marine and freshwater domains and are representative of response options for biodiversity in terms of nexus challenges, relevant scales, actors and interactions with other sectors were selected: 1. B01 Area-based conservation using a nexus approach, as an improvement on classical conservation strategies and addressing the nexus governance challenge (see Section 1.1.2.2). 2. B02 Urban nature-based solutions, as an illustration of a management unit that necessitates a nexus approach and addresses the nexus scaling challenge when applied to where most people will live globally (see Section 1.1.2.4). 3. B09 Integrated landscape and seascape approaches (ILSA), as a holistic management of different realms, nexus elements and coexisting actors, addressing the nexus complexity challenge (see Section 1.1.2.1). 4. B10 Rights-based approaches in Indigenous lands, sea, waters, territories and communal landscapes and seascapes, as people-oriented options that address the nexus values challenge (see Section 1.1.2.3). 5. B14 Reconnecting people with nature, as a critical instrument changing social relations with nature and perceptions that can enable biodiversity conservation beyond the scale of implementation that also address the nexus values challenge (see Section 1.1.2.3). Each of the 14 overall assessed response options3 is analysed in terms of their outcomes for the five nexus elements, the social and ecological aspects of their implementation processes and their feasibility, with a special focus on participation and dimensions of equity . Where evidence allows, social and economic outcomes are reported. The potential of the response options for transformative change considering criteria from the IPBES Thematic Assessment of the Underlying Causes of Biodiversity Loss and the Determinants of Transformative Change and Options for Achieving the 2050 Vision for Biodiversity (Transformative Change Assessment) are also evaluated. Figure 5.1.2. Impacts of biodiversity response options on nexus elements. Colours indicate positive impacts (blue; i.e., synergies) and negative impacts (red; i.e., trade-offs). Intensity of colour represents the magnitude of impact (light = small; medium = medium impact; dark = large;). Grey colouring indicates a score of 0. White colouring indicates no score or a non-numerical value. Where impacts differ depending on context, impact ranges are shown by multiple colours within a cell. NA indicates not applicable, NE indicates no evidence, and INC indicates the evidence is inconclusive. Figure 5.1.3. Biodiversity response options within the context of social, economic and political dimensions, international frameworks and transformative change potential. Colours indicate a positive impact (blue; i.e., synergies) and a negative impact (red; i.e., trade-offs). Intensity of colour represents the magnitude of impact (light = small; medium = medium impact; dark = large;). Grey colouring indicates a score of 0. White colouring indicates no score or a non-numerical value. Where impacts differ depending on context, impact ranges are shown by multiple colours within a cell. NA indicates not applicable, NE indicates no evidence, and INC indicates the evidence is inconclusive. Figure 5.1.4. Temporal and spatial scales and technical feasibility of the biodiversity response options. Ranges shown temporal and spatial scales and feasibility indicated by Technology Readiness Level.4 5.1.3.1 Area-based conservation (B01) Area-based conservation with a nexus approach uses protected areas , other effective conservation measures (OECMs) and other relevant area-based conservation strategies to conserve biodiversity across land and sea, including territories of IPLC (Chapters 1, 2). Based on a review of the literature, area-based conservation strategies were categorized into five types (Table 5.1.6), but analysis focused on the first four on which spatially explicit data were available. Overall, area-based conservation has a positive impact on biodiversity by increasing or maintaining area in good condition, enhancing ecological function and diversity, and reducing land and sea degradation (Figure 5.1.2) . Table 5.1.6. Area-based conservation approaches with a nexus approach. The approaches illustrate both shared attributes as well as unique features based on a review of > 600 case studies . Some area-based conservation approaches share similar attributes with other response options in this chapter, e.g., area-based conservation approach 3 in the table below with integrated landscape and seascape approaches (see Section 5.1.3.9 (B09)). The key difference is that the primary management focus in area-based conservation approach 3 is to manage protected areas to conserve biodiversity . Similarly, people-centred approaches (area-based conservation approach 1 in the table below) may have shared attributes with rights-based approaches (Section 5.1.3.10 (B10)), but the latter focuses on the rights of IPLC to land and territory not protected areas for biodiversity conservation. Approach Key features Example strategies Case studies 1. People-centred approaches to protected area management and other effective area-based conservation measures (OECMs) * Pursuit of integrated conservation and socioeconomic development goals * Utilization of diverse systems of knowledge, including ILK * Devolution of decision-making authority to non-state actors at the local level representing the interests of IPLC * Promotion of broad-based participation in biodiversity conservation efforts * Recognition of the legitimacy of local institutions and customary property rights of IPLC * Community-based conservation * Biocultural approaches to conserving Indigenous lands and territories (Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework Target 3), including Indigenous community conserved areas * Convivial conservation * Integrated conservation and development projects * Co-management of conserved areas with IPLC * Rights-based approaches , see Section 5.1.3.10 (B10, Rights based Approaches) for rights of IPLC over ownership of Indigenous lands and traditional territories as primary focus * The Communal Areas Management Programme for Indigenous Resources (CAMPFIRE) of Zimbabwe * Ghana’s Community Resource Management Area (CREMA) program * Territorial user rights for fisheries (TURFs) in Chile * Co-management of forests and protected areas in Ghana * Locally managed marine areas in Kenya, Madagascar and Mozambique ; Indonesia ; Fiji and other Pacific islands . 2. Social-ecological resilience approaches * Pursuit of integrated management goals with a focus on building social-ecological resilience, such as customary and biocultural approaches of IPLC * Recognition of reciprocal relationships between social and ecological systems across space and time, including relationships of IPLC with nature * Utilization of diverse sources and types of knowledge, including both ILK and scientific knowledge * Reliance on inclusive decision-making processes and flexible institutional mechanisms that connect diverse actors across multiple levels * Adaptive co-management * Resilience-based management * Adaptive management * Adaptive governance * Kristianstads Vattenrike Biosphere Reserve, Sweden * The Great Barrier Reef of Australia 3. Transboundary landscape and seascape approaches * Adoption of a regional approach to managing biodiversity beyond the existing boundaries of protected areas * Delineation of management boundaries based on interactions among hydrologic, ecological, sociocultural and economic components of the system * Management of trade-offs and synergies between local sociocultural, economic and ethical interests, as well as ecological objectives * Reliance on scientific and technical knowledge, but with room for recognition of other forms of knowledge, including ILK * Promotion of meaningful participatory processes, including involvement of IPLC * Ecosystem-based management of protected areas and natural resources * UNESCO Biosphere Reserves that promote mixed uses in buffer zones around PAs * Bioregional planning * Systematic conservation planning * Transboundary PAs management * The Northwest Forest Plan in the USA * Lake Rachen watershed, Sweden * The Greater Yellowstone Ecosystem, USA 4. Ecological and environmentally focused spatial approaches * Aims at enhancing ecological connectivity among a system of protected areas and OECMs * Delineation of management boundary based on particular species, hydrologic boundaries or other ecological attributes * Reliance on scientific and technical knowledge * Involvement of local communities in participatory management processes to some extent * Corridors and ecological networks * IUCN protected area management categories * Designation of biogeographical regions * Sustainable management of ocean fisheries outside protected areas * Natura 2000 reserve network in Europe * Yellowstone to Yukon conservation initiative in the USA and Canada * The Mesoamerican Biological Corridor 5. Private sector-led and incentive-based approaches * Engagement of the private sector in biodiversity conservation efforts to address Government funding limitations and inefficiencies * Utilization of economic tools that account for the value of nature, thereby reducing the trade-offs between biodiversity conservation and socioeconomic development objectives * Private protected areas * Non-state market-driven initiatives that support funding for area-based conservation * Public-private partnerships * User fees, taxes and international donor contributions earmarked for conservation * Use of economic incentives in biodiversity conservation, e.g., payments for ecosystem services and the Reducing Emissions from Deforestation and Forest Degradation and the role of conservation, sustainable management of forests and enhancement of forest carbon stocks in developing countries (REDD+) program * Payments for ecosystem services in Costa Rica * Private land conservation in South Africa * Private parks in Costa Rica Area-based conservation also positively impacts other nexus elements, particularly human health and well-being, but also climate change adaptation, food and water (Figure 5.1.2). For example, the implementation of ecosystem-based management approaches in Sweden and the United States of America has resulted in the enhanced provision of various nature’s contributions to people, including fish and wildlife habitat, water quality and recreational opportunities . However, data on impacts of area-based conservation on water are limited except for wetlands . Evidence for climate change mitigation outcomes of area-based conservation are also less frequently reported , although a global study estimated that protect areas account for about one-fifth of the carbon sequestered by all land ecosystems annually . Improved environmental performance of food production and access to healthy food through area-based conservation are more commonly reported from marine protected areas which contribute to improving fish populations within the protected area as well as across the ecosystem . However, evidence on the impact of land-based AbC on food production is limited. Governance mechanisms in area-based conservation increasingly rely on polycentric institutional arrangements, composed of multiple centres of decision-making authority with some degree of diversity and autonomy at each level . Decision-making processes are also highly participatory nexus approaches to area-based conservation (Figure 5.1.3) (e.g. ), though with less evidence for positive impacts on vulnerable groups on land, largely due to reduced access to food and cultural needs. Participatory and multilevel governance offers opportunities for conflict management as well as mobilization of knowledge and resources across scales for transformative biodiversity governance Area-based conservation strategies also have positive economic impacts (Figure 5.1.3), as seen in participatory protected area governance in several countries , collaborative forest management in Ghana and ecosystem-based management of forests in the United States of America . The implementation of area-based conservation strategies in Australia, Finland and Canada has also been shown to contribute to economic growth in local communities as well as increased revenue to local governments . Moreover, it has been estimated that the Hoge Veluwe forest, one of the largest and most well-known protected areas in the Netherlands (Kingdom of the), generates economic benefits worth at least three times more than the per hectare-value generated by nearby agricultural land . However, examples of economic benefits from marine area-based conservation are still few and largely limited to income from tourism and improved fish catches . The majority of area-based conservation case studies had positive impacts on all aspects of equity (Figure 5.1.3) consistent with participatory and inclusive approaches to protected areas conservation where positive impacts are found to be strongly linked to genuine devolution of power and rights to the local level and involvement of diverse actors and multiple perspectives . Furthermore, most area-based conservation approaches utilize diverse types of knowledge , and their management goals focus on addressing multiple values . Although new area-based conservation initiatives can have impact within five years , many have been in existence for several decades, e.g., Kakadu National Park since the 1970s , the Great Barrier Reef Marine Park since the 1980s and the Northwest Forest Plan (United States of America) since the 1990s . Notably, accrual of biodiversity benefits with area-based conservation improves over decades (Figure 5.1.4). The need to embrace complexity, recognizing the effects of telecoupling, lags and legacies (see Sections 1.1.2.1 and 4.5.4), is not strongly evident in the area-based conservation case studies, although it is a key requirement of emerging resource governance regimes for managing across nexus elements . Barriers to area-based conservation include increased fragmentation of responsibilities and lack of political will , power imbalance among stakeholders , inadequate funding and lack of technical resources and capacity within IPLC , while enablers include availability of funding, enabling legislation, leadership and bridging organizations. Designing effective monitoring and evaluation in biodiversity conservation is challenging with only one third of area-based conservation cases having effective monitoring and evaluation. Coverage of nexus elements and integration of collaborative and adaptive planning processes in monitoring and evaluation are also relatively low. Of note, the Great Barrier Reef provides a strong example of quantitative biodiversity data for monitoring and evaluation . AbC approaches vary in their potential to advance transformative governance but have a high potential for transformative social and institutional change. Cultural change is less evident, a finding consistent with the literature . Box 5.1.2. Locally managed marine areas5 Locally managed marine areas (a generic term, LMMAs) are people-centred approaches to area-based conservation in which Indigenous Peoples and local communities (IPLC) either lead or co-manage specific areas of marine space for livelihoods, such as fisheries and mangrove forests, and biodiversity conservation . They have proliferated in coastal regions in the Indo-Pacific, notably in Fiji, Madagascar, Kenya and Indonesia. IPLC are highly dependent on marine and coastal biodiversity for food, traditional practices and livelihoods . Locally managed marine areas represent a grassroots approach to area-based conservation, demonstrating the importance of devolution of power and rights to the local level and involvement of diverse actors and multiple perspectives through inclusive processes . They typically include a diverse range of inclusive and participatory governance processes with IPLC in both lead and target roles . Locally managed marine areas are often formalized through local government by-laws or through customary law and have strong potential to qualify as other effective area-based conservation measures (OECMs) . In several countries in the western Indian Ocean, their recognition would more than double the percentage of marine areas under protection or management for national targets , thereby contributing to target 3 of the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework (see Section 5.1.3.11, B11, Multilateral environmental agreements) for achieving 30 per cent protection by 2030 . Evidence for the effectiveness of LMMAs in protecting biodiversity as well as addressing other nexus elements, notably food through sustainable fisheries, is not always clearly documented. Typically, coral reef fish biomass is taken as a metric for both biodiversity and healthy populations of fish and by implication sustainable fisheries . Moderate to high increases in biodiversity metrics as well as fishery metrics are reported from LMMAs in Indonesia and Kenya, but not all . LMMAs are often established as an area-based approach to managing small-scale fisheries which provide food, income and healthy nutrition to coastal IPLC throughout much of the tropical Indo-Pacific. Establishing LMMAs to manage small-scale fisheries sustainably and control access to a primary food source (the fishery resources) is documented in Indonesia , Kenya and Mozambique . In eastern Africa, small scale fishing is a way of life for coastal peoples contributing on average ~50 per cent of household income to coastal communities . Historically in Melanesian Pacific island nations, LMMAs were a customary practice to manage marine resources which were traditionally the only source of protein for these nations. Locally managed marine areas continue to reinforce customary governance and tenure systems in Pacific island nations . Key challenges for LMMAs include: maintaining long-term funding and other external support for management activities ; and interference from other actors outside the LMMAs, notably through illegal fishing or weak enforcement of fishery regulations . 5.1.3.2 Urban nature-based solutions (B02) Urban nature-based solutions are a common response option to address multiple sustainable development goals in urbanized populated areas . Urban nature-based solutions range from urban forests to sustainable urban drainage systems , and are regarded as an “umbrella concept of established urban ecosystem-based approaches” . However, implementation remains geographically concentrated in the Global North . Differences in infrastructure, attitudes towards public spaces and more decentralized governance in the Global South may limit the utility of conclusions from the academic literature . This is even more salient considering a key factor for urban nature-based solutions implementation is attention to context . To assess urban nature-based solutions implementation, 15 case studies, randomly selected among 118 published studies, were scored (Figure 5.1.5). These showed that implementation of urban nature-based solutions is currently overwhelmingly focused on providing protection from climate risks that are exacerbated in urban areas (e.g., disaster risk reduction) . Urban nature-based solutions are “explicitly solution-orientated” , but their current implementation is often skewed to resolving a single issue. Most often this is climate change: urban nature-based solutions comprise half of all urban climate change adaptation interventions to date . This single issue focus is also seen for water , health and food . Rarely are other nexus elements considered holistically , with negative impacts almost never quantified . Urban nature-based solutions have a higher chance of success when the level of co-design and participation of those living in proximity is high . Socioenvironmental justice is a key consideration as nature-based solutions may not benefit all actors equally, potentially even displacing some . The level of co-design and participation is variable across cases, as is the integration of multiple world views: projects tend to focus on purely anthropocentric value, with a heavy bias towards academic knowledge systems . This echoes calls for more research into effective, more inclusive participatory approaches than basic consultation or partnership . Despite the potential for urban nature-based solutions to redefine the relationship between people and nature, their governance and funding remains centralized. This underpins the often limited participation and merely reformist or incremental transformative change potential . Despite monitoring and evaluation being highlighted as a key goal , the uptake of urban nature-based solutions is currently compromised by its lack of effectiveness. Beneficial holistic outcomes are often implicitly assumed , consistent with holistic outcomes being rarely reported for nature-based solutions in general . When reported, implementation tends to flow through municipal planning and zoning instruments developed by local authorities, in some cases responding to regional or national policy. Finance for urban nature-based solutions is overwhelmingly provided by the public sector, with little to no examples of public-private partnerships . Urban nature-based solutions are occasionally supported by local community organizations, but even fewer cases involve rights-based instruments and customary norms or social and cultural options. However, their wide-scale adoption is still limited, with a need to include nature-based solutions in urban governance and planning agendas . Decision making is mostly hierarchical, with very few involving polycentric or inclusive governance, despite polycentric governance being identified as an enabler for urban nature-based solutions elsewhere . Monitoring and evaluation is critical for documenting environmental impacts of nature-based solutions , and frequency of measurement needs to be high as nature-based solutions are dynamic and uncertain . Earth observation of nature-based solutions can be a key tool to assess changes in land cover, but ability to document urban metabolism modification (e.g., energy, water and carbon fluxes and balances) still remains underexplored . Nevertheless, this analysis corroborates conclusions for nature-based solutions more broadly that more synergies than trade-offs are reported when considering other elements of the nexus: as little as 2 per cent of nature-based solutions studies report across a broad spectrum of outcomes, with many assuming implementation delivers multiple benefits . There is widespread agreement that urban nature-based solutions represent efficient solutions to facilitate adaptation to climate change and increase the resilience of cities. Yet, evidence is inconclusive on where or when urban nature-based solutions are effective, let alone how this effectiveness might extend into the future or to the surrounding landscape. Regional spillover impacts are rarely considered, despite the importance of considering these interactions . There is also little assessment of their future effectiveness: many interventions may not be effective in the future due to implementation that does not consider native or climate-adapted species . Evidence on expected timeframes of interventions and transformative change potential is inconclusive. Cases are mostly medium term (decades) and involve the new implementation of existing solutions, or a continuation of traditional top-down power structures . Figure 5.1.5. Global map of the number of studies on urban nature-based solutions compared to the percentage of urban population by country.6 Box 5.1.3. Wetland restoration for climate change adaptation in the Maldives One “bright spot” for just and transformative urban nature-based solutions is the implementation of wetland-mediated climate change adaptation in Addu City, Maldives . Funded and implemented by the World Bank, the Maldives Climate Change Adaptation Project exemplifies climate-resilient, future-proof planning through an inclusive, multi-sectoral approach. The project comprises four components: 1. Wetlands conservation in protected areas around the atolls of the Maldives. 2. Enhancing coral reef monitoring to improve decision-making and management. 3. Minimising environmental risk to natural capital by developing an integrated solid waste program for Addu City. 4. Mainstreaming climate change into island development planning. The project incorporates diverse stakeholders by setting targets for the inclusion of women and youth in the project cycle. The project also ran an extensive resettlement plan for any local communities affected by the project. Its report details biodiversity pre- and post-baselines in the Coral Database, which was completed with an array of existing and new partners, increasing sites covered in the National Coral Reef Monitoring Program by 10. Awareness of climate change adaptation and waste management was improved in 83 per cent of targeted island households due to the project. 5.1.3.3 Agroecology (B03) Agroecology is an integrated approach that simultaneously applies ecological and social concepts and principles to the design and management of food and agricultural systems . Other sustainable farming approaches closely related to agroecology include ecological intensification ( (see Sections 5.3.3.4 (F04) and 5.3.3.5 (F05)) and regenerative agriculture , which vary more in their social, cultural and political focus than in their ecological and agricultural foundations . They all utilize the functions of non-domesticated biota and genetic diversity in crops and livestock species to support sustainable production by restoring carbon, soil nutrients and water dynamics, controlling weeds, pest and diseases, and supplying pollination . Some other sustainable farming approaches, like sustainable intensification, benefit biodiversity and ecological processes without necessarily relying on them for production; they focus mainly on reducing environmental impact by increasing input efficiency (see Section 5.3.3.3 (F03) and Section 5.5.3.2 (C02)). Agroecology is supported by many farming practices, such as agroforestry (see Section 5.5.3.11 (C11)), crop and pasture rotation, intercropping and cover crops, among others . Agroecology is implemented worldwide to address the negative impacts of conventional or industrial agriculture and especially trade-offs between food, biodiversity, climate change and people . Agroecology is highlighted in the FAO’s 10 Elements of Agroecology framework (see also Section 5.3.1 and Box 5.3.1), and is reflected in the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework under target 10 on sustainable production systems . The implementation of agroecology is framed by specific policies: while it is highly regulated in the European Union, in other regions such as North America policies are lagging, while they are expanding slowly in the Global South . Agroecology benefits multiple nexus elements by increasing their synergies and reducing trade-offs. For example, agroforestry is one of the current options for agriculture with the greatest synergistic benefits for nature’s contributions to people, with positive impacts on food production, managed and wild biodiversity, water and climate change (see Section 5.5.3.11 (C11)). Regarding trade-offs, it is generally considered that agroecology has lower yields than conventional agriculture . However, this might be explained by confounding agroecology with organic agriculture . Recent studies nevertheless suggest that better management practices might reduce yield gaps in comparison to conventional agriculture . Moreover, even with lower yields, reduced production costs contribute to higher profitability . In many local communities globally, agroecology builds on knowledge (e.g., traditional ecological knowledge), practices (e.g., rotational and shifting farming) and world views (e.g., plural valuation) of IPLC, embedded in regionally-specific sociocultural frameworks and institutions, generating positive impacts on the different dimensions of equity . Its implementation is commonly supported by market instruments such as certification and short food supply chains, underpinned by sociocultural changes in consumer preferences . The scaling out and up (see Section 4.4) of agroecology faces challenges such as power struggles with agribusiness, uncertainties regarding yield gaps with conventional agriculture, the necessity for farmer skill development, shifts in values among farmers and consumers, ageing farmers, land tenure issues and path dependencies in infrastructure or financial commitments . 5.1.3.4 Ecosystem-based adaptation in rural landscapes (B04) Ecosystem-based adaptation is increasingly incorporated into national and international biodiversity and climate policies to address climate change challenges and achieve the SDGs . Ecosystem-based climate change adaptation uses biodiversity and ecosystem services to reduce people’s vulnerability to climate change by facilitating adaption and building resilience to its adverse effects . In rural landscapes the conservation, management and restoration of natural or biodiversity rich semi-natural ecosystems for climate change adaptation delivers multiple benefits to biodiversity and other nexus elements . The management and restoration of forests, wetlands and coastal vegetation provides ecological disaster risk regulation, improves water quality, secures resilient food production, wood for energy and construction and can mitigate climate change. In some cases of forest restoration, it can however reduce food and water quantity . The conservation, restoration and extensive, often traditional, management of high biodiversity, semi-natural grasslands supports adaptation by delivering climate-resilient livestock production, wild foods and medicines, sequestering soil carbon and contributing multiple social and cultural benefits . While implementation of ecosystem-based adaptation is often led by local public institutions (e.g., municipalities or local governments), multiple models of governance often coexist within individual regions. These have varying levels of co-design and participation, and inclusion of multiple knowledge systems and world views, especially ILK so that Indigenous and traditional values and knowledge are represented . In Japan, wetland restoration for geese populations and flood risk management was led by a conservation NGO with government support, which has now scaled out to multiple sites across the region by proving benefits to farmers initially reticent regarding yield loss . In Germany, governance of nature-based flood management and mitigation spans four governance models: top-down government, government-led co-design, public and citizen cooperation and initiatives, and citizen-led governance . Although fewer reported cases involve rights-based instruments and customary norms or social and cultural options, consideration of multiple values and world views alongside knowledge systems (including ILK) are essential to nature-based transformative adaptation of rural landscapes . Transformative adaptation is defined as systemic changes to societal paradigms, visions, goals, rules and knowledge in response to actual or anticipated changes in driver variables that fundamentally change the properties of a social-ecological system . Three critical mechanisms for nature-based adaptation and transformation in rural landscapes directly relate to empowerment and multi-stakeholder co-production: (i) traditional management of multifunctionality, especially at the landscape level; (ii) collective management and governance; and (iii) construction of values and uses for nature’s contributions to adaptation . Scaling ecosystem-based adaptation is a pressing challenge, requiring the amplification of local successful projects to multiple locations (scaling out) and scaling up to required changes in knowledge, governance and their interactions . 5.1.3.5 Forest landscape restoration (B05) Forests and trees offer powerful tools for building inclusive, sustainable and resilient landscapes. They provide a multitude of benefits for people, including climate change mitigation and adaptation , biodiversity habitat , water security and sources of food, energy, medicine and jobs . This forest-based bioeconomy plays a critical role in achieving climate neutrality and addressing nexus challenges . Forest landscape restoration goes beyond simply increasing tree cover . It is a continuous process aiming to regain ecological functionality, improve human well-being and restore landscape functions in degraded areas . This dynamic approach also aligns with existing nature-based solutions standards, contributing to the recovery of essential nature’s contributions to people . While many actors are committed to forest landscape restoration , successful large-scale implementation remains challenging due to complex ecological, social, policy, market and institutional factors . Payment for forest ecosystem services has been proposed as a financing mechanism , with examples such as China's large-scale payment for forest ecosystem services policies . However, evidence for payment for forest ecosystem services schemes delivering both social and ecological benefits across large landscapes remains limited . The Restoration Opportunities Assessment Methodology, successfully implemented in Rwanda , offers a promising path forward by building on existing policies and targets. However, documenting the economic and social success of forest landscape restoration globally is crucial, as addressing social critiques of inappropriate afforestation is vital for long-term success. The complexity of forest landscape restoration as a socioecological nature-based solutions presents both challenges and opportunities . Spatial relationships within land-use systems can create trade-offs, such as increased crop raiding by wildlife due to forest proximity or unequal benefit distribution between local and external stakeholders . Such projects associated with agricultural land conversion can lead to social and ecological trade-offs for local communities due to direct losses in production, access restrictions, land tenure issues, justice and power asymmetries . Extensive rangelands can synergize with recovered forests, enhancing biodiversity and pastoral livelihoods while addressing climate change and desertification concerns . The risks and trade-offs depend on the activity’s nature, scale, agreed definitions, regulations and monitoring and adherence to key principles. Examples such as failed restoration projects in Northern Malawi highlight the importance of clear goals and community understanding . Three key aspects for the success of forest landscape restoration are ecological and social balance, supportive policies, and monitoring and adaptation . 5.1.3.6 Restoration of coastal and marine systems (B06) Coastal and marine nature-based solutions, including coral, seagrass and mangrove restoration, aim to protect, conserve and restore ecosystems while enhancing human quality of life . These actions aim to restore habitats essential for economies and livelihoods, and reduce vulnerabilities against natural disasters for communities relying primarily on nature’s contributions to people . Seagrass, mangrove, algae and shallow and deep coral reef restoration promote habitat integrity, connectivity and resilience, benefitting intraspecific genetic variation, biodiversity and ecosystem functioning and, thereby, food security and health . Restoring seagrasses, seaweeds and mangroves also helps mitigate climate change through “blue carbon” sequestration . Restoration projects at the local scale are long-term processes that can take decades and require balancing of conservation goals, human needs and ecological values with other strategies to promote synergies at different scales . Restoration efforts are facilitated by building and supporting trust and coordination in multi-stakeholder collaboration. This requires addressing concerns, breaking down social barriers, respecting traditional customs, implementing systematic planning and ensuring monitoring, financing and equal decision-making among scientists, traditional owners (e.g., IPLC), the private sector and governmental and non-governmental bodies . This collaboration also brings additional benefits. For example, actively involving local communities and volunteers in restoration increases awareness of their ecosystem dependency. It also increases coordination between local stakeholders, with traditional knowledge, and national stakeholders in the implementation of restoration actions while reducing costs . Such coordination exists, for example, in Australia, where coastal ecosystem management is a national environmental concern and coordination of stakeholder networks is implemented . New approaches for restoring marine and coastal ecosystems are needed, as this process requires long-term commitment due to the timescale of species growth and succession and impacts of global drivers such as climate change. Projects are often short-term and only measure success in terms of species survival, hindering the measurement of the full and long-term outcomes of restoration for ecosystem functioning and socioeconomic benefits for stakeholders, such as cost savings in managing for flood risk . Implementing metrics to evaluate cost-effectiveness, documenting the causes of failures and assessing impacts on society’s inclusion (e.g., cultural, educational, gender and health) across governance levels is a critical need . Ethical considerations in ecological restoration also need to be addressed by governments and scientists. These include: the higher cost of restoration versus preserving ecosystems; matching actions to drivers of ecological degradation; careful selection of techniques and species used; identifying sites based on factors of ecological degradation; minimum ecological requirements for restoration; and evaluating small-scale actions before large-scale implementation . 5.1.3.7 Restoration of inland water systems (B07) Inland water ecosystems, such as lakes, rivers, wetlands and other waterbodies are among the most degraded ecosystems globally. For example, more than 80 per cent of wetlands have been lost , leading to a decline in their associated nature’s contributions to people . Inland water systems have been heavily modified and face multiple threats, including water withdrawal for human use; impacts of other natural resource uses, such as extraction of aggregates in mining and impacts on biodiversity from fisheries ; and flow modification driven by flood management, dams and channelization . Thus, their restoration is critical for both nature and people. The restoration of inland waters can encompass diverse activities that lead to healthier and more natural flows, cleaner states and the sustainable use of biodiversity . Activities such as dam removal, reduced pollution and responsible fisheries restore the health of inland water ecosystems by maintaining key ecohydrological processes of natural flooding and fluvial connectivity . Inland water restoration requires a cohesive framework to guide the evaluation of freshwater nature’s contributions to people, their trade-offs and sustainable management that takes account of the multiple, sometimes conflicting, dimensions of human and biodiversity dependence . Besides contributing to the conservation of freshwater and terrestrial biodiversity, inland water restoration also interacts, largely positively, with the other nexus elements. It can support healthier wild-capture inland fisheries, which are crucial aquatic food systems for millions globally, especially in sub-Saharan Africa and Asia . Access to an affordable source of protein and micronutrients is crucial for the health of vulnerable groups . Preserving riparian habitat, especially in upper catchment areas , positively contributes to water security by improving water quality and can be facilitated by urban-rural payment schemes like payment for ecosystem services . Healthy inland waters are also significant contributors to global carbon capture and hence can contribute to climate change mitigation. However, some trade-offs exist, such as the impact of irrigation for food production on surface waterbodies and groundwater or reservoir construction for the water security of dense urban areas . The restoration of inland waters often poses a difficult governance challenge due to the presence of multiple diverse actors with sometimes conflicting objectives and power . Some freshwater resource management strategies including Integrated Water Resources Management are viewed as top-down and may fail to recognize and account for the cultural and societal values of ecosystems . Community-based approaches are more inclusive and customary ILK-based management systems of IPLC play a vital role for inland water conservation and restoration . While policies often account for inland waters as part of the terrestrial realm , which can lead to underrepresentation or less relevant conservation and restoration efforts , the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework mentions ‘inland waters’ specifically, including the importance of the contribution of ILK and IPLC customary systems to inland waters conservation and restoration. 5.1.3.8 Rewilding (B08) Rewilding is an open-ended restoration approach focused on the recovery of ecological processes and interactions. It is particularly relevant in the face of climate change, where uncertainties in ecosystem responses to interventions challenge prescriptions of specific end states in ecological restoration . Rewilding initiatives are predominantly local or sub-national, but their integration into national policies and multilevel environmental governance remains limited . Evidence from large-scale passive rewilding, however, demonstrates the potential for significant long-term social and cultural transformation . Promising rewilding interventions include dam removal , wetland and peatland restoration , forest landscape restoration (see Section 5.1.3.1, B05, Forest landscape restoration) and closer-to-nature forestry . Contentious issues include the return of large carnivores and the substitution of costly agri-environmental measures with rewilding practices such as natural grazing . These controversies may be addressed with intermediate approaches such as agricultural rewilding, which combines ecological restoration with limited agricultural production , alongside with cultural change in views and values of nature (see also Section 5.1.3.14, B14, Reconnecting people with nature). Rewilding can contribute to multiple nexus benefits . The well-documented biodiversity benefits of rewilding include habitat creation, restoration of ecological processes and improved connectivity , particularly in expanding the range and abundance of keystone wildlife species . However, mitigation of biological invasions needs more evidence . The removal of dams and dikes, by enabling the up- and down-stream movement of aquatic wildlife and sediments and reconnecting rivers to their floodplains, benefits freshwater and marine biodiversity and water quantity and quality . These biodiversity benefits can support food security and health through provisioning of wild foods and other non-timber forest products . Climate change benefits of rewilding are less documented and likely context-dependent . The rewetting of wetlands and peatlands, forest secondary succession, proforestation (the protection of existing forests, including degraded and secondary forests, towards full ecological maturity) and changes in trophic networks that increase soil carbon pools are promising for climate change mitigation. Benefits for climate change adaptation through enhanced ecosystem and social resilience are also expected. Rewilding can be implemented in diverse ways following guideline principles , but its social and economic impacts remain understudied. The involvement of local communities, particularly IPLC, and considerations of equity are central challenges . The environmental and economic costs and benefits of scaling out rewilding initiatives, and the development of novel policies and markets to enable it , present challenges for widespread adoption and may require cultural shifts (see also Section 5.1.3.14, B14, Reconnecting people with nature). 5.1.3.9 Integrated landscape and seascape approaches (B09) Integrated landscape and seascape approaches are comprehensive strategies to address the intricate interplay between socioeconomic and environmental objectives such as food production, local livelihoods, biodiversity conservation and climate action at landscape and seascape scales. Integrated landscape and seascape approaches encompass various frameworks including integrated landscape approaches , landscape stewardship , foodscapes , forest and landscape restoration , multifunctional scapes and seascape approaches . Integrated landscape and seascape approaches emphasize the integration of nexus elements across terrestrial, freshwater, coastal and marine ecosystems and leverage various ecosystem-based approaches to address societal challenges . While the terminology may vary, the core concept revolves around holistic, long-term and adaptive management of landscapes and seascapes to achieve socioecological objectives through collaboration among stakeholders from different sectors and social groups . Integrated landscape and seascape approaches are a promising and well-practiced response option for the nexus as they operate across higher-order interactions (e.g., water-food-biodiversity, water-health-climate change) and place a particular emphasis on connectivity across socioecological land- and seascapes and the terrestrial, freshwater and marine realms . Recent survey-based analysis suggests that highly integrated initiatives that include many sectors have an overall better performance for agriculture, nature conservation, livelihoods and effective governance in comparison to less integrated (siloed) initiatives which are biased towards conservation or agriculture . Integrated landscape and seascape approaches are implemented in all inhabited continents and include global scale programs such as the Satoyama Initiative, the UNESCO Man and Biosphere reserves and the FAO Globally Important Agriculture Heritage Systems, among others. Thirty integrated landscape and seascape approaches case studies were analysed in this subchapter: seven from Africa, twelve from the Americas, four from Asia and Oceania, and seven from Europe (Table 5.1.7). This quantitative analysis was complemented by conclusions from global syntheses . These diverse examples demonstrate the effectiveness of ILSA in benefitting multiple elements of the nexus and sustainability dimensions (ecological, social, and economic). Relative cost-effectiveness and affordability are also features that make integrated landscape and seascape approaches an attractive nexus response option . Table 5.1.7. Integrated landscape and seascape approaches analysed in this subchapter, with examples of case studies. Integrated landscape and seascape approaches Examples Socioecological production landscapes and seascapes promoted by the International Partnership for the Satoyama Initiative * Multi-stakeholder collaboration and adaptive co-management in the Xinshe socioecological production landscapes and seascapes, China * Community conserved areas and rotational farming in Nagaland socioecological production landscapes and seascapes, India * Conservation of coastal wetland biodiversity in Résidences La Chaux ‘Barachois’ SEPLS, Mauritius * Agriculture using underground irrigation in canals in dry and semi-dry socioecological production landscapes and seascapes, Iran * Nature-friendly agriculture in socioecological production landscapes and seascapes in the state of Queensland, Australia * Local knowledge and community participation in Vavau socioecological production landscapes and seascapes, Kingdom of Tonga World Bank’s BioCarbon Fund Initiative for Sustainable Forest Landscapes * Integrated watershed management in Reggung Watershed, Central Lombok, Indonesia * The Forest-River-Village-Sea eco-agriculture Initiative in the Xinshe SEPLS, China * Integrated natural resource Management in drought-prone and salt-affected SEPL, Türkiye Rainforest Alliance Certification * Sustainable coffee, tea, cocoa and fruit production in various regions of the Global South (Côte d’Ivoire, Guatemala, Honduras, Rwanda, Costa Rica, Kenya) High Nature Value landscapes promoted by the Fundatia ADEPT * Productive and sustainable farming initiatives in Europe, focused on biodiversity, community, farming and innovation (Romania and the UK) Afforestation, reforestation, agroforestry and improved land management promoted by Scolel’te ecosystem services project * Carbon sequestration and ecosystem services in Mexican forests, in partnership with community groups, smallholder farmers and social organizations Biosphere reserves * Monarch Butterfly Biosphere Reserve (Mexico) * Yasuni Biosphere Reserve (Ecuador) Integrated landscape and seascape approaches generally focus on food production and nature conservation as entry points, but also include and benefit water and climate change , and health , with most initiatives impacting three or more nexus elements. For example, the integrated landscape approach with IPLC engagement in decision making in Nagaland, India, strengthened and linked community conserved areas for conservation across three villages—Sukhai, Ghukhuyi, and Kivikhu—protecting 939 hectares . Hunting, tree felling, and destructive fishing were prohibited across the remaining forest and river landscapes. A capacity-building program reconnected the community with their forests, fostering conservation support through livelihood opportunities, such as ecotourism training, and involvement in biodiversity monitoring. The initiative revived the traditional practice of conserving community forests, secured by customary rights and reinforced by a joint management committee. Mobilization efforts and synergy-building enhanced nature’s contributions to people, improving productivity in shifting cultivation (jhum) areas. This outcome impacted all nexus elements though wildlife conservation and revival of wild populations, increased diversity; food from forest, restoration of food production; carbon storage in recovered/protected forest; traditional medicine secured and general wellbeing of community; enhanced watershed services of protected forest. The benefits of initiatives aimed at sustainable food production for improved terrestrial and coastal water quality and biodiversity conservation are well documented. This is particularly visible at the landscape-seascape interface, where environmentally-friendly agricultural production activities upstream enhance plant and faunal diversity across the watershed and safeguard coastal and marine organisms and ecosystems . Synergies and trade-offs in relation to health and climate change (apart from disaster risk reduction) are much less reported in the literature. This may be explained by the difficulty of measuring and monitoring them within a short project span (two to three years) of most documented integrated landscape and seascape approaches . Policy instruments used within integrated landscape and seascape approaches include biodiversity-focused spatial planning, zoning regulations, habitat restoration subsidies, payments for ecosystem services, product certification and support for sustainable farming practices . These instruments aim to incentivize sustainable land and resource management practices while addressing competing interests and values. While financial resources, technical capacities and infrastructure can pose challenges, evidence suggests that well-implemented integrated landscape and seascape approaches can achieve high social, economic and environmental effectiveness . Integrated landscape and seascape approaches involve multiple actors including governments, IPLC, academia, private sector and other relevant stakeholders in a collaborative, adaptive, context-specific and scale-sensitive way, with attention to multiple knowledge types, values and objectives . Participatory, collaborative and adaptive governance is essential for the success of integrated landscape and seascape approaches (Box 5.1.4). Inclusive governance mechanisms that involve IPLC play a crucial role in ensuring that integrated landscape and seascape approaches are human rights-based, socially acceptable and equitable . However, governance arrangements need to go beyond consultation and actively involve stakeholders in co-management, decision-making and effectiveness evaluation processes. Moreover, intermediate actors such as non-governmental organizations, academia and media can play a critical role in bridging gaps between stakeholders and facilitating inclusive governance processes . Despite their advantages and increasing popularity, integrated landscape and seascape approaches are vulnerable to some implementation challenges, including setting and checking boundary and scale; degree of multi-stakeholder engagement and participation; identification of priority issues in the beginning and throughout the process; monitoring and evaluation of effectiveness; ability to be sustained over the long-term (continuity); and scaling up of experiences . More consistent and evidence-based documentation of integrated landscape and seascape approaches globally, supported by both quantitative and qualitative long-term data, presents a high opportunity for addressing these challenges, which in turn, is critical for scaling this response option . Box 5.1.4. The Xinshe forest-river-village-ocean eco-agriculture initiative (the Xinshe integrated landscape and seascape approach), Hualien County, China Long-term adaptive co-management of the Xinshe socioecological production landscape and seascape (Hualien County, China) presents a successful example of integrated landscape and seascape approach implementation from 2016 to date. Restoration of the forest cover and sustainable eco-agricultural activities have resulted in improved water quantity and quality as well as freshwater and coastal biodiversity over time (3-5 years) . Sustainable watershed management in terraced paddy fields has further led to a gradual restoration of coastal ecosystems (removal of 54 per cent ammonia and 43 per cent nitrate by constructed wetlands) , which is anticipated to act as an effective buffer against climate change-related extreme events in the future . The Xinshe integrated landscape and seascape approach employed an evidence-based participatory monitoring and evaluation tool for assessment of socioecological resilience on a regular basis every three to four years . The resilience assessment helped to identify time- and place-sensitive soci-ecological priority issues in the Xinshe socioecological production landscape and seascape. Its results were effectively integrated as action tasks into the adaptive co-management cycle of the Xinshe integrated landscape and seascape approach. There are several lessons learned from the operationalisation of the Xinshe integrated landscape and seascape approach (Figure 5.1.6): (i) inclusive and participatory processes for the locally sensitive boundary setting and checking; (ii) diversity of facilitation tools and engagement strategies for the continuity of multi-stakeholder interest and engagement; (iii) attention to both environmental and socioeconomic issues; (iv) regular, consistent and locally sensitive monitoring and evaluation for (v) continuity of the Xinshe integrated landscape and seascape approach; and (vi) upscaling the Xinshe experiences from the local to the island-wide scale via a Satoyama Initiative partnership (. The partnership is a multi-stakeholder partnership network that fosters policy support, knowledge and experience sharing, and on-the-ground integrated landscape and seascape approaches practitioner exchanges across China . Figure 5.1.6. The Xinshe socioecological production landscape and seascape, Xinshe Village, Hualien County, China. Photo credit: Vision Way Communication Ltd. under licence CC BY-NC-ND 5.1.3.10 Rights-based approaches (B10) Rights-based approaches to conservation encompass the application of human rights principles to the ownership, governance, access and management of natural resources and biodiversity (Figure 5.1.7) (see Chapter 4, Box 4.11). They also include the rights of nature, the rights of Mother Earth and rights of other non-human entities . This ensures that conservation-related laws/policies, plans and processes do not violate rights and promote human and natural rights as much as possible . Rights-based approaches can help address the multiple links between conservation and rights, recognizing that exclusionary approaches to conservation can violate rights; that rights are key to supporting conservation contributions of IPLC, women and other rights-holders; and that all people have rights to a healthy, sustainable environment . Rights-based approaches refer to both collective and individual procedural rights, such as participation, access to information and Free Prior and Informed Consent , as well as substantive rights, e.g., water, land, territory, food and cultural rights . Figure 5.1.7. Rights-based approaches. These approaches aim to ensure rights are respected, protected and promoted, including rights to govern, access and use resources, guided by values and knowledge. Securing rights can, in turn, contribute to both conservation and well-being. Source: Dawson under licence CC BY-NC 4.0. A number of concepts and mechanisms exist to support the implementation of rights-based approaches in conservation, such as co-management ; recognition and strengthening of customary and collective rights ; the interdependence of biological and cultural diversity ; ‘decolonization’ approaches ; and legal demarcation, titling and reparations for territories . Rights-based approaches generally go beyond inclusive and participatory conservation efforts; they are firmly grounded in recognizing rights. This includes, in particular, the leadership and rights of IPLC in decision-making, management, benefit-sharing and ownership of territories . Women’s rights are also central in rights-based approaches to conservation, particularly through seeking to promote gender equality and empowerment . Rights-based approaches recognize the unique contributions of women to conservation, ensure their rights to land and natural resources, and secure their rights to be free from gender-based violence . Rights-based approaches implementation involves both rights-holders (entities who have rights) and the duty-bearers responsible for upholding their rights (mainly States). There is also increasing recognition of non-state actors responsibilities for human rights, such as business and conservation organizations . These sectors need to assume responsibilities and avoid causing or contributing to negative human rights impacts in their operations towards biodiversity conservation. International and regional human rights instruments recognize many rights that can be (positively or negatively) impacted by conservation. This includes the UN Declaration of the Rights of Indigenous Peoples (UNDRIP), which has highlighted rights to, inter alia, self-determination, language, sociocultural identity, collective actions, lands, waters and territories, among other forms of rights, and notes that communities should not be deprived of these rights by displacement from protected areas or other conservation measures . Rights-based approaches can thus help avoid and remediate previous “fortress” models that led to disempowerment and displacement of many IPLC . International environmental law also increasingly recognizes related rights. The Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework, for example, includes rights-based approaches principles in several targets, including targets 21 and 22 that note that IPLC have procedural, lands, waters and resource rights, while target 23 discusses rights of women and girls to land and natural resources as well as “their full, equitable, meaningful and informed participation and leadership at all levels” of conservation action . Targets 1 and 3 also provide notice of safeguards of rights of IPLC in the expansion of spatial conservation. The rights-based approach to biodiversity conservation needs to address a range of interconnected challenges. Expanding the notion of rights and access to consider the nexus between biodiversity and water, food, health and climate change (see Figure 5.1.8 and Chapter 4, Box 4.11) helps understanding of the interplay of actors involved and the political struggles that drive the sharing of benefits of nature among social groups and to nature itself . Figure 5.1.8. Relationship between the nexus elements of biodiversity, water, food, health and climate change centred on peoples’ rights and access to resources. Resource availability is driven by many interactions among these elements. A) For visualisation purposes only a few examples of two-way interactions among nexus elements are shown that more directly influence people. B) Every interaction is embedded in the climate change element where change may lead to both trade-offs and synergies among the other elements. Adapted from Melo under licence CC BY-NC-SA 4.0. There are many recommendations and road maps for applying rights-based approaches to conservation. These include ones focused on development , conservation , rights and leadership of IPLC , governance of tenure ( and related guidelines) and many decisions taken by the Conference of the Parties to the Convention on Biological Diversity, among others. Looking across these, successful rights-based approaches have in common fully recognizing and integrating rights and equity in policy, governance and management (including through strong safeguards and grievance mechanisms); recognizing and respecting rights-holders as leaders and partners; understanding and addressing inequities and their underlying causes, including in power relationships; and ensuring accountable action, including through monitoring of processes and outcomes and strengthening the capacity of rights-holders (to assert and claim their rights) and duty-bearers (to uphold their obligations). Meta-reviews of rights-based approaches have strongly concluded that positive biodiversity outcomes are better achieved when Indigenous or local autonomy and control, based on local priorities and knowledge systems, form the basis for conservation actions . Expansion of rights-based approaches therefore has the potential to deliver benefits to hundreds of millions of people worldwide whose rights to territories are not currently recognized and who are facing threats of dispossession and violence . IPLC recognition and rights contribute to climate change mitigation; for example, 30 per cent of the Amazon Basin’s above-ground forest carbon is estimated to be in Indigenous territories, which have been more successful than traditional protected areas in conserving these stocks . There are powerful examples of rights-based approaches where IPLC rights - including over their land and territories (e.g., through titling or other appropriate mechanism), governance, knowledge systems and management - are appropriately recognized and supported (Box 5.1.5). Secure rights - including tenure - can also help ensure sustained, effective outcomes. For example, in the Brazilian Amazon, IPLC territories with full enforceable property rights have shown a significant decrease in deforestation, whereas those with incomplete or less secure rights have not; causality has been clearly linked to the tenure processes through use of data sets of deforestation from before and after titling . Despite the increasing successes, implementation of rights-based approaches continues to face challenges. State-governed and managed protected areas continue to be contested for their exclusionary boundaries and approaches, and gaps remain around grievance and restitution mechanisms so that rights-based approaches can be effectively enforced . Power relationships among rights-holders, duty-bearers and other actors in rights-based approaches implementation can be complex and asymmetrical . For example, courts in different countries often interpret rights-based approaches in different ways and enforcement varies widely . Mismatches across scales, with higher levels retaining decision-making authority and paying only lip service to rights-based approaches, has also been a barrier to effectiveness . These power imbalances can lead to marginalization, lack of meaningful participation and limited resource allocation for IPLC . Rights-based approaches are also not always able to tackle interlinked nexus issues, particularly where external actors and pressures remain unchanged. For example, in New Zealand, granting of co-management of fishing rights to M?ori communities was not enough to conserve lake resources, as upstream dairy farms continued to produce nitrogen runoff that could not be stopped by the rights-based approaches implemented downstream . Box 5.1.5. Recognizing Indigenous and traditional territories and areas conserved by Indigenous Peoples and local communities: A key to rights-based approaches to conservation There is growing evidence that Indigenous Peoples and local communities (IPLC) play outsized roles in global conservation through governance, management and custodianship of their lands and territories . Indigenous Peoples and local communities are estimated to invest the equivalent of $3-4.5 billion each year in conservation, an estimated 15-23 per cent of total global conservation funding . In many places (including both within and beyond areas in de jure government and private protected and conserved areas) this custodianship has long been sustained despite widespread lack of recognition of rights and in the face of threats . The Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework includes rights-affirming language, resulting in large part from sustained IPLC engagement through the International Indigenous Forum on Biodiversity . Target 3 calls for recognizing Indigenous and traditional territories, along with centring equitable governance and respect for IPLC rights. The Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework also calls for a human rights-based approach overall, and acknowledges IPLC contributions and commits to respecting their rights under goal C. There are many examples of rights-based recognition for Indigenous and traditional territories and areas conserved by IPLC. This includes diverse forms of recognition in national legal systems (including, in some cases, as protected areas or other effective conservation measures (OECMs), and under diverse local names) . Target 3 can also be understood as “provid[ing] a third pathway for Indigenous Peoples and local communities to seek appropriate recognition of their rights and responsibilities to their ancestral territories, beyond state systems of protected areas and OECMs” . They may also be further connected and supported through IPLC networks and initiatives, through which they have been able to exchange knowledge, share experiences and gain international recognition for their conservation efforts . Growing international recognition is also reflected in programming of organizations like the International Union for Conservation of Nature and the United Nations Development Programme. In summary, a range of formal legal rights, agreements and policies have been used to recognize (and in some cases create) and support territories and areas conserved by IPLC, as have local relations and attention to shared values in more informal processes of recognition . Rights-based recognition has a number of benefits, including sustaining and enhancing Indigenous livelihoods, e.g., by combining conservation with biocultural values and food provisioning; greater recognition of governance and management; and improved biodiversity outcomes . However, there remain some key challenges. These include: limited recognition and involvement in IPLC-governed marine areas ; external threats (especially where rights are not recognized); insufficient or inappropriately structured funding; restrictive burdens on decision-making; lack of capacity (including of duty-bearers) ; and instances where recognition and/or creation are not grounded in self-determination or free, prior and informed consent. For example, imposing conservation ideas from outsiders and expecting IPLC to pick up underfunded protected areas mandates can put unwanted pressure on communities . Despite these challenges, many IPLC continue to support appropriate recognition of territories and areas they conserve as one way to exercise their rights and secure support for their efforts. For example, the Brazilian Articulation of Indigenous People is an example of a national consortium that represents the collective struggle of Indigenous Peoples in Brazil, focused on protecting and guaranteeing Indigenous rights, including recognition of territories, self-determination and world views. In Brazil, there are around 415 territories officially demarcated as Indigenous, with a land area of 117 million hectares, 98 per cent of which are located in the Amazon region. Many are adjacent to other protected areas dedicated to biodiversity conservation, creating a dual buffer effect of protection for both conservation and Indigenous well-being . 5.1.3.11 Multilateral environmental agreements (B11) Multilateral environmental agreements provide powerful solutions to transboundary problems through collective action that can involve all elements of the nexus. As national priorities are often divided, such global guidance solutions are a key tool for providing regulatory framings and focus on conservation priorities, as seen in the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework . However, effective implementation is often lacking . Over 747 MEAs have been produced within the last 150 years creating a complex network of agreements that makes implementation challenging , amplified by the sectoral approach taken . For example, a recent review of 195 Nationally Determined Contributions under the Paris Agreement reported that none of the commitments mention of synergies or trade-offs with biodiversity or land degradation goals . While multilateral environmental agreements should be formulated using clear transboundary goals , their implementation at the national level gains resilience (i.e., implementation is more likely to succeed and be long-lasting) when undertaken with flexibility and responsiveness to local needs and knowledge . This includes active participation of a range of actors, such as representatives of multiple national government departments, non-governmental organizations and local communities, each with defined responsibilities . Such implementation of multilateral environmental agreements can aid multiple nexus elements; for example, regional or ocean scale management of fisheries invoking multilateral environmental agreements and other conventions (e.g., United Nations Convention on the Law of the Sea, UNCLOS) has the potential to reverse biodiversity loss, improve ocean resilience to climate change, recognize IPLC rights and enhance food security . A successful example is seen in eight Western and Central Pacific Island states who sustainably manage their shared tuna fisheries through the Nauru Agreement Concerning Cooperation in the Management of Fisheries of Common Interest (https://www.pnatuna.com). Successful implementation of multiple multilateral environmental agreements can be achieved through intergovernmental cooperation between agencies to promote synergies , with examples between the SDGs and the United Nations Convention to Combat Desertification’s Land Degradation Neutrality goal or between the SDGs and Reducing Emissions from Deforestation and Forest Degradation and the role of conservation, sustainable management of forests and enhancement of forest carbon stocks in developing countries (REDD+) . Or, alternatively, through consideration of multiple national and international priorities, such as food production, land degradation, climate change and biodiversity goals . For example, Indonesia has established a secretariat that focusses on synergies between the implementation of Indonesia’s medium-term development plan and the attainment of the SDGs. The implementation of the plan therefore takes into account local needs for development, encourages private sector involvement and promotes interconnectedness between sectors . A contrasting example of interdepartmental cooperation and synergistic planning for multiple elements of the nexus, but without responsiveness to local needs, is the ambitious land-use management plan published by China within its 14th Five Year Plan. The plan considers a range of development and health goals of the SDGs as well as biodiversity and climate change. It creates zones for conservation and development using a range of factors; however, implementation has been delayed due to a lack of consideration of local needs and values . 5.1.3.12 Land and sea planning (B12) Land and sea planning (hereafter ‘planning’) is a technical-political-administrative process aimed to configure the use of terrestrial and marine territory in the long-term, considering potential and limitations of the area, aspirations of people and sustainable development objectives . Marine spatial planning is now a key process within the current United Nations Decade of Ocean Research . Planning is typically manifested through public policies, with a focus on developing medium- and long-term strategic plans and balancing trade-offs between multiple different uses of the natural environment . The state plays a crucial role as a guarantor of territorial sustainability against multiple development pressures, within a process designed to be highly participatory and equitable, incorporating multi-stakeholder consultations and other tools . A key outcome of planning is limiting accelerated urbanization by respecting natural and cultivated areas . Thus, planning also conserves biodiversity and water, mitigates climate change and supports food production through agriculture, aquaculture and fisheries . Well implemented spatial plans also enhance human health and well-being through providing recreational access to natural environments , and more recently meeting sociocultural needs . Ninety examples of implementation of land planning examined7 showed regional differences. In Latin America, planning emphasized social and cultural aspects, serving as a tool for communities to protect their identities and rights linked to the territory . Elsewhere, land planning tends to be more technical, focusing on zoning of uses and achieving pre-established objectives, with less consideration of social and cultural aspects. However, recent sea planning cases now include ILK and transparent processes to reduce user conflicts, with ecosystem service models for multi-objective planning, especially in data poor high-value sectors, and strategic mapping products for different stakeholders . However, the complexity of coordination and costs in the ocean are challenging. For example, seafloor mapping is intensive and expensive, leaving most of the planet unmapped . Additionally, while land planning often occurs at local scales, sea planning typically spans national and sometimes transnational scales. Planning has high transformative potential, particularly in changing structures, values and practices, by fostering innovative participatory processes at the local level, and promoting social cohesion through collaborative building of prospective scenarios for a common territory . The main challenges in implementing these large-scale plans include the complexity of coordinating policy across multilevel governments, insufficient citizen participation and representation of local interests, power imbalances among stakeholders, inadequate institutional capacities and insufficient funding for monitoring and enforcement over time . Implementation failures due to these challenges can lead to medium- and long-term loss of biodiversity and transformation of ecosystems . Conversely, positive impacts on ecological and cultural aspects of territories occur when these processes are carefully evaluated and adjusted 5.1.3.13 Natural capital accounting (B13) Natural capital accounting is designed as an economic and financial approach to account for the value of natural assets, aiming to shift current economic and market dynamics towards sustainable nature use by identifying, valuing and possibly pricing natural assets. It introduces financial incentives or disincentives for the activities of economic actors, thus addressing interactions between nexus elements in an economy . As an emerging framework at national and regional levels, natural capital accounting seeks to integrate nature’s values into public and business accounts, potentially laying the groundwork for new fiscal systems and biodiversity payments (see also Section 6.2.6.1). Natural capital accounting measures the resources consumed by economic entities and their impacts on ecosystem services in either or both physical and monetary terms. This measurement aims to incorporate benefits of nature historically excluded from markets and treated as externalities, thereby informing economic decision-making . However, integrating natural capital accounting into actionable steps presents challenges, including the balance between the system's complexity and its usability for decision-makers. Often, national policies simplify these accounts to biophysical measures, e.g., areas or tons, and thus neglect sociocultural values . Natural capital accounting enriches traditional accounting frameworks by incorporating natural capital data, enabling the analysis of trade-offs among natural, built and human capital within standard economic indicators such as gross domestic product for informed decision-making . The System of Environmental Economic Accounting framework, developed by the United Nations, represents the most advanced natural capital information system, expanding national accounts to include ecosystem accounting (Figure 5.1.9) . This accounting framework facilitates the establishment of environmental goals, policy design, progress monitoring and evaluation, covering sustainability issues from biodiversity to climate change, and is recognized in international commitments such as the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework and the SDGs . Natural capital accounting addresses sustainable development and sustainable use of natural resources in general by enabling the accounting of the non-market benefits of nature, but it is not specifically linked to one particular nexus element. Rather, a broad variety of topics that involve nexus elements and interactions can be addressed using the natural capital accounting concept, such as land use planning, green economic growth or designing policy measures (taxes, regulations) . In developing nations, the focus is mainly on energy and water accounts due to data challenges, while developed countries prioritize accounts on material flows, environmental taxes and subsidies, especially concerning energy. This contrast reflects differing environmental priorities, implementation rates and the focus of account compilation, as well as disparities in funding and access to technical assistance . The effectiveness of natural capital accounting in shaping real-world economic policies requires further refinement and closer integration with economic models . Additionally, criticisms of the System of Environmental Economic Accounting framework for its top-down approach and exclusion of embracing traditional and biocentric knowledge underscores the need for methodological enhancements. Adopting an integrated valuation of ecosystem services as a methodological enhancement could more accurately reflect previously excluded knowledge and overlooked sociocultural values and IPLC perspectives . 5.1.3.14 Reconnecting people with nature (B14) Reconnecting people with nature encompasses the idea of restoring or fostering a deep connection between humans and natural environments. In current societies, many people have become increasingly disconnected from nature due to urbanization, technology and lifestyles . This disconnection has negative impacts on mental and physical health, particularly among people in Western societies and cities, and on environmental awareness and conservation efforts . Efforts to reconnect people with nature include outdoor education programs, physical activity in nature, green space management or gardening in urban areas, nature-based spiritual practices or therapies, sustainable living practices, nature-based arts and crafts, and environmental advocacy (Table 5.1.8). These efforts can be supported by government agencies developing green spaces in cities, environmental NGOs facilitating nature-based experience, healthcare providers promoting nature-based therapies, or educational institutions integrating outdoor learning into curricula . Most activities to reconnect people with nature contribute to diverse types of connections and have multiple effects on the nexus elements (Figure 5.1.9). For example, community gardens reconnect people with nature through material (e.g., food) and experiential connections (sensory) and contribute to cognitive (knowledge, inspiration) or emotional connections (feelings, appreciation) . Among the various types of knowledge that can be mobilized when reconnecting with nature, ILK is crucial because it offers cultural perspectives and practices that can foster a deeper understanding and appreciation of the natural world (Box 5.1.6). As evidenced from 1990 case studies synthesized in 23 reviews and meta-analyses, reconnecting people with nature yields numerous benefits for the nexus elements: direct benefits on health, food and biodiversity, as well as indirect benefits for water and climate change. However, scientific evidence is often geographically and socially biased towards Western societies and urban populations. While physical and mental health, cognitive performance and pro-environmental behaviours garner substantial attention, social and spiritual benefits remain underexplored, despite their significant potential implications . Reconnecting people with nature has well-established positive impacts on physical and mental health . Physical connections improve health by boosting activity levels and providing additional benefits such as lowered blood pressure, cortisol reduction, enhanced immune function and faster recovery from illness or surgery . Mental health benefits derive from both improved physical health and direct exposure to nature. For example, activities such as walking or running in natural settings can alleviate anxiety, anger or sadness , while natural sounds such as water or bird songs can reduce stress and enhance positive moods . Effective nature-based interventions such as gardening, green exercise and nature therapy further improve mood and reduce anxiety . However, connections with nature can also have negative physical impacts (e.g., animal attacks) , as well as psychological impacts . The need to reconnect people with nature incentivizes nature protection and habitat restoration, with positive impacts on biodiversity, water and climate change. For example, urban green spaces designed for enhancing well-being can also benefit urban biodiversity, water regulation, carbon sequestration and climate change adaptation . However, these benefits can have adverse effects, including the overuse of water or pesticides in community gardens , the carbon footprint of travelling to natural areas and degradation due to overcrowding in protected areas . Reconnecting people with nature can also directly benefit food production and access to healthy food. Community gardens enhance access to fresh produce and promote values of food sharing and cooking . Nature-based therapies may involve preparing food from natural products , whereas hikes in natural areas offer opportunities for foraging wild foods . Reconnecting people with nature influences attitudes and behaviours, leading to pro-environmental actions that indirectly benefit biodiversity, water resources and climate change . Individuals feeling connected to nature are more inclined to use climate-friendly transportation, renewable energies and eco-friendly products, to care for animals, to support local biodiversity protection initiatives, or to volunteer for conservation . Furthermore, the physical and mental health benefits derived from connecting with nature can enhance people's capacity to adapt to climate change . Reconnecting people with nature is considered economically efficient, as it leads to health improvements that can reduce the high economic and social costs associated with physical and mental health problems . Strengthening physical and psychological connections with nature can be accomplished at a moderate cost through targeted interventions, such as developing urban greenspaces, greening schoolyards or facilitating access to nature (e.g., transportation, information, education) and to nature-based mindfulness activities . It can also reduce environmental injustice by improving equitable access to green spaces and natural areas for all communities . Such initiatives must address the widespread inequities in physical access to nature, especially in cities, which stem from spatially-skewed distribution of green spaces among socioeconomic groups, transportation costs and lack of resources . Additionally, environmental education to promote psychological connections to nature can reduce existing inequities in childhood experiences or education about nature. Reconnecting people with nature is considered a leverage for transformation towards sustainability, particularly through psychological connections, which are more likely to provide systemic and deep societal changes than material or experiential connections . However, there are major barriers to overcome such as existing land-use configurations, including urbanization, which has led to the loss of natural experiences and increased distance from green areas. Other major barriers are modern lifestyles dominated by technology and screen time, which reduce motivation and opportunities for engaging with the natural world . Table 5.1.8. Types of connections (or disconnections and reconnections) according to Ives and possible actions to reconnect people with nature depending on the type of connections. Types of connection Definition or examples Examples of actions to reconnect Examples of enabling factors Material * Use of products from nature * Community gardens for urban dwellers to grow their own food * Use of locally grown food or other natural products (e.g., strengthening local supply chains, labelling local sourcing, prioritizing local food in restaurants) * Land planning policies * Procurement procedures * Legal instruments to support local food systems Experiential * Physical interactions * Sensory interactions * Activities in nature: leisure (e.g., camping), exploration (e.g., hiking), sport (e.g. climbing, swimming), productive activities (e.g., gardening, foraging) * Transportation networks * Accommodation facilities * Information to visitors * Facilitation by IPLC of access to Mother Nature experiences Cognitive * Knowledge about nature * Values, beliefs and attitudes * Inspiration * Education in nature * Observation and research (e.g., birdwatching) * Exposure to ILK (Indigenous science, innovations and practices) * Exploration * Education policies * Information networks * Media * Recognition and respect of ILK in science and policies * Facilitation by IPLC of Mother Nature-based education Emotional * Feelings, emotions, attachment, sense of awe, wonder and appreciation for the natural world * Sensory experiences * Visual art, literature and poetry that explore themes of nature * IPLC arts and rituals * Sharing stories * Meditation, reflection and contemplation * Cultural policies * Funding to artists and art institutions * Initiatives to share personal experiences and emotions in nature * Training on storytelling Philosophical * World views on nature * Reflections on the role of people as nature stewards * Perspectives on ecological justice and non-humans * Nature-based spiritual practices or therapies (e.g., forest bathing, mindfulness, wilderness therapy) * Exposure to philosophical traditions * Engagement with Indigenous perspectives (e.g., Indigenous cosmovision and cultural/spiritual practices) * Activism and advocacy * Education policies * Cultural policies * Initiatives that promote inclusive communities, support religious diversity and respect Indigenous spiritual practices * Recognition and respect of the relationship of IPLC with Mother Nature in science and policies Figure 5.1.9. Reconnecting people with nature. The types of connections mobilized by activities to reconnect people with nature (blue), their impacts on the nexus elements (green), the correlations and causalities identified in the literature (yellow) and transformative potential (red). Box 5.1.6. Weaving Indigenous knowledge into education to reconnect people with nature Nature-based environmental education can foster a deep connection to nature and has been suggested as a holistic approach to enhance pro-environmental behaviour. Indigenous wisdom and varied practices of Indigeous Peoples and local communities (IPLC) can enrich educational initiatives, all the while respecting the active involvement and rights of IPLC, including their entitlement to Free, Prior and Informed Consent . Weaving Indigenous perspectives into education programs enriches students’ comprehension of environmental issues through diverse cultural viewpoints and sustainable living methods. Studying Indigenous knowledge fosters an appreciation for the sacred respect for nature found in Indigenous cultures, deepening understanding of the intrinsic connection between humans and the natural world . Indigeous Peoples and local communities can engage directly in collaborative learning experiences, for example, Indigenous elders, knowledge holders and community members proposing experiential activities, storytelling and traditional practices. For instance, a primary school in Ghana integrated Indigenous ecological knowledge via outdoor learning activities led by Indigenous elders, aiming to deepen children’s understanding of human-nature connections and shape their environmental attitudes . To ensure Indigenous knowledge is included in education curricula, scholars must interact with local Indigenous communities and navigate the complexities of cross-cultural interactions . Such work means negotiating between Indigenous and Western knowledge systems to reinterpret meanings, but also challenging the authority of Western knowledge systems, honouring Indigenous viewpoints and encouraging self-reflection ." ;
