    ipbes:hasDescription "In chapter 18 of the IPCC’s Sixth Assessment Report, five domains are identified for rapid transitions to facilitate climate change mitigation, adaptation and sustainable development: (i) land, oceans and ecosystems; (ii) energy; (iii) settlement and infrastructure; (iv) industrial; and (v) societal systems . These system transitions are used for organizing the response options that have been assessed in this subchapter. Land, oceans and ecosystems transitions: Land, oceans and ecosystems are experiencing significant transitions driven by climate change, with implications for resilience and nature’s contributions to people (NCP) . Energy systems transitions: Energy systems require shifts towards low carbon (including renewable) energy sources, improved energy efficiency and carbon capture and storage to meet climate change mitigation objectives and abate emissions of CO2 from fossil fuels . Ongoing energy transitions respond to climatic and non-climatic considerations, with sustainable development priorities driving change . Settlement and infrastructure systems transitions: Urban and rural settlements and infrastructure play a crucial role in climate-resilient futures but face risks from climate stresses . Enhancing urban resilience can be achieved through investments in disaster risk reduction, climate-resilient green infrastructure and updated building codes . Integrating nature-based solutions and green infrastructure can enhance resilience and support economic development . Industrial systems transitions: Industrial systems transitions comprise dematerialization and decarbonization , supported by effective governance, enlightened policies, meticulous regulations and empowering corporate strategies . Social systems transitions: Societal systems transitions focus on changing behaviours, attitudes, values and consciousness across society . To select a set of response options for assessment, a non-exhaustive long list of about 70 options, representative of the five system transitions, were drawn mainly from previous IPCC and IPBES reports , supplemented by others identified by the chapter author team. The contribution of each response option to climate change adaptation or mitigation at the global scale and its contribution to the other nexus elements was assessed from the literature. Details of these response options, their spatial scale of implementation, the primary actors needed to implement them, the relative cost of implementation, and the contribution to mitigation (gigatonne carbon dioxide equivalent per year) and adaptation (millions of people who could benefit) at the global scale, and their contribution of other nexus elements: biodiversity, water, food and health (with supporting references) are available in data management report.2 From the long list of about 70 response options, 15 response options (Tables 5.5.2 and 5.5.3) were selected to analyse in greater detail by scoring against the common criteria3 used by all subchapters of Chapter 5 (see Chapter 5.0, Table 5.0.1). Additional criteria for selection of the fifteen were to ensure a spread of case studies across the following dimensions: representation across the five system transitions, strong climate change adaptation or mitigation potential, coverage of different geographical regions, response options applicable to land/freshwater/ocean, examples from rural/urban, examples with a strong component on Indigenous and local knowledge (ILK), and with a focus on solutions-oriented examples that show nexus benefits. Of the 15 response options assessed, five focal response options were selected or more detailed consideration: C11, Agroecology (Section 5.5.3.11); C12, Forest-based practices to address climate change (Section 5.5.3.12); C13, Restoration of coastal and marine ecosystems for carbon sequestration (Section 5.5.3.13); C14, Urban nature-based solutions (Section 5.5.3.14) and C15, Sustainable healthy diets (Section 5.5.3.15). These focal response options are not intended to represent priorities or recommendations. Rather, they are chosen to illustrate the range of issues and actors involved in response options and provide real-world examples of response options in action. These fifteen response options, including the five focal response options, are described in this section. Section 5.5.4 on nexus governance also delineates 21 distinct policy instruments and enablers for supporting the implementation of the response options. Table 5.5.2. Brief descriptions of the 15 climate change response options assessed. Climate change response option Brief description of response option Section C01 Increase soil organic carbon Interventions in managed ecosystems and landscapes to increase carbon inputs to soils, enable carbon to persist in soils for longer periods of time or reduce carbon losses from soils. 5.5.3.1 C02 Sustainable intensification Increasing agricultural yields without adverse environmental impacts and without expanding the land base used for agriculture (i.e., avoiding the conversion of intact ecosystems for the purpose of agriculture expansion). 5.5.3.2 C03 Integrated multitrophic aquaculture A traditional aquaculture method that uses waste products and uneaten food from species of a higher trophic level (e.g., farmed fish) to cultivate species at a lower trophic level (e.g., plants, invertebrates); the method reduces waste and increases harvestable biomass. 5.5.3.3 C04 Wetland conservation and restoration Restoring degraded and conserving intact peatlands and non-coastal wetlands to improve or sustain the structure and functions of these ecosystems and the contributions they provide to people. 5.5.3.4 C05 Offshore wind power The use of offshore wind energy to decarbonize energy systems and mitigate climate change. 5.5.3.5 C06 Solar photovoltaics on land The use of photovoltaics (i.e., solar panels or solar cells) in solar energy installations on land to produce renewable energy and mitigate climate change. 5.5.3.6 C07 Sustainable bioeconomy An economic model based on the use of renewable natural capital, including biological resources, that minimizes waste and reduces the use of fossil-based energy and products to conserve nature, mitigate climate change and support sustainable, equitable development. 5.5.3.7 C08 Reduce short-lived climate pollutants A range of strategies to reduce emissions of pollutants with strong near-term warming effects generated from the combustion of fossil fuels, biomass burning and agricultural activities; provides benefits to human health and mitigates climate change. 5.5.3.8 C09 Multi-hazard early warning systems Early warning systems designed to anticipate, predict and generate warnings for individual or multiple hazards that threaten human health and well-being and ecosystems; they are integral to disaster risk reduction strategies. 5.5.3.9 C10 Global cooperation for finance and technology International partnerships and commitments that include both public and private financing to support climate change mitigation and adaptation and to enable cooperation and equitable access to and benefits from technology. 5.5.3.10 C11 Agroecology A variety of practices to support sustainable management of agricultural lands with environmental, social and economic benefits; this assessed option particularly focuses on agroforestry systems, in which trees and woody shrubs are integrated into crop and livestock farming systems. 5.5.3.11 C12 Forest-based practices to address climate change Conserving, restoring and sustainably managing forests to support climate change mitigation and adaptation, conserve biodiversity and sustain nature’s contributions to people from forest ecosystems. 5.5.3.12 C13 Restoration of coastal and marine ecosystems for carbon sequestration Restoring mangrove, seagrass and salt marsh ecosystems to conserve and support biodiversity, contribute to climate change mitigation and adaptation and provide benefits to human health and well-being, including securing livelihoods. 5.5.3.13 C14 Urban nature-based solutions The implementation of nature-based solutions in urban areas to support climate change mitigation and adaptation, increase resilience to climate change, conserve and protect biodiversity and natural resources and promote human health and well-being. 5.5.3.14 C15 Sustainable healthy diets Sustainable healthy diets promote all dimensions of individuals’ health and well-being, have low environmental pressure and impact, are accessible, affordable, safe and equitable, and are culturally acceptable. 5.5.3.15 Table 5.5.3. Classification of the 15 selected climate change response options according to system transitions. Climate change response option Response option classification Land & oceans ecosystems transitions Energy systems transitions Settlement & infrastructure systems transitions Industrial systems transitions Social systems transitions C01 Increase soil organic carbon ? C02 Sustainable intensification ? C03 Integrated multitrophic aquaculture ? C04 Wetland conservation and restoration ? C05 Offshore wind power ? C06 Solar photovoltaics on land ? C07 Sustainable bioeconomy ? C08 Reduce short-lived climate pollutants ? C09 Multi-hazard early warning systems C10 Global cooperation for finance and technology C11 Agroecology ? C12 Forest-based practices to address climate change ? C13 Restoration of coastal and marine ecosystems for carbon sequestration ? C14 Urban nature-based solutions ? C15 Sustainable healthy diets ? Four of the fifteen response options (C01, C07, C09, C11) deliver exclusively positive outcomes for all elements of the nexus, with a further seven (C02, C03, C11, C12, C13, C14, C15) potentially delivering positive outcomes across all nexus elements (Figure 5.5.3). The remaining four (C04 Wetland restoration and conservation, C05 Offshore wind power, C06 Solar photovoltaics on land, C08 Reduce short lived climate pollutants) have negative impacts on at least one nexus element, though even for these response options, they have potential positive impacts on three or more nexus elements. For some of the potential negative impacts, methods of implementation or additional measures could be taken to minimize the risk of negative outcomes, as shown by cells with mixed outcomes (Figure 5.5.3). Figure 5.5.3. Impacts of climate change response options on nexus elements. Colours indicate positive impacts (blue; i.e., synergies) and negative impacts (red; i.e., trade-offs). Intensity of colour represents the magnitude of impact (light = small; medium = medium impact; dark = large;). Grey colouring indicates a score of 0. Where impacts differ depending on context, impact ranges are shown by multiple colours within a cell. NA indicates not applicable, NE indicates no evidence, and INC indicates the evidence is inconclusive. Figure 5.5.4 provides scoring of the response options on political priorities and international frameworks, social acceptability, economic and equity implications and transformative change potential. All response options have the potential for high social acceptability, are well aligned with political priorities, and all are well aligned with political priorities for biodiversity, climate change and the SDGs (see Section 5.5.6 for further discussion of alignment with global goals). All response options have potential gross and individual economic costs associated with them, although all also have potential for economic benefits, and all except two (C07, C08) have the potential to lower economic risks. In terms of equity, all response options have the potential to promote inclusion and/or recognition and procedural equity, and all either unequivocally support distributional equity (C01, C02, C06, C07, C09, C10, C11, C12, C13, C15), can support it if implemented well (C03, C04, C08, C14), with only C05 judged to mostly have a negative impact on distributional equity. All response options are judged to have positive transformative change potential (Figure 5.5.4). Figure 5.5.4. Climate change response options within the context of social, economic and political dimensions, international frameworks and transformative change potential. Colours indicate positive impacts (blue; i.e., synergies) and negative impacts (red; i.e., trade-offs). Intensity of colour represents the magnitude of impact (light = small; medium = medium impact; dark = large;). Grey colouring indicates a score of 0. Where impacts differ depending on context, impact ranges are shown by multiple colours within a cell. NA indicates not applicable, NE indicates no evidence, and INC indicates the evidence is inconclusive. Figure 5.5.5 illustrates temporal and spatial scales and technical feasibility of the response options. Most response options are applicable all over the world, though some are limited to specific systems (e.g., urban nature-based solutions to urban areas, peatland restoration to where peats occur), but most are implemented at the micro- or meso-scales. Most response options are implemented over the short- to long-term, but five (C01, C02, C05, C11, C15) deliver benefits only in the medium- to long-term, nine (C03, C04, C06, C07, C09, C10, C12, C13, C14) over all timescales, with C08 delivering benefits only in the short- and medium-term. Most response options are already widely implemented (Technology Readiness Level, TRL = 7-9), with only two (C08, C11) having components at a lower TRL level, though some components of these response options are at TRL level 9 (Figure 5.5.5). Figure 5.5.5. Temporal and spatial scales and technical feasibility of the climate response options. Ranges shown temporal and spatial scales and feasibility indicated by Technology Readiness Level.4 A detailed assessment of the response options, which led to the scoring in Figures 5.5.3, 5.5.4 and 5.5.5, is described in the following Sections 5.5.3.1 to 5.5.3.15. 5.5.3.1 Increase soil organic carbon (C01) Soil organic carbon (SOC) content can be increased through management interventions in croplands, grazing lands and managed forests that either increase carbon inputs to the soil, cause the carbon to persist longer in the soil, or reduce losses from the soil by decreasing soil disturbance or placing carbon at deeper layers of the soil . Carbon inputs can occur through traditional forms such as litter and manure, but also through newer technologies such as biochar . Increasing SOC content is a response option that can be applied globally on all managed land (Figure 5.5.5). It is beneficial across all nexus elements, with very large potentials for climate change mitigation potential (0.4–8.6 Gt CO2eq/yr) and adaptation (up to 3200 million people potentially benefiting) (Figure 5.5.3). However, soil carbon sinks, like all biological carbon sinks, are time-limited (due to sink saturation) and reversible (through, for example, poor management or extreme events such as fire) and could be impacted by climate change . For water, well-managed soils with high SOC content have high infiltration capacity that increases green water (available for plants to enhance crop production and nature) and regulates blue water (fewer floods) (Figure 5.5.3). Soils also function as a filter for pollutants, improving water quality . For food and health, higher SOC levels also give rise to higher crop yields which could alleviate the burden of undernutrition in some areas (with a subsequent strong positive impact on health), though there is limited evidence on its impact on food access or the nutritional value of food. For biodiversity, soil organic matter is a headline indicator of soil health. With around 120 thousand km2 of land lost to degradation every year , management practices that increase SOC would help increase the area in better condition, thereby improving soil health and biodiversity. Since increasing SOC content is practiced only on managed land, it has no impact on the area of land protected or other aspects of native biodiversity, but practices that lead to increased soil organic matter also tend to improve soil biodiversity . Increased SOC also improves ecological functioning, since soils higher in SOC function better than degraded soils with lower levels . Additionally, it also improves ecological resilience as soils higher in SOC are more resilient to drought and floods and less prone to soil erosion . Practices to increase SOC levels, which increase carbon inputs or decrease carbon losses, are already widely implemented through best management practices in agriculture (e.g., conservation agriculture) and forestry (Figure 5.5.5). There are no known social acceptability issues with implementing this response option and it aligns strongly with policies to improve soil and water quality, to combat land degradation and desertification, to increase food production and to tackle climate change (Figure 5.5.4). Increasing SOC is a relatively low-cost option though monitoring, reporting and verification (MRV) costs can be high (Figure 5.5.4). Since it leads to higher yields and improves resilience to drought and erosion , it enhances economic benefits and reduces risk. In terms of equity, many IPLC management practices are known to result in high carbon levels and biodiverse soils . For example, traditional homestead and landscape-level interventions in the Andaman and Nicobar Islands of India focusing on biodiversity, including the use of over 7500 plant species, contributed to SOC improvement through the enhancement of ecosystem diversity and resilience . When combined with other response options (e.g., B03/C11, Agroecology; Section 5.1.3.3; Section 5.5.3.11), it has high transformative change potential (Figure 5.5.4). 5.5.3.2 Sustainable intensification (C02) Sustainable intensification is a globally applicable response option (Figure 5.5.5) for increasing agricultural and livestock production efficiency while reducing environmental impact and avoiding land conversion. This can be achieved through a range of practices, including improved use of water, fertilizer and pesticides, improved crop varieties and protection of crop wild relatives, better management of soil health and pests, and the application of traditional and modern breeding techniques that take account of other concerns such as animal welfare . Sustainable intensification also considers the social and economic dimensions of agriculture and livestock production, ensuring that all stakeholders benefit. It is achieved using three non-linear stages: first efficiency, to reduce waste and improve resource use within existing systems; second substitution, through the replacement of less efficient technologies and practices with alternatives; and third redesign, transforming agroecosystems to deliver sustainability across all dimensions . According to estimates, around 163 million farms, comprising 29 per cent of global farms, have adopted SI practices on 453 million hectares of agricultural land, representing 9 per cent of the global total . SI can be implemented on all managed land, without favouring any vision or approach to agriculture. Sustainable intensification has large potential for climate change mitigation, with an estimated global reduction of approximately ~ 0.9-1.05 Gt CO2e/yr , and benefits adaptation for up to 3.2 billion people, reducing land pressure and enhancing production (Figure 5.5.3). It also has positive impacts on the water, food and health nexus elements. Sustainable intensification can decrease water use in irrigated croplands to meet the annual domestic water needs of nearly 1.4 billion people . It prioritizes not only current and future food production but also food’s diversity, types and nutritional value. Adoption of sustainable intensification practices enables farmers to enhance diets with crop diversity and increase overall food production , reducing the burden of undernutrition. SI practices fulfil basic human needs, promote landscape resilience and contribute to well-being . The impact of agriculture and livestock on biodiversity exhibits regional variations (Figure 5.5.3). It is estimated that nearly half the world’s land could be suitable for diversified systems to achieve sustainable intensification, but different regions benefit from various approaches depending on biophysical potential: expansion (e.g., Europe) or intensification (e.g., tropics), with some areas suited for both (e.g., West Africa) . Land clearing and habitat fragmentation pose significant threats to biodiversity, making a “land sparing” approach (like sustainable intensification) combined with “land sharing” (i.e., agroforestry) crucial to minimize biodiversity loss . In reducing the impact of agriculture and livestock on the environment sustainable intensification makes land available for nature; however, other factors related to governance, regulations, public policies and markets still must be considered to ensure the protection of nature. Despite numerous advantages of sustainable intensification, its implementation carries the potential for two negative consequences: the rebound effect and leakage. The rebound effect occurs when increased productivity leads to increased cropland utilization. Leakage occurs when a policy intervention displaces deforestation-related activities to other regions. Evidence suggests that agricultural intensification can have a short-term rebound effect, particularly in middle-income countries . For example, a study in Brazil found that intensification led to temporary reductions in local deforestation, but crop intensification was associated with increased deforestation over longer periods . This suggests that farmers may respond to increased productivity by expanding their cropland area. Related to leakage, a study in South America found that a decrease in beef imports from biomes with more stringent deforestation regulations was associated with an increase in beef imports from other countries . Interventions that limit production are likely to result in leakage unless demand is reduced or met by other means, indicating important trade-offs between land use, intensification and nature conservation. In other words, agricultural intensification can lead to land sparing for nature, but only if accompanied by policies to control cropland expansion, otherwise, the rebound effect and leakage may lead to increased land use and deforestation. The transition of farming systems to sustainable intensification considers multiple elements linked together. A significant upfront investment in smallholder sustainable agriculture is necessary, but it will yield long-term economic returns, e.g., it is estimated that a transformative investment of $10-20 billion per year for 10-15 years is required to support smallholders in Africa adopt sustainable agricultural practices . Smallholder farmers need financial support from government, investors and development organizations to successfully adopt sustainable intensification practices (Figure 5.5.4). Studies highlight potential disparities in the distribution of well-being impacts from sustainable intensification. While some argue that better-off farmers benefit more, others suggest equal distribution over time as the gap narrows during diffusion and adoption . Adequate support and addressing barriers and trade-offs are essential to transition to sustainable intensification. 5.5.3.3 Integrated multitrophic aquaculture (C03) Integrated multitrophic aquaculture is a traditional aquaculture method that has been practiced in Asia and the Mediterranean since ancient times . The method involves using the waste products and uneaten food generated by a high tropic level species (e.g., farmed fish) to cultivate other species at lower trophic levels, thereby reducing waste and mitigating its negative impacts on the environment and promoting sustainable food production . The system is based on the idea of combining several species, including fish and shrimp as main target species, with bivalves that feed on suspended particulate organic matter, seaweeds that convert dissolved inorganic carbon and nutrients into biomass, and occasionally deposit feeders on the bottom sediment such as echinoderms . Recently, there has been a shift towards combing sustainable food production with climate change adaptation and mitigation measures . This includes focusing on seaweed cultivation to increase CO2 sequestration, as well as mitigating the negative effects of climate change such as acidification on shellfish aquaculture . As a derivative, there is also a movement to integrate CO2 sequestration by natural blue carbon ecosystems with aquaculture . Recently seaweed farming has become a major crop of integrated multitrophic aquaculture in some regions , while finfish and shellfish aquaculture, previously the major product, is sometimes being used to alleviate nutrient limitation on seaweed growth . Integrated multitrophic aquaculture exhibits comparatively lower environmental impact and smaller spatial footprints compared with both capture fisheries and land-based agriculture . This is further emphasized in the shift from fed fish to non-fed species, such as filter feeding bivalves, algal grazers, detritivores and autotrophic plants (mainly macroalgae), as these are considered some of the lowest impact aquaculture in ambient waters, resulting in the lowest negative impact on the environment and biodiversity . In terms of food and biodiversity, integrated multitrophic aquaculture can provide nutritious and safe food to consume with lower risk of exposure to harmful microbial and chemical contaminants (Figure 5.5.3). In the case of seaweeds and bivalve farming, culture platforms can simultaneously act as nurseries for native biodiversity and boost productivity of wild organisms . Integrated multitrophic aquaculture effectively combines multiple species to utilize waste products for cultivation, enhancing environmental sustainability and providing nutritious, low-impact food while fostering biodiversity and aiding in climate change adaptation. By enhancing resource efficiency and profitability through reduced feed costs and improved overall yield, supports local communities by offering diverse employment opportunities and improving food security. Additionally, the adoption of integrated multitrophic aquaculture aligns with global sustainability goals and fosters international cooperation in sustainable aquaculture practices. 5.5.3.4 Wetland conservation and restoration (C04) A huge quantity of carbon is stored in peatlands and wetlands , but around 12 per cent of global peatlands have been drained and degraded . Restoration and reduced conversion of peatlands and non-coastal wetlands involves rewetting by blocking drainage channels and can include other interventions such as erosion prevention by stabilizing eroded peats through physical means (mounding or adding netting) and revegetation . Restoration can be applied anywhere that peatlands / wetlands have been degraded (Figure 5.5.5). Peatlands occur on all continents and cover 3-4 per cent on the Earth’s land surface . The global mitigation potential for reducing greenhouse gas emissions and increasing carbon storage through peatland / wetland restoration is 0.6-2.0 GtCO2e/yr, but there are no global estimates of adaptation potential (Figure 5.5.3). Degraded peatlands store less water than intact peatlands. Peatland restoration upstream in catchments is used as a nature-based solution for flood control, e.g., managing flood / highwater peaks downstream after heavy rainfall. The storage and regulation of water flow in wetlands may also buffer against the effects of drought . While tropical peats in South-East Asia have been substantially drained and cleared for non-food commodities (e.g., pulp and paper, and oil palm), large areas of tropical peatlands have been cleared for food production, and their restoration could displace food production and damage local food supply in these areas. The same is true for cultivated northern peatlands . At a local level, while peatland restoration on land used for agriculture could displace food production, the impacts to regional and global food security would be minimal (Figure 5.5.3). For health, rewetting of formerly drained areas in regions where vector-borne diseases (from organisms which have part of their lifecycle in water) are endemic, could lead to a greater disease burden, but can improve public health through decreased fire-caused pollutants locally and distributed more widely (Figure 5.5.3). Large-scale changes in the water balance of peatlands and type of vegetation influence evaporation, which has a cooling effect on the local or regional climate. This can influence local or regional weather cycles. Rewetting drained peatlands can reduce heat, and reduced heat has a positive impact on public health . Peatlands in good condition support a range of specialist and unique species, so peatland/wetland restoration will benefit biodiversity (Figure 5.5.3). Globally, only a small percentage of peatlands are protected and, in many regions, there is no protection at all . Hence, protection of peatlands / wetlands will improve the area of land protected, which enhances habitat connectivity and reduces fragmentation of intact peatlands, making them more resilient to, for example, the threat of erosion . Drained and degraded peatlands lose many peatland functions, including carbon and water storage, so peatland / wetland restoration also restores these ecological functions and a range of NCP . Restoration and reduced conversion of peatlands/wetlands is at TRL 8-9 (Figure 5.5.5), but there could be public acceptance issues around restoring wetlands in areas where vector-borne diseases are endemic due to health concerns (Figure 5.5.4). It aligns strongly with policies to improve soil and water quality, to combat land degradation and desertification and to tackle climate change . Costs can be relatively high. Humpenöder use one-time costs of $7,000 ha-1 (2005 market exchange rate) for the initial rewetting, and recurring costs of $200 ha-1 yr-1 for maintenance, based on an indicative cost estimate of £200–10,000 ha-1 for rewetting, and £25–400 ha-1 yr-1 for maintenance . Monitoring, reporting and verification costs, and opportunity costs associated with lost agricultural production, may be high. Direct economic benefits are limited, though carbon finance has provided income for peatland / wetland protection / restoration projects . In terms of equity, it provides benefits to IPLC (e.g. , and can enable inclusive and participatory processes (e.g. . When combined with other response options it could be transformative (Figure 5.5.4). 5.5.3.5 Offshore wind power (C05) Offshore wind energy is becoming a widely used solution for the decarbonization of the energy system. Its mitigation potential has been estimated at a maximum of 10 GtCO2/yr by 2050 and from 0.3-0.8 ºC by 2100 . Offshore wind farms employ fixed-foundation or floating turbines. The latter can be placed in deeper waters and are less impactful, but knowledge gaps exist . Offshore wind farms negative environmental impacts, such as noise, contaminants, electromagnetic fields, and risk of collision and entanglement, with particularly detrimental effects on migrating birds and bats, seabirds, marine mammals, reptiles and on seabed habitats and sedimentary processes, and they could also facilitate species invasions ; though the same is true of fossil fuel infrastructure. Although biodiversity and water quality are negatively affected, these impacts are low when compared with those of other energy systems (Figure 5.5.3). Offshore wind farms can have positive impacts. The exclusion of trawling can contribute to biodiversity and conservation and even augment neighbouring fisheries (spillover effects). OWF also create artificial hard substrates that increase biodiversity , which increases carbon assimilation (Figure 5.5.4). Combining offshore wind farms with integrated multitrophic aquaculture (C03; Section 5.5.3.3) is currently being explored , as an option to enhance food production. Optimal siting of offshore wind farmsshould minimize their negative effects (outside bird migration corridors) and maximize their positive benefits (homogeneous soft-bottoms) . Floating turbines can be used where possible. Ecosystem-based management principles are encouraged to avoid the not-in-my-backyard effect as seen in the Mediterranean Sea and minimize the detrimental effects on well-being. While operating offshore wind farms can be regarded as a renewable energy, the construction of the turbines and decommissioning phases currently require fossil fuels. Despite the drop in costs of this technology , there is a risk that large parts of the globe lag behind unless transitional costs in developing countries are addressed (Figure 5.5.4). Recycling and circular (at least spiral) economy principles would help to improve sustainability, e.g., replacing critical components by common and/or renewable materials, reusing rare earth elements or increasing the lifespan of turbines. Deep-sea mining to meet this demand, thereby impacting pristine marine areas, could have adverse consequences for biodiversity . 5.5.3.6 Solar photovoltaics on land (C06) Utility-scale solar energy installations (solar farms) to produce electricity by photovoltaics are among the most important options to produce renewable energy and mitigate climate change , with estimates of global mitigation potentials up to 7 GtCO2/yr and specific primary energy yields ranging from about 600 to 3000 GJ per ha . Solar PV can be deployed in different environments, from ground-mounted panels in rural areas to panels integrated into the built environment. Solar energy is key for improving adaptation capacity to climate change of local communities, as it secures an off-grid energy access (especially roof-top solar PV) . Solar photovoltaics interact with all nexus elements, in both positive and negative ways (Table 5.5.4; Figure 5.5.3). The main sustainability trade-offs of solar energy are connected to the possible competition with agricultural land, but these risks are reduced when solar panels are deployed on contaminated areas or over building/infrastructure roofs. Agro-photovoltaics, i.e., elevated PV panels in agricultural land that provide shading to the crops beneath them to preserve soil moisture and reduce drought stress, are an adaptation-mitigation strategy that can reduce the trade-offs associated with land use competition . A potential large-scale deployment of photovoltaic solar farms in a relatively limited area (e.g., over the Sahara Desert) has the potential to impact global cloud cover formation, precipitation patterns and increase surface temperature through disturbed atmospheric teleconnections induced by changes in biophysical surface properties . Off-grid and small-scale photovoltaic systems have the highest levels of social acceptance among renewables (Figure 5.5.4). Although it might have high infrastructure costs for households, supporting policies (tax credits, installation subsidies, etc.) and long-term extra earnings make micro- and small-scale applications affordable , even for remote regions in developing economies where PV systems can enable basic rights, such as electricity access and promotion of education, emergency healthcare and early-warnings of natural disasters . Table 5.5.4. Nexus interactions of solar photovoltaics. Nexus element Positive interactions Negative interactions Climate change High climate change mitigation and adaptation potential (see text). Large-scale deployment in a limited area risks altering moisture circulation pathways and inducing other climate feedbacks (e.g., changes in surface and cloud albedo) . Biodiversity The humid micro-habitat that forms underneath solar panels favour the presence of various invertebrates, pollinators and insects . Installation of solar farms on land has been associated with a degradation of natural habitats, habitat fragmentation and disorientation/damages of wildlife species because of the atypical sunlight or infrastructure concentration . These negative effects can be mitigated through a variety of measures, such as revegetating areas between and under the rows of solar panels . Water When deployed on land, solar PV offers drought resilience and groundwater sustainability, as it preserves soil humidity, and the water consumption of solar farms is one of the lowest among the different renewable energy alternatives (much lower than that of thermal power plants) . The water needs to clean solar panels that are exposed to dust in arid regions can increase competition for water with other uses, especially in areas affected by water scarcity . Food The use of solar energy in food processing in remote rural settings near the harvest site supports delivery of quality food at low cost, as it improves options for transportation and storage (e.g., enhancing refrigeration, drying or ventilation) and reduces post-harvest food losses . The main food system impacts of solar energy are connected to the possible competition with agricultural land, as PV panels can replace food crops and induce market-mediated effects . These risks do not occur when solar panels are integrated within urban systems, but their climate change mitigation potential is lower . Health Solar energy can induce benefits to human health by reducing air pollution relative to thermal plants and by securing electricity access for health facilities in poor and/or remote areas . A proper disposal of solar panels is necessary when plants are decommissioned to avoid chemical pollution to the environment and exposure to carcinogenic gases . 5.5.3.7 Sustainable bioeconomy (C07) Circular bioeconomy is an innovative concept that combines the principles of circular economy and bioeconomy to harness renewable natural capital, including biological resources for economic, environmental and social benefits. Circular bioeconomy contributes to several crucial outcomes across different sectors (Figure 5.5.3). It protects ecosystems and environmental integrity, prevents biodiversity loss, minimizes natural resource consumption, reduces pollution and GHG emissions, and promotes sustainable management of biological resources. Reducing the use of pesticides and fertilizers, improving soil health and water quality, and promoting habitat connectivity positively influence biodiversity. Investments in circular bioeconomy can also contribute up to 200 MtCO2/yr of sequestration by 2050 . A circular bioeconomy improves water quality and quantity by implementing bio-based products, water recycling and water-efficient measures in agriculture and industry. It addresses issues such as agricultural runoff, water efficiency, advanced wastewater treatment and wetland restoration . In terms of food production, a circular bioeconomy focuses on reducing food waste, developing technologies for food waste recycling, lowering food prices, improving nutrition and diversifying food supply through unconventional sources. It optimizes resource utilization and waste reduction, and encourages sustainable practices using breeding technologies, bio-based products and energy, including energy from waste, resulting in improved environmental performance . Examples of circular bioeconomy are found in many countries (Table 5.5.5). While the circular bioeconomy approach can align with socio-cultural values by integrating ILK, respecting cultural diversity and involving stakeholders, concerns have been raised regarding its effectiveness in decoupling economic development from finite resource consumption (Figure 5.5.4). Proactive risk management strategies and policy frameworks are essential to ensure long-term success and sustainability. Equity should also be prioritized by acknowledging and respecting the diverse identities, knowledge systems, world views and values of communities and stakeholders . A circular bioeconomy aids in the conservation of terrestrial, inland water and marine ecosystems, mitigates climate change, reduces poverty and improves health and well-being . Thus, the circular bioeconomy approach offers a promising approach to address environmental, economic and social challenges while promoting sustainable development and biodiversity conservation. Table 5.5.5. Global initiatives in the sustainable bioeconomy. Country Initiative/Strategy Focus areas Climate benefits References Finland Finland's Bioeconomy Strategy Forestry, bioenergy, biochemicals, sustainable resource management Mitigation through reduced GHG emissions and sustainable forest management Lipiäinen & Vakkilainen Brazil Integrated Crop-Livestock-Forestry Systems Biodiversity, carbon sequestration, integrated land use Both adaptation and mitigation through enhanced carbon storage and ecosystem resilience Bungenstab Sweden Bioeconomy Region Initiative Wood-based products, bioenergy Mitigation by reducing carbon footprints through sustainable forest use Fischer Germany BioEconomy Cluster Industrial biotechnology, bio-based materials and energy Mitigation by replacing fossil-based industrial processes Macht Canada Industrial Bioeconomy Strategy Bioenergy, biowaste valorisation Mitigation through bioenergy production and waste reduction Madadian Kenya Biogas Programs Renewable energy from organic waste, soil fertility Both adaptation (soil health, agriculture support) and mitigation (renewable energy, reduced methane emissions) Mwirigi Japan Biomass Town Initiative Local biomass use for energy and materials, waste reduction Mitigation by promoting energy self-sufficiency and reducing waste Goh United States of America BioPreferred Program Promotion of biobased products, market development Mitigation through reduced reliance on petroleum, decreased GHG emissions Rogers India National Biomass Mission Crop residue management, bioenergy production Both mitigation (reduced stubble burning and emissions) and adaptation by improving soil quality A. Kumar France National Bioeconomy Strategy Sustainable agriculture, forest management, renewable materials Both adaptation (enhanced agricultural practices) and mitigation (forest carbon sinks) Stadler & Chauvet Australia Waste to Biofuels Initiative Conversion of urban waste to biofuels Mitigation by reducing landfill waste and producing cleaner fuels Dastjerdi China Biomass Renewable Energy Projects Biomass for heat, power, and biofuels production Mitigation by replacing coal with biomass energy, reducing air pollution Chung 5.5.3.8 Reduce short-lived climate pollutants (C08) Short?lived climate pollutants are highly reactive compounds frequently co-emitted with CO2 in combustion processes for energy production or transport . They include aerosols, such as particulate matter (PM) or black carbon, sulphates (SOx), nitrates, ammonium (NH3), and ozone (O3) precursors such as nitrogen oxides (NOx) or volatile organic carbon. Short?lived climate pollutants persist in the atmosphere from a few hours to a couple of months. Their climate effect predominantly occurs in the first two decades after emission (or formation) and consists of both warming and cooling, with potential changes in cloud formation and precipitation patterns . SLCPs are also the main contributors to air pollution, damaging human health and negatively influencing land and ocean ecosystem productivity with implications for food security, water quality and biodiversity (Figures 5.5.3 and 5.5.6) . Emission sources and atmospheric concentrations of short?lived climate pollutants are highly heterogenous. The largest surface concentrations of particulate matter occur in Southern Asia, Eastern Asia and the Middle East, where the dominant anthropogenic emission sources are biomass and coal fuel-based cooking and heating, and, especially in Eastern Asia, energy and industry (Figure 5.5.5). At a global scale, road transport emissions from gasoline and diesel vehicles are the dominant contributor to surface ozone concentrations in populated areas . Aviation is mostly responsible for emissions of NOx, aerosols and formation of persistent condensation trails (contrails) , while shipping is an important polluting factor (mainly SOx, NOx, and PM) in coastal areas along the major trade routes . The type of short?lived climate pollutant and impacts to be prioritized reflect the local context. For example, emissions from the residential and commercial sectors (fossil and biomass use for cooking and heating) and the energy sector (fossil fuel production, distribution and combustion) contribute the most to human exposure to PM, and mitigation measures in each region primarily target the main local source of pollution (i.e., the residential sector in Southern Asia and Africa, or the industry and energy sector in Central and Eastern Asia). Alternatively, emissions from energy and land transportation sectors contribute the most to ozone exposure and are relevant within cities affected by poor air quality. Short?lived climate pollutant emission reduction strategies include measures such as energy efficiency, zero-emissions vehicles, reduced use of private vehicles, efficient and clean stoves for heating and cooking, soot filters, flue gas scrubbers and cleaner industrial processes. The interactions with nexus elements are multiple and complex (Table 5.5.6; Figure 5.5.3). The evident and quantifiable health benefits of short?lived climate pollutant abatement favor their social acceptance, but proper instruments should ensure a just transition, particularly when addressing the energy and transport sectors in poorer urban areas (Figure 5.5.4). Co-benefits for air quality and human health of short?lived climate pollutant mitigation can offset the costs of climate measures . The transformation of the energy system included in nationally determined contribution pledges following the Paris Agreement can reduce local air pollution and globally avoid 71 to 99,000 premature deaths annually by 2030 (1.5 million by 2050 under stricter mitigation scenarios), thereby offsetting the costs of climate policies . Table 5.5.6. Interactions of reductions in emissions of short-lived climate pollutants with the nexus elements. Although the term short?lived climate pollutants sometimes includes short-lived greenhouse gases such as methane and some halogenated compounds, this response option only considers aerosols and ozone precursors. Nexus element Positive interactions Negative interactions Climate Change The effects of air quality policies on climate change mitigation (and vice versa) are generally immediate and positive, especially if warming components such as black carbon and O3 precursors are abated, but the magnitude of the benefits differs regionally due to varying background atmospheric compositions and inter-continental transport of pollutants . However, as SCLPs have a short-term climatic effect that mostly contributes to the rate of climate change, their importance for longer-term temperature stabilization is reduced . The synergies between climate benefits and air quality are largely dominant in the case of a worldwide transition to cleaner energy and transport systems Reduction of direct and indirect aerosols that scatter solar radiation (e.g., NOx, and SOx) can be associated with warming. For example, decreasing the amount of sulphate aerosols from maritime transport improves air quality but results in a warming effect because sulphate aerosols contribute to cooling by reflecting incoming sunlight . However, cooling compounds are typically co-emitted with CO2 and their abatement frequently occurs in combination with the additional benefits of reductions in CO2 emissions . Biodiversity Reducing emissions of reactive nitrogen decreases the fertilization effect of ecosystems from N deposition, reducing risks of eutrophication, acidity of soils and water bodies and altered plant growth competition, which all result in biodiversity reductions in terrestrial and aquatic ecosystems . Reducing emissions of ozone-precursors decreases surface ozone concentrations and the associated damages to photosynthesis and vegetation growth . Ozone-induced losses in vegetation carbon uptake are estimated to be up to 20 per cent in polluted regions . It also reduces the ozone stress that can cause shifts in species composition in diverse plant communities . The reduction of tropospheric aerosols reduces their negative impact on land and ocean ecosystem productivity arising from a changed physical climate and meteorology Reductions in the deposition of reactive nitrogen (i.e., NH3 and NOx) emitted from anthropogenic activities weakens the stimulation of plant growth and carbon sequestration in N-limited areas, thereby weakening natural carbon sinks and decreasing the positive effect on crop yields . Water Little or no relevant interactions. Little or no relevant interactions. Food Reducing emissions of ozone precursors decreases surface ozone concentrations and associated damages to crop yields . Tropospheric ozone is estimated to cause annual losses of approximately 100 million tonnes of staple crops (wheat, rice, maize and soybean) . In China, air quality targets are expected to increase yields of maize, rice and wheat from about 3 to 8 per cent . Similarly, black carbon damages crops when deposited on leaves . Reducing emissions of reactive nitrogen reduces the positive effects from N deposition on crop yields .. Health Reduction of emissions of SLCPs is a key measure to support public health in densely populated areas. A few weeks after reduction in exposure, respiratory and irritation symptoms, such as shortness of breath, cough, phlegm and sore throat, disappear and related mortality significantly decreases . This certain large response in a relatively short period makes the benefits of SLCPs emission reductions cost-effective, emphasizing their urgency and importance . None. Figure 5.5.6. Sources, processes and effects across the nexus elements of mitigation of emissions of short-lived climate pollutants. Data for anthropogenic emissions by sectors and regions are from and the main impact pathways are adapted from . Short-lived climate pollutants affect the climate system by inducing changes in solar radiation, surface albedo and clouds or precipitation, resulting in either cooling or warming effects. Climate change can in turn induce changes in emissions of short-lived climate pollutants and atmospheric chemistry and composition (climate feedbacks). Short-lived climate pollutants also affect human health, biodiversity, water and food systems through a variety of mechanisms. Interventions aiming to reduce emissions of short-lived climate pollutants are thus linked to multiple implications for the nexus elements and are usually associated with abatements in emissions of CO2 (as they are typically co-emitted from combustion processes). SOx = sulphates; NOx = nitrogen oxides; BC = black carbon; OC = organic carbon; CO – carbon monoxide; VOC = volatile organic carbon; NH3 = ammonium; PM = particulate matter; O3 = ozone precursors. 5.5.3.9 Multi-hazard early warning systems (C09) The Sixth IPCC Assessment Report underscores the direct effects of climate change on extreme events and associated hazards that threaten both human health and ecosystems, particularly the most vulnerable populations living in fragile environments (Figures 5.5.6 and 5.5.7). Multi-hazard early warning systems are designed to anticipate, predict and generate warnings for individual or multiple hazards, allowing communities and administrations to prepare, respond and recover effectively and thus build resilience against socio-economic and environmental changes . However, the one-way flow of information is limiting in addressing multi-dimensional risk communication that influences local response attributed to various factors such as trust, knowledge, heuristics, experience or socio-cultural contexts . Integrated risk communication with multi-hazards early warning systems thus ensures that communities receive, understand and act on impending hazard alerts and that they are adapted to local circumstances and needs . By providing timely warnings for multiple hazards across the globe, such as thunderstorms, flooding, cyclones, heatwaves, water scarcity, food security, earthquakes and diseases, multi-hazard early warning systems and risk communication aid both key stakeholders and communities and thus impact the nexus elements (Figure 5.5.3). Multi-hazard early warning systems collect and process data then communicate and coordinate through assigned authorities of a jurisdiction. This process necessitates collaborative actions from related stakeholders. For example, in healthcare, multi-hazard early warning systems monitor heat, water-borne and vector-borne diseases in changing ecological scenarios that affect agriculture, health and cities . However, the adoption of warning systems varies across regions and hazards, with only half of global countries accessing early warnings. Regions such as Africa and small island developing states especially need support . A recent initiative from the United Nations aims for “early warning for all” by 2027, highlighting the commitment to regional and social equality . Multi-hazards early warning systems are also integral to the Sendai Framework of Disaster Risk Reduction (2015-2030) . The incorporation of ILK with multi-hazards early warning systems and risk communication could further increase their effectiveness through enhanced participation of IPLC (Figure 5.5.4). The ongoing efforts to mainstream traditional knowledge of risk communication are irregular and tend to vary across countries and regions 5.5.3.10 Global cooperation for finance and technology (C10) Global cooperation in finance and technology is pivotal for climate action, with international partnerships and commitments acting as central catalysts . Public and private financing shapes adaptive strategies and mitigation efforts, crucial in climate-vulnerable sectors. The commitment by the Conference of the Parties the UNFCCC at its 15th meeting (COP 15) to mobilize $100 billion annually by 2020 for climate action in developing countries highlights this effort, despite a continuing shortfall acknowledged at 2021 United Nations Climate Change Conference, where contributions reached only $89.6 billion by 2021, and favoured mitigation over adaptation . The UNEP adaptation gap report underscores the inadequacy of current financing, estimating necessary adaptation costs at $194 to $366 billion annually, far surpassing current flows of finance . Efforts to balance financial allocations between adaptation and mitigation according to the Paris Agreement have seen calls for developed nations to double their adaptation finance by 2025 from 2019 levels . The glaring financial gap hinders effective adaptation in developing countries, necessitating increased contributions from both developed and developing nations, with the latter focusing on earmarked allocations for climate interventions . The looming economic impact of climate-related loss and damage, projected between $290 and $580 billion by 2030, emphasizes the urgency of operationalizing finance facilities for loss and damage . The interconnectedness of climate and biodiversity finance is apparent, with target 19 of the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework advocating for a significant increase in biodiversity funding (currently around $200 billion annually), aiming to bridge the funding gap of at least $0.3 to 1 trillion (see Section 6.2) and aligning financial strategies for climate and biodiversity to achieve broader sustainability goals (Figure 5.5.3). Moreover, there is an urgent need to mainstream technology transfer into finance, particularly to operationalize the technology transfer provisions established by the UNFCCC, such as the Climate Technology Centre and Network. This could involve exemptions from patentability or compulsory licensing to facilitate benefit-sharing . Countries with poor intellectual property rights (IPR) protections have fewer initiatives as low-carbon technology patents are mostly held by the Global North which is an impediment for the Global South to decarbonize . At the 2023 United Nations Climate Change Conference the fiscal constraints and increasing costs to adapt were acknowledged, and the need for public and grant-based resources for adaptation in developing country Parties was reiterated . Technological cooperation is essential for ensuring equitable access to resources and adaptive strategies, fostering innovation and mitigating the effects of climate change. Addressing intellectual property rights and promoting open innovation are necessary to lower deployment costs of low-carbon technologies, integrating them as global public goods . Strengthening international collaboration in technology transfer enables developing countries to meet their climate commitments while contributing to sustainable development goals, though challenges remain in ensuring equitable benefits from global technology development (Figures 5.5.4 and 5.5.5). 5.5.3.11 Agroecology (C11) Agroecology (see also Section 5.1.3.3, B03, Agroecology) includes a variety of practices for diversification and more sustainable management of farmland, such as agroforestry, intercropping, crop and pasture rotation, silvopasture, organic amendments, cover crops and organic farming, among others . These options have the potential to co-deliver climate change adaptation and mitigation while promoting biodiversity conservation, livelihoods, health and equity . Multiple agroecology-based practices exist. For illustrative purposes, this response option primarily focuses on agroforestry, given its wide implementation potential (Table 5.5.5), and briefly refers to other practices (agricultural diversification and conservation agriculture) that are representative of different geographical contexts and nexus interactions, as discussed in the Box 5.5.1. In agroforestry, trees and woody shrubs are integrated into agricultural land and have the potential to restore ecosystem functional integrity into intensively managed landscapes for securing critical NCP . It can be practiced in nearly all climatic zones and farming systems, but the main environmental benefits are usually in tropical and semi-arid areas . Agroforestry has positive effects on all nexus elements, but some trade-offs can also occur (Figure 5.5.3, Table 5.5.7). Barriers to the adoption of agroforestry are mostly connected to lack of knowledge, relatively high opportunity costs of establishment (e.g., potential for direct reduction of cultivated land), maintenance costs (e.g., the presence of trees can affect the types of equipment used for harvesting crops) and fragmented or missing actor networks . The modest establishment costs can be mitigated by considering that profits from monocrops tend to decrease over time from unsustainable soil use, while agroforestry can secure yields and diversified products (fruits, fuelwood and fodder), stabilizing incomes in the long-term . Social barriers can be overcome by building social capital among key actors and local communities , so to gradually make agroforestry a mainstream practice of local farming . Direct involvement of gender diversity in the adoption of agroforestry can both facilitate its deployment and favour beneficial outcomes for women in rural communities . Overall, active engagement and participation of local communities, use of local tree species and legitimization of Indigenous ontologies are a prerequisite for realizing the multiple potential benefits of agroforestry systems, including equity and social justice (Figure 5.5.4). Agricultural diversification, such as mixed cropping or livestock systems, crop rotations, and intercropping, promotes plant variety and diversification of income in agricultural landscapes as a measure to enhance food system resilience, increase NCP supply (pollination, pest control, nutrient cycling) and reduce management inputs . An example is the Landrace maize diversity in the Indigenous polyculture milpa system in the Santa Marta Mountains, Mexico . Within this system, each variety contributes with its special characteristics to support the others. The 15 variations of native maize have improved adaptation capacity to local climatic conditions, further promoting biodiversity and NCP in cropping systems. Conservation agriculture includes practices such as reduced tillage, residue retention, cover crops and mulching. It aims to reduce the environmental impact of intensive farming and prevent losses in soil fertility, with additional benefits for soil stability by increasing protection from erosion, floods and droughts . It supports climate action by enhancing soil carbon storage, biodiversity and natural biological processes above and below ground, with effects on increased water and nutrient use efficiency that can secure yields (Miralles-Wilhelm, 2021). For example, cultivation of additional cover crops in winter favoured by warmer temperatures in cold regions is an option to increase soil carbon sequestration, reduce use of synthetic fertilizers (by using legume cover crops), promote climate change adaptation (by reducing soil erosion and adverse effects from extreme events) and secure additional income for farmers (if cash crops are planted) (Kaye and Quemada, 2017). The proportion of time under cover crops usually has stronger impact than crop diversity on soil microbial diversity, soil multifunctionality and yields (Garland, 2021). Increasing crop cover can thus enhance yields, soil functioning and habitat for soil microorganisms. No-tillage is particularly successful in arid regions, where evidence shows win-win outcomes in terms of increased soil carbon storage, crop yields (Sun, 2020) and improved water use efficiency . Table 5.5.7. Interactions of agroforestry with the nexus elements. Nexus element Positive interactions Negative interactions Climate change Agroforestry contributes to climate change mitigation by sequestering CO2, with global technical potential estimates up to 5.7 GtCO2/yr , but the achievement of high mitigation rates is limited by competition with row crops . It supports climate change adaptation by stabilizing yields through regulation of field-scale microclimate, including wind barriers, soil quality and generally improving farmers’ capacity to cope with risks and extreme events . Little or no relevant interactions. Biodiversity The strategic placement of trees in agricultural landscapes can enhance NCP supply, such as pollination, aesthetic value, habitat and diversified products . Generally, agroforestry systems support higher biodiversity of birds, insects and small rodents, but the effects vary over space and time (Wurz, 2022, Mupepele, 2021). If trees with low genetic diversity or non-native species are planted, benefits are reduced and the trees are at higher risk of suffering from environmental changes or disease, while a genetically diverse agroforestry system is more resilient . Little or no relevant interactions. Water Agroforestry can increase soil water content and nutrient retention by reducing water runoff and nutrient leaching, so increasing crop resilience to droughts and reducing demand for irrigation and fertilizers . Planting non-native tree species and/or trees with excessive evapotranspiration rates can compete with crops for water and progressively induce soil moisture declines. For example, a large-scale tree planting programme in the Loess Plateau of China (a semi-arid region) successfully reduced soil erosion but increased depletion of soil moisture and exacerbated competition for water between trees, agriculture and human needs (Feng, 2016, Ge, 2020). Food By retaining a higher concentration of soil nutrients, agroforestry can increase soil nutrient availability for crop growth and reduce fertilization costs , which are key benefits for poor rural areas where farmers often cannot afford commercial fertilizers or soil nutrient content is low. Agroforestry can increase crop yields while maintaining regulating and provisioning NCP, e.g., in sub-Saharan Africa, but lower yields can occur near older trees that are not actively managed (Kuyah, 2016). Agroforestry can induce a short-term trade-off with food production due to reduction in cultivated areas or to a temporary pause in farming operations for the period of tree establishment, leading to its low social acceptance (as farmers likely prefer annual monocultures with early incomes and short time-lag on returns) . Health Agroforestry contributes to health by provisioning micronutrient-rich fruits, leafy vegetables, and nuts , and by lowering ambient temperature reduces heat exposure risks . It also provides wind barriers and prevents dispersion of mineral dust and particles in the air, thereby reducing inhalation of particulate matter that is connected to several health problems in semi-arid areas. Tree products also serve key medicinal functions in many parts of the world . Agroforestry offers opportunities for their cultivation and stabilization of supply, preventing unsustainable harvest from wild trees. Agroforestry might increase risks of infectious disease outbreaks, especially if implemented alongside tropical forest margins as it reduces habitat fragmentation (Rosenstock, 2019). This influences species abundance and distribution, with higher risks of human exposure to pathogens either via bush meat consumption, livestock transmission or direct contact. Box 5.5.1. Agroecology: Andhra Pradesh Community Managed Natural Farming Initiative The Andhra Pradesh Community Managed Natural Farming (APCNF) initiative in India (Figure 5.5.8) represents a transformative shift towards agroecological farming to address the socio-economic and environmental impacts of conventional chemical-intensive agriculture . The Reconstruction Credit Institute, better known by its German name, Kreditanstalt für Wiederaufbau, serves as the financing partner, while Rythu Sadhikara Samstha, established by the Andhra Pradesh government, is responsible for executing the project . The initiative seeks to bolster biodiversity, water conservation, food security, public health and climate resilience. APCNF addresses the dual challenge of socio-economic and environmental sustainability in agriculture. It leverages the principles of agroecology to institute a transformative farming model, enhancing the ecological services provided by agricultural systems . Figure 5.5.7. The Andhra Pradesh Community Managed Natural Farming initiative in India. Photo credit: Yash Srivastava under licence CC BY-ND. Scale of intervention: The APCNF initiative is being implemented across 721 Gram Panchayats (village councils) spread across all 26 districts of Andhra Pradesh, engaging over 10,000 skilled community resource persons across clusters to advance natural farming. Women’s self-help groups and village organizations are instrumental in farmer mobilization and planning . By March 2025, the programme aims to encompass over one million farmers. Stakeholder engagement is crucial, with the involvement of women and men farmers, local NGOs and government agencies being key contributors to APCNF’s success and its foundational strategies. Elements of implementation: The APCNF initiative strategically incorporates agroecological practices known as the four wheels of natural farming - Jeevamrutha, Beejamrutha, Achhadana, and Waaphasa, which help in enhancing the soil microbiome, bolstering plant health and fostering a conducive environment for essential biological processes . (Table 5.5.8). The project implementing agency shared that pre-monsoon, dry sowing aimed at maintaining green cover and mulching at 65 per cent cover generates additional revenue (52 per cent increase) and personal consumption of crops (63 per cent increase). Kitchen gardens are promoted as a stepping-stone for farmers to promote chemical-free farming. The APCNF programme promotes agroecological practices that include maintaining year-round crop cover, minimal soil disturbance, application of biostimulants, use of Indigenous seeds, cultivation of diverse crops, crop rotation, integration of animals into farming, enhancing soil with organic residues, botanical pest management and avoidance of synthetic fertilizers and pesticides . Nexus benefits: The initiative has produced measurable benefits across several nexus elements. Agrobiodiversity has been strengthened, water management systems have been improved and the programme has contributed to climate change mitigation efforts, mainly through carbon sequestration , as shown by the principles in Table 5.5.7. The health of local communities has also improved, as indicated by the decrease in pesticide residues and the enhancement of food quality . Sustainable integration: The APCNF initiative is firmly grounded in local wisdom and participatory approaches, fostering a regenerative economy pivotal to its achievements . These practices enhance ecological health and bolster the vitality and fiscal resilience of the farming community, reflecting APCNF’s dedication to sustainable and just agricultural progress . The programme’s approach has reduced dependence on external agricultural inputs, yielding financial benefits. There has been a noted improvement in soil health, evidenced by a rise in beneficial soil microbes and organic carbon levels, which benefits local water systems . Moreover, APCNF has empowered socio-economic groups, particularly women within self-help groups, creating a sense of community-led agricultural stewardship and shared prosperity . Table 5.5.8. Four wheels of natural farming. Principle Purpose Composition/ Method Application Benefits Jeevamrutha Enhances soil microbiome, aligned with organic amendments to foster soil health through microbial enhancement Fermented mixture of cow dung, cow urine, jaggery, pulse flour, water and soil Applied to soil to increase microbial population and improve fertility Increases microorganism count in the soil, improving soil fertility and health Beejamrutha Provides microbial seed coating, complementing organic amendments by offering a protective and nutritive seed coating Mixture of cow dung, cow urine, lime and soil Seeds are treated before sowing to protect against diseases and aid in germination Removes toxins, acts as a fungicide, and gives disease resistance to the plant Achhadana Protects soil, retains moisture, and prevents weed growth; integrates with mulching and cover cropping practices for soil conservation and health Soil mulch: soil cover between plants; Straw mulch: organic waste cover; Live mulch: cover crops Applied on soil surface to conserve moisture, add organic matter and encourage a healthy soil ecosystem Creates a conducive environment for biological processes in the soil Waaphasa Ensures soil aeration, supporting soil aeration principles for improved soil structure and health Minimal soil disturbance to maintain structure Practices that allow natural soil aeration, promoting the activity of soil microorganisms and fauna Enhances soil aeration, which is critical for root development and nutrient uptake Conclusions: The APCNF initiative is a significant advancement in sustainable agriculture. It marks a departure from traditional, chemical-intensive farming towards an environmentally, economically and socially sustainable model. The initiative’s sustained success depends on continuous engagement and support, crucial for maintaining a robust and long-lasting farming system. 5.5.3.12 Forest-based practices to address climate change (C12) Optimized management of existing forests, prevention of forest losses, reforestation, forest restoration and afforestation are among the main multi-purpose and cost-effective options for climate change mitigation via solutions based on nature . Although forest carbon sequestration and storage are not a substitute for urgent reductions of fossil CO2 emissions , the conservation, restoration and sustainable management of forests offer contributions to achieve climate and biodiversity targets. For example, reduced deforestation and forest degradation plays an important role in climate change mitigation and adaptation. Likewise, improved and sustainable forest management (ISFM) maintains and enhances the social, economic, ecological and cultural values of forests for the benefit of present and future generations . Reduced forest degradation, forest restoration and ISFM are strongly interconnected in terms of their objectives and approach. The mitigation potential of reduced deforestation and forest degradation is high (up to 5.8 Gt CO2-eq/yr) (Figure 5.5.3). ISFM also has a high mitigation potential as it maintains forest carbon sinks (estimates are up to about 2 GtCO2/yr) , it makes forests more resilient to climate-driven disturbances and it temporarily stores carbon in long-lived products . Reforestation has a large mitigation potential (up to 10 GtCO2/yr, with realistic figures around 3 GtCO2/yr) . It can be implemented globally, but the highest benefits occur in the tropics where carbon sequestration rates and concentration of endangered species that live in the forests are high (Figure 5.5.5). In the tropics, biophysical effects of reforestation also reinforce the cooling contributions from CO2 removal, while in areas with seasonal snow cover, the cooling benefits of reforestation are reduced due to changes in surface albedo . In terms of forest restoration potential, global forest carbon storage is currently below its natural potential (with an estimated total deficit of 226 GtC). Since most of this potential lies in areas with existing forests where human footprint is low, effective ecosystem protection and management can promote forest restoration up to 139 GtC by allowing forests to recover to maturity . All forest-based solutions for climate action have the potential to co-deliver across multiple nexus elements (Table 5.5.9; Figure 5.5.3; Box 5.5.2). Interactions are mostly positive, with the main potential trade-offs connected to land competition and food security. Forest projects should be conducted in light of the local context and community to be successful, and be developed together with measures that improve the efficiency in the agri-food sector at both local and global levels (e.g., closing yield gaps in developing countries, reducing beef consumption) to decrease pressure on land resources and reduce inequalities . Forest-based solutions are relatively inexpensive and may enable distributional, recognitional and procedural equity, provided that identities, knowledge, values and views of local communities are considered (Figure 5.5.4). There is evidence that reforestation programmes have helped to support local livelihoods and decrease poverty, but economic benefits are dependent on CO2 sequestration pricing schemes, support for nature conservation benefits and existence of national targets and/or private investments . In cases where equity was not recognized or enhanced, the risks of suboptimal outcomes have been higher . Social acceptability of forest-based options is usually higher in developed countries, where forests are regulated via certified practices of management and various levels of protection, while it tends to be lower in developing countries and remote areas that are highly dependent on wood supply for heating or cooking (Figure 5.5.4). Participatory processes, education and transfer of rights to local communities can contribute to procedural equity and challenge power imbalances . More generally, community forest management in developing countries is a viable model for sustainable forestry, carbon sequestration and livelihoods . These activities benefit from being implemented by prioritizing local communities’ rights to manage and restore forests . The implementation may require financial and technological support to minimize trade-offs between socio-economic and environmental outcomes, with active consideration of gender dynamics . Table 5.5.9. Interactions of forest-based solutions with the nexus elements. Nexus element Positive interactions Negative interactions Climate change High climate change mitigation and adaptation potential (see text). Preserving or expanding the presence of forests in areas affected by seasonal snow cover can reduce surface albedo, thereby decreasing the cooling benefits associated with carbon sequestration and/or storage Biodiversity Forest-based solutions are usually highly positive for biodiversity conservation . Forest conservation, especially through community engagement, can increase species richness and abundance , and generally protecting or expanding forests provides a thermal buffer against climate change, thus serving as climate change refugia for biodiversity . Biodiversity value is usually higher for reforestation, forest conservation and ISFM than afforestation. In general, the biodiversity benefits from forest expansion depend on the previous land use and type of trees: benefits are higher for mixed native species in unmanaged plantations than monoculture/exotic species in managed plantations . For example, planting trees on native grassland can induce losses in biodiversity and generally cause a reduction in multiple NCP (pollination services, natural pest regulation, forage production, soil quality, wild food and cultural appreciation of landscape) . Water Forests usually offer positive regulation of water services . Forest soils have a relatively high water retention and filtering capacity, which can positively impact freshwater quality and quantity, and reforestation can increase precipitation through enhanced moisture recycling . When fast-growing non-native tree species with high evapotranspiration rates have been planted in semi-arid areas (e.g., Loess Plateau of China), shortages in soil moisture have been exacerbated leading to negative impacts on water availability . Food Forest delivers NCP for food production, such as pollinator bees and other animals that depend on nearby forests for nesting and foraging . Forests also contribute to food security through the consumption of forest food, contributing to IPLC diets Preventing deforestation can thus avoid negative impacts on IPLC diets. For example, deforestation in rural Tanzania caused a reduction in household fruit and vegetable consumption and thus vitamin A adequacy of diets . Forest protection prevents the expansion of cropland, which can be a threat to food security either directly or via leakage effects (i.e., increases in food imports that cause environmental damages elsewhere) . Achievement of ambitious climate stabilization and biodiversity conservation targets expect large areas of agricultural land to be reforested , which can create increased competition for land and ultimately lead to higher food prices . Regulating access to forests for biodiversity conservation can threaten access and the right to food of IPLC . Health Forests induce a positive effect on health and well-being . Healthy forests provide vital NCP, promoting good health by reducing noise, air pollution and stress, among others . In many cases, there has been an association with improved air quality, psycho-physical wellness, disaster prevention and ecological integrity . Forest conservation also reduces risks of infectious diseases associated with pathogen spillover from wildlife to humans , deaths and health damages from floods (Bhattacharjee & Behera, 2018; Dittrich, 2019) and diseases caused by forest fires and smoke . Little or no relevant interaction. Box 5.5.2. Deforestation monitoring and control in the Brazilian Amazon The 70 per cent reduction in deforestation in the Brazilian Amazon between 2005 and 2012 is an important example of a biodiversity-climate-health nexus. The deforestation reduction was tied to a top-down governmental programme (Portuguese acronym: PPCDAm, in the Portuguese acronym) supported by an international fund (the Amazon Fund) . The programme integrated state-of-the-science satellite monitoring, law enforcement on the ground, the establishment of new protected areas and Indigenous territories, and market or credit restrictions on illegal landholdings and high-deforesting municipalities . Notably, during the same period the GDP in the region increased by >140 per cent, mostly associated with commodity agriculture (Figure 5.5.8). Although there is no causality between the decrease in deforestation and the increase in GDP, it shows that reducing deforestation does not harm the economy. From 2013 onward, however, changes in the environmental legislation and a weaker commitment to the programme’s implementation caused deforestation and violation of Indigenous rights to increase again . The reduction in deforestation in the Amazon had clear climate change mitigation benefits, avoiding the emission of 3.2 GtCO2 into the atmosphere . It also improved climate adaptation of the local human population by reducing the vulnerability of socio-ecological systems. It is reasonable to assume that it had a positive impact on biodiversity through habitat conservation, and on water security by buffering the adverse impacts of Amazon deforestation on reducing precipitation . Reducing deforestation in the Amazon is associated with many benefits to human health. Much of the deforestation in the Brazilian Amazon is associated with land clearing practices by using fire, especially for cattle ranching . The reduction in deforestation between 2004 and 2021 is estimated to have prevented roughly 400 to 1,700 premature adult deaths annually associated with smoke pollution due to forest fires . A much smaller fraction of deforestation in the Amazon is associated with gold mining, but its health impacts are enormous, especially for Indigenous Peoples. Illegal gold mining in the Amazon, utilizing mercury (Hg) for gold extraction, poses health risks, including motor and cognitive impairments in both adults and children (see Chapter 2, Box 2.12) . It also correlates with malaria outbreaks and the transmission of respiratory infections (pneumonia, tuberculosis and COVID-19) . Altering river courses alongside mercury contamination depletes fish and game populations, exacerbating food insecurity as traditional sources dwindle, as illustrated by the 2023 humanitarian crisis among Yanomami Indigenous Peoples . Illegal gold mining in the Amazon also fosters alcohol abuse and prostitution, leading to increased rates of sexually transmitted diseases . Finally, deforestation is a driver of emerging infectious disease events, as it increases the contact of people with wildlife carrying new viruses . Thus, the reduction in deforestation in the Amazon – a hotspots of emerging zoonotic diseases – likely lowered the risk of pathogens spillover from wildlife to humans . Figure 5.5.8. Deforestation in the Brazilian Amazon over time. Gross Domestic Product (GDP) data from the Brazilian Statistics Institute ; deforestation data from the Brazilian National Institute for Space Research . Data refers to the Brazilian states within the Legal Amazon.5 Figure 5.5.9. Deforestation caused by illegal mining in the Yanomami Indigenous Peoples’ territory. Mercury river contamination and deforestation deplete fish and game populations, bringing about food insecurity as traditional sources decline. Photo credit: Bruno Kelly/Amazônia Real via Flickr under licence CC BY-NC-SA 2.0. 5.5.3.13 Restoration of coastal and marine ecosystems for carbon sequestration (C13) Coastal blue carbon ecosystems (CBCE) are marine habitats composed of rooted vegetation , namely mangroves , seagrasses and salt marshes . Some authors also include marine sediments, components of the biological carbon cycle (e.g., whales), calcifying organisms and macroalgae in blue carbon estimates. This response option focuses on mangroves, seagrasses and salt marshes, which are responsible for 45 per cent of the organic carbon burial in the ocean , and the emerging macroalgae. CBCE have many impacts on climate change mitigation and adaptation, as well as the nexus elements of biodiversity, water, food and health (Figure 5.5.3, Table 5.5.10). Table 5.5.10. Impacts of the restoration of blue carbon ecosystems on biodiversity, water, food, health and climate change mitigation and adaptation. Nexus element Impacts References Climate change mitigation CBCE habitats capture and store carbon up to 30-50 times more efficiently than tropical forests due to their anoxic soils and sediments, high productivity and unique ability to trap particles from the water flow. Carbon stored in marine soil can be trapped for millennia if the seabed remains undisturbed. Land reclamation for multiple purposes and poor management have deteriorated or eliminated these ecosystems. Conversely, angiosperm macroalgae do not develop extensive rooting systems and so do not contribute to long-term soil carbon storage. However, evidence suggests that they produce highly recalcitrant compounds. Recent studies also underline the large carbon export fluxes of seaweeds, and that organic carbon may be buried in sediments or transported to the deep sea and thus stored for thousands of years. As such, kelps are starting to being considered as CBCE although knowledge gaps exist. Chmura ; Donato ; Duarte ; Hiraishi ; Krause-Jensen & Duarte ; Krause-Jensen ; Lovelock & Duarte ; Macreadie ; Mcleod ; Ortega ; Pendleton ; Pessarrodona ; Queirós ; Trevathan-Tackett Climate change adaptation CBCE function as buffers against ocean acidification and alleviate the damage caused by flooding and storms, reducing coastal erosion and saline water intrusion. Mangroves shelter economic activity during tropical cyclone exposure. These coastal ecosystems can naturally adapt to changes in sea levels by their capacity to adjust accretion rates to sea-level rise. Combining seagrasses and salt marshes or mangroves (depending on latitude), where possible, increases their adaptation effectiveness. Barbier ; Carey ; Duarte Fujita ; Hochard ; Hori ; James ; Kirwan & Mudd ; Kirwan ; Løvås & Tørum ; Lovelock ; Ondiviela ; Twomey ; Worthington & Spalding ; Zhu ; Biodiversity All CBCE habitats create 3D structures that alter their surrounding physical environment, providing habitat, shelter and food to many species. Kelp forests and mangroves support greater biodiversity than previous structureless unvegetated areas. Seagrasses are nursery grounds, an important food source for megaherbivores such as turtles, dugongs and manatees, and play an important role in trophic transfers to adjacent ecosystems. The high productivity of salt marshes and their location right at the land-sea interface makes them highly biodiverse. Barbier ; Beck ; Bromberg Gedan ; Eger ; Friess ; Heck ; Mitsch & Gosselink ; Orth ; Smale ; Steneck ; Waycott ; Worthington & Spalding Water CBCE substantially improve water quality by nutrient cycling, suspended organic matter retention, acidification regulation and oxygenation. Salt marshes have long been acknowledged as a natural system for treatment of wastewater and mangroves help prevent salination of groundwater. Breaux ; Hochard Food Blue carbon ecosystems provide essential habitats for commercial species and have a positive effect on wild capture fisheries through recruitment. Mangroves also provide collateral provisioning services, such as mangrove honey, and can support sustainable aquaculture practices, such as oyster farming. Some seagrass species are directly consumed as food in East Africa or the Solomon Islands, and so are seaweeds, particularly in Asia, as well as saltmarsh plants. Seaweeds are a source of hydrocolloids and salt marshes are also used as pasture lands for livestock. Betanzos-Vega ; Boesch & Turner ; Bromberg Gedan ; Crow & Carney ; de la Torre-Castro & Rönnbäck ; Eger ; D. Friess ; Fujita ; Lauer & Aswani ; Mackenzie & Dionne ; Orth ; Patel ; Walters ; Worthington & Spalding Health Mangroves provide supporting (recruitment) and provisioning (fisheries, harvesting-collecting and aquaculture) NCP that result in improved human nutrition through diversified food. They also provide regulating NCP, such as pollution control (e.g., heavy metal bioremediation). However, mangroves can provide disservices (infectious disease), but these are outweighed by their health benefits. To some extent, mangroves deliver traditional remedies based on ILK, although most mangrove-sourced medicines remain untested by Western science. Some salt marsh species have been used to treat gastrointestinal issues and are recognised for their anti-inflammatory, antioxidant and anti-cancer properties. Seagrasses produce phytochemicals that can inhibit human pathogens like faecal coliforms. All CBCE contribute to well-being through cultural services. CBCE are also a source of pharmaceuticals, the outstanding antibiotic bioactivity of mangrove extracts is particularly promising. Awuku-Sowah ; Bandaranayake ; de los Santos ; Lomartire & Gonçalves ; Oueslati ; Patel ; Quevedo & Kohsaka ; Rhee & Park Mangrove forests support Indigenous institutions and cosmologies around the world, e.g., African Joola , American Wayampi , in Brunei or Indian festivals and celebrations . As such, mangroves sustain ILK often by women . Kelp forests are central to Canadian Haida Gwaii people, whose ethics and values have been adapted to support restoration and are aligned with ecosystem-based management principles . Kelp forests are also part of the traditional knowledge of the Kawésqar people of southern Chile . Saltmarshes are sacred places for the North American Gullah Geechee and native Cajuns . In contrast, seagrass meadows have limited societal recognition , although examples of local communities’ involvement exist in the Philippines , the Solomon Islands or Zanzibar . They also play a role in preserving valuable submerged archaeological and historical heritage (Figure 5.5.4). Restoring CBCE is at TRL 9 (Figure 5.5.5). However, there are issues involving land tenure and jurisdictional boundaries that should be considered within marine spatial planning . Lack of monitoring, communication barriers and poor implementation have been reported for mangroves and seagrasses . Coastal wetlands are also often perceived as a source of pests and diseases, danger and odour . Seaweeds necessitate a different approach as they involve management of the fate of the carbon they produce and the locations in which carbon accumulates, which may be distant from the sites of production (e.g., in deep water) . This response option aligns with several SDGs and with the United Nations Decade for Ocean Sciences and the United Nations Decade on Restoration (2021-2030) (Figure 5.5.4). Despite its limited carbon removal potential (3 per cent at its upper range) , its high co-benefits makes CBCE a ‘low or no regret’ response option . This is illustrated by the Sine-Saloum delta in Senegal, which supports diverse livelihoods through its mangrove ecosystem (Box 5.5.3). However, the area faces environmental challenges and social issues, necessitating integrated management and community-based restoration projects to ensure sustainable development and climate change mitigation. Box 5.5.3. Blue carbon ecosystem restoration in the Sine-Saloum delta, Senegal The delta of the Sine-Saloum in Senegal (West Africa) is a tropical mangrove forest (Figure 5.5.10). It supports food production through artisanal fisheries (fish, shrimps), collection of molluscs (bivalves, snails), crustaceans (crabs) and bush meat (note the likely trade-off with health). Oyster farming and beekeeping (mangrove honey) directly rely on this ecosystem while rice cultivation and salt production take place in adjacent areas. The mangrove provides other goods and services, such as the examples given in Table 5.5.10. The Sine-Saloum delta faces a suite of environmental challenges, including a decline of freshwater, saltwater intrusion, coastal erosion and salinization of groundwater reserves due to sea-level rise, over-exploitation of timber and fishery resources, land clearance for agriculture and ill-planned developments . Furthermore, new offshore oil and gas developments (Sangomar field) pose a major risk . In addition, the transfer of ownership and control to private companies (investment funds) from previously communal (collective customary) territories has led to environmental injustice . Sector-based management is a barrier and integrated management plans are needed. More than 40 per cent of mangroves of the Sine-Saloumn delta have been lost since the 1970s due to droughts, wood and rhizophores extraction, and the establishment of barriers to the natural hydrological cycle (roads, saltworks, paddy field dikes) . This has fed a vicious cycle of deforestation and poverty with cascading knock-on effects across nexus elements. Estimates of fish catches have dropped from 30,000 tonnes in the 1970s to 10,000 in the 1990s . In the 1980s, several restoration programmes started in the region and since the 2000s a number of initiatives fuelled by carbon investment funds (such as Reducing Emissions from Deforestation and Forest Degradation and the role of conservation, sustainable management of forests and enhancement of forest carbon stocks in developing countries (REDD+)) were established. The “plante ton arbre” 2011 programme was the first to be registered with the UNFCCC in agroforestry. Figure 5.5.10. Sine-Saloum delta. Left: mangroves at Simal, Photo Credit: Marcos Llope – CC BY-NC-ND 4.0; right: sacred baobab tree at Saloum Island. Photo Credit: Marie-Christine Cormier-Salem under licence CC BY-NC-SA 4.0. These initiatives have not always had the expected results. For example, high species mortality rates have been reported where a single species has been planted in unsuitable sites, leading to the criticism that the choice of sites was mainly determined according to their visibility. Moreover, some of these conservation initiatives negatively impacted livelihoods. While the initiatives could be considered successful in terms of reforested surfaces and international visibility, they often performed poorly in terms of biological and cultural diversities and led to spatial injustice . One of the main current projects is a restoration project validated under the Verified Carbon Standard developed and funded by WeForest and implemented by Océanium. The project claims to be among the first and largest carbon-certified mangrove projects in the world. The project follows the community-based ecological mangrove restoration framework , which empowers local communities to restore and steward the mangrove forests while offering sustainable coastal livelihoods. It takes ecological considerations into account by avoiding monoculture planting, as well as the underlying hydrological fluxes to increase freshwater flows, tidal exchanges and sediment supply so that the mangrove can naturally regenerate. Currently three species: Rhizophora mangle (75 per cent), R. racemosa (15 per cent) and Avicenia spp. (10 per cent) are being planted. The project’s objectives are: (i) climate change mitigation; (ii) improvement of local community well-being; (iii) restoration of degraded wetlands; and (iv) increasing mangrove knowledge through scientific research activities. In order to achieve these objectives, 7019.6 ha will be planted: 4118 ha within the Sine-Saloum delta (59 per cent) and the rest in Casamance. The project’s governance structures are designed to ensure the “empowerment and involvement of stakeholders”. A feedback and grievance redress procedure that incorporates traditional conflict resolution methods is in place. Beneficiaries are local communities, particularly fishermen, women (oyster collectors) and young people, beekeepers and customary/traditional village chiefs, as well as government, majors and educational/research institutes. Participation of communities in the decision-making and full recognition of customary rules is crucial for the success of the initiative. Outreach is undertaken through various means such as radio podcast, cinema debates or workshops . Neighbouring populations also benefit from spillover effects. It has been estimated that the ecosystem provides livelihoods to more than 100,000 inhabitants, with its ecosystem services amounting to €1.5 billion, while cumulative labour income from wetland-derived industrial activity including harvesting, fisheries, agriculture and tourism amounts to €3 billion over a period of 10 years . Other examples of nexus-wide benefits from the project are given in Table 5.5.11. Table 5.5.11. Expected benefits for climate change mitigation and adaptation and the other nexus elements estimated over a 10-year period . If * is indicated, values could not be disaggregated for the Sine-Saloum delta and include the mangrove restoration project in the Casamance region . Nexus element Benefits Climate change mitigation Only native species are used, namely: Rhizophora mangle, Avicennia germinans and A. africana. To a lesser extent and conditions allowing: R. racemosa, R. harisonnii, Laguncularia racemosa and Conocarpus erectus. * Carbon sequestration, including the Casamance region, is expected to reach 132,609 tCO2e in 2039 . Previous estimates for Sine-Saloum gave values of 1,936 tonnes C in 2 years . This ecosystem service amounts to €110 million over 10 years. Climate change adaptation Mangrove forests decrease temperature by around 3ºC when compared with bare mudflats. Protection against saltwater and waves have made it possible to harvest rice on previously abandoned plots. Protection against coastal erosion and extreme weather events has been estimated to be €172 million. * 70 per cent of households noted these benefits after restoration. * 15 per cent of abandoned rice fields could be restored, with yields increased by 10 per cent. At the household level 84 per cent reported better regulation of salt in rice fields. Biodiversity Positive changes in biodiversity include abundance and population structure of fish, molluscs and crustaceans. Increased seascape and landscape connectivity attracts wild species, providing habitat to rare and endangered species: West African manatee, Atlantic humpback dolphin and marine turtles. Mangrove flowering attracts pollinating insects, such as bees. Biodiversity services are valued at €128 million. Water Quality of water for the ecosystem increases due to filtration and reduced siltation. Water filtration and nitrogen removal amount to €173 million and €76 million, respectively. Food Seafood and rice are the main food provisioning services. Others are honey, salt, fermented beverages, edible plants and fodder. Goats graze on fallen leaves and propagules and cattle occasionally graze on leaves and diaspores . * Households reported increases of fish catches (70 per cent, after 5 years), oysters (55 per cent, after 7-8 years), shrimps (40 per cent) and shellfish (20 per cent). Fish stocks increased by more than 4,200 tonnes/year. Fish, oyster and shrimp production were valued at €5.68 million in 2018. * 15 per cent of previously abandoned rice fields were restored, rice fields further offshore increased their yields by 10 per cent. Additional rice production is estimated at €64,000. Benefits are seen after 6-7 years. Edible plant harvesting has been estimated to amount to €172 million. Health The most important health impact is through a diversified diet (seafood). * 95 per cent of households reported to have improved their diets. 5.5.3.14 Urban nature-based solutions (C14) Cities are major contributors to climate change, accounting for over 70 per cent of global CO2 emissions . Within a changing climate, complex socio-economic systems like urban areas require dynamic and flexible approaches to adapt to climate change impacts, reduce emissions and engage with different knowledge systems in order to respond to evolving climate needs . Urban nature-based solutions, in line with the agreed definition of nature-based solutions in UNEA Resolution 5/5, are aligned with targets 1 to 4, 7 to 8 and 10 to 12 of the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework , the SDGs, particularly 3, 11, 13, as well as being relevant to the Paris Agreement (Figure 5.5.4). They have a high positive impact across the nexus, contributing to climate change adaptation and (to a lesser extent to mitigation), and to biodiversity, water, food, health and well-being benefits, and thus increasing overall urban climate resilience (Figure 5.5.3) (see also Section 5.1.3.2; B02, Urban nature-based solutions; Section 5.2.3.12; W12, Water-sensitive urban infrastructure; Section 5.4.3.9, H09, Urban green infrastructure). Urban nature-based solutions have very high potential for climate change adaptation, as 56 per cent of the global population (4.4 billion inhabitants) live in cities . The main adaptation benefits include reduction of urban heat and prevention of the impacts of extreme weather events, such as heat waves, heavy rains and floods . For example, rain gardens and management of living rivers and coasts can contribute to the prevention of river and coastal flooding and disaster risk reduction . Urban nature-based solutions have limited climate change mitigation potential as the extent of areas for implementation is restricted. Urban ecosystems (including biomass and soil) have a technical potential to sequester 1.7 to 3.6 GtCO2/yr . Additional measures or policies are needed to limit emissions and enhance the effectiveness of nature-based solutions (e.g., C10, Global cooperation in finance and technology, Sections 5.5.3.10 and 5.5.4). For example, the development of connected green pathways, in combination with policies supporting public and/or active transportation (walking and cycling), can contribute to climate change adaptation by reducing urban heat, while also reducing urban transport GHG emissions, improving air quality and promoting physical and mental health . Nexus approaches combining climate change adaptation and mitigation can help the additional 2.5 billion people predicted to live in cities by 2050 to be less exposed to heat waves and air pollution, and to reduce energy use, waste and related emissions . Urban nature-based solutions can improve biodiversity by supporting ecological function, connectivity and condition , which increases climate resilience . Agroecological urban and peri-urban gardens provide local fresh foods for urban residents, while contributing to climate change adaptation, promoting water storage and reuse of urban organic wastes, reducing urban energy use, and generating benefits to health and well-being by lowering temperatures . Urban reforestation and the creation of green spaces are other options to mitigate urban heat impacts and reduce heat-related stress, air pollution, and mortality, while enhancing air quality, general physical health, and alleviating stress and mental health disorders . In combination with non-nature-based solutions urban planning solutions, they can further contribute to positive health outcomes. For example, increasing green spaces, alongside increasing reflectivity of pavement surfaces in cities can substantially reduce urban heat island effects. By integrating nature-based solutions in urban environments, cities can improve future liveability, particularly for the economically and socially most vulnerable . The effects on vulnerable groups and social acceptance vary across different nature-based solutions and countries (Figure 5.5.4). If implemented effectively, considering equity and specific characteristics of the location, urban nature-based solutions can have a positive effect on vulnerable communities . Otherwise, nature-based solutions might lead to negative impacts and cause resistance towards the response option . Integrating environmental justice in urban planning is critical for preventing negative effects such as widening inequity gaps . Implementation of urban nature-based solutions can lead to green gentrification by making the area more attractive and increasing property prices, thus displacing local communities . To avoid unintended consequences, the implementation of urban nature-based solutions can be accompanied by measures to ensure affordable housing and social infrastructure . For urban nature-based solutions to be effective, they must consider communities’ vulnerabilities, needs, identities (e.g., IPLC, gender, race, ethnicity), values and knowledge, and engage them in design and evaluation . Monitoring of urban nature-based solutions after implementation is also key to successfully addressing interactions with people and across the nexus elements . Urban nature-based solutions are increasingly mainstreamed in national priorities and policies . The costs depend on the location where specific nature-based solutions are implemented , and are higher in historical cities than in new ones. However, even high costs can be offset by benefits in the long-term (Figure 5.5.4), although there are challenges in accounting for their non-monetary benefits due to their long-term nature and nexus-wide influence. This response option is a promising option for transformative change and can enable shifts to more equitable and sustainable futures (Figure 5.5.4). However, its transformative change potential depends on governance and different systemic settings as noted above. Box 5.5.4. Medellin Green Strategy: Addressing climate change and social cohesion Medellín, Colombia is located in a tropical zone influenced by the El Niño Southern Oscillation (ENSO), and climate variability and extreme weather events frequently result in floods and landslides. The city, which used to be one of the most dangerous in the world, is now an “Urban Lab” aiming for inclusiveness, peace and sustainable development. As part of the Green Corridors Strategy to address crime and climate change, the city led social cohesion initiatives focusing on integrated nature-based solutions (with climate, biodiversity and social benefits) and environmental education. A group of innovative mayors, in an unprecedented example of urban inclusion, laid out transformative plans to integrate the poorest and most violent hillside neighbourhoods in the city centre of Medellin. The objectives of the Medellin Green Strategy were to: (1) constrain irregulated growth and sprawl in the hillsides around the city and reduce the risk of floods and landslides during extreme weather events (i.e., climate change adaptation); (2) contribute to climate change mitigation through a forest carbon sequestration (“More Forests for Medellin”); (3) protect water basins and forests that are critical to regional biodiversity; (4) provide citizens with new recreation, education, sport and community meeting facilities; and (5) deliver environmental education (“A greener Medellin for you”) on infrastructure and the environment. The strategy consisted of multiple linked climate, biodiversity and social actions (Table 5.5.11). Table 5.5.12. Integrated urban nature-based solution actions in Medellin with climate-biodiversity-social focus. Source: C40 . Climate adaptation actions Climate mitigation actions Biodiversity conservation actions * Prevent/manage desertification, soil erosion and landslides * Green areas to reduce urban heat * Rain gardens to prevent floods * Green urban nature for carbon storage (wetlands, tree cover) * Improve carbon sequestration through use of adaptable species * Raise awareness of behaviours and lifestyle with mitigation potential * Protection and restoration of urban habitats, ecosystems and connectivity * Protection of species/native species * Conservation governance and biodiversity offsets The beneficiaries of the strategy were young people and children, marginalized groups and low-income citizens. Up to 50 per cent of the residents living in high-risk zones are poor rural-to-urban migrants displaced by the armed conflict. The municipality planned the Green Strategy, supported by the University of Colombia. Citizens affected by extreme events were engaged in the implementation phase of the strategy and, hence, the Green Corridors are supported by the population. The strategy shows that a combination of smart investments in poor communities with well-planned integrated urban nature-based solutions transportation infrastructure and education can be transformative, providing a model for social inclusion while also being profitable. In Medellin an investment of $16.3 million is expected to bring $136 million benefits between 2020 and 2030 (Table 5.5.13). Initially large investments may be necessary for a city-based nature-based solutions project, however, higher returns for nature, people, peace and prosperity are expected on the long-term. Technical expertise and local knowledge, as well as indicators and data, are required for the planning and assessment of such urban integrated nature-based solutions (i.e., for baseline and monitoring and evaluation). Table 5.5.13. Benefits of the Medellin Green Strategy for the nexus elements Source: WWF . Nexus element Benefits Climate change adaptation * Decrease average temperature (-2°C) * Reduced impacts of heat waves, heavy rains and floods * Disaster risk reduction Climate change mitigation * Reduction and sequestration of GHG emissions * -160,787 kg of CO2/yr Biodiversity * 36 green corridors created * 8,800 trees planted * 90,000 species of lesser plants seeded * 65 ha preserved Water * Blue spaces * Improved water quality and management Health * Improved air quality * 678 heart-related deaths avoided * Recreational sport areas * Well-being Other environmental benefits * Increased area of green spaces and habitat (number of species present) * Improved soil quality * Waste management Green jobs/ Equity * 75 locals from disadvantaged backgrounds hired as gardeners * Environmental education and training 5.5.3.15 Sustainable healthy diets (C15) Dietary patterns are changing towards a higher consumption of animal foods, and low intake of fruits and vegetables, leading to an increased risk in non-communicable diseases, particularly type II diabetes, coronary heart disease and some cancers, and playing a significant role in environmental degradation and climate change . Currently the food system accounts for more than 30 per cent of total GHG emissions, 70 per cent of water use and more than 60 per cent of terrestrial biodiversity loss, with animal-based foods being a major contributor to these environmental changes . Meeting future demand for food, animal feed, fibre and bioenergy crops is projected to result in a loss of millions of square kilometres of natural ecosystems, which threatens biodiversity: 87.7 per cent of species affected will lose habitat to agricultural expansion by 2050, with 1,280 species projected to lose ?25 per cent of their habitat . Policies targeting how, where and what food is produced and consumed could reduce these threats, with a combination of approaches potentially preventing most of these losses while contributing to healthier human diets (see also Section 5.3.3.11, F11, Sustainable healthy diets; Section 5.4.3.6, H06, Reduce meat overconsumption). Sustainable healthy diets are defined as dietary patterns that promote all dimensions of health and well-being; have low environmental impact; are accessible, affordable, safe and equitable; and culturally acceptable . Dietary change toward plant-rich, healthy diets is a globally applicable response option (Figure 5.5.5), with high potential for food systems transformation (Figure 5.5.4) and benefits for climate change mitigation, adaptation, health, biodiversity protection, water conservation, resilience and equity (Figure 5.5.3). Shifting towards sustainable healthy diets is the demand-side option with the highest mitigation potential, contributing a global reduction in GHG emissions of 0.7 to 8.0 GtCO2e/yr . The adaptation potential of this response option is also high since it could facilitate access to healthy diets for 1.8 billion people living in extreme poverty and in water stressed areas and suffering acute food insecurity and malnutrition . Dietary shifts to plant-rich healthy diets (i.e., in line with WHO recommendations on healthy eating) could reduce global mortality by 6-10 per cent due to reductions in non-communicable diseases related to low consumption of fruits and vegetables and to the overconsumption of meat, and decrease food-related GHG emissions by 29-70 per cent by 2050 . It is a response option that can be adapted to different local climatic and geographical realities and socio-economic and cultural contexts. This requires consideration of ecosystems and populations particularly impacted by climate change (e.g., in the arid and semi-arid lands and small island developing states) and malnutrition, the importance of protecting Indigenous Peoples’ local food systems, traditional livelihoods (e.g., hunting, fishing), food preferences and values. For example, pastoralists in drylands, such as Maasai communities, historically depend on livestock and the majority of calories consumed come from domestic animal products. The main trade-offs associated with this response option include the potential increase of GHG emissions from producing healthy diets for all in low- and medium-income countries . Limitations are related to food prices and affordability. With more than 3 billion poor people not having access to affordable healthy diets , food price is the key barrier to its social acceptability, particularly for low-income groups (Figure 5.5.4). This is evidenced in studies on efficacy of subsidies to increase consumption of healthy foods; and the effects of taxation to reduce intake of unhealthy foods . The Technology Readiness Level of this option is high (Figure 5.5.5). Many countries have food-based dietary guidelines aligned with WHO, and an increasing number of countries are integrating sustainability criteria in their food-based dietary guidelines. However, adoption of this response option by national institutions, cities or communities is context specific. Transitioning to sustainable and healthy diets depends on policy commitments, eco-agri-food systems and landscapes, dietary patterns, population nutritional and health status, food environments, affordability, access, education and socio-cultural and ethical values. Implementation requires that coherent health, agriculture, environmental, social and public policies are in place that support sustainable food systems and access to healthy diets, including through social protection and public food procurement, e.g., for school meals programmes . Mainstreaming equity and social justice into transitions to sustainable diets by establishing safety nets for the most vulnerable, can bring high benefits (Herrero et al 2023). Sustainable public food procurement can be cost-effective and drive shifts to sustainable food systems and healthy diets , and is already implemented across Europe to promote sustainable healthy diets as part of the European Union’s Farm to Fork strategy under the umbrella of the European Green Deal. To promote systemic change, sustainable food procurement, such as requiring increased content of organic plant-rich, diverse foods, fruit and vegetables, and reduced food waste, is a promising option . Boxes 5.5.5 and 5.5.6 present two different initiatives that are promoting shifts into sustainable healthy diets through public food procurement in very different contexts, in the Amazonian basin in Para?, Brazil and in Los Angeles County in the United States of America. This response option is central to the National Food and Nutrition Action Plans and the Food Systems National Pathways of many countries and aligns with the long-term global goals of the Paris Agreement and the SDGs (Figure 5.5.4). If implemented properly, widely and equitably, this response option is key to addressing malnutrition in all its forms (SDG2). The socio-economic and structural costs associated with the transition to healthy and sustainable diets are context specific and need to be assessed on a case-by-case basis. Box 5.5.5. Promoting the Arara traditional, local, healthy diets through school meals: direct food acquisition from Indigenous Peoples and public food procurement6 The Arara are Indigenous Peoples of recent contact, living in the Amazonian basin in Brazil. Before contact with the non-Indigenous society in the 1980s, the Arara were semi-nomadic hunters and gatherers. Climate change is particularly impacting these and other Indigenous Peoples who depend on natural resources. Rising temperatures and changing precipitation patterns negatively affect traditional livelihoods related to hunting and fishing, food security and nutrition . After contact, the Arara settled and their traditional diets (Figure 5.5.11) were replaced with unhealthy foods, leading to health issues not previously reported, such as hypertension and diabetes. The National School Meals Program, while key for ensuring food security and nutrition in Brazil, has been one of the main drivers of the increasing consumption of unhealthy foods in Indigenous Peoples and was in part responsible for the change in their diets. The current generation of Arara children have little knowledge of their traditional foods. To address these challenges, several initiatives have been created to promote the Arara’s traditional, local, healthy diets through school meals. These aim to: (1) promote the Arara People’s health through increased availability and access to traditional, healthy diets rich in diverse local Indigenous foods through school meal programmes; (2) launch a pilot to integrate traditional dishes of the Arara People in the school meals of four Indigenous schools in Altamira, Para? through direct food acquisition from local Arara people (to be scaled-up by the National School Meals Program in Brazil); (3) democratize access to public policies by allowing School Meals Programs to acquire foods directly from the nearby Indigenous Peoples and local communities; and (4) create spaces in the Arara schools for learning and appreciation of ancestral traditions, where transmission of food knowledge between generations is encouraged and strengthened. Figure 5.5.11. Traditional dishes of the Arara Indigenous Peoples. Left panel: fish; right panel: wa?t tynondem (baked fish wrapped in banana leaves). Photo credits: Priscila Tapajowara / ISA (Instituto Socio Ambiental) under single and exclusive use for the assessment. Implementation started by connecting Indigenous research and public food policies. Action research was carried out by Indigenous postgraduates from the University of Brasi?lia and the Instituto Socio-ambiental (the largest Indigenous Peoples NGO in Brazil) to identify and recover the high diversity of traditional local foods. This research connected the knowledge of elders with the systematization of information by young students in schools in the Arara Indigenous territories . In 2023, traditional dishes (wàt tynondem (baked fish wrapped in banana leaves), karak’ kuréum (the edible leaves of a non-toxic variety of elephant ear plant) and onatji magarapa (corn cake)) were successfully integrated into the Arara’s school meals. The inclusion of traditional, local foods in public purchasing programmess and in school meals was the result from the inter-institutional dialogues promoted by the People's Traditional Food Commissions, both in the states and nationally. The Federal Public Ministry (MPF) of Brazil played a fundamental role in enabling schools to purchase products directly from communities and has been connecting government processes to local realities. The MPF has issued directions adopting this model for all Indigenous Peoples and traditional communities in Brazil. This action democratizes access to public policies, providing opportunities to generate income in rural areas, encouraging the provision of school meals to respect the ancestry and culture of Indigenous Peoples and traditional communities. The implementation and scaling-up of the elements above require the recognition of IPLC’ territorial rights . The Arara’s Indigenous territories were demarcated by the Brazilian government in 2023, empowering the Arara to access, manage and protect their forest-riverine ecosystems, which are sources of diverse foods, water, medicinal plants, fibres, hunting / fishing/ tools and cooking biomass. Their territorial rights are critical for the Arara’s governance of their forests and biodiversity conservation efforts, for recovery of their traditional livelihoods, health, cultural and spiritual identity, and to contribute to climate and biodiversity outcomes (Table 5.5.14). Table 5.5.14. Benefits of promoting traditional, local, healthy diets through school meal programs in the Arara’s Indigenous territories. Nexus elements Benefits Climate change adaptation * Direct food acquisition programmes of local traditional foods from the Arara for school meals is key for climate change adaptation to secure livelihoods, water and food security. Climate change mitigation * Traditional diets are sustainable (seasonal, plant-rich, local foods), with negligible GHG emissions. * Arara’s traditional food systems do not have transport and waste-related emissions. * Reforestation and protection of the local forest-riverine mangrove systems contribute to carbon storage. Biodiversity conservation * Recovery of Arara’s land rights allows governance of reforestation and biodiversity conservation efforts. * Indigenous knowledge is critical to the conservation of native, rare and vulnerable species and their habitats, and the sustainable management of natural resources. Water * Arara’s traditional local food systems do not require water inputs. * Water in Indigenous territories riverine areas can be polluted from mining. Food * In 2023 Indigenous Peoples delivered 56 varieties of traditional foods benefiting 211 Arara students. * In 2024 traditional foods were allocated to 90 municipal schools serving +2,500 students. Health * Promotion of Arara’s health through access to healthy diets and clean water in the school meals. * Protection and management of local forests and water resources prevents fires and air pollution. * Access to medicinal plants. * Physical and spiritual well-being. Cultural and spiritual * Recovery of traditional foods consumed by Arara elders. * Transmission of traditional food knowledge between Arara generations (including hunting/ fishing methods). * Strengthened Arara cultural and spiritual identity. Box 5.5.6. Promoting sustainable healthy diets through plant-based public food procurement in Los Angeles County, United States of America Los Angeles County, once the largest agriculture producer in the United States of America, has supported two bottom-up food justice initiatives: the “Good Food for All” movement and the Los Angeles Food Policy Council. The Good Food agenda engages farmers and communities in the development of local food systems, aiming to produce good food for all (healthy, sustainable, affordable, fair) and to facilitate access through public procurement, farmers’ markets and community gardens. Despite these efforts, 30 per cent of resident households (1 million people) were food insecure in 2023 and 50 per cent of adults had diabetes or pre-diabets. Climate change is also impacting Los Angeles County with more frequent and intense heat waves, droughts, wildfires, rainstorms and rising sea levels, leading to land and biodiversity losses and food yield reductions that collectively have resulted in higher food costs. Los Angeles County developed an innovative Sustainability Plan in 2019, focusing on community well-being, an economy based on renewables, reduction of environmental impacts and adaptation to climate change . The Plan includes 12 cross-cutting goals, and goal 10 refers to a “Sustainable and just food system that enhances access to affordable, local, healthy foods”. Action 134 of the Plan calls to ‘Promote plant-based menu options through nutrition and food procurement policies in food service settings such as county facilities, hospitals, higher learning institutions, school districts, jails, among others”. In this context, Los Angeles County has enacted a new law in February 2024 aiming to address environmental and health impacts associated with public food purchasing by shifting towards plant-based food procurement. The new law mandates the following: * Update of nutritional standards: The Los Angeles County Department of Public Health will update its nutritional standards to incorporate best practices for purchasing, selling and serving more plant-based foods for integration in the Los Angeles County food service requests for proposals and contracts. * Recommendations for plant-based procurement: The Department of Public Health in consultation with the Chief Executive Office, the Departments of Internal & Health Services and other partners will provide recommendations for increasing plant-based food options in Los Angeles County’s food services. * Greenhouse gas emissions tracking: A review of the county’s food purchasing carbon footprint and recommendations on how to increase plant-based food options, how to reduce animal products and food waste, and when feasible, align with the County Food Equity Roundtable Action Plan. To bring equity and transparency to institutional purchasing, the Los Angeles Food Policy Council developed a values-based procurement policy: the Good Food Purchasing Program, which includes standards on local economies, sustainability, workers’ rights, animal welfare and nutrition. The City of Los Angeles and the Los Angeles Unified School District adopted the Good Food Purchasing Program in 2012, and the programme was scaled-up at national level becoming the Center for Good Food Purchasing in 2015. Several universities and hospital groups have shifted to serving healthy sustainable diets in order to reduce GHG emissions. The University of California Los Angeles (UCLA) Health increased its plant-based food procurement for students and patients. UCLA Health signed the “Cool Food Pledge” which is a commitment to a target of reducing GHG emissions associated with the food they serve by 25 per cent by 2030 in alignment with the Paris Agreement long-term mitigation and adaption global goals." ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.5.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Nexus response options for climate change" .

