    ipbes:Report <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/NXS24> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Food systems are remarkable in their diversity, which remains one of the greatest joys of the human experience as the source of cultural identity and cross-cultural exchange. The tension between urgently needing to secure enough food within planetary boundaries for approximately 10 billion people by 2050, and food production and consumption as a deeply humanistic, ecological and cultural act is a real, driving concern about oversimplifying scalable response options . The large productivity gains of the green revolution contributed to global food security; however, its impacts on small-holder systems, family farms, sustainable healthy diets and the loss of a diversity of culturally rich production systems, and concomitant negative environmental impacts including loss of agrobiodiversity among others, has been transformative . Plausible response options assessed in the subchapter were identified from the catalogue of national pathways for shaping sustainable and equitable food systems submitted by countries for the 2020-2023 United Nations Food System Summit (Figure 5.3.2). In developing their pathways, countries drew on actions identified by the scientific community which produced more than 76 science briefs, nearly 1700 food system dialogues with 110,000 individuals from civil society, and surveys led by five action tracks yielding over 6,000 individual responses on candidate response options. The identified actions in the pathways cover broad themes, all with strong linkages to food systems, that include, but are not limited to, equity and fairness, economic and finance issues, use of technologies, partnerships and synergies, and policy and regulatory frameworks. Figure 5.3.2. Distribution of 45 themes related to the nexus elements in the United Nations Food Systems Summit pathways. Themes were identified through word searches of national pathways across the five nexus elements and “other”, which often included cross-cutting actions. The relative size of the box indicates the number of countries including the themes in their pathways. Abbreviations: RGSS – restoring grasslands and shrublands and savannahs; RSV – resilience to stress and vulnerabilities. Source data: FAO Statistics Division . All response options identified in the United Nations Food Systems Summit pathways are important, but they have varying degrees of impact on the nexus elements. The expert opinion of the author team was used to identify a subset of the response options that impact multiple nexus elements. This emphasized the options associated with action tracks 1, 2, and 3: access to safe and nutritious foods, sustainable consumption and nature-positive food production, respectively. In addition, eleven solutions were proposed by United Nations Food Systems Summit under the action track on livelihoods and equity. These cover essential procedural options which underpin the successful implementation of response options. From these, gender, ILK and local food systems are emphasized in this chapter. The fifth United Nations Food Systems Summit action track focused on resilience and included an important list of cross-cutting actions. However, many of the resilience solutions have indirect impacts on nexus elements. Countries have struggled to articulate important nexus interactions in their pathways (Figure 5.3.2). This iterative process yielded 16 response options that are assessed in this subchapter. Borrowing from conservation , these can be categorized into five major groups of actions (Table 5.3.3): (1) avoiding the conversion of remaining terrestrial and aquatic ecosystems of high ecological integrity; (2) restoring degraded lands and waters either with the aim of increasing contributions to climate change mitigation, biodiversity conservation or food production; (3) regenerating the environmental performance of food by intensifying ecological interactions in production systems, notably managing soil communities to improve soil health, increase long-term belowground carbon capture and storage, and increasing aboveground carbon such as with production of perennials; (4) reducing soil, water and air pollution impacts from food systems; (5) transforming to just food systems through alignment on healthy, culturally diverse diets, urban-rural food systems, including local participation and access to natural resources and land (notably by women, youth and Indigenous communities) and ensuring that public spending on food generates positive impacts across nexus elements. Table 5.3.3. The 16 food system response options assessed classified by the United Nations Food Systems Summit action category. The table includes the percentage of countries (n = 126) mentioning the United Nations Food Systems Summit theme associated with each response option in the first iteration of the United Nations Food Systems Summit pathways. Note that sustainable intensification (F08) and reducing pollution (F07-F09) were not mentioned as themes, although 5 countries refer to sustainable intensification and 14 countries make reference to reducing pollution. Action category Response option assessed Countries (per cent) Avoid conversion F01 Halt conversion of ecosystems of high ecological integrity 28 Restore production F02 Restore soil health 49 Regenerate ecological function F03 Sustainable intensification 4 F04 Ecological intensification - croplands 72 F05 Ecological intensification - rangelands 56 F06 Ecological intensification - aquatic foods 66 Reduce pollution and waste F07 Reduce nutrient pollution 10 F08 Reduce pesticide pollution 10 F09 Reduce plastic pollution 10 F10 Reduce food loss and waste 71 Transform foods F11 Sustainable healthy diets 92 F12 City region food systems 18 F13 Reform public spending 83 F14 Foster gender-inclusive approaches 85 F15 Indigenous food systems 67 F16 Access to natural resources and land 70 Critical aspects of each response option are summarized in this section based on the synthesis of evidence and examples of implementation (Figures 5.2.3, 5.2.4 and 5.2.5). Each response option has been analysed and scored2 based on assessed evidence against 10 common criteria (Chapter 5.0, Table 5.0.1) related to its potential to produce benefits across multiple elements of the nexus, its feasibility and breadth of applicability, its impact on equity and its potential to advance the goals of existing global policy frameworks. Table 5.3.4 provides brief descriptions of the 16 food response options; the response options are described in more detail in Sections 5.3.3.1 to 5.3.3.16. Of the 16 response options assessed, four focal response options were selected for more detailed consideration: F06, Ecological intensification – aquatic foods (Section 5.3.3.6); F11, Sustainable healthy diets (Section 5.3.3.11); F12, City region food systems (Section 5.3.3.12) and F14, Foster gender-transformative approaches (Section 5.3.3.13). These focal response options are not intended to represent priorities or recommendations. Rather, they are chosen to illustrate the range of issues and actors involved in response options and provide real-world examples of response options in action. Table 5.3.4. Brief descriptions of the 16 food response options. Food response option Brief description of response option Section F01 Halt conversion of ecosystems of high ecological integrity Stopping the conversion of ecosystems of high ecological integrity and reducing expansion of the land base used for food production. 5.3.3.1 F02 Restore soil health Preventing soil degradation, reducing existing soil degradation and restoring degraded soils (including restoring function and structure) to support soil biodiversity and secure nature’s contributions to people provided by soils. 5.3.3.2 F03 Sustainable intensification Increasing agricultural yields without adverse environmental impacts and without expanding the land base used for agriculture (i.e., avoiding the conversion of intact ecosystems for the purpose of agriculture expansion). 5.3.3.3 F04 Ecological intensification – croplands Managing and using ecological processes and biodiversity in, and reducing external inputs to, cropland systems to sustainably improve cropland productivity, conserve or restore habitat, enhance and sustain nature’s contributions to people and secure farmers’ livelihoods. 5.3.3.4 F05 Ecological intensification – rangelands Managing and using ecological processes and biodiversity in, and reducing external inputs to, rangeland systems to sustainably improve rangeland productivity, conserve or restore habitat, enhance and sustain nature’s contributions to people and secure farmers’ livelihoods. 5.3.3.5 F06 Ecological intensification – aquatic foods A range of approaches in freshwater, coastal and marine ecosystems to increase food production while protecting biodiversity, conserving and/or restoring ecosystems and securing nature’s contributions to people. 5.3.3.6 F07 Reduce nutrient pollution Reducing nutrient pollution from agricultural systems. 5.3.3.7 F08 Reduce pesticide pollution Reducing pesticide pollution from agricultural systems. 5.3.3.8 F09 Reduce plastic pollution Reducing the use of plastic in food systems. 5.3.3.9 F10 Reduce food loss and waste Reducing food loss and waste throughout food supply chains, from production to consumption. 5.3.3.10 F11 Sustainable healthy diets Sustainable healthy diets promote all dimensions of individuals’ health and well-being, have low environmental pressure and impact, are accessible, affordable, safe and equitable, and are culturally acceptable. 5.3.3.11 F12 City region food systems Linking urban, peri-urban and rural communities to enable and support sustainable food systems, including food production, processing, distribution and consumption, protect the environment, provide economic opportunities and secure human health and well-being. 5.3.3.12 F13 Reform public spending Eliminating, phasing out or reforming agricultural subsidies that support unsustainable food production practices and undermine small-scale producers’ livelihoods to foster public spending models that enable sustainable food production and consumption and support producers. 5.3.3.13 F14 Foster gender-transformative approaches A range of options to end gender-based discrimination in the context of food systems; for women, this includes increasing access to resources and markets, securing land tenure, inclusion in value chains, improved labour conditions and economic empowerment. 5.3.3.14 F15 Indigenous food systems Recognizing and respecting Indigenous Peoples’ food production systems and food requirements, formalizing and securing their land tenure rights to traditional territories and supporting safe, healthy and sovereign Indigenous food systems. 5.3.3.15 F16 Access to natural resources and land Promoting, enabling and securing equitable access to natural resources and land, and securing land tenure rights for vulnerable and marginalized groups, including Indigenous Peoples and local communities. 5.3.3.16 Assessment of the 16 response options shows that the majority have strong impacts across nexus elements though these impacts can vary significantly (Figure 5.3.3). However, the success of the response options is strongly dependent on contextual factors and implementation effort, with social, political and economic dimensions being essential for successful implementation (Figure 5.3.4). The economic assessment of the response options was the most challenging aspect to assess considering that hidden costs are often poorly factored or not attributed to inconsistencies between social, political and economic dimensions of implementation efforts. Figure 5.3.3. Impacts of food response options on nexus elements. Colours indicate positive impacts (blue; i.e., synergies) and negative impacts (red; i.e., trade-offs). Intensity of colour represents the magnitude of impact (light = small; medium = medium impact; dark = large;). Grey colouring indicates a score of 0. White colouring indicates no score or a non-numerical value. Where impacts differ depending on context, impact ranges are shown by multiple colours within a cell. NA indicates not applicable, NE indicates no evidence, and INC indicates the evidence is inconclusive. Figure 5.3.4. Food response options within the context of social, economic and political dimensions, international frameworks and transformative change potential. Colours indicate positive impacts (blue; i.e., synergies) and negative impacts (red; i.e., trade-offs). Intensity of colour represents the magnitude of impact (light = small; medium = medium impact; dark = large;). Grey colouring indicates a score of 0. White colouring indicates no score or a non-numerical value. Where impacts differ depending on context, impact ranges are shown by multiple colours within a cell. NA indicates not applicable, NE indicates no evidence, and INC indicates the evidence is inconclusive. Figure 5.3.5. Temporal and spatial scales and technical feasibility of the food response options. Ranges shown temporal and spatial scales and feasibility indicated by Technology Readiness Level.3 5.3.3.1 Halt conversion of ecosystems of high ecological integrity (F01) Halting the conversion of ecosystems of high ecological integrity for food production is a response option that can be applied globally and in the short-term, with long-term benefits (Figure 5.3.5). It has strong positive impacts across the nexus, although it has the potential for negative impacts on food production and access to food (Figure 5.3.3). Food demand is projected to drive agricultural expansion and the loss of 2-10 million km2 of ecosystems of high ecological integrity in coming decades . Halting this conversion could have a negative impact on food production and access to food, particularly in the Global South and for the most marginalized in society . However, this negative impact could be avoided by producing more food from existing agricultural and degraded land (Sections 5.3.3.2 (F02), 5.3.3.4 (F04) and 5.3.3.5 (F05)), reducing demand by reducing food waste (Section 5.3.3.10 (F10)) and shifting to sustainable healthy diets that require less land to support (Section 5.3.3.11 (F11)) . Halting conversion of ecosystems of high ecological integrity has very large positive impacts on biodiversity and climate change. Intact ecosystems are irreplaceable for conserving native species ; ecosystem function and resilience ; reducing or preventing fragmentation ; and carbon storage and sequestration . Halting conversion of these ecosystems also benefits human health and well-being by reducing the probability of zoonotic disease transmission to people and livestock , reducing vulnerability to climate change and temperature extremes and benefiting mental, social and cultural well-being . It also increases water provision by regulating streamflow, improving water quality and increasing precipitation, although there may be local-scale complexity in precipitation changes . Multiple actions exist for halting conversion (Table 5.3.5). The most appropriate, effective and equitable actions will depend on social, economic, political and agricultural contexts. Halting conversion of ecosystems of high ecological integrity is at Technical Readiness Level 9 (Figure 5.3.6); direct costs are relatively low, but local opportunity costs may be very high if the land is needed to meet food demand (Figure 5.3.4). This response option is aligned strongly with multiple international frameworks . Alignment with national and local priorities, social acceptability and impacts on equity and IPLC will depend on the action taken, scale of opportunity costs, their distribution and their mitigation. There is limited evidence of transformative change potential for this response option alone, but it has high transformative potential when combined with yield-increasing response options (Figure 5.3.4). Table 5.3.5. Examples of approaches for halting conversion of ecosystems of high ecological integrity. Approach Key actors Examples References State-governed protected areas National, regional and local governments Protected areas in International Union for Conservation of Nature (IUCN) categories Ia, Ib, II Private protected areas Non-governmental organizations, private landowners, land-owning companies Colombian Network of Natural Preserves of Civil Society, Chico Mendes Institute for Biodiversity Conservation in Brazil, Conservation Reserve Programme in the United States, PlainsTender incentive scheme in Australia Indigenous Peoples’ and local communities’ governance of ecosystems Indigenous Peoples and local communities, state authorities Indigenous lands in the Amazon Nolte ; Sze Regional regulation of ecosystem conversion National, regional and local governments Amazon Soy Moratorium Gibbs ; Heilmayr Commodity-wide or company commitments Producers and commodity traders Wilmar International’s “No Deforestation, No Peat, No Exploitation” commitment, Brazil’s cattle agreements Gibbs ; Jopke & Schoneveld ; Pereira Market-based approaches Commodity traders; producers; consumers Zero deforestation commitments; transparent and traceable supply chain commitments, certification Gardner ; Lambin & Furumo ; Tayleur 5.3.3.2 Restore soil health (F02) Soils are the basis for producing more than 95 per cent of the food society eats . Soil erosion by water is estimated to annually reduce global agricultural food production by 33.7 million tonnes . Reverting soil degradation is central to restoring the productive capacity of degraded lands (Figure 5.3.5). Healthy soils constitute the foundation of agriculture and a critical resource providing society with food, feed, fibre and clean water . Restoration of degraded soils and conservation of soil productivity in agriculture is associated with management practices that increase soil organic carbon content (see also Section 5.5.3.1). Soil organic carbon influences key soil functions such as crop yield and biomass production; storage and regulation of soil carbon, nutrient and water; control of erosion; filtering of wastes and pollutants from water that may compromise access to healthy food and well-being; and providing habitat for soil biodiversity (Figure 5.3.3). International efforts led by the United Nations Convention to Combat Desertification, the United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change and the Convention on Biological Diversity, particularly its Plan of Action (2020-2030) for the International Initiative for the Conservation and Sustainable Use of Soil Biodiversity led by the Global Soil Partnership hosted at FAO, have triggered increased interest in soil restoration, particularly the restoration of soil functions driven by soil organic carbon and biodiversity (Figure 5.3.4). Recent evidence highlights the significant impact that the presence of earthworms alone can have on global food production, with contributions estimated at 6.5 per cent of global cereal production and 2.3 per cent of legume production, corresponding to over 140 million metric tons annually (Figure 5.3.3). Management practices that enhance perennial vegetation significantly contribute to enhancing soil organic carbon content . Agroforestry has been increasingly recognized and practiced as a multifunctional land management option that can simultaneously contribute to income, food security and nutrition, soil health and carbon restoration, the conservation of biodiversity and nature’s contributions to people, and climate change adaptation and mitigation (Figure 5.3.3). Perennial ground cover through hedges, food-bearing shrubs and grass stripes are important means to broaden agroforestry options in agricultural landscapes. Food producing perennial options can be means to mediate the challenges posed by integration of trees in some environments where competition is high to produce sufficient food from limited land and water resources . Shrubs, vines and other semi-perennial crop integration can also address gender equity barriers as access to input and output markets and the right to grow trees is often limited for women farmers and women-headed households (Figure 5.3.4). Agroforestry has high established benefits across the nexus of biodiversity, food, health, climate change, energy, land and natural resource management (Figure 5.3.4). Nevertheless, the success at scale of perennial revegetation efforts to recover soil health needs to be socially acceptable, economically feasible and in alignment with political priorities (Figure 5.3.4). This is well illustrated by the Great Green Wall Initiative in Africa (Box 5.3.2) which has evolved to embrace the need to include multiple stakeholder concerns and respect traditional customs, implement systematic planning and monitoring, and secure financing and ensure equity in decision-making among stakeholders. Box 5.3.2. Farmer-managed natural regeneration agroforestry and the Great Green Wall Initiative Farmer-managed natural regeneration stands out as having proven soil restoration benefits at scale. Farmer-managed natural regeneration is a low-cost agroforestry practice that consists of protecting and managing naturally sprouting plantlets of native trees on farmland, as well as shoot regrowth produced by tree stumps . This agroforestry practice has contributed to increasing soil carbon across the Sahel , regreening close to 5 million hectares in south-Central Niger and generating soil fertility improvements resulting in as much as 30 per cent cereal yield increases contributing to income generation and improved livelihoods (Figure 5.3.6). Low costs have made farmer-managed natural regeneration a widely applicable practice increasingly making important contributions to the Great Green Wall Initiative (Great Green Wall Initiative) which has united 21 African countries and international partners towards achieving 100 million hectares of restored degraded land, 250 million tons of carbon sequestered, and ten million green jobs created by 2030 (Figure 5.3.7; Figure 5.3.8) Figure 5.3.6. Farmer-managed natural regeneration in Niger. Photo credit: C. P. Reij under licence CC BY 4.0. Figure 5.3.7. The Great Green Wall Initiative. Photo credit: United Nations Convention to Combat Desertification (UNCCD) under licence CC BY-NC-SA 4.0. Figure 5.3.8. Building Africa's Great Green Wall: Landscape restoration needs and opportunities. Figure from FAO under licence CC BY-NC-SA 4.0. Successful implementation of farmer-managed natural regeneration for restoration requires presence of desired tree species in the vicinity, complemented with tree planting if natural regeneration is insufficient, and protection of regeneration from grazing animals . Combining local and scientific knowledge to select suitable native species for large-scale natural capital restoration has proven successful in the Great Green Wall Initiative . In its current configuration, the Great Green Wall Initiative has evolved from its original “wall of trees” conception to that of a mosaic of diverse landscape-scale actions co-designed to provide long-term options for improved environmental and socioeconomic conditions, for example the establishment of women-run communal vegetable gardens and new water access points and promotion of small-scale ecotourism along the GGW path . Evaluation of socioeconomic impacts through household surveys conducted in intervention areas between 2016 and 2020 showed significant income improvement after interventions particularly in Nigeria and Senegal, while food insecurity dropped 31 per cent in Senegal and 11 per cent in Niger Farmers and land managers often face strong economic, structural and technical barriers to adopting soil restoration practices at scale. For example, insecure land tenure has been often associated with limited adoption of resource conserving agricultural practices that generate delayed or reduced short-term economic benefits . These barriers must be addressed and need to be adapted to variation in social, economic and ecological context through collaboration with local communities and relevant authorities if farmer-managed natural regeneration, or any restoration and diversification response option, is to achieve its full potential for nexus-wide benefits. Box 5.3.3. Agroecology, sustainable intensification and ecological intensification The sustainability of food production includes a large and growing number of terms and definitions which are often conflicting. This makes the assessment of sustainable agriculture particularly challenging, for example distinctions between nature-positive production, agroecology, regenerative agriculture, sustainable intensification, ecological intensification or even organic agriculture. These terms confound specific practices (conservation tillage), norms (organic agriculture), actions (diversification), objectives (regenerative) and transformative approaches (agroecology), to name but a few. Agroecology studies, recognizes and utilizes biodiversity’s contribution to ecosystem processes and functions to and from agriculture with the aim of maintaining or recovering these functions . Agroecological research and actions similarly emphsize social and economic dimensions of production system . Diversification, agroecological or regenerative agricultural practices are overlapping in intensifying these ecological processes (ecological intensification) and include a diversity of management options from fields to landscapes. Any form of ecological intensification explicitly manages biodiversity and ecological processes in production systems, including in support of sustainable intensification . Sustainable intensification, in contrast, can be applied without ecological intensification by focusing on practices that enhance input use efficiency and reduce negative impacts on the environment. There is good evidence that agroecological knowledge can be applied to the sustainable and ecological intensification of a range of production systems from traditional to modern, smallholder to large, terrestrial to aquatic . Achieving impact across the nexus requires actions that enable food producers of all types to meaningfully engage in sustainable and healthy food production. As seen in Figure 5.3.9 there exist many alternative transition pathways to agroecological production systems along a gradient of increasing farming intensity. contrasting ecological and sustainable intensification pathways. Figure 5.3.9. Alternative pathways for the transition to agroecological systems. Transition pathways are shown for traditional smallholder systems, industrial farming systems and degraded farmland. Adapted from under Springer Nature licence number 5875380249562. 5.3.3.3 Sustainable intensification (F03) Sustainable intensification can be confused with other terms related to the sustainable production of food production (Box 5.3.3). IPBES defines sustainable intensification as “a process or system where agricultural yields are increased without adverse environmental impact and without the conversion of additional non-agricultural land” . Sustainable intensification can be applied globally on all arable and pastoral land due to yield gaps in many regions and has the potential to reduce environmental impacts while maintaining yields elsewhere (Figure 5.3.5). It can be achieved through a wide range of approaches with differing impacts and timescales (Table 5.3.6). Table 5.3.6. Examples of sustainable intensification approaches. Note there is some overlap between sustainable and ecological intensification approaches (Sections 5.3.3.4, 5.3.3.5 and 5.3.3.6). Approach Examples References Locally adapted conventional approaches More targeted or appropriate use of nitrogen fertilisers, cropping techniques and planting dates Chen ; Cui Precision agriculture Technology assisted, precise use of agrochemicals, irrigation and other management at a sub-field scale Finger Novel feeds Use of novel, or under-utilised, livestock feeds such as algae, food waste or insects Kelly & Kebreab ; Madeira ; van Huis ; zu Ermgassen Indoor farming Greenhouses or vertical farming for crops; indoor breeding, raising or finishing for livestock Clark & Tilman ; van Delden Development of new crop varieties and livestock breeds Breeding or genetic modification of varieties to increase yields or produce multiple crops, or to adapt to specific environmental conditions or pests Barrows ; Cooper ; Griffiths ; Hayes ; Sharma ; Tadele Sustainable intensification has positive impacts across nexus elements (Figure 5.3.3). Yield-increasing response options (i.e., F02 Restore soil health (Section 5.3.3.2); F03, Sustainable intensification (Section 5.3.3.3); F04, Ecological intensification – croplands (Section 5.3.3.4); F05 Ecological intensification – rangelands (Section 5.3.3.5) and F06 Ecological intensification – aquatic foods (Section 5.3.3.6)) are needed to achieve these nexus benefits even if consumption changes (e.g., F10, Reduce food loss and waste (Section 5.3.10); F11, Sustainable healthy diets (Section 5.3.11)) are achieved . However, realizing benefits may require policy or economic mechanisms to avoid additional agricultural expansion . Sustainable intensification has limited the conversion of natural ecosystems avoiding up to 590 GtCO2e emissions from 1961-2005 . It is projected to reduce future emissions by 2-2.45 GtCO2e per year, largely through limiting agricultural expansion , and climate adaptation benefits achieved through sustainable intensification could impact up to 163 million people . Limiting agricultural expansion has also benefited biodiversity and future sustainable intensification could have huge benefits for native biodiversity and the area available for protection , as well as off-farm benefits on ecological function, connectivity and resilience, while on-farm impacts vary depending on the approach . Sustainable intensification improves crop water-use efficiency, potentially reducing water use or producing more food for the same amount of water . In livestock systems, it may increase blue and grey water use due to greater consumption of irrigated feeds . It also increases water quality by reducing nutrient run-off and soil erosion . Sustainable intensification can have variable impacts on human health. Intensive livestock systems limit the probability of zoonotic disease spillover by reducing livestock-wildlife interactions, but may increase the probability of zoonotic disease take-off, antimicrobial resistance, foodborne diseases and air pollution . Interlinkages between sustainable intensification practices, air pollution and health are described in the case study in Box 5.3.4. Benefits through increased nutrition will only be realised if production is linked to sustainable healthy diets (Section 5.3.3.11; Section 5.4.3.6, H06, Reduce meat overconsumption; Section 5.5.3.15, C15, Sustainable healthy diets). Sustainable intensification approaches vary in social acceptability (e.g., intensive livestock production may be unacceptable due to animal welfare concerns) and economic costs and benefits (Figure 5.3.4). Many approaches are at Technological Readiness Level 9 (Figure 5.3.5), with low direct costs, but the investment required may still be beyond the poorest farmers, or bring unacceptable risks from changing production techniques, raising equity concerns (Figure 5.3.4). There is an apparent alignment of sustainable intensification approaches with multiple international frameworks . Sustainable intensification stands in contrast to ecological intensification, as there is limited common understanding of scope and hypotheses underlying sustainability whereas there is a large body of research findings to be drawn on from the science of ecology . There is limited evidence of transformative change potential, in part due to contestation around its definition (Figure 5.3.4). Box 5.3.4. Sustainable intensification production practices, pollution and human health: Case studies in California and the Punjab The impacts of pollution from food production are particularly important when they are located in proximity to populous cities with health costs that are carried by individuals and public health authorities. The City of Sacramento legislated a rice straw burning ban in the 1990s which precipitated a rapid transition to winter wetting to facilitate decomposition. The Sacramento Delta of California is one of the most productive rice growing regions in the world. Rice straw burning, used to eliminate silica rich rice straw, was banned in 1992 out of concern of air quality impacts on the urban residents of Sacramento (pop. 1.5 million). Very rapidly farmers transitioned to winter flooding to deal with rice straw with the unintended consequence of nearly doubling winter wetland habitat for over-wintering migratory waterfowl in the region. To reduce methane emissions from flooded rice, farmers collaborated with conservation organizations to time flooding with the arrival of winter bird migrants, now an important part of central valley tourism . Similar bans are in place, but poorly enforced in the Punjab which continues to suffer severe annual air pollution challenges. Rice straw burning, an easy way to quickly clear rice fields before planting wheat, contributes approximately 25 per cent of the poor air quality faced by inhabitants of the Punjab (pop: 27 million) . Air quality in the winter of 2024 was recorded at 500 mg m-3 (safe levels are 12 mg m-3), shutting schools and businesses. Shifting to existing no-till technology, such as the “happy seeder” (no-till planter) could eliminate the rice straw burning problem and would contribute to increasing soil organic matter capture with important yield improvements. However, it is unaffordable for most small-holder farmers. Considering costs of nexus interactions on health and climate change could fundamentally alter the affordability of no-till systems by recognizing their public value, rather than private cost to farmers. 5.3.3.4 Ecological intensification – croplands (F04) Ecological Intensification “entails the environmentally friendly replacement of anthropogenic inputs and/or enhancement of crop productivity, by including regulating and supporting ecosystem services management in agricultural practices” (e.g., pollination, pest regulation, soil formation) and is the basis of agroecological practices (Box 5.3.3). Ecological intensification practices span traditional and modern innovations originating from local, Indigenous, farmer and scientific knowledge systems . Garibaldi proposed ten science-based policy recommendations for ecological intensification of agriculture with recent work suggesting that minimum habitat per km2 should be between 10-25 per cent : 1. Enhance above- and belowground species diversity 2. Reduce synthetic inputs 3. Enhance soil health 4. Maintain and/or restore natural and semi-natural habitat areas with recent values suggested to at least 10-25 per cent per km2 5. Enhance habitat diversity 6. Implement a landscape design plan to integrate practices 7. Protect and efficiently use water resources 8. Evaluate agricultural productivity and ecosystem services over the long-term 9. Consider multiple outcome benefits 10. Facilitate participatory action, multiple knowledge systems and farmer training Ecological intensification of croplands can be applied across the 1,244?Mha of croplands globally (Figure 5.3.5). Ecological intensification can increase the water holding and infiltration capacity of soils, improving crop production, while also strongly mitigating drought impacts and modestly reducing flood impacts . Increasing soil organic matter is a “no regrets” intervention with multiple food, water and soil biodiversity benefits. It also has a positive impact on mitigating climate change globally although the scale of impact is less certain ranging between 0.9-1.85 GtCO2e year-1 . Above ground, ecological intensification intercepts land-based runoff of nutrients (see Section 5.3.3.7) and biocides (see Section 5.3.3.9) before they enter waterways . It also creates habitat and habitat connectivity and provides pest regulation, pollination and nutrient interception and has the potential to create barriers to pest dispersal . This reduces dependency on pesticides, which has positive impacts on human health and biodiversity . Favouring cropping systems that more closely match the ecological function capacity of the biome they are located in has very strong climate change mitigation potential equal to 42-47 percent of the difference between current and potential biomass stocks . Habitat elements can be used by natural pest regulating and pollinating species which are essential for fruit, nuts and legumes production . Retaining >20 per cent habitat per km2 is required to maintain pest regulation, pollination, pollution interception and soil erosion reduction; between 33-66 per cent of croplands are below this threshold globally . When ecological intensification practices are adapted to local contexts they can provide increases in both environmental performance and long-term yield , including in highly intensified conventional systems . Trade-offs between yield, and one or more indicators of environmental performance, can arise and are best handled through combined application of local and scientific knowledge to adapt practices to local landscapes, capacities and contexts . Ecological intensification is dependent on farmers for uptake but can be hampered by increased labour and material costs, loss of productive lands, or weed and disease spread (Figure 5.3.4). Subsidies, payments for ecosystem services or certification, supported by policy measures, are significant means of enabling farmers participation (Section 5.3.3.13). Embracing agroecological transitions towards sustainable food systems are supported by the development of effective and innovative policies, institutions and markets that enable and support transformative change . Visual narratives using the 10 Elements of Agroecology offer a flexible tool to support integrated policymaking and food system transformation (Box 5.3.5). The concept of visual narratives can be applied in food systems policy analysis to highlight promising entry points for policy interventions, identify opportunities for enhancing co-benefits through the nexus approach and help build policy packages to catalyse food systems transformation . Box 5.3.5. Visual narratives using the 10 elements of agroecology can facilitate alignment of actions and integrated policymaking for system diversification Application of the 10 elements of agroecology (Box 5.3.1), specifically diversity, to illustrate how the approach can help design a supporting policy package to facilitate system diversification at scale that produces co-benefits among the nexus elements of biodiversity, water, food, health and climate change . This can be applied to different ecological intensification options but is illustrated here using agroforestry as an example of the visual narrative flow (Figure 5.3.10). Figure 5.3.10. Designing a policy package facilitating agroforestry promotion to produce co-benefits among biodiversity, water, food, health and climate change. Source: FAO under licence CC BY-NC-SA 4.0. (1) Farm diversification: Place-based approaches that value culture, knowledge and food traditions have helped define which components (e.g., crops, livestock, trees) are included in agroforestry production systems. Experience developed locally and globally on useful combinations of plant and/or animal types or species that encourage synergies can contribute to enhancing recycling that reduces the need for fertilizer inputs and water pollution and to enhancing climate change adaptation and mitigation. Local knowledge and experience on the differences in availability, taste, ease of cooking, nutritional quality or intended use can strongly influence these decisions. The importance of farmer agency to promote adaptation and scaling is highlighted through participatory methods that facilitate co-creation processes aiming at blending local and scientific knowledge. This approach can ensure system components are selected to build synergies and minimize trade-offs. (2) Niche marketing: Certification and labelling have been effective for influencing consumers and increasing demand for safer, more diverse and nutritious diets. This demand also encourages producers to re-design and diversify their agricultural systems to minimize pesticide use, promote greater resource-use efficiency and recycling and adapt fertilizer use to local contexts. These actions all contribute to reduced inputs and improved production, reducing costs and enhancing profitability. (3) Land tenure and traditional markets: Poor market access and land tenure insecurity reduces producers’ willingness to invest in practices such as agroforestry that provide long-term nexus-wide benefits. Reforming land tenure can help remove this barrier. Markets for agroforestry products can also be strengthened through governmental policies (e.g., pricing policies, title deeds for informal markets) supported by shorter value chains and the reconnection of producers and consumers through local markets and local economic development. Expanded market infrastructure for Indigenous crops and other overlooked agricultural products can also provide an enabling context for diversified farming systems. 5.3.3.5 Ecological intensification – rangelands (F05) Rangelands cover a greater area than any other terrestrial food system (Figure 5.3.5). Ecological intensification of rangelands can make them more productive while also providing benefits to other nexus elements (Figure 5.3.3). This can be achieved through a variety of mechanisms, including regenerative grazing (modifying the intensity and duration of grazing to mimic natural systems), pyric herbivory (integration of fire and grazing in a shifting mosaic of patches), silvopasture (a form of agroforestry, involving adding trees to grazed lands (Box 5.3.6) and fence-free herding. Ecological intensification has neutral to positive effects on food production from rangelands. Silvopasture increases yields of forage and wood products by increasing the production area of each , while regulating microclimate through shade , reducing heat stress and providing shelter for livestock and additional fodder. Pyric herbivory can maintain livestock production levels . Rangeland ecological intensification benefits biodiversity even more strongly. Regenerative grazing results in higher soil biome and grass diversity than continuous grazing , combining fire with grazing benefits rare species and grassland bird communities while managing invasive species . Fence-free herding reduces fragmentation in rangelands, allowing wildlife to access heterogeneous resources critical in arid environments . While silvopasture capacity to increase soil and aboveground carbon is well documented , the climate change mitigation potential of grasslands is variable. Regenerative grazing can increase soil carbon compared to continuous grazing , but can take 5-20 years to take hold . Optimizing grazing intensities across all grasslands globally could increase soil carbon sequestration potential by 148 to 699 MtCO2e year-1 . In Mediterranean environments, grazing has been used to adapt to climate change by reducing fuel-loads and fire risk notably around cities . Rangeland ecological intensification has a limited but generally positive effect on water for ecosystems and humans. Pyric herbivory can enhance water retention and silvopasture can increase water infiltration . Ecological intensification is often practiced by IPLC and is threatened by a variety of policies, including land privatization and sedentarization schemes . Economic costs and savings vary widely depending on where and how it is implemented (Figure 5.3.6). Diversified systems tend to be more economically stable but often require up front capital and specific capacity-building to facilitate transition. Rangeland ecological intensification is aligned with multiple SDGs and the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework target 10 on sustainable production (Figure 5.3.4). Ecological intensification of rangelands can help facilitate halting conversion of ecosystems of high ecological integrity (Section 5.3.3.1) and restoring soil health (Section 5.3.3.2). It will be most effective when combined with reforming public spending (Section 5.3.3.13) and promoting ILK (Section 5.3.3.15) in recognition of the importance of Indigenous pastoralist practices. Transformative change will require combining ecological intensification of rangelands with shifting to sustainable healthy diets (Section 5.3.3.11) to reduce overall demand for meat to allow more sustainable stocking rates. Box 5.3.6. Common ecological intensification practices in orchards and rangelands: Mediterranean grape farming Ecological intensification includes a diversity of practices and innovations. While core principles are possible, the most effective interventions include the adaptation of these principles in local contexts as the best means to manage trade-offs and leverage synergies. For example, Mediterranean grape farmers control emergent weeds each spring. Three practices are common: (a) herbicide applications; (b) mechanical weeding; and (c) sheep and goat grazing between rows. Yields between all three options are comparable. Option (a) reduces soil carbon capture and increases pollution risk (water and health impact). Option (b) does not have a chemical pollution risk but reduces soil carbon capture and increases soil erosion risk (water pollution by sediments). Option (c), ecological intensification with ruminants, reduces the risk of chemical pollution, increases soil carbon storage, contributes to soil fertility and adds a second product to the farm productivity. Herds are often moved after the spring, including for use in ecological pasturing in peri-urban spaces to reduce fuel load and fire risk . 5.3.3.6 Ecological intensification – aquatic foods (F06) Ecological Intensification of aquatic foods focuses on enhancing the ecological processes within aquatic ecosystems, such as nutrient cycling, habitat structure maintenance, and trophic interactions, to increase the productivity and resilience of freshwater and marine food sources. Ecological intensification of aquatic food systems is a response option that can be applied across much of the world’s marine and freshwater systems (Figure 5.3.5), with consistently positive impacts on other nexus elements (Figure 5.3.3). Benefits are greatest when combined with halting the conversion of ecosystems of high ecological integrity, both aquatic and terrestrial (Section 5.3.3.1), and reducing demand through reduced food loss and waste (Section 5.3.3.10) and shifting to sustainable healthy diets (Section 5.3.3.11). It involves a range of approaches in both wild caught and aquaculture systems (Table 5.3.7). Table 5.3.7. Ecological intensification of aquatic food system approaches and examples. Approach Examples References Sustainable fisheries management * Marine protected areas * Use of monitoring technologies * Implementing catch shares and quotas * Use of low-impact gear * Minimizing bycatch and harmful subsidies Costello ; Melnychuk ; Orofino ; Silva Restore aquatic ecosystems * Coral reef restoration * Mangrove restoration * Seagrass restoration * Wetlands and riparian zone restoration Free ; Mellin ; Sala ; Sievers Shifting to more sustainable aquaculture production systems * Recirculating aquaculture systems * Novel protein fish feed sources * Processing fish and shellfish byproducts * Plant-based proteins and oils * Microbial proteins Ahmed & Turchini ; Froelich ; Nguyen ; Stentiford Seaweed cultivation * Integrated multitrophic aquaculture * Line and rope cultivation * Spore settlement * Use of seaweed hatcheries Behera ; Hamilton ; Lal ; Pawar ; Ecological intensification of aquatic food systems could have significant positive impacts on food production, access to food and the environmental performance of food. It could increase aquatic food production by 36-74 per cent (~21-44 million tonnes) by 2050 , potentially benefiting more than 800 million people who depend on aquatic ecosystems . This is particularly important as demand for aquatic foods is projected to double by 2050 . Ecological intensification of aquatic food systems includes strategies such as coral reef restoration , mangrove restoration , seagrass restoration, and wetlands and riparian zone restoration. These provide the triple benefit of biodiversity protection, fisheries productivity and securing marine carbon stocks . The restoration and avoided conversion of coral reefs, mangroves, seagrass beds, wetlands and riparian zones could deliver 0.3–3.1 GtCO2e yr–1 in total . Mangrove forests alone store ~10.8 billion tons of carbon , which can be secured through ecological intensification of food production alongside halting conversion of these ecosystems (Section 5.5.3.1). Approximately 650 million hectares of ocean could support seaweed production. If used for ruminant feed, this could mitigate approximately 2.6 GtCO2e yr?1 of greenhouse gas emissions . As a substitute in the human diet, global seaweed production could spare up to 110 million hectares of land . Collectively, kelp forests sequester 4.91 megatons of carbon from the atmosphere per year . Approximately 52 per cent (~1.9 million ha) of deforested mangroves can be attributed to coastal aquaculture, with shrimp farming accounting for over 70 per cent of the loss . In some cases, restoration could displace food production and damage local food supply , pushing some actors to adopt more efficient but potentially harmful practices . The potential economic gains from some conservation strategies might be optimistic and do not necessarily trickle down to benefit the poor and most vulnerable groups (Figure 5.3.4). Ecological intensification of aquatic food systems through sustainable fisheries management practices, such as quotas, limiting bycatch, using low-impact gear and promoting offshore fisheries, can result in improved health, climate, biodiversity and food security outcomes . Implementing these measures can positively affect biodiversity by enhancing conservation efforts and increasing the productivity of aquatic ecosystems . By addressing micronutrient deficiencies, ecological intensification of aquatic food systems could play a significant role in preventing several non-communicable diseases . Approximately 2.3 billion people have diets lacking at least one micronutrient, which accounts for over 1 million premature deaths annually . Strategies to reduce pollution from marine litter , microplastics , pesticides, fertilizers and heavy metals in aquatic and freshwater systems can reduce human health risks. The intensification of aquatic food production calls for holistic policymaking and effective governance systems . This typically involves integrating various considerations for aquatic foods across policy systems and engaging a diverse set of governance actors and strengthening collective action (Table 5.3.8). This approach could be transformative, as it necessitates fundamental changes to many of the institutional structures that currently govern food systems and sustainability (Figure 5.3.4). Table 5.3.8. Three policy actions to enable diverse blue foods to contribute to healthy and sustainable food systems. Source: Tigchelaar . Policy action area Specific actions 1. Integrate blue foods into decision-making about food system policies, programs and investments to enable effective management of production, consumption and trade, and the interconnections with terrestrial food production * Create governance structures that integrate green and blue foods * Govern blue foods as a food system * Include blue foods in food system policies 2. Understand, protect and develop the potential of blue foods for ending malnutrition and fostering production of accessible, affordable nutritious foods * Recognize the centrality of the right to food in blue food trade and policy * Harness the nutritional value of blue foods * Halt the loss of nutrients from blue food systems * Improve the distributional equity of blue food production and consumption 3. Support the central role of small-scale actors, with governance and finance that are responsive to their diverse needs, circumstances and opportunities * Include small-scale actors in decision making * Expand capabilities, institutions and investment in environmental conservation and protection * Support diversification and sustainable intensification * Secure economic and nutritional benefits through trade policies and through the development and protection of local and national markets Box 5.3.7. Flood pulse ecosystems Flood pulse ecosystems are often high value food production landscapes with unique ecological and sociocultural properties including the production of land-based crops and nutrient dense aquatic foods. These locations provide high value and highly threatened biodiversity habitat. The Barotse people (Zambia) use flood pulses to time rice and vegetable cultivation. They have seasonal access to foods with small fish during flood periods. They use flood recession waters for rice cultivation and small irrigation systems for summer vegetable production. The pulse is critical to biodiversity conservation but would be lost by conversion to intensive rice monocultures . The Tonle Sap (Cambodia) is home to unique communities living in floating or stilted villages. Rice is produced with recession waters, while fish are captured year-round. The lake is a critical UNESCO heritage site home to unique bird and fish diversity. The pulse of the Tonle Sap is threatened by hydropower infrastructure on the Mekong, providing renewable energy to large populations . Many parts of the Amazon are flood pulse systems with communities adapted to fast production crops cultivated on riverbanks and fisheries for food. At large scales, these flood pulses are important for soil fertility and formation. Dam infrastructure to control flooding and generate clean energy reduces flood pulses and their predictability. Maintaining pulses, even with hydropower infrastructure, can preserve these processes, ILK systems and the foods they produce with positive impacts on biodiversity conservation . The Central Valley of California was a major flood pulse system that has been lost with the development of highly productive agriculture. The Sacramento delta however continues to make use of this pulse co-managed by farmers, the city of Sacramento, the Army Corps of Engineers and local conservation organizations for rice cultivation, hunting, flood protection, wildlife conservation and air quality . Rice produced in the delta is amongst the highest quality and most productive (12 tha-1) in the world. Flood protection, hunting and habitat values of the Delta surpass those of rice cultivation . 5.3.3.7 Reduce nutrient pollution (F07) Feeding the world while reducing nutrient pollution can be applied at the global scale (Figure 5.3.5), requiring nutrient use efficiencies between 60 and 77 per cent. Reductions can be prioritized in regions where intensive agriculture is pervasive with increased nutrient use in regions where productivity remains low . Reducing nutrient use is beneficial to overall environmental performance but can result in lower yields, although the evidence for this relationship is weak . Reducing nutrient pollution has strong benefits for reducing the risk of contamination of surface water used for human consumption, preserving water quality for humans and ecosystems, and decreasing overall environmental exposure to algal blooms (Figure 5.3.3). It also benefits human health as cyanobacterial blooms and the accumulation of several cyanotoxins in water bodies pose a severe risk to global public health and aquatic organisms . Algal blooms have been associated with several water-borne diseases (e.g., cholera) and other morbidities including neurological disorders . Reducing nutrient pollution has positive outcomes for native biodiversity and ecological functions, although evidence is weak. Actions such as improved nutrient use efficiency, precision application and creating effective buffers between agricultural systems and adjacent aquatic systems could have net positive impacts on terrestrial and aquatic biodiversity, while simultaneously creating habitat and connectivity . Reducing nutrient pollution has minimal impacts on climate change, as the reduction in nutrient application to reduce pollution in intensive agricultural areas is nearly equal to the increases required to close yield gaps in areas where productivity is low . Nevertheless, 15 per cent of global fossil fuel use is for creating nutrient inputs for food . Public opinion is generally supportive of reducing nutrient pollution. However, farmers’ perspectives may be less supportive due to tension between nutrients as an important agricultural input and a pollutant (Figure 5.3.4). Reduced nutrient use is associated with reduced yield risks, but also with improved nutrient use efficiencies for which investment in advanced technologies is required . Normative tools providing a locally adaptable framework and a voluntary set of practices to guide different stakeholders directly or indirectly involved with fertilizer use and management can play a significant role in reducing nutrient pollution (e.g., the International Code of Conduct for the Sustainable Use and Management of Fertilizers . Several other response options also support reductions in nutrient pollution with generally positive outcomes, including F10, Reduce food loss and waste (Section 5.3.3.10); F11, Sustainable healthy diets (Section 5.3.3.11) and improving nutrient recycling and agricultural production practices (see F04, Ecological intensification – croplands (Section 5.5.3.4) and F05, Ecological intensification – rangelands (Section 5.3.3.5). All these options have an impact on reducing both nitrogen and phosphorus pollution . However, implementation at scale is very costly and often limited (Figure 5.3.5). The efficacy of actions is often dependent on agricultural systems, climate, soil types, topography and the nutrient management approaches employed . 5.3.3.8 Reduce pesticide pollution (F08) Reducing pesticide pollution can be applied globally but should be prioritized in geographic areas with large areas under intensive agriculture where pollution risks are high (Figure 5.3.5). Ten percent of the 3 million tonnes of pesticides used annually in agriculture remains in the upper soil horizons as residue, 7 per cent leaches into the aquifers and 0.1 per cent enters rivers and oceans . These residues put 64 per cent of agricultural land at risk of pollution and 31 per cent at high risk of pollution . Biocides residues have a wide spectrum of half-life and cycle and can persist in the environment as pollutants over the medium to long-term . Reducing pesticide pollution has very high benefits for reducing surface and groundwater pollution , preserving water quality for humans and ecosystems and decreasing overall environmental exposure to persistent pesticides (Figure 5.3.3). Reducing pesticide residues in the environment also benefits soil biodiversity that is essential for maintaining soil health and performance – notably carbon storage . However, in the absence of alternative pest management practices (Section 5.3.3.4), reducing pesticide use may decrease yield protection efficiency and increase costs . Decreasing pesticide transport via surface water and wind drift to non-treated land can have positive effects on preserving native biodiversity in protected areas and the wider landscape. This can improve associated ecological functions, which in turn can preserve/improve ecosystem resilience capability . Riparian buffers are useful for reducing pesticide flow into freshwater systems, which have important climate and biodiversity co-benefits . However, similar to reducing nutrient pollution (Section 5.3.3.7), there is currently no evidence of the impact of reducing pesticide pollution on climate change, although an estimated 15 per cent of global fossil fuel use is allocated to agricultural inputs including pesticides . The response option is highly beneficial to human health as pesticide residues are present in fresh and processed foods, leading to toxicological risks . Documented evidence shows that reducing pesticide use decreases environmental loads of residues and potential human exposure to them . This in turn decreases the associated burden of diseases , as well as pesticide application operators’ consciousness of exposure risk and associated mental well-being . While social acceptability of the response option is high, alignment to political agendas is intermediate to low mainly due to industrial assets, intellectual property and trading balance in imports/exports of individual countries (Figure 5.3.4). In contrast, technical feasibility is well developed by means of a number of alternatives including integrated pest management as organic farming , biological pest control , precision farming and protected farming (Figure 5.3.5), although there are some limitations on their application at scales and associated costs . Normative tools providing a framework that guides government regulators, the private sector, civil society and other stakeholders on best practice in managing pesticides throughout their lifecycle can play a significant role in reducing pesticide pollution . 5.3.3.9 Reduce plastic pollution (F09) Reducing plastic pollution as a response option can be applied globally given the threat it poses to nexus elements notably, water (Figure 5.3.6). Growing demand for food and food convenience, while increasing food safety and reducing food loss and waste, has increased food packaging, including single-use plastics . Plastics can accumulate and persist in the environment for several decades, requiring short to prolonged impact mitigation strategies . Reducing plastic pollution has several impacts on the nexus elements (Figure 5.3.3). It can reduce access to healthy food, and reductions in plastic packaging for perishable foods can lower their shelf life reducing availability and increasing food waste. These impacts may be higher than the detrimental impacts of plastics although there is currently low confidence and high uncertainty in these numbers . Implementation of measures to reduce single-use plastics directly reduce plastic waste in water courses and hence improves water quality , which has positive impacts on wildlife and added benefits of reducing flood risk from blocked sewage systems. There is also good evidence that reducing plastic waste in soils improves soil quality (by reducing soil bulk density and improving soil porosity and hydraulic conductivity), resulting in higher yields . For example, shifting from use of plastic mulch to biodegradable mulch in farming can reduce plastic residue accumulation in soils, with numerous environmental benefits including enhanced food production . A higher percentage of recycled plastic content could reduce health risks, resulting in ‘longer human life per tonne of plastic recycled’ . In addition, reducing plastic pollution is associated with reduced exposure to carcinogenic or endocrine disrupting chemicals . Reducing plastic pollution through substitutes and recycling are socially acceptable but there are geographical disparities and information gaps and the cost of alternatives can preclude change (Figure 5.3.4). Political will to reduce pollution from plastics broadly exists but implementation is often problematic. Bans of single-use plastics have not been effective in some countries, and subsidizing recycling often requires costly government intervention . There is limited evidence of increased costs to consumers from novel packaging materials . There are high production costs associated with shifting from use of plastics in the food system yet the economic, social and environmental performance of biodegradable plastics is relatively low and uncertain . Depending on specific interventions, technical feasibility varies (Figure 5.3.5). Substitution of plastics (e.g., edible films and coatings) are still in less advanced stages and could create leakage to raw materials (e.g., wood and crops). Although policy-based options are better developed, such as those guided by biodegradable and compostable plastic product standards , they are weakly implemented or in a piecemeal manner at best . 5.3.3.10 Reduce food loss and waste (F10) Globally, about one third of all produced food for human consumption is lost and wasted across the entire supply chain annually . Such loss and waste are 1.3 billion tons per annum, equivalent to $1 trillion, and enough to feed more than double the number of undernourished people. Similarly, 8-10 percent of total greenhouse gas emissions are associated with food loss and waste . Reducing food loss and waste (food loss and waste) can be applied from local to global levels in different production and post-harvest supply chains (Figure 5.3.5), with particularly strong impacts on climate change mitigation (global potential: 1.4-3.3 GtCO2e yr-1) and adaptation (1.26 billion people) (Figure 5.3.4). Reducing food loss and waste can save up to 21 per cent of freshwater which could otherwise be used in growing food, or the equivalent of 21 m3 of freshwater per capita per year . Reducing food loss and waste has the potential to close the gap between food needed in 2050 and food available in 2010 by more than 20 per cent. It would avoid approximately 2.78 million km2 of intact land being converted to agriculture . Halving food loss and waste reduces the global need for cropland area by around 14 per cent . Reducing food loss and waste by 75 per cent could yield a 24 per cent decline in environmental pressures. If diets are optimized to reduce their environmental footprints, the pressure on cropland could be reduced by 50 per cent . There is also a risk that food loss and waste would increase due to a shift to sustainable healthy diets (Section 5.3.3.11) considering increased demand for more perishable foods (fruits and vegetables); the land savings offered by reduced food loss and waste require strong governance measures, and complementary measures to reduce expansion of agricultural lands (Section 5.3.3.1). Food loss and waste and public health intersect primarily in the domains of food security, food safety and nutrition, though it has no impact on food access or the nutritional value of food . The use of cold chain and packaging technologies, which can help reduce food loss and waste, increase energy and materials use in the food system . There are no known social acceptability issues associated with reducing food loss and waste (Figure 5.3.4). Economic analyses propose that reducing 25 per cent of food loss and waste would yield a $455 billion economic benefit, with an investment cost of $30 billion per year to 2025, and creating $255 billion in business opportunities . Reducing food loss and waste is strongly aligned with multiple international frameworks such as the SDGs , the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework, Paris Agreement and United Nations Food Systems Pathways. Approaches that have been adopted at different contexts and scales to reduce food loss and waste include developing technologies and infrastructures, changing behaviour, enhancing food management skills (e.g., sorting, processing, cooking), developing protocols and legislation, and redistributing food . Despite adopting different approaches, the SDG12.3 is woefully off-target. In order to achieve this target by 2030, many governments and businesses need to aggressively pursue the target-measure-act approach to reduce food waste, set a reduction target aligned with SDG target 12.3, measure food loss and waste to identify hot spots and monitor progress, and act more boldly to reduce food loss and waste . 5.3.3.11 Sustainable healthy diets (F11) To be healthy, diets must meet four core principles driven by human biology and universal in their application. Specifically, healthy diets are adequate in essential nutrients, without excess, diverse in a variety of foods for various food groups, balanced in energy intake and sources, and moderate in the consumption of nutrients and foods associated with negative health outcomes. Shifting to healthy diets includes both increasing access to the diversity of culturally relevant under-consumed foods while significantly reducing unhealthy over-consumption, notably of sugar, salt and fat including red-meat, impacting nearly 2 billion adults today (see also Section 5.4.3.6, H06, Reduce meat overconsumption; Section 5.5.3.15, C15, Sustainable healthy diets). Insufficient access to healthy diets is the most significant driver of premature mortality today affecting more than half of the global population and accounting for six of the top 11 risk factors driving the global burden of disease . A shift to healthy diets alone represents highly significant reduction in environmental pressure including a 29 per cent reduction in food-based GHG emissions and a 5-9 per cent reduction in other environmental impacts including reduced nutrient pollution, blue water consumption and land allocated to production. Shifts to healthy diets that also focus on sustainability could result in a 56 per cent reduction in food-based GHG emissions, with a 6-22 per cent reduction in other environmental factors without compromising health (Figure 5.3.3). Impacts on biodiversity and climate change are dependent on policies and actions that halt the conversion of ecosystems of high ecological integrity (Section 5.3.3.1). There are weaker effects on aquatic biodiversity which are more dependent on sustainable intensification (Section 5.3.3.3), ecological intensification (Sections 5.3.3.4, 5.3.3.5 and 5.3.3.6) and reduced pollution (Sections 5.3.3.7, 5.3.3.8 and 5.3.3.9). Shifts to healthy diets have weak impacts on water savings and may require additional freshwater for fruit, nut and vegetable production . They also have weak effects on water quality, increasing dependencies on nutrient applications and pollution (Sections 5.3.3.3, 5.3.3.4 and 5.3.3.7). Significant increases in diet-related non-communicable disease incidence have been observed in most countries, including where malnutrition is in decline (Section 2.4.1). Regionally adapted shifts to healthy diets would reduce 11 million premature deaths per year . Between food group diversity is particularly important to human health and nutrition , while within food group diversity is important to individual and cultural food choices compatible with healthy diets. This emphasizes that crop and food diversity receive insufficient attention despite their significant impacts on nexus issues. Current food production is incompatible with healthy diets with significant underproduction of major food groups . Alignment would require increases in fruit and vegetable production of 8.72 per cent yr-1 , and nut production by 150 per cent by 2050 , zero to minimal growth in cereal production , and significant global reductions in the production of ruminant meat independent of changes in meat production practices . Shifts to healthy diets are amongst the most cost-effective food system response option available (Figure 5.3.4). Diet-related non-communicable diseases currently cost $11 trillion in health costs alone; this is marginally lower than the economic value of food estimated at $12 trillion annually. Several actions are possible. Aligning agricultural, trade and subsidy policies, as well as health policies and actions, to food-based dietary guidelines (Box 5.3.8) and public food procurement (Box 5.3.9) have significant potential (see also Section 5.3.3.13). Consumer education and food health and sustainability labelling, alone or in combination, can guide consumer choice while providing a common standard for food companies. However, these are insufficient as stand-alone interventions . Healthy diets have been found to potentially contribute 70 per cent of the benefits of a comprehensive food system transformation through their direct impacts on health and their indirect impacts on production, including stemming pressures for land use change (Section 5.3.3.1) and promoting biodiversity (Sections 5.3.3.3, 5.3.3.4 and 5.3.3.5). Sustainable healthy diets are necessary to stay within the Paris Agreement long-term global goal to limit the increase in global average temperature to 1.5°C above pre-industrial levels and GBF targets . However, there is inadequate implementation of policies, programs and interventions – even those with proven efficacy – with only limited spending going to improving nutrition by national governments in their own countries and by international development organizations . Box 5.3.8 National food-based dietary guidelines Over 100 countries have established and revised food-based dietary guidelines aimed at aiding individuals and populations to make daily decisions on food-based intake . An increasing number of these now consider environmental impacts . Guidelines should be tailored to regional and cultural preferences within the frame of healthy, allowing people to follow diets that are both healthy and respectful of their culinary heritage . By adding sustainability, food-based dietary guidelines play a crucial role in shifting towards sustainable healthy diets by offering practical recommendations that promote both individual well-being and the well-being of the planet . Dietary guidelines highlight the importance of appropriate portion sizes to avoid overconsumption (Section 5.3.3.11) and food waste (Section 5.3.3.10), which decrease greenhouse gas emissions associated with food production and disposal. They can advise on sustainable consumption from aquatic ecosystems and increase the visibility of sustainable blue food consumption in their recommendations (Section 5.3.3.6). Incorporating sustainability guidance in food-based dietary guidelines enables diverse actors to understand the impacts of food choice on nexus elements, such as biodiversity, water and climate change. Food-based dietary guidelines can be used to shape policies that encourage healthy diets and sustainable food systems. Such guidelines provide an important opportunity to ensure a coherent approach to health and sustainability across ministries, notably in implementing regulation and incentives that support the recommendations of the guidelines. These can include taxes on sugary beverages or subsidies for biodiversity-friendly production practices (Sections 5.3.3.3, 5.3.3.4, 5.3.3.5 and 5.3.3.6). Food-based dietary guidelines can serve as the basis for clear, informative and standardized food labelling that helps consumers make healthier and more sustainable choices . They can also establish level precompetitive (before market) playing fields for private sector actors to disclose the environmental and health impact of their products, allowing consumers to make informed choices. International cooperation to develop global dietary guidelines that address the interconnected issues of health and sustainability can provide a consistent framework for collaboration. Box. 5.3.9. National school feeding programme in Brazil Public food procurement has proven to be an effective policy measure in advancing sustainable development, facilitating the shift towards sustainable production and fostering the adoption of healthy diets . A notable example is Brazil’s national school feeding programme (Programa Nacional de Alimentação Escolar), that emerged from a law specifying that at least 30 per cent of food purchases dedicated to public schools should be acquired directly from family farmers and a price premium given for certified organic and agroecological production . The proramme fostered the establishment of a structured, large-scale and predictable demand for sustainably produced, diversified and nutritious food that effectively incentivized family farmers to diversify their farming methods, diminish reliance on external inputs and embrace principles and practices of agroecology. Additionally, the programme has played a pivotal role in reducing hunger and child malnutrition and curbing school absenteeism . The success of the programme has generated great demand for scaling up the program to other countries. Since 2018, the Sustainable School Feeding Network serves as an international cooperation mechanism established by the Brazilian government to promote sustainable school feeding initiatives in collaboration with the Brazilian Cooperation Agency, the National Fund for Educational Development and the Food and Agriculture Organization through the Consolidation of School Feeding Programs in Latin America and the Caribbean project . Figure 5.3.11. School feeding programme in Dominican Republic. Photo credit: Fran Afonso/FAO under licence CC BY-NC 4.0. Box 5.3.10. Visual narratives using the 10 elements of agroecology can facilitate alignment of actions and integrated policy making for a shift to sustainable healthy diets Visual storytelling (Figure 5.3.12) has the potential to bring together various transitions into recognizable patterns, aiding in the development of response strategies for confronting complex sustainability issues. These challenges often stem from similar underlying narratives, assumptions, and reactions to factors driving change . The following example illustrates how a stepwise visual narrative using consumers, constituent of the agroecology element of circular and solidarity economy, as an entry point can identify plausible theories of transformative change and help design a supporting policy package to facilitate a system shift to sustainable healthy diets at scale that produces co-benefits for the biodiversity-water-food-health-climate change nexus. This involves changing consumption patterns towards nutritious, healthy and sustainable foods and thereby creating a demand for shifting markets and production patterns as follows: Figure 5.3.12. Designing a policy package facilitating the promotion of sustainable healthy diets to produce co-benefits along the biodiversity, water, food, health, climate change nexus. Adapted from FAO under licence CC BY-NC-SA 4.0. i) Awareness raising and advocacy: Food policy councils and other types of multistakeholder governance mechanisms that involve coalitions of producers and consumers can foster the design and prioritization of food-based dietary guidelines, the development of guidelines on appropriate public procurement practices at the municipal level, and advocate for legislative changes and the improvement of public procurement rules at the national level. Food-based dietary guidelines can play a key role in raising awareness about the importance of healthy, sustainable and culturally appropriate diets and promoting alternative dietary options, contributing to changes in consumer preferences and public demand for agroecological products. ii) Connecting demand and supply: Knowledge co-creation and sharing processes can foster the co-development of innovations that encourage shorter food supply chains. At the same time alliances between public canteens and local producers can be forged by policies to increase the share of locally produced healthy food in public food procurement. Strengthened links between production and consumption at the local level may encourage diversification of food production in the field and in food consumption. Public food procurement programmes create a structured demand for healthy, diverse and culturally appropriate ingredients, while creating at the same time opportunities for on-farm diversification aimed at continuous adequate supply of nutritious food throughout the year. iii) Shift to sustainable healthy diets: Sustainable public food procurement initiatives can boost local food production by creating synergistic alliances and reconnecting local and smallholder food producers with consumers. It can help create a structured demand for agroecological products allowing producers to diversify their production systems and enhance their resilience. Public canteens can further strengthen positive alliances through the recycling of organic residues (i.e. food waste) to provide local farmers with feed resources or organic matter for composting, thus helping to recycle nutrients back to the soil. Public procurement innovations to strengthen shorter supply chains will produce benefits in terms of less energy required for transport of food and to produce fertilizers. Shorter supply chains also have greater capacity to withstand, respond and adapt to shocks and stresses associated with climate change. 5.3.3.12 City region food systems (F12) City region food systems address sustainability of urban food systems through integrated strategies that include social values (liveability), health (diets, pollution, mobility) and total environmental footprint . The city region focus provides the capacity to integrate impacts across supply chains, account for localized externalities and leverage proximate environmental and commercial relationships with adjacent rural communities. City region food systems borrow from the concepts of integrated landscape approaches (see also Section 5.1.3.9, B09, Integrated landscape and seascape approaches) with the distinction that municipal governance provides an integrated policymaking space that is frequently missing in integrated landscape approaches . City governance, with its focus on enhancing liveability, demonstrates a heightened sensitivity to human well-being compared to governance at other scales . Moreover, this scale is better suited to tackle complex challenges, such as the need for integrated governance, territorial development and the ramifications of food, water, and environmental changes on human health . City region food systems emphasize both green spaces within city limits and in the proximate peri-urban spaces, largely rural, as uniquely adapted to providing nature-based solutions in spaces with dense human occupation (see also B02/C14, Urban nature-based solutions in Section 5.1.3.2 and Section 5.5.3.14). Such nature-based solutions, implemented by rural farming communities, include, but are not limited to, improving air quality, food production, disaster risk mitigation, recreational space contributing to mental health, and regulating water quality and quantity . Proximity to peri-urban systems enables closed-loop material flows and fosters socioeconomic connections between rural producers and urban consumers, reducing the anonymity and marginalization of farmer communities . It enhances farmer responsibility for reducing degradation pressures and elevates recognition for their contributions to sustainability . For example, pollution from food production affects dense urban populations directly , incurring mitigation costs such as water treatment and health costs associated with respiratory ailments (Figure 5.3.3). City region food systems have strong demands on, and incentives to improve, the management of water. A growing number of cities are recognizing and actively managing peri-urban agriculture to mitigate flood risks and to improve water quality while reducing treatment costs (Box 5.3.10). Cities concentrate food waste with unique economic opportunities to reduce and transform waste (Section 5.3.3.10), including waste water, to resource compost , feed or energy . Full recovery of wastewater could recover and reuse 13.4 per cent of global demand for nutrients (Section 5.3.3.7) while decreasing pollution though is increasingly challenged by chemical pollution of sewage sludge originating from urban sources . Making urban and peri-urban land available for food production generates localized employment and connects rural and urban citizens . Fully food-sovereign cities, however, may have reduced resilience to environmental change, such as climate variability, natural hazards, or local crop failures. By combining local production and consumption (i.e., short value chains) with trade, cities can mitigate risks, ensure a steady food supply, and address nexus challenges, thereby enhancing overall resilience . City leaders shape food environments with zoning laws, influencing access to healthy foods across neighbourhoods, prohibiting advertising and sale of unhealthy foods around schools and parks, and increasing walkability . This creates greater capacity to allocate green space to low and no emission mobility (e.g., shaded bike paths and walkways), reducing GHG emissions and improving health. Cities play a pivotal role in driving food system transformation, as evidenced by initiatives such as the Milan Urban Food Policy Pact , the C40 Good Food City Network , and the FAO Food for Cities program. Collectively these initiatives represent over 450 million inhabitants and over 621 urban food system practices and focus on five key actions: addressing food loss and waste (Section 5.3.3.10), enhancing food governance, promoting social and economic equity (Section 5.3.3.15), supporting sustainable food production (Sections 5.3.3.3, 5.3.3.4, 5.3.3.5 and 5.3.3.6), improving food supply and distribution, and advocating for sustainable healthy diets and nutrition (Section 5.3.3.11) . Despite the common and growing evidence by cities investing in systemic solutions there is scant mention of urban food systems in the United Nations Food Systems Summit Pathways (23 of 127 countries). This signals an important knowledge and policy gap despite the importance of cities in achieving global goals (Figure 5.3.4). Box 5.3.11. Sustainable food strategies and investments in the City of Paris In 2017, the city of Paris engaged with a diversity of stakeholders to develop an ambitious food system intervention strategy and action plan comprised of 40 specific actions across four themes . Collectively these actions impact all nexus elements. Adopted unanimously by the Paris Council in 2018, the city started by implementing actions where they have direct procurement capacity, with commitments to increase the proportion of sustainable food to 90 per cent by 2050 in local government-operated canteens and restaurants. Additional measures were to increase the share of agricultural land in the Paris basin by 50 per cent, while increasing the food consumed from this same area by 50 per cent. In collaboration with Eau de Paris, the city has invested €47 million to transition farmers in the basin to organic and other sustainable practices to reduce pesticide and nutrient pollution in water, thus, reducing municipal water purifications costs. To ensure long-term markets for engaged farmers, the city has set the target to increase organic food consumption to 75 per cent, also in line with their target of increasing local supply of healthy food to improve food and nutritional security while reducing GHG emissions from local food production and transportation. In addition to these integrated measures, the city is encouraging shifts to healthy flexitarian diets, reducing obesity and eliminating hunger. 5.3.3.13 Reform public spending (F13) When agricultural products or processes have externalities, two public instruments for including those characteristics in the market price are subsidies (for positive externalities, e.g., soil erosion reduction) and taxes (for negative externalities, e.g., practices generating health or environmental problems). Nevertheless, many countries implement agricultural subsidies, mainly focused on commodity crops and fisheries, which are mostly harmful to human health and the environment as they promote unsustainable practices . Most harmful subsidies influence international market costs and supply, distorting free trade rules and impacting small farmers in developing countries . Three of them are particularly important: (1) subsidies coupled with production inputs and/or outputs that promote commodity production even when market prices are below production costs; (2) market price support acting as border measures (e.g., tariffs, licenses and quotas) that increase or decrease domestic prices; and (3) subsidies decoupled from production inputs or outputs that may or may not be associated with agri-environmental practices . Agricultural subsidies are estimated to be $540 billion per year and rise to $1.8 trillion per year by 2030 under a business-as-usual scenario . Implicit subsidies (in the form of hidden costs or negative externalities) of food systems are estimated to be at least $12.4 trillion per year (with a range of $5.3 trillion to $19.8 trillion) valued at 12.4 per cent of global gross domestic product . In the context of low and middle income countries, explicit and implicit agricultural subsidies ($4.42 person day-1) can exceed by up to 38 per cent the value of direct payments needed to bring families above the poverty level ($3.2 person day-1) . Other harmful subsidies include: fisheries with $35.4 and $83 billion per year in implicit and explicit subsidies, respectively ; and fossil fuel subsidies valued at $1.3 and $5.7 trillion per year in explicit and implicit subsidies, respectively, which in the context of agriculture relates to subsidies benefiting reduced fuel, irrigation, refrigeration and fertilizer costs . Harmful subsidies could be removed or redirected towards public goods, including those impacted by the nexus elements (Figure 5.3.3). Several different options that consider a range of reforming or reducing of harmful subsidies have been recently analysed by referent institutions (i.e., International Food Policy Research Institute, World Bank, OECD) (Gautam, 2022; Henderson & Lankoski, 2019) and authors (Springmann & Freund, 2022) through global and farm-level models (Table 5.3.9). Two examples of models that compare impacts from reforming to different aims have provided an estimate of an increase in food production (crops 16 per cent, livestock 11 per cent, vegetables -2.5 to 19 per cent) and consumption (dairy products 16 per cent, fruits and vegetables 0.3 to 12 per cent and sugar 28 per cent), a reduction in agricultural prices (21 per cent) and in the costs of healthy diets (18 per cent), and a reduction in diet-related mortality and in GHG emissions, as well as positive impacts on biodiversity (restoration and avoided conversion) . A European farm-level model estimates multiple scenarios: one scenario shows the negative impact of market price support on habitat quality, GHG emissions and fertilizer use and another shows the positive impact of agri-environmental schemes on fertilizer use reduction (Table 5.3.9). Reforming harmful subsidies should be carefully planned and monitored as, for example, subsidies implemented to improve farmers’ livelihoods in low and lower-middle-income countries, may or may not help reduce socioeconomic and/or health problems . Redeploying agricultural subsidies away from harmful production and towards healthy food have shown a return-on-investment of more than 200:1 in Mexico . Some countries have tested medium to long-term subsidies transitions (e.g., transforming coupled subsidies into decoupled subsidies), sometimes incentivizing environmental-friendly practices and generating interesting examples (see Annex 5.3.2). This response option is aligned with most SDGs and Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework targets, notably target 18 (Figures 5.3.4 and 5.3.22). It is not directly dependant on other food systems response options but can influence all of them (Figure 5.3.21). Power imbalances among civil society, government, farmers and companies strongly influence the effectiveness and fairness of the response option. A guide for the reforming process has been proposed with six key actions : (1) estimate current support; (2) estimate impacts; (3) design the plan; (4) estimate future impact; (5) review the strategy; and (6) monitor. Although finance helps, successful reforming also depends on other key issues (many not linked to the economy) such as ethics, community-based-decisions, cultural patterns, effective institutions and key complementary measures such as: * Policy instruments including regulatory instruments that directly impose environmental requirements on farmers (e.g., standards); hybrid instruments that combine environmental requirements with options for farmers to participate in other non-environmental policies (e.g., cross-compliance mechanisms) and economic instruments like tariffs, tradeable allowances, taxes and subsidies (e.g., agri-environmental payment schemes) . * Internalizing externalities on certain agricultural products, foods and beverages . Here positive examples exist for tackling non-communicable diseases . Internalizing externalities through subsidies and taxes could change producer and/or consumer behaviour and have opposite effects to those generated by harmful subsidies such as price reduction of feed grains. Internalization could reduce the demand for unhealthy food, promoting healthy diets in populations over-consuming meat, while facilitating ecological restoration or the conversion of feed-producing lands to food-producing lands . * Improving relationships and interactions between rural producer communities and urban consumer communities through education, information and capacity-building regarding sustainable production and consumption and ecosystem conservation . * Considering research and development funding as subsidies . Green innovation with just 1 per cent of the value of farm output could increase productivity by 30 per cent and reduce GHG emissions. This could promote greater impacts of agroecological practices at scale, recognizing that investment in sustainable production practices pales in comparison to investment in conventional practices (Box 5.3.12) . Table 5.3.9. Nexus outcomes from models analysing options for reforming current subsidy policies. Model and scenario Food production Health Biodiversity (land) Greenhouse gas emissions Water: nutrient runoff Economic benefits Model A. Scenario: Reforming for green innovation through research and development: $70 billion (1 per cent agriculture output) by 2040. Results compared with business-as-usual scenario Increase global production: +16 per cent crops; +11 per cent livestock 21 per cent reduction in agricultural prices 18 per cent reduction in cost of healthy diet Consumption: +16 per cent in dairy products; +12 per cent in fruit & veg.; +28 per cent in sugar 1 per cent reduction in poverty ($1.9 person/day) 2.2 per cent of agricultural lands converted to natural use; 48 M ha restored; 56 M ha avoided conversion 40.5 per cent reduction in emissions (nearly 2.8 GtCO2eq) 1.6 per cent increase in gross national income at a global level ($2.4 trillion) Model B. Scenario: 100 per cent reforming of subsidies for health and environment by 2030. Results compared with business-as-usual scenario Horticultural production in: OECD countries: +19 per cent; Non-OECD countries: +3 per cent; Countries without subsidies: -2.4 per cent Increased fruit and vegetables consumption: OECD countries: +55 g/d (+10 per cent); Non-OECD countries: +31 g/d (+5 per cent); Countries without subsidies: +2 g/d (+0.3 per cent) Diet-related mortality: -444,000 deaths Changes in emissions: OECD countries: -1.7 per cent, Non-OECD countries: -0.2 per cent, Countries without subsidies: +0.5 per cent Increased labour supply: $ 12 billion Model C. Basic Scenario: no subsidies Habitat quality index value: 42.3 Total emissions 671,539kg CO2-eq./yr Nitrogen: 1,862 kg/year Phosphorus: 72 kg/year Profit €146,159 yr-1 Model C. Scenario: Market price support is valued at 10 per cent of the value of all outputs produced. Results in per cent compared to Basic Scenario: no subsidies -2.8 per cent +0.3 per cent Nitrogen: +7.3 per cent Phosphorus +4.6 per cent Model C. Scenario: Agri-environmental schemes, payment for fertilizer use reduction: is valued at 10 per cent of the value of the crop output. Results in per cent compared to Basic Scenario: no subsidies 0.0 per cent -0.9 per cent Nitrogen: -7.4 per cent Phosphorus: -1.5 per cent Box 5.3.12. Agroecology-based large-scale agriculture expands in Argentina without agricultural subsidies despite additional agricultural taxes Typically, government-citizen-farmer relationships mostly consist of a combination of low public spending, cheap food and sufficient profitability but with increasing sustainability. Hence, economically, input reduction is critical. Large-scale agroecology-based practices in the Argentinean Pampas are not supported by agricultural subsidies but, as in all large-scale farming, have additional agricultural taxes (export rights, a border measure). For example, these taxes are 12 per cent, 12 per cent, 33 per cent and 9 per cent for wheat, corn, soybean and beef, respectively . These taxes decrease domestic prices of grains, both for human and animal consumption, and thus serve as harmful subsidies that benefit animal industrial production. After 18 years of implementing agricultural taxes (export rights) for farmers, and with a historical lack of national support, the national government created the National Agroecology Directorate, which provided promotional activities for agroecology such as planning, education, information and capacity-building. This type of support is included in the public services category of subsidies and considered positive for society and the environment . While the national government maintained the export rights, these activities served as a first step towards agroecology promotion. Although some research projects on agroecology are currently being developed, they are very few compared to the support directed to conventional (high inputs) systems research. Therefore, reforming harmful subsidies from animal industrial production would be beneficial, particularly focusing on research and development for critical issues in agroecology such as sowing technologies (Figure 5.3.20). Yet, even with such economic disadvantages and little national support, large-scale agroecology-based practices (Figure 5.3.13) are rapidly expanding, promoting ecological stability, reduced dependence on external inputs, yields stabilization, cost reduction and increased profitability in certain production systems (wheat, beef grazing on native grasslands). Thus, agroecology is becoming a transformative option to significantly improve government-citizen-farmer relationships . After 35 years of herbicide-dependent no-till agriculture, 4 per cent of farmers (in 2014) implemented large-scale use of service crops; this increased to 19 per cent of farmers by 2020 . Between 2014 and 2023, large and small-scale agroecological approaches were applied to more than 100,000 ha, supported by 85 technical advisors and promoted in 225 meetings across 81 localities . Different actors and institutions were important to enable the observed change : * Consumers’ increased demand for healthy food and nature-friendly agriculture created fresh-food markets with direct consumer-producer contact promoted by NGOs. * Small-scale farmers adjacent to cities formed cooperatives and provided these local markets with agroecology-based, high-quality and low-price foods * Urban dwellers’ concerns for health risks of biocides, including protests against them . * Farmers interested in pesticide-free agriculture formed collaborative networks to share experiences and innovations . * Farmers, motivated by increasing herbicide-resistant weeds, rising water tables and production and income losses, sought technological alternatives . * Scientists, technicians and others raised concerns over the unsustainability of high-input agriculture and provided evidence of comparative productivity, GHG balance, reduction of pesticide and fossil fuel use , biodiversity-friendly productivity and biodiversity-weeds relationships and pesticides-health associations . Figure 5.3.13. Three examples of conventional practices adapted to meet agroecological needs in Argentina. The practices use technologies to sow crops in large-scale agriculture without using herbicides or tillage practices. Left photo: a machine designed to apply pesticides was modified to create equipment that applies seeds and fertilizers over a mature or green growing crop (e.g., corn). Middle photo: this machine cuts the roots of unwanted plant species 3-12 cm under the soil surface leaving most of the ground cover intact. Right photo: rolling crimper acting on winter service crops (e.g., rye, oat, vetch) generates mechanical drying of the plants and helps prepare the soil and organic cover for summer crops (e.g., corn, soybean). These management practices might be combined with herbicides or tillage in certain moments or years but, in that case, with required frequencies well below the historical use. Photo credits: Left: E. Pivetta under licence CC BY 4.0; middle: X. Vottero uder licence CC BY 4.0; right: D. Pettovello & F. Alvira-Tekoporá under licence CC BY-NC 4.0. 5.3.3.14 Foster gender-transformative approaches (F14) Women play key roles in food systems ranging from producing, processing, distributing, marketing and consuming (. Gender-based discrimination and human rights violations are major causes of poverty and food and nutrition insecurity . Gender often interacts with other forms of discrimination, such as socioeconomic class, age, ethnicity and disability, requiring intersectional analyses and policy responses (see Chapter 4, Box 4.12). Responses to address this situation include strengthening women’s access to and control of assets, notably land; better inclusion in value chains, including better access to markets and improving labour conditions and legislation and greater women’s (economic) empowerment (Figure 5.3.14). However, relatively few studies assess the effectiveness of these responses and even fewer address gender equity in urban or aquatic food contexts . Reviews show mixed impacts on nexus elements of increased tenure security for women (Figure 5.3.4). Studies in Uganda and Rwanda demonstrate positive impacts on soil conservation, notably among female-headed households . found weaker evidence of impacts on natural resource management, food security, technology adoption or productivity but strong impacts on bargaining power and decision-making on consumption and (human) capital investment. These mixed findings may relate to how tenure is institutionalized and experienced , highlighting the need to distinguish legal security and contextual or de facto security . This option is more effective when bundled with other response options, such as access to credit, information and government services for women . Women face many hidden costs related to market access and integration, which are systematically overlooked by public and private sector actors and institutions . Such costs are multi-dimensional (economic, social and/or political), creating obstacles for marginalized food entrepreneurs to professionalize and upscale their businesses. They influence risk perceptions, time and resource allocation, and business investment decisions. Innovations supporting inclusive business are only effective when applicable to specific problems and contexts, affordable and accessible to all. Better market access and integration does not automatically improve nutrition security. A review of thirteen projects in Africa and Asia, aimed at improving market access and integration, shows that this is due to a focus on cash crop production and prioritizing business investments and other costs such as health care over buying high quality food . Economic inclusion programming - including social safety nets, livelihoods, job and financial inclusion programs - strengthens women’s economic opportunities, income, and asset ownership and supports shifts in gender norms, such as increased mobility . Cash or in-kind transfers combined with high-quality complementary programming, such as agricultural extension produce significant improvements in agriculture and nutrition outcomes . Generally, efforts to improve gender equity often adopt a narrow focus on women, overlooking men or gender relations . This risks putting the blame, or burden for change, on women . Figure 5.3.14. Gendered food system. Source: Njuki under licence CC BY 4.0. Box 5.3.13 Women food entrepreneurs in Kenya and Burkina Faso Women food entrepreneurs in Kenya and Burkina Faso city slums play an important role in the food and nutrition security of vulnerable populations. Yet, their contributions and challenges are poorly understood and not visible in policy. Women food entrepreneurs in Ouagadougou and Kisumu operate under precarious conditions, amidst rapid urban development. They form a heterogeneous group whose ambitions and motivations differ. Group participation increases women’s voice and empowerment and access to resources and markets. Moreover, bottom-up and co-created innovations leverage business viability and sustainability by introducing new food mixtures, higher added value and sales, and reduced input costs and food waste. Women’s own knowledge constitutes the departure point for developing hybrid technologies, upscaling and professionalization, but support and mutual validation by governmental actors and private sector stakeholders is required. Tailor-made policy instruments should enable women food entrepreneurs to address hidden costs faced due to their social-economic status and marginality. Moreover, women food entrepreneur groups require tangible knowledge on soil management and sustainable farm inputs and technology to shift towards circular agriculture. Innovation can come from multiple directions. Co-creation between different women food entrepreneur groups, science and the private sector offer a promising route towards adopting affordable, applicable and accessible innovations in small-scale agri-business . In Kisumu, Kenya, a group of female traders established the Kibuye market traders’ group. This group participated in a joint action research project, involving a range of public and private partners and knowledge institutes in Kenya, Burkina Faso and the Netherlands (Kingdom of the). The Kibuye market traders’ group developed an initiative to turn market food waste into compost. This compost was packaged and sold after testing and certification by a local research institute. Collecting food waste at the market became an important business opportunity, contributing to better incomes for the traders’ group members. In addition, it provided access to cheaper and more sustainable input for food farming in an urban context, which contributed to greener cities, healthier diets and better incomes . In the process of knowledge sharing and co-creation, local government and private sector stakeholders have become more attuned to women food entrepreneurs’ diversified priorities and needs to access land and water resources, market space, and to enhance added values for food commodities, food security and income opportunities. Women food entrepreneur groups have become more aware of the commonality of the obstacles and constraints that are shared across different groups in producing, processing and marketing food commodities in urban and peri-urban slums. This has prompted them to seek strength in collective efforts by identifying and establishing new linkages with other women food entrepreneur groups, private business and local government stakeholders. Finally, all stakeholders involved have developed a new understanding of a range of sustainable soil amendments that can be locally sourced, either from organic manure that some women food entrepreneurs self-produce already or from local mines in the form of rock dust (now a waste product), and of sustainable food processing methods using hybrid technology . 5.3.3.15 Indigenous food systems (F15) Indigenous Peoples and local communities’ (IPLC) food systems integrate cultural dimensions to food . These include access to food from ancestral and structural assets, cosmological and web of life values, sovereignty and sustainable production, and integrative health (Table 5.3.10). However, these food systems face challenges related to land tenure problems , gender issues , social justice , horizontal cooperation and the embracing of plural common vision , which are important considerations in addressing trade-offs between the nexus elements. The future of IPLC food systems depends on the formalization of sustainable and sovereign land use and territory aiming at integrative health that encompasses public health actions, promotion of sustainable healthy diets and respect of traditional food diversity and production. Table 5.3.10. Dimensions of Indigenous Peoples’ and local communities’ relationships to their food systems. Dimension Indigenous Peoples’ and local communities’ food system relationship Ancestral & structural assets Relates to the traditional values of food production. It implies further recognition of the structural assets from both Indigenous Peoples and local communities in the way the food is produced under dynamic responses in a historical perspective. For example, advances in diversification, production, storage and reproduction of potato species in the high Andes . Cosmological & web of life values Relates to the preservation, study and feedback with the cosmological and web-of-life values of local food systems. It includes the understanding of food requirements that connects IPLC with their cosmovision, cultural assets and well-being (e.g., the trust hunt ritual in the Siberian Northeast ). Sustainability & sovereignty Relates to the eco-cultural sustainability of IPLC food systems from a historical perspective and scenarios of climate change. It implies the development of alliances for governance through dialogue between Indigenous, non-Indigenous and local communities when involving social participation in social-ecological systems . Integrative health Encompasses the support for and development of safe, healthy, sovereign and purposeful IPLC food systems from the joint work of conventional, complementary and traditional medicine. It develops nutritional, psychological and physical approaches to improve well-being in IPLC communities (e.g., “the food as a way to health mind, body and spirit” ). Indigenous and local knowledge provides a distinct understanding of the nexus in relation to the interlinkages among IPLC food systems, biodiversity conservation and human habitats . These systems are diverse and imply multiple perspectives related to ethics, values and world views . Critical to IPLC food systems are land access and distribution and an emphasis on community-based lifestyles . IPLC food systems are highly adept at navigating sustainable futures, including adaptive responses to water scarcity and climate change (Figure 5.3.3). ILK is used to recognize biodiversity benefits of small-scale production systems, wild foods or extensive rangeland systems . Foods gathered or produced based on ILK underpin community health and nutrition . Importantly, IPLC world views question contested values related to extractivism and overconsumption and offer novel ways of understanding people’s relationships with food, nature and its sustainable management (see Chapter 7, online Annex 7.1). Two case studies in this section highlight lessons from IPLC food systems. These reflect important aspects of Indigenous knowledge and values embedded within IPLC food systems. The first case study, in the Yucatan Peninsula, Mexico, describes the Mayan milpa that has sustained the population for more than 3,500 years until today (Box 5.3.14). The milpa produces food, while fostering identity and culture, offering complete and quality nutrition, and has allowed evolution with the productive diversity of home gardens . In the second case study from Malaysian Borneo, the role of valuing regional food heritage and long-standing sustainable food production and foraging practices through the adoption of geographical indication and cultural festivals is highlighted (Box 5.3.15). Shared elements that are important to facilitate successes seen in these case studies include respecting local food diversity and production systems, and actions to support IPLC rights for collective land tenure recognition, e.g., through cross-sectoral committees to formalise access and declare legal tenure of traditional territories. Two further case studies of Indigenous food systems are provided in the online Annex 5.3.3: Indigenous agricultural practices in Khambashe, South Africa and the traditional knowledge and sustainability of the Tikuna, Cocama and Yagua food system. Box 5.3.14. Yucatecan Mayan food systems4 The Mayan identity is associated with food – maya signifies “children of the corn”. The Mayan milpa continues to evolve today as one of the most powerful examples of agroecology originating from Indigenous knowledge (Figure 5.3.15). Productive polycultures based on Indigenous crops including maize, beans and squash (also referred to as the three sisters) constitute a core part of Indigenous knowledge systems which recognize the complementarity between the species often planted in the milpa. The maize provides a support structure for the climbing beans, whose foliage reduces maize losses to bird predation. The nitrogen fixed by the beans benefits the maize and the squash, with its higher photo-oxidation rates, growing in the lower light understory below the maize and beans. Few modern systems match its capacity as an efficient polyculture, providing whole-of-diet nutritional value, while matching the environmental conditions of nutrient- and water-poor lands of the Yucatan Peninsula. The milpa is part of FAO’s Globally Important Agricultural Heritage Systems (GIAHS) and the most important source of Mayan food . The milpa, while focused on the “three sisters” of maize, beans and squash, can include up to 50 edible species. There are substantial household nutritional contributions of the milpa, including from more recent species diversification such as the addition of potato . In companion home gardens up to nearly 400 edible species are grown, and beekeeping with up to 17 stingless bee species is practiced. Livestock integrated into the system add 10 species of animals and 5 species of fish. Adjacent forests are a source of as many as 250 species used for food and other uses. Located close to the kitchen, raised wooden bed structures locally known as ka’an che’, are used to grow frequently used species such as: cebollina (Allium schoenoprasum), radish (Raphanus sativus), onion (Allium cepa), epazote (Dysphania ambrosioides), cilantro (Coriandrum sativum), Yucatan taro (Xanthosoma yucataense) and different chiles (Capsicum spp). Modern polycultures have borrowed from this system with legume rotations, but unlike the milpa system where the three species are sown together in the same hole, current practices often separate the species in space (between rows) or time (rotations). Legume polycultures are foundational practices in ecological and sustainable intensification in both modern and traditional systems. Figure 5.3.15. Mayan food systems in the Yucatan peninsula. Left photo: Milpa showing the three sisters – maize, beans and squash. Right photo: Raised wooden bed structures locally known as ka’an che’ located near the household kitchen and to grow frequently used species. Photo credits: Francisco Rosado-May under licence CC BY-NC 4.0. An increasing challenge of Yucatec Mayan food systems is the rapid erosion of local knowledge which is leading to increased dependence on agrochemical inputs for agricultural production and compromising sustainability. The concept of intercultural co-creation of knowledge as an important pathway to recover lost sustainability features demands understanding the thinking that drives decisions and actions from the Mayan culture, including understanding the cosmogony of culture. Using the 10 elements of agroecology (Box 5.3.1) as a guide could be useful, given that each of the 10 elements (diversity, co-creation and sharing of knowledge, synergies, efficiency, recycling, resilience, human and social values, culture and food traditions, responsible governance, and circular and solidarity economy) are present in the Maya thinking and vocabulary although their interactions and arrangement are distinct. The design and management of the Mayan food system rests on several core values which continue to drive evolution and co-creation of the system while emphasizing community. All three then set conditions that lead to recycling, efficiency and diversity – three elements that are necessary for all synergies to occur. These result in food-based tradition at the core of community resilience. The elements are connected by responsible governance, which in turn reflects values, which in turn reinforce governance. A common expression among Yucatec Mayan farmers is “producing food to sustain my family is hard, therefore it is critical to think about designing systems that can be as self-sustainable as possible, so that I can have time to manage different food subsystems such as the milpa, homegarden, beekeeping, ka’an che’, hunting and gathering”. This means diversity of food subsystems as well as diversity within each subsystem is key for the Yucatecan Mayan food systems. Box 5.3.15. Valuing regional food heritage in the heart of Borneo4 Bario is a village located in the highlands of Borneo in the Malaysian state of Sarawak and bordering the Indonesian state of Kalimantan. It is the homeland of the Kelabit community, a close-knit community traditionally residing in longhouses, and the Penan community, a nomadic community, both living harmoniously in these highlands . While some areas within the highlands have been cleared for cultivation, the remaining is rich in biodiversity and covered by tropical rainforests . Land ownership in the Kelabit and Penan communities is protected under Native Customary Rights Land, either through inheritance from ancestors or through rights acquired by felling primary forest under their shifting cultivation practices . The Kelabit community combines irrigated wet rice cultivation with agro-silvo pastoral systems, sustainably accessing resources from nearby forests and salt springs (Figure 5.3.16, left). Traditionally each family cultivates 1 to 2 ha of rice, primarily for their own consumption, utilizing an intricate system of irrigation through bamboo pipes . This is a slow-growing variety of rice that takes six months to mature, with one crop cycle per year, that relies on low mechanisation approaches and the use of buffalo for tillage . During the fallow period buffalo reside in the fields, contributing to soil fertility and weed control . The rice of these villages serves as food, but also underpins cultural identity and serves as a symbol of status . Today the rice is registered as a product of geographical indication and is sold as premium grade rice, increasing the income of its producers . In 2005, the Kelabit community initiated “Pesta Nukenen”, a celebration of its culinary heritage, rice farming and wild harvest of a diversity of forest foods (Figure 5.3.16, right). Figure 5.3.16. Kelabit community food heritage in Borneo. Left photo: paddy fields and locally grown rice. Right photo: wild harvested foods. Photo credits: Maysoun Mustafa under licence CC BY-NC 4.0. A tension exists within the highlands with regards to the introduction of mechanisation in farming techniques, which improve productivity but have unintended consequences . While modernisation has made life easier, it has environmental and economic costs . The community has identified conservation, eco-tourism, agriculture and research as preferred approaches for long-term development by the community . Agroforestry and integrated farming systems are valued as means of food and energy production and sustainable livelihoods that is compatible with highland ecosystem conservation . 5.3.3.16 Access to natural resources and land (F16) Secure access to land is fundamental to sustainable food systems and to addressing the challenges caused by habitat and biodiversity loss, land and water degradation, and GHG emissions (Figure 5.3.4). Unequal access to natural resources encompasses aspects of social, ethnic and gender inequalities, and reflects power differentials (Figure 5.3.4). Recognizing the importance of equitable access, the produced voluntary guidelines on the responsible governance of tenure of land, fisheries and forests, specifically addressing the needs of vulnerable and marginalized groups such as smallholders, IPLC and women. The FAO guidelines emphasize the importance of local contexts, and the various mechanisms and institutions – formal and informal, individual and communal – through which natural resources are accessed (cf. Ostrom ; Peluso & Ribot ), which need to be taken into account when designing tenure systems to secure access. Moving beyond titling and formal registration as the main mechanisms for securing access, the guidelines propose a wider range of policies and legislation, as well as advocacy programmes, to strengthen tenure security for vulnerable groups to promote more equitable and secure access to land, fisheries and forests for these groups. Here, the FAO recognizes that strengthening tenure security relates both to legal security and contextual or perceived security, and refers to the confidence that rights will be acknowledged and upheld by all other actors, including the state . Formal registration and individual titling have long been a dominant response to address tenure insecurity . Research, however, shows that for some marginalized groups, formal registration and titling may actually undermine tenure security – notably for women . Individual titling can, especially among the poor, be counterproductive, resulting in distress sales for low prices ; . Without specific provisions for vulnerable groups, national land laws often fail to protect the individual and collective rights of peasants, tenants, IPLC and other vulnerable groups, and without proper recourse mechanisms may even undermine their rights . Recognition of these flaws resulted in calls for fit-for-purpose land administration systems , and an increased focus on titling and registration communal lands – notably to safeguard IPLC tenure security . Examples of communal land registration and protection exist, for instance, in Brazil , Mozambique and Scotland . Registration, however, needs to be accessible, both in terms of costs as well as procedures and transparency of conditions . A review of 33 community land formalizations in fifteen countries showed that this is quite often not the case. The review confirmed the importance of the intention by other actors – notably the state and companies – to acknowledge and uphold community rights. State actors often allowed companies to take shortcuts to land registration, undermining community rights. The FAO voluntary guidelines, revised in 2022, recognize the complexities, and hence, as mentioned above, propose a more localized and comprehensive approach to strengthen tenure security. Reviews of the impacts of strengthening land tenure and access rights on the nexus elements report mixed findings (Figure 5.3.3). Regarding food security, a number of studies report positive impacts of increased security on agricultural productivity . Increased security results are associated with better access to loans, and a willingness to invest in agriculture . Others, however, have found mixed or little support for this positive relation with increased productivity . They relate this to differences between de jure and de facto security, i.e., how individuals or groups perceive tenure security . Reviews do not always clearly distinguish between different tenure security interventions. Furthermore, increased productivity does not always translate automatically in food or nutrition security . Supporting land and forest tenure results in mostly positive impacts on environmental outcomes, particularly for soil and (agro)biodiversity conservation . Impacts, however, vary according to the type of tenure intervention. A large-scale individual titling programme in the Brazilian Amazon resulted in an increase in deforestation, especially among medium and smallholders, and was connected to crop and cattle prices increases . This is consistent with the finding that strengthening individual tenure in combination with the promotion of market-based food production (framing food as a commodity) has negative impacts on soil and biodiversity conservation . Collective land titling, on the other hand, is more often associated with positive outcomes for biodiversity conservation , though a study by Kraus and colleagues found that this depends on community capacities to enforce compliance with conservation measures vis-à-vis third parties. Hence, titling alone is not sufficient for positive environmental outcomes, it requires active community support and supporting legislation in other state sectors such as trade and environmental affairs . Evidence of impacts on water quality is lacking, but positive impacts are found in sustainable watershed management . A comprehensive review of tenure on human well-being reported positive outcomes in most cases (82 per cent) but found evidence of trade-offs between human well-being and environmental outcomes . Strengthening customary communal rights through legal recognition of these rights and local institutions, and/or communal titling, is especially important in forestry , fisheries and pastoralism . While this is recognized by many governments, a review of forestry tenure reforms in 23 countries concluded that community tenure is less robust than the tenure of companies and smallholders, reducing possibilities for community livelihood and ecological benefits (see also ). Environmental and human well-being impacts of tenure security in pastoralism and fisheries are severely understudied. There is some evidence of pastoralists benefitting from rights-based approaches to biodiversity conservation in terms of access to grazing, but these come with trade-offs regarding biodiversity conservation . Several reviews stress the importance of bundling responses, such as combining strengthening tenure security with capacity-building, but this is less studied ." ;
    dcterms:identifier "5.3.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Nexus response options for food" .

